window.UNITS=[{"id": 1, "title": "Drama", "overview": "Is unit mein Greek tragedy se lekar Elizabethan, Restoration, 18th-19th century, modern British, American, Irish, Absurd, Indian English aur world drama tak sab aata hai, saath mein dramatic terms bhi. NET mein zyada sawal author-work matching, chronology, characters, famous quotes aur terms ke coiner par aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Greek Tragedy and Aristotle's Poetics", "points": ["Three great Greek tragedians: Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides (chronological order).", "Aeschylus: Oresteia trilogy (458 BC) - Agamemnon, The Libation Bearers, The Eumenides; Prometheus Bound; added the second actor.", "Sophocles: Oedipus Rex (c. 429 BC), Antigone (c. 441 BC), Electra, Oedipus at Colonus; added the third actor.", "Euripides: Medea (431 BC), Hippolytus, The Trojan Women, The Bacchae (405 BC); famous for frequent use of deus ex machina.", "Greek tragedies were staged at the City Dionysia in Athens as tetralogies: three tragedies plus one satyr play.", "Aristotle's Poetics (c. 335 BC): tragedy is 'the imitation of an action that is serious, complete, and of a certain magnitude' producing catharsis through pity and fear.", "Six parts of tragedy (Aristotle): plot (mythos), character, thought, diction, song (melos), spectacle (opsis). Plot is 'the soul of tragedy'.", "Aristotle insisted only on unity of action; unities of time and place were formulated by Castelvetro (1570) - neo-classical rule of three unities.", "Key Aristotelian terms: hamartia, peripeteia, anagnorisis, catharsis, mimesis; Oedipus Rex is his model tragedy.", "Nietzsche, The Birth of Tragedy (1872): tragedy born from Apollonian and Dionysian forces (Apollo = form, Dionysus = ecstasy)."]}, {"title": "Greek and Roman Comedy; Seneca", "points": ["Old Comedy: Aristophanes - The Clouds (423 BC, satirises Socrates), The Birds (414 BC), Lysistrata (411 BC), The Frogs (405 BC, contest of Aeschylus and Euripides).", "Parabasis: in Old Comedy the chorus directly addresses the audience on the poet's behalf.", "New Comedy: Menander (Dyskolos / The Grouch) - domestic plots, stock characters; model for Roman comedy.", "Roman comedy: Plautus (Menaechmi - source of Shakespeare's The Comedy of Errors; Miles Gloriosus - braggart soldier) and Terence (The Eunuch, The Brothers).", "Seneca (c. 4 BC - AD 65): closet tragedies like Thyestes, Medea, Phaedra - five acts, ghosts, revenge, bloodshed, rhetorical speeches.", "Senecan tragedy shaped Elizabethan revenge tragedy: Kyd's The Spanish Tragedy, Shakespeare's Titus Andronicus and Hamlet.", "Horace, Ars Poetica: drama should have five acts; 'decorum'; violence not to be shown on stage (Medea should not kill her children before the audience).", "Stock characters from Roman comedy: miles gloriosus (braggart soldier), parasite, clever slave, senex (old man) [purane fixed type ke kirdar]."]}, {"title": "Medieval English Drama", "points": ["Drama re-emerged from church liturgy: the Easter trope 'Quem Quaeritis' (Whom do you seek?) c. 10th century.", "Mystery / Miracle plays: Bible-based cycles staged by trade guilds on pageant wagons at Corpus Christi - York, Chester, Wakefield (Towneley), N-Town cycles.", "The Second Shepherds' Play (Wakefield cycle, by the 'Wakefield Master') - Mak the sheep-stealer; mixes farce with the Nativity.", "Morality plays: allegorical personified virtues and vices - The Castle of Perseverance (c. 1425), Mankind, Everyman (late 15th c.).", "Everyman: summoned by Death; only Good Deeds accompanies him to the grave; Fellowship, Kindred and Goods desert him.", "Vice: comic tempter figure of moralities - ancestor of Iago and Richard III.", "Interludes: short secular plays - John Heywood's The Four PP; Henry Medwall's Fulgens and Lucrece (c. 1497), the earliest known secular English play.", "Ralph Roister Doister (Nicholas Udall, c. 1553): first regular English comedy; Gammer Gurton's Needle: early English comedy (c. 1553-60).", "Gorboduc (Thomas Sackville and Thomas Norton, 1561/62): first English tragedy in blank verse, Senecan model, with dumb shows."]}, {"title": "University Wits, Kyd and Marlowe", "points": ["University Wits: John Lyly, George Peele, Robert Greene, Thomas Lodge, Thomas Nashe, Christopher Marlowe (Thomas Kyd often grouped with them though not a university man).", "John Lyly: courtly prose comedies Campaspe, Endimion; euphuism (from his prose romance Euphues, 1578).", "George Peele: The Old Wives' Tale, The Arraignment of Paris, David and Bethsabe.", "Robert Greene: Friar Bacon and Friar Bungay, James IV; in Greene's Groatsworth of Wit (1592) attacked Shakespeare as an 'upstart crow, beautified with our feathers'.", "Thomas Kyd: The Spanish Tragedy (c. 1587) - founded English revenge tragedy; Hieronimo, ghost, play-within-play; probable author of a lost Ur-Hamlet.", "Marlowe: Tamburlaine the Great (c. 1587), Doctor Faustus (c. 1592), The Jew of Malta (c. 1589, Barabas), Edward II (c. 1592), Dido Queen of Carthage.", "Ben Jonson praised 'Marlowe's mighty line' - Marlowe perfected dramatic blank verse.", "'Was this the face that launched a thousand ships / And burnt the topless towers of Ilium?' - Faustus on Helen of Troy.", "'Why, this is hell, nor am I out of it' - Mephistopheles in Doctor Faustus.", "Marlowe's heroes are over-reachers (Harry Levin, The Overreacher, 1952) driven by lust for power, knowledge, wealth."]}, {"title": "Shakespeare", "points": ["First Folio (1623) compiled by John Heminges and Henry Condell: 36 plays in Comedies, Histories, Tragedies; Ben Jonson's tribute 'He was not of an age, but for all time'.", "Globe Theatre built 1599 by the Lord Chamberlain's Men (King's Men from 1603).", "Major tragedies: Hamlet (c. 1600-01), Othello (c. 1603-04), King Lear (c. 1605-06), Macbeth (c. 1606); also Romeo and Juliet, Julius Caesar, Antony and Cleopatra, Coriolanus.", "Problem plays (term by F. S. Boas, 1896): Measure for Measure, All's Well That Ends Well, Troilus and Cressida.", "Romances / last plays: Pericles, Cymbeline, The Winter's Tale, The Tempest (c. 1611).", "Quotes: 'Frailty, thy name is woman' (Hamlet); 'The quality of mercy is not strained' (Portia, Merchant of Venice); 'All the world's a stage' (Jaques, As You Like It).", "Quotes: 'Life's but a walking shadow... a tale told by an idiot' (Macbeth); 'O brave new world' (Miranda, The Tempest); 'Ripeness is all' (Edgar, King Lear).", "Coleridge on Iago: 'the motive-hunting of motiveless malignity'; Johnson, Preface to Shakespeare (1765): Shakespeare is 'the poet of nature'.", "A. C. Bradley, Shakespearean Tragedy (1904): studies Hamlet, Othello, King Lear, Macbeth; character-based criticism.", "T. S. Eliot, 'Hamlet and His Problems' (1919): called Hamlet 'most certainly an artistic failure'; coined objective correlative.", "Jan Kott, Shakespeare Our Contemporary (1964); G. Wilson Knight, The Wheel of Fire (1930)."]}, {"title": "Jacobean and Caroline Drama", "points": ["Ben Jonson: comedy of humours - Every Man in His Humour (1598), Volpone (1606), Epicoene (1609), The Alchemist (1610), Bartholomew Fair (1614); also masques with Inigo Jones.", "Volpone characters: Volpone (fox), Mosca (fly), Voltore (vulture), Corbaccio (raven), Corvino (crow) - beast-fable names.", "John Webster: The White Devil (1612, Vittoria Corombona, Flamineo) and The Duchess of Malfi (c. 1613-14; Bosola, Ferdinand, the Cardinal).", "'Cover her face; mine eyes dazzle: she died young' - Ferdinand; 'I am Duchess of Malfi still' - the Duchess.", "T. S. Eliot: 'Webster was much possessed by death / And saw the skull beneath the skin' (Whispers of Immortality).", "The Revenger's Tragedy (1606, now attributed to Middleton, earlier to Tourneur) - Vindice.", "Middleton and Rowley, The Changeling (1622) - Beatrice-Joanna and De Flores; Middleton, Women Beware Women; A Chaste Maid in Cheapside.", "John Ford: 'Tis Pity She's a Whore (pub. 1633) - incest of Giovanni and Annabella; The Broken Heart.", "Beaumont and Fletcher: Philaster, The Maid's Tragedy; Beaumont: The Knight of the Burning Pestle (1607) - early metatheatrical parody.", "Thomas Dekker: The Shoemaker's Holiday (1599, Simon Eyre); Marston: The Malcontent (1603-04); Massinger: A New Way to Pay Old Debts (Sir Giles Overreach).", "Puritans closed the theatres in 1642; they reopened with the Restoration of Charles II in 1660."]}, {"title": "Restoration Drama (1660-1700)", "points": ["After 1660 two patent companies (Killigrew and Davenant); women actresses appeared on the English stage for the first time.", "Comedy of manners: witty, cynical comedies of fashionable London - Etherege, Wycherley, Congreve, Vanbrugh, Farquhar.", "George Etherege: The Man of Mode (1676) - Dorimant (rake), Sir Fopling Flutter (fop).", "William Wycherley: The Country Wife (1675) - Horner, Margery Pinchwife, 'china scene'; The Plain Dealer (1676).", "William Congreve: The Old Bachelor (1693), Love for Love (1695), The Way of the World (1700) - Mirabell, Millamant, Lady Wishfort, the 'proviso scene'.", "Vanbrugh: The Relapse (1696), The Provoked Wife (1697); Farquhar: The Recruiting Officer (1706), The Beaux' Stratagem (1707).", "Aphra Behn: The Rover (1677) - first English woman to earn a living by writing.", "Heroic drama (rhymed couplets, love vs honour): Dryden's The Conquest of Granada (1670-71), Aureng-Zebe (1675); Dryden's All for Love (1677) is in blank verse on Antony and Cleopatra.", "The Rehearsal (1671) by the Duke of Buckingham satirised heroic drama through the character Bayes (Dryden).", "Thomas Otway: Venice Preserv'd (1682), The Orphan.", "Jeremy Collier, A Short View of the Immorality and Profaneness of the English Stage (1698) attacked Restoration comedy."]}, {"title": "Eighteenth-Century Drama", "points": ["Sentimental comedy: Colley Cibber's Love's Last Shift (1696) regarded as the first; Richard Steele's The Conscious Lovers (1722) the classic example.", "John Gay: The Beggar's Opera (1728) - first ballad opera; Macheath, Polly Peachum; satire on Walpole; source of Brecht's The Threepenny Opera (1928).", "Joseph Addison: Cato (1713), neo-classical tragedy; George Lillo: The London Merchant (1731) - domestic/bourgeois tragedy.", "Henry Fielding's political satires (Tom Thumb 1730, The Historical Register) led to Walpole's Licensing Act of 1737 - the Lord Chamberlain's censorship.", "Oliver Goldsmith: 'An Essay on the Theatre; or, A Comparison Between Laughing and Sentimental Comedy' (1773) attacked sentimental comedy.", "Goldsmith: The Good-Natur'd Man (1768), She Stoops to Conquer (1773) - Marlow, Kate Hardcastle, Tony Lumpkin; subtitle 'The Mistakes of a Night'.", "R. B. Sheridan: The Rivals (1775) - Mrs Malaprop (malapropism), Lydia Languish, Captain Absolute, Bob Acres.", "Sheridan: The School for Scandal (1777) - Lady Sneerwell, Joseph and Charles Surface, the 'screen scene'; The Critic (1779), a burlesque of theatre.", "David Garrick: leading actor-manager of Drury Lane; revived naturalistic Shakespeare acting."]}, {"title": "Nineteenth-Century Drama: Romantics to Wilde and Shaw", "points": ["Romantic poets wrote closet/poetic drama: Shelley's The Cenci (1819) and Prometheus Unbound (1820, 'lyrical drama'); Byron's Manfred (1817); Coleridge's Remorse; Wordsworth's The Borderers.", "Popular stage dominated by melodrama and spectacle; Theatres Act 1843 ended the patent theatres' monopoly.", "T. W. Robertson: Caste (1867), Society - realistic 'cup-and-saucer' drama.", "Arthur Wing Pinero: The Second Mrs Tanqueray (1893); Henry Arthur Jones: Mrs Dane's Defence - English problem plays.", "Oscar Wilde: Lady Windermere's Fan (1892), A Woman of No Importance (1893), An Ideal Husband (1895), The Importance of Being Earnest (1895), Salome (written in French).", "The Importance of Being Earnest: 'A Trivial Comedy for Serious People'; Jack Worthing, Algernon, Lady Bracknell ('A handbag?'), Gwendolen, Cecily; Bunburying.", "G. B. Shaw: The Quintessence of Ibsenism (1891); Widowers' Houses (1892), Mrs Warren's Profession (1893), Arms and the Man (1894), Candida (1894).", "Shaw: Man and Superman (1903, Life Force; 'Don Juan in Hell' interlude), Major Barbara (1905), Pygmalion (1913, Henry Higgins, Eliza Doolittle), Heartbreak House (1919), Saint Joan (1923).", "Shaw won the Nobel Prize in 1925; mocked the well-made play as 'Sardoodledom'.", "Edwardian realism: John Galsworthy - Strife (1909), Justice (1910); Harley Granville-Barker - The Voysey Inheritance; J. M. Barrie - Peter Pan (1904)."]}, {"title": "Modern and Contemporary British Drama", "points": ["Verse drama revival: T. S. Eliot - Murder in the Cathedral (1935, Becket, Four Tempters, Chorus of Women of Canterbury), The Family Reunion (1939), The Cocktail Party (1949); Christopher Fry - The Lady's Not for Burning (1948).", "John Osborne, Look Back in Anger (Royal Court, 8 May 1956): Jimmy Porter, Alison, Cliff, Helena - launched the 'Angry Young Men'.", "Kitchen-sink drama: Arnold Wesker's trilogy (Chicken Soup with Barley, Roots, I'm Talking About Jerusalem); Shelagh Delaney's A Taste of Honey (1958).", "Harold Pinter: The Room (1957), The Birthday Party (1958, Stanley), The Caretaker (1960, Davies), The Homecoming (1965), Betrayal (1978, reverse chronology); Nobel 2005.", "Tom Stoppard: Rosencrantz and Guildenstern Are Dead (1966), Jumpers (1972), Travesties (1974), Arcadia (1993).", "John Arden: Serjeant Musgrave's Dance (1959); Edward Bond: Saved (1965, stoning of a baby) and Lear (1971); Robert Bolt: A Man for All Seasons (1960).", "Peter Shaffer: Equus (1973), Amadeus (1979); Joe Orton: Entertaining Mr Sloane (1964), Loot (1965); Alan Ayckbourn: The Norman Conquests.", "Theatres Act 1968 abolished the Lord Chamberlain's stage censorship.", "Caryl Churchill: Cloud Nine (1979), Top Girls (1982, Marlene), Serious Money (1987) - feminist and Brechtian techniques.", "1990s 'in-yer-face theatre' (Aleks Sierz): Sarah Kane's Blasted (1995), Mark Ravenhill's Shopping and F***ing (1996).", "Other names: David Hare (Plenty), Michael Frayn (Copenhagen, Noises Off), Hanif Kureishi (Borderline)."]}, {"title": "Irish Drama", "points": ["Irish Literary Theatre founded 1899 by W. B. Yeats, Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn; Abbey Theatre, Dublin, opened 1904 (funded by Annie Horniman).", "Yeats: Cathleen ni Houlihan (1902, with Lady Gregory), On Baile's Strand (1904), At the Hawk's Well (1916, Noh-influenced), Purgatory (1938).", "J. M. Synge: In the Shadow of the Glen (1903), Riders to the Sea (1904, Maurya loses all her sons to the sea), The Playboy of the Western World (1907).", "The Playboy of the Western World (Christy Mahon, Pegeen Mike) caused riots at the Abbey in January 1907.", "Sean O'Casey's Dublin trilogy: The Shadow of a Gunman (1923), Juno and the Paycock (1924), The Plough and the Stars (1926, also caused riots).", "Juno and the Paycock: 'Captain' Jack Boyle and Joxer Daly; 'The whole worl's in a terrible state o' chassis.'", "Samuel Beckett (Irish, wrote in French and English): Waiting for Godot (French 1953, English 1955), Endgame (1957), Krapp's Last Tape (1958), Happy Days (1961); Nobel 1969.", "Brendan Behan: The Quare Fellow (1954), The Hostage (1958).", "Brian Friel: Philadelphia, Here I Come! (1964), Faith Healer (1979), Translations (1980, first Field Day production, Ordnance Survey of Ballybeg), Dancing at Lughnasa (1990).", "Later: Tom Murphy, Frank McGuinness (Observe the Sons of Ulster...), Marina Carr, Martin McDonagh (The Beauty Queen of Leenane)."]}, {"title": "American Drama", "points": ["Provincetown Players (1915-16) launched Eugene O'Neill and Susan Glaspell (Trifles, 1916 - feminist one-act).", "O'Neill: Beyond the Horizon (1920, Pulitzer), The Emperor Jones (1920), The Hairy Ape (1922, Yank - expressionism), Desire Under the Elms (1924), Strange Interlude (1928).", "O'Neill: Mourning Becomes Electra (1931, Oresteia set in post-Civil War New England), The Iceman Cometh (1946, Hickey), Long Day's Journey into Night (written 1941, staged 1956; Tyrone family); Nobel 1936.", "Elmer Rice: The Adding Machine (1923, Mr Zero); Clifford Odets: Waiting for Lefty (1935); Thornton Wilder: Our Town (1938, Stage Manager), The Skin of Our Teeth (1942).", "Tennessee Williams: The Glass Menagerie (1944, memory play - Tom, Laura, Amanda Wingfield), A Streetcar Named Desire (1947 - Blanche DuBois, Stanley Kowalski, Stella), Cat on a Hot Tin Roof (1955).", "'I have always depended on the kindness of strangers' - Blanche DuBois.", "Arthur Miller: All My Sons (1947), Death of a Salesman (1949 - Willy Loman, Biff, Happy, Linda), The Crucible (1953, Salem witch trials as allegory of McCarthyism; John Proctor), A View from the Bridge (1955).", "Miller's essay 'Tragedy and the Common Man' (1949): the common man is as apt a subject for tragedy as kings.", "Edward Albee: The Zoo Story (1959), The American Dream (1961), Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf? (1962 - George, Martha, Nick, Honey).", "African American drama: Lorraine Hansberry, A Raisin in the Sun (1959, title from Langston Hughes' 'Harlem'); Amiri Baraka, Dutchman (1964); Ntozake Shange, for colored girls who have considered suicide / when the rainbow is enuf (1976, choreopoem).", "August Wilson's Pittsburgh (Century) Cycle of ten plays: Ma Rainey's Black Bottom, Fences (1985, Troy Maxson), The Piano Lesson.", "Later: Sam Shepard - Buried Child (1978), True West; David Mamet - Glengarry Glen Ross (1983); Tony Kushner - Angels in America (1991-93)."]}, {"title": "Theatre of the Absurd", "points": ["Term coined by Martin Esslin in The Theatre of the Absurd (1961); philosophical basis in Albert Camus' The Myth of Sisyphus (1942).", "Features: no logical plot, circular structure, breakdown of language, meaningless waiting, man's existence without purpose.", "Waiting for Godot: Vladimir (Didi), Estragon (Gogo), Pozzo, Lucky, a Boy; subtitle 'a tragicomedy in two acts'; opens with 'Nothing to be done.'", "Vivian Mercier: Godot is a play in which 'nothing happens, twice'.", "Endgame characters: Hamm, Clov, Nagg and Nell (in dustbins); Krapp's Last Tape: Krapp and his tape recorder; Happy Days: Winnie buried in a mound.", "Eugene Ionesco: The Bald Soprano (1950, 'anti-play' - the Smiths and the Martins), The Lesson (1951), The Chairs (1952), Rhinoceros (1959, Berenger).", "Jean Genet: The Maids (1947), The Balcony (1956), The Blacks; Arthur Adamov; Fernando Arrabal.", "Esslin included Pinter, N. F. Simpson and Edward Albee (The Zoo Story, The American Dream) among absurdists.", "Precursors: Alfred Jarry's Ubu Roi (1896), Pirandello, Kafka, Dada and Surrealism."]}, {"title": "European and World Drama", "points": ["Henrik Ibsen ('father of modern drama'): Peer Gynt (1867), A Doll's House (1879, Nora's door slam), Ghosts (1881), An Enemy of the People (1882), The Wild Duck (1884), Hedda Gabler (1890), The Master Builder (1892).", "August Strindberg: The Father (1887), Miss Julie (1888, its Preface is a naturalist manifesto), A Dream Play (1902), The Ghost Sonata (1907) - expressionist.", "Anton Chekhov: The Seagull (1896), Uncle Vanya (1899), Three Sisters (1901), The Cherry Orchard (1904, Ranevskaya, Lopakhin); staged by Stanislavski's Moscow Art Theatre (founded 1898).", "Luigi Pirandello: Six Characters in Search of an Author (1921), Henry IV (1922) - metatheatre; Nobel 1934.", "Bertolt Brecht: The Threepenny Opera (1928), Mother Courage and Her Children (1939, staged 1941), Life of Galileo, The Good Person of Szechwan, The Caucasian Chalk Circle - epic theatre.", "Federico Garcia Lorca's rural trilogy: Blood Wedding (1932), Yerma (1934), The House of Bernarda Alba (1936).", "Jean-Paul Sartre: No Exit / Huis Clos (1944) - 'Hell is other people'; Friedrich Durrenmatt: The Visit (1956).", "Wole Soyinka (Nigeria, Nobel 1986): The Lion and the Jewel (1959), A Dance of the Forests (1960), The Road (1965), Death and the King's Horseman (1975, Elesin, Olunde).", "Athol Fugard (South Africa): The Blood Knot, Sizwe Bansi Is Dead (1972), 'Master Harold'... and the Boys (1982).", "Derek Walcott (St Lucia, Nobel 1992): Dream on Monkey Mountain (1967), Ti-Jean and His Brothers, Pantomime.", "Ngugi wa Thiong'o with Micere Mugo: The Trial of Dedan Kimathi (1976); with Ngugi wa Mirii: I Will Marry When I Want (Ngaahika Ndeenda, 1977); Ama Ata Aidoo: The Dilemma of a Ghost (1965)."]}, {"title": "Indian Drama (Classical and Indian English)", "points": ["Bharata's Natyashastra: earliest Indian treatise on drama; rasa theory (8 rasas; shanta added later as ninth); roles of sutradhara and vidushaka.", "Kalidasa: Abhijnanashakuntalam (translated into English by Sir William Jones, 1789), Vikramorvashiyam, Malavikagnimitram; Sudraka: Mricchakatika (The Little Clay Cart); Bhasa: Svapnavasavadatta.", "Rabindranath Tagore: Chitra (1913), The Post Office (Dak Ghar; Amal), Red Oleanders (Raktakarabi), Sacrifice, The King of the Dark Chamber.", "Early Indian English drama: Krishna Mohan Banerji's The Persecuted (1831) - first Indian English play; Sri Aurobindo: Perseus the Deliverer; Harindranath Chattopadhyaya; Asif Currimbhoy: Inquilab, The Doldrummers.", "Girish Karnad: Yayati (1961), Tughlaq (1964), Hayavadana (1971; from Kathasaritsagara via Thomas Mann's The Transposed Heads), Naga-Mandala (1988), Tale-Danda (1990), The Fire and the Rain (Agni Mattu Male); Jnanpith 1998.", "Vijay Tendulkar (Marathi): Silence! The Court Is in Session (Shantata! Court Chalu Aahe, 1967; Leela Benare), Ghashiram Kotwal (1972, Nana Phadnavis), Sakharam Binder (1972), Kamala (1981).", "Badal Sircar (Bengali): Evam Indrajit (1963), Pagla Ghoda; founder of 'Third Theatre' (anganmanch / street-and-courtyard theatre).", "Mohan Rakesh (Hindi): Ashadh Ka Ek Din (1958, on Kalidasa), Adhe Adhure (1969); Habib Tanvir: Charandas Chor (1975); Mahesh Elkunchwar: Wada Chirebandi.", "Mahesh Dattani: Dance Like a Man (1989), Tara (1990, conjoined twins), Bravely Fought the Queen (1991), Final Solutions (1993), On a Muggy Night in Mumbai (1998).", "Dattani was the first Indian English playwright to win the Sahitya Akademi Award (1998) for Final Solutions and Other Plays.", "Manjula Padmanabhan: Harvest (1997, organ trade; Onassis Prize); Gurcharan Das: Larins Sahib; Poile Sengupta: Mangalam; Partap Sharma: A Touch of Brightness."]}, {"title": "Dramatic Theory and Criticism", "points": ["Philip Sidney, An Apology for Poetry (pub. 1595): defended the unities and mocked 'mongrel tragicomedy'.", "John Dryden, An Essay of Dramatic Poesy (1668): dialogue of Crites (ancients), Eugenius (moderns), Lisideius (French) and Neander (English; Dryden himself).", "Samuel Johnson, Preface to Shakespeare (1765): rejected unities of time and place - 'the spectators are always in their senses'; defended mixing of tragedy and comedy.", "Hegel: tragedy as collision of two equally justified rights (Antigone vs Creon).", "Gustav Freytag, Technique of the Drama (1863): pyramid of exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, catastrophe/denouement.", "Emile Zola, 'Naturalism in the Theatre' (1881); Stanislavski's system (An Actor Prepares) - basis of Method acting.", "Antonin Artaud, The Theatre and Its Double (1938): Theatre of Cruelty.", "Jerzy Grotowski, Towards a Poor Theatre (1968); Peter Brook, The Empty Space (1968) - deadly, holy, rough and immediate theatre.", "Augusto Boal, Theatre of the Oppressed (1974): forum theatre, spect-actor.", "George Steiner, The Death of Tragedy (1961); Raymond Williams, Modern Tragedy (1966); Lionel Abel, Metatheatre (1963); Eric Bentley, The Life of the Drama (1964)."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Hamartia", "pron": "हा-मार-टी-आ (haa-maar-TEE-uh)", "hindi": "नायक की गलती या भूल जो उसके पतन का कारण बनती है", "net": "Aristotle's term (Poetics) for the error, mistake or 'missing the mark' in judgement by which a tragic hero falls from prosperity to misery; loosely rendered as 'tragic flaw'."}, {"term": "Hubris", "pron": "ह्यू-ब्रिस (HYOO-bris)", "hindi": "घमंड, हद से ज़्यादा अहंकार", "net": "Excessive pride or self-confidence that leads the protagonist to ignore divine warnings or moral law; in Greek tragedy a common form of hamartia."}, {"term": "Peripeteia", "pron": "पेरि-पे-टी-आ (pe-ri-pe-TEE-uh)", "hindi": "किस्मत का अचानक उलटा पलटना", "net": "Aristotle's 'reversal of situation': a change by which the action veers round to its opposite, as when the messenger in Oedipus Rex brings news meant to cheer but which ruins him."}, {"term": "Anagnorisis", "pron": "एन-ऐग-नो-रि-सिस (an-ag-NOR-i-sis)", "hindi": "सच की पहचान, आँखें खुलना", "net": "Aristotle's 'recognition' or discovery: a change from ignorance to knowledge, ideally coinciding with peripeteia, as in Oedipus' recognition of his identity."}, {"term": "Catharsis", "pron": "कै-थार-सिस (kuh-THAR-sis)", "hindi": "मन का शुद्धिकरण, भावनाओं का बाहर निकलना", "net": "Aristotle's term for the purgation (or purification/clarification) of pity and fear which tragedy effects in the audience (Poetics, Ch. 6)."}, {"term": "Mimesis", "pron": "मि-मी-सिस (mi-MEE-sis)", "hindi": "नकल, जीवन का अनुकरण", "net": "Imitation or representation of reality; Plato condemned art as imitation of an imitation, while Aristotle defined tragedy as mimesis of an action."}, {"term": "Chorus", "pron": "को-रस (KOR-us)", "hindi": "मंच पर समूह जो गाकर घटना पर टिप्पणी करता है", "net": "In Greek drama a group of performers who sing, dance and comment on the action; reduced in Elizabethan drama to a single prologue-speaker (Henry V) and revived by Eliot in Murder in the Cathedral."}, {"term": "Deus ex machina", "pron": "डे-उस एक्स मा-कि-ना (DAY-us eks MAH-ki-nuh)", "hindi": "अंत में अचानक आकर समस्या सुलझाने वाला दैवी हस्तक्षेप", "net": "'God from the machine': a god lowered by crane to resolve the plot, frequent in Euripides; any improbable contrivance to resolve a plot. Aristotle criticised it."}, {"term": "Stichomythia", "pron": "स्टि-को-मि-थी-आ (stik-o-MITH-ee-uh)", "hindi": "एक-एक पंक्ति में तेज़ नोक-झोंक वाला संवाद", "net": "Dialogue in alternate single lines, often in argument, used in Greek and Senecan tragedy and imitated by Elizabethans (e.g. Richard III, Hamlet)."}, {"term": "Three Unities", "pron": "थ्री यूनिटीज़ (three YOO-ni-teez)", "hindi": "समय, स्थान और कार्य की एकता का नियम", "net": "Neo-classical rules of action, time (one day) and place (one locale), formulated by Castelvetro (1570) from Aristotle, who prescribed only unity of action; rejected by Johnson in the Preface to Shakespeare (1765)."}, {"term": "Protagonist", "pron": "प्रो-टैग-अ-निस्ट (pro-TAG-uh-nist)", "hindi": "नाटक का मुख्य पात्र", "net": "The chief character of a play (Greek protagonistes, 'first actor'); opposed by the antagonist; the deuteragonist and tritagonist are second and third actors."}, {"term": "Soliloquy", "pron": "स-लि-ल-क्वी (suh-LIL-uh-kwee)", "hindi": "अकेले मंच पर अपने मन की बात ज़ोर से कहना", "net": "A speech in which a character alone on stage utters thoughts aloud, revealing inner mind to the audience, e.g. Hamlet's 'To be, or not to be'."}, {"term": "Dramatic irony", "pron": "ड्र-मै-टिक आय-र-नी (druh-MAT-ik EYE-ruh-nee)", "hindi": "दर्शक सच जानते हैं, पात्र नहीं", "net": "A situation in which the audience shares knowledge of which a character is ignorant, so that the character's words carry a meaning beyond his understanding; tragic irony when it foreshadows doom (Oedipus Rex)."}, {"term": "Comic relief", "pron": "को-मिक रि-लीफ़ (KOM-ik ri-LEEF)", "hindi": "गंभीर दृश्य के बीच हँसी का पल", "net": "A humorous scene or character in a serious play that relieves tension and often heightens tragic effect, e.g. the Porter in Macbeth, the Gravediggers in Hamlet."}, {"term": "Revenge tragedy", "pron": "रि-वेंज ट्रै-ज-डी (ri-VENJ TRAJ-uh-dee)", "hindi": "बदले पर आधारित खूनी त्रासदी", "net": "Elizabethan-Jacobean genre derived from Seneca, established by Kyd's The Spanish Tragedy: ghost, revenge for murder, feigned madness, play-within-play and a bloody catastrophe; e.g. Hamlet, The Revenger's Tragedy."}, {"term": "Machiavel", "pron": "मै-की-अ-वेल (MAK-ee-uh-vel)", "hindi": "चालाक, बेईमान षड्यंत्रकारी खलनायक", "net": "Stock Elizabethan villain modelled on a popular distortion of Machiavelli's The Prince: a cunning, ruthless schemer, e.g. Barabas (The Jew of Malta, whose prologue Machevil speaks), Iago."}, {"term": "Malcontent", "pron": "मैल-कन-टेंट (MAL-kun-tent)", "hindi": "दुनिया से नाराज़, कड़वा व्यंग्य करने वाला पात्र", "net": "A cynical, discontented railer of Jacobean drama, named from Marston's The Malcontent (1603-04); e.g. Bosola, Flamineo."}, {"term": "Tragicomedy", "pron": "ट्रै-जि-को-म-डी (traj-i-KOM-uh-dee)", "hindi": "दुख और सुख मिला हुआ नाटक", "net": "A play mixing tragic and comic elements, threatening disaster but ending happily; Guarini's theory; Beaumont and Fletcher's Philaster; Sidney called it 'mongrel'; Beckett subtitled Godot 'a tragicomedy'."}, {"term": "Masque", "pron": "मास्क (MAHSK)", "hindi": "राज दरबार का संगीत-नृत्य वाला भव्य नाटक", "net": "A courtly entertainment of poetry, music, dance and spectacle with masked performers, perfected by Ben Jonson and designer Inigo Jones; Milton's Comus (1634) is a famous example."}, {"term": "Antimasque", "pron": "ऐंटी-मास्क (AN-tee-mahsk)", "hindi": "मास्क से पहले का हास्य या भद्दा दृश्य", "net": "A grotesque or comic interlude preceding the main masque as a foil, introduced by Ben Jonson (The Masque of Queens, 1609)."}, {"term": "Mystery play", "pron": "मिस्-ट-री प्ले (MIS-tuh-ree play)", "hindi": "बाइबिल की कहानियों पर आधारित मध्ययुगीन नाटक", "net": "Medieval vernacular drama dramatising Biblical history from Creation to Doomsday, performed in cycles (York, Chester, Wakefield, N-Town) by trade guilds; distinct from miracle plays on saints' lives."}, {"term": "Morality play", "pron": "म-रै-लि-टी प्ले (muh-RAL-i-tee play)", "hindi": "गुण-दोष को पात्र बनाकर शिक्षा देने वाला नाटक", "net": "Allegorical drama of the 15th-16th centuries in which personified virtues and vices contend for the soul of Mankind, e.g. Everyman, The Castle of Perseverance."}, {"term": "Comedy of humours", "pron": "कॉ-म-डी ऑफ़ ह्यू-मर्स (KOM-uh-dee of HYOO-merz)", "hindi": "एक खास सनक वाले पात्रों की कॉमेडी", "net": "Comedy developed by Ben Jonson (Every Man in His Humour, 1598) in which each character is dominated by one ruling passion or eccentricity, based on the medieval physiology of four humours."}, {"term": "Comedy of manners", "pron": "कॉ-म-डी ऑफ़ मै-नर्स (KOM-uh-dee of MAN-erz)", "hindi": "ऊँचे समाज के तौर-तरीकों पर चुटीली कॉमेडी", "net": "Witty, satirical comedy of the sophisticated upper class, centring on intrigue, sexual wit and repartee; typified by Restoration comedy (Etherege, Wycherley, Congreve) and later Sheridan and Wilde."}, {"term": "Sentimental comedy", "pron": "सें-टि-मेन-टल कॉ-म-डी (sen-ti-MEN-tl KOM-uh-dee)", "hindi": "आँसू और नैतिकता वाली भावुक कॉमेडी", "net": "18th-century comedy that sought tears rather than laughter, showing virtuous middle-class characters rewarded; Cibber's Love's Last Shift, Steele's The Conscious Lovers; attacked by Goldsmith."}, {"term": "Heroic drama", "pron": "हि-रो-इक ड्रा-मा (hi-ROH-ik DRAH-muh)", "hindi": "प्रेम और सम्मान के टकराव वाला भव्य नाटक", "net": "Restoration tragedy in heroic couplets dealing with conflicts of love and honour amid epic grandeur, e.g. Dryden's The Conquest of Granada; satirised in Buckingham's The Rehearsal (1671)."}, {"term": "Ballad opera", "pron": "बै-लड ऑ-प-रा (BAL-ud OP-ruh)", "hindi": "लोकगीतों की धुनों वाला व्यंग्य संगीत नाटक", "net": "A comic play with songs set to popular ballad tunes, originated by John Gay's The Beggar's Opera (1728)."}, {"term": "Domestic tragedy", "pron": "ड-मेस-टिक ट्रै-ज-डी (duh-MES-tik TRAJ-uh-dee)", "hindi": "आम घर-परिवार के लोगों की त्रासदी", "net": "Tragedy of ordinary middle-class or lower-class people in private life rather than kings, e.g. Arden of Faversham, Lillo's The London Merchant (1731); ancestor of modern realist tragedy."}, {"term": "Closet drama", "pron": "क्लॉ-ज़िट ड्रा-मा (KLOZ-it DRAH-muh)", "hindi": "पढ़ने के लिए लिखा नाटक, मंच के लिए नहीं", "net": "A play written to be read rather than performed, e.g. Seneca's tragedies, Milton's Samson Agonistes, Shelley's Prometheus Unbound, Byron's Manfred."}, {"term": "Well-made play", "pron": "वेल-मेड प्ले (WEL-mayd play)", "hindi": "कसे हुए प्लॉट वाला सूत्रबद्ध नाटक", "net": "Pièce bien faite: a tightly constructed play with careful exposition, suspense, a scène à faire and logical resolution, perfected by Eugène Scribe and Victorien Sardou; mocked by Shaw as 'Sardoodledom'."}, {"term": "Problem play", "pron": "प्रॉ-ब्लम प्ले (PROB-lum play)", "hindi": "सामाजिक समस्या या उलझी नैतिकता वाला नाटक", "net": "(1) Drama treating a social problem for discussion, after Ibsen (Shaw, Galsworthy); (2) F. S. Boas's term (1896) for Shakespeare's Measure for Measure, All's Well and Troilus and Cressida."}, {"term": "Naturalism", "pron": "नै-च-र-लि-ज़्म (NACH-ruh-li-zum)", "hindi": "जीवन को बिना सजाए हूबहू दिखाना", "net": "A movement presenting life as determined by heredity and environment with documentary exactness; Zola, 'Naturalism in the Theatre' (1881); Strindberg's Preface to Miss Julie (1888); Antoine's Théâtre Libre."}, {"term": "Expressionism", "pron": "एक्स-प्रे-श-नि-ज़्म (ik-SPRESH-uh-ni-zum)", "hindi": "बाहरी सच नहीं, मन के भीतर की भावना दिखाना", "net": "Early 20th-century anti-realist mode distorting the outer world to project inner states, using types, episodic scenes and stylised dialogue: Strindberg's A Dream Play, Kaiser, O'Neill's The Hairy Ape, Rice's The Adding Machine."}, {"term": "Fourth wall", "pron": "फ़ोर्थ वॉल (forth WAWL)", "hindi": "मंच और दर्शक के बीच की अदृश्य दीवार", "net": "The imaginary wall of a box-set through which the audience watches the action in realist theatre; 'breaking the fourth wall' means addressing the audience directly."}, {"term": "Epic theatre", "pron": "ए-पिक थि-ए-टर (EP-ik THEE-uh-ter)", "hindi": "दर्शक को सोचने पर मजबूर करने वाला ब्रेख्त का रंगमंच", "net": "Non-Aristotelian theatre of Erwin Piscator and Bertolt Brecht, using episodic narrative, songs, placards and direct address to make the audience think critically rather than identify emotionally."}, {"term": "Alienation effect", "pron": "एलि-ए-ने-शन इ-फ़ेक्ट (ay-lee-uh-NAY-shun i-FEKT)", "hindi": "दर्शक को भावनाओं में बहने से रोकना", "net": "Brecht's Verfremdungseffekt (V-effect): techniques that make the familiar strange so the audience stays critically detached and sees social conditions as changeable."}, {"term": "Theatre of the Absurd", "pron": "थि-ए-टर ऑफ़ द ऐब-सर्ड (THEE-uh-ter of thee ab-SURD)", "hindi": "जीवन की निरर्थकता दिखाने वाला बेतुका नाटक", "net": "Martin Esslin's term (1961) for post-war drama of Beckett, Ionesco, Genet, Adamov, Pinter and others which presents the human condition as meaningless, abandoning logical plot and language; rooted in Camus' The Myth of Sisyphus."}, {"term": "Comedy of menace", "pron": "कॉ-म-डी ऑफ़ मे-नस (KOM-uh-dee of MEN-us)", "hindi": "हँसी के बीच छिपा डर और ख़तरा", "net": "Irving Wardle's phrase (1958), adapted from David Campton's subtitle, for plays such as Pinter's The Birthday Party where comic everyday talk is shot through with unexplained threat."}, {"term": "Kitchen-sink drama", "pron": "किचन-सिंक ड्रा-मा (KICH-un sink DRAH-muh)", "hindi": "मज़दूर वर्ग के घरेलू जीवन का यथार्थ नाटक", "net": "Late-1950s British realist drama of working-class domestic life, e.g. Osborne's Look Back in Anger, Wesker's Roots, Delaney's A Taste of Honey; term borrowed from painting criticism."}, {"term": "Theatre of Cruelty", "pron": "थि-ए-टर ऑफ़ क्रू-अल-टी (THEE-uh-ter of KROO-ul-tee)", "hindi": "दर्शक को झकझोरने वाला शारीरिक, अनुष्ठानिक रंगमंच", "net": "Antonin Artaud's concept (The Theatre and Its Double, 1938) of theatre that assaults the senses through sound, light, gesture and ritual rather than text, to shatter the audience's complacency."}, {"term": "Poor theatre", "pron": "पुअर थि-ए-टर (poor THEE-uh-ter)", "hindi": "बिना साज-सज्जा, सिर्फ़ अभिनेता पर टिका रंगमंच", "net": "Jerzy Grotowski's theatre (Towards a Poor Theatre, 1968) stripped of costume, sets and lighting effects, centred on the actor-spectator relationship."}, {"term": "Metatheatre", "pron": "मे-टा-थि-ए-टर (MET-uh-thee-uh-ter)", "hindi": "नाटक के भीतर नाटक, खुद को नाटक मानने वाला", "net": "Lionel Abel's term (Metatheatre, 1963) for drama aware of its own theatricality, as in the play-within-the-play (The Mousetrap in Hamlet), Pirandello and Stoppard."}, {"term": "Memory play", "pron": "मे-म-री प्ले (MEM-uh-ree play)", "hindi": "किसी पात्र की यादों के रूप में चलने वाला नाटक", "net": "Tennessee Williams's term for The Glass Menagerie (1944), in which the action is filtered through the narrator Tom's memory, hence non-realistic and dimly lit."}, {"term": "Choreopoem", "pron": "को-री-ओ-पो-एम (KOR-ee-oh-poh-um)", "hindi": "कविता, नृत्य और संगीत मिला नाट्य रूप", "net": "Ntozake Shange's term for her form combining poetry, dance, music and song, as in for colored girls who have considered suicide / when the rainbow is enuf (1976)."}, {"term": "In-yer-face theatre", "pron": "इन-यर-फ़ेस थि-ए-टर (in-yer-FAYS THEE-uh-ter)", "hindi": "चौंकाने वाला, हिंसक और खुला नाटक", "net": "Aleks Sierz's label (In-Yer-Face Theatre, 2001) for 1990s British drama of shock and explicit violence and sex: Sarah Kane, Mark Ravenhill."}, {"term": "Freytag's Pyramid", "pron": "फ़्राय-टाग्स पि-र-मिड (FRY-tahgz PIR-uh-mid)", "hindi": "कथानक की पाँच सीढ़ियाँ", "net": "Gustav Freytag's model (Technique of the Drama, 1863) of dramatic structure: exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and catastrophe (denouement)."}, {"term": "Denouement", "pron": "डे-नू-माँ (day-noo-MAHN)", "hindi": "कहानी की गुत्थी का अंत में सुलझना", "net": "'Unknotting': the final unravelling of the plot after the climax, when complications are resolved; called catastrophe in tragedy."}, {"term": "Rasa", "pron": "र-स (RUH-suh)", "hindi": "नाटक से मिलने वाला भाव-आनंद", "net": "Bharata's Natyashastra concept of the aesthetic emotion relished by the spectator, arising from vibhava, anubhava and vyabhichari bhava; eight rasas, with shanta added later (Abhinavagupta)."}, {"term": "Sutradhara", "pron": "सूत्र-धार (SOO-truh-dhaar)", "hindi": "नाटक का सूत्रधार, कथा जोड़ने वाला", "net": "'Holder of the strings': the stage-manager-narrator of Sanskrit drama who opens the play, revived by modern Indian playwrights such as Karnad (the Bhagavata in Hayavadana) and Tendulkar (Ghashiram Kotwal)."}, {"term": "Third Theatre", "pron": "थर्ड थि-ए-टर (third THEE-uh-ter)", "hindi": "गली-आँगन का मुफ़्त, साधारण रंगमंच", "net": "Badal Sircar's alternative theatre combining folk and Western forms, performed free in open spaces (anganmanch) without proscenium or elaborate sets."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (a) Look Back in Anger (b) A Doll's House (c) Murder in the Cathedral (d) Waiting for Godot (English)", "options": ["b, c, d, a", "b, d, c, a", "c, b, a, d", "b, c, a, d"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1879, 1935, 1955, 1956."}, {"q": "Who among the following was NOT one of the University Wits?", "options": ["Robert Greene", "George Peele", "Ben Jonson", "Thomas Nashe"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Jonson was not a University Wit; Greene, Peele, Nashe, Lyly, Lodge and Marlowe were."}, {"q": "The phrase 'comedy of menace' is most closely associated with the early plays of:", "options": ["John Osborne", "Harold Pinter", "Arnold Wesker", "Tom Stoppard"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Irving Wardle applied it to Pinter's The Birthday Party (1958)."}, {"q": "Match List I with List II: (a) Volpone (b) The Changeling (c) The Duchess of Malfi (d) 'Tis Pity She's a Whore -- (i) John Webster (ii) John Ford (iii) Ben Jonson (iv) Middleton and Rowley", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Volpone-Jonson, Changeling-Middleton and Rowley, Malfi-Webster, 'Tis Pity-Ford."}, {"q": "Mrs Malaprop, whose verbal blunders gave English the word 'malapropism', is a character in:", "options": ["She Stoops to Conquer", "The School for Scandal", "The Rivals", "The Beaux' Stratagem"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Sheridan's The Rivals (1775)."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Goldsmith opposed the vogue of sentimental comedy. Reason (R): In 'An Essay on the Theatre' he compared laughing and sentimental comedy and favoured the former.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "The 1773 essay is Goldsmith's direct argument against sentimental comedy."}, {"q": "The famous 'proviso scene' between Mirabell and Millamant occurs in:", "options": ["The Country Wife", "The Way of the World", "The Man of Mode", "Love for Love"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Congreve's The Way of the World (1700), Act IV."}, {"q": "In Dryden's An Essay of Dramatic Poesy, which speaker represents Dryden's own views?", "options": ["Crites", "Eugenius", "Lisideius", "Neander"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Neander defends English drama and is Dryden's spokesman."}, {"q": "A Short View of the Immorality and Profaneness of the English Stage (1698) was written by:", "options": ["Jeremy Collier", "Colley Cibber", "Thomas Rymer", "John Dennis"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Jeremy Collier attacked Restoration comedy in 1698."}, {"q": "Girish Karnad's Hayavadana draws on Kathasaritsagara and on which work?", "options": ["Goethe's Faust", "Thomas Mann's The Transposed Heads", "Kafka's Metamorphosis", "Ibsen's Peer Gynt"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Karnad acknowledged Mann's The Transposed Heads, itself from the Vetala tale."}, {"q": "Mahesh Dattani became the first Indian English playwright to win the Sahitya Akademi Award (1998) for:", "options": ["Tara", "Dance Like a Man", "Final Solutions and Other Plays", "Collected Plays"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The award was for Final Solutions and Other Plays."}, {"q": "Which play provoked riots at the Abbey Theatre, Dublin, in 1907?", "options": ["Riders to the Sea", "Juno and the Paycock", "The Playboy of the Western World", "Cathleen ni Houlihan"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Synge's Playboy offended nationalists in January 1907."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT part of Sean O'Casey's Dublin trilogy?", "options": ["The Shadow of a Gunman", "Juno and the Paycock", "The Plough and the Stars", "Red Roses for Me"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Red Roses for Me (1942) is a later play."}, {"q": "Arthur Miller defended the tragic stature of characters like Willy Loman in the essay:", "options": ["Tragedy and the Common Man", "The Death of Tragedy", "Modern Tragedy", "The Tragic Fallacy"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Miller's 1949 New York Times essay."}, {"q": "'I have always depended on the kindness of strangers' is spoken by:", "options": ["Amanda Wingfield", "Blanche DuBois", "Maggie the Cat", "Laura Wingfield"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Blanche's last line in A Streetcar Named Desire (1947)."}, {"q": "The term 'Theatre of the Absurd' was coined by:", "options": ["Albert Camus", "Eugene Ionesco", "Martin Esslin", "Jean-Paul Sartre"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Esslin's book The Theatre of the Absurd (1961)."}, {"q": "Verfremdungseffekt (alienation effect) is a key concept of:", "options": ["Antonin Artaud", "Bertolt Brecht", "Konstantin Stanislavski", "Jerzy Grotowski"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Brecht's epic theatre aims at critical detachment."}, {"q": "Which play did Ionesco subtitle an 'anti-play'?", "options": ["The Chairs", "Rhinoceros", "The Bald Soprano", "The Lesson"], "answer": 2, "exp": "La Cantatrice chauve (1950) is called an anti-pièce."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of Aristotle's six constituent parts of tragedy?", "options": ["Plot", "Thought", "Spectacle", "Catharsis"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The six are plot, character, thought, diction, song, spectacle; catharsis is the effect."}, {"q": "The first English tragedy written in blank verse is:", "options": ["The Spanish Tragedy", "Gorboduc", "Tamburlaine", "Ralph Roister Doister"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Gorboduc (1561) by Sackville and Norton."}, {"q": "Who referred to Shakespeare as an 'upstart crow, beautified with our feathers'?", "options": ["Thomas Nashe", "Ben Jonson", "Robert Greene", "Christopher Marlowe"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Greene's Groatsworth of Wit (1592)."}, {"q": "Match the playwright with the play: (a) Vijay Tendulkar (b) Badal Sircar (c) Mohan Rakesh (d) Habib Tanvir -- (i) Evam Indrajit (ii) Charandas Chor (iii) Ghashiram Kotwal (iv) Ashadh Ka Ek Din", "options": ["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Tendulkar-Ghashiram Kotwal, Sircar-Evam Indrajit, Rakesh-Ashadh Ka Ek Din, Tanvir-Charandas Chor."}, {"q": "Match Nobel laureate dramatists with the year of award: (a) G. B. Shaw (b) Eugene O'Neill (c) Samuel Beckett (d) Wole Soyinka -- (i) 1969 (ii) 1986 (iii) 1925 (iv) 1936", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Shaw 1925, O'Neill 1936, Beckett 1969, Soyinka 1986."}, {"q": "'Hell is other people' is a line from:", "options": ["Waiting for Godot", "The Maids", "No Exit", "The Visit"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Garcin's line in Sartre's Huis Clos (1944)."}, {"q": "Arrange Shaw's plays chronologically: (a) Pygmalion (b) Saint Joan (c) Arms and the Man (d) Man and Superman", "options": ["c, d, a, b", "d, c, a, b", "c, a, d, b", "a, c, d, b"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1894, 1903, 1913, 1923."}, {"q": "The family at the centre of O'Neill's Long Day's Journey into Night is:", "options": ["The Mannons", "The Tyrones", "The Lomans", "The Cabots"], "answer": 1, "exp": "James, Mary, Jamie and Edmund Tyrone."}, {"q": "Brecht's The Threepenny Opera (1928) is an adaptation of:", "options": ["The Beggar's Opera", "The London Merchant", "The Recruiting Officer", "The Rehearsal"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Based on John Gay's ballad opera (1728)."}, {"q": "Walpole's Licensing Act of 1737 was provoked largely by the political satires of:", "options": ["John Gay", "Henry Fielding", "Oliver Goldsmith", "Richard Steele"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Fielding's satires such as The Historical Register led to the Act."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Dr Johnson defended Shakespeare's neglect of the unities of time and place. Reason (R): Johnson held that spectators are always in their senses and know that the stage is only a stage.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Preface to Shakespeare (1765) argues exactly this."}, {"q": "Match the critical term with its coiner: (a) Objective correlative (b) Problem play (for Shakespeare) (c) In-yer-face theatre (d) Metatheatre -- (i) Lionel Abel (ii) Aleks Sierz (iii) F. S. Boas (iv) T. S. Eliot", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Eliot 1919, Boas 1896, Sierz 2001, Abel 1963."}]}, {"id": 10, "title": "Research Methods and Materials in English", "overview": "Is unit mein research ke types, hypothesis, primary/secondary sources, literature review, MLA Handbook 9th edition ke citation rules, APA/Chicago, plagiarism aur UGC rules, ethics, bibliography, textual criticism, thesis structure, archives aur digital humanities aate hain. NET mein MLA format, Latin abbreviations, UGC plagiarism levels, sources ki pehchaan aur research tools/thinkers ke matching sawaal zyada poochhe jaate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "What is Research: Types and Approaches", "points": ["Research is a systematic, objective and verifiable inquiry to discover new facts or reinterpret known facts (vyavasthit khoj).", "Basic (fundamental/pure) research builds theory and knowledge for its own sake; applied research solves an immediate practical problem.", "Descriptive research describes 'what is' without manipulating variables; analytical research critically evaluates existing facts and information.", "Conceptual research works with abstract ideas and theories (common in literature); empirical research relies on observation, experiment or data.", "Exploratory research studies a little-known problem to form hypotheses; explanatory (causal) research tests cause-effect relations.", "Historical research studies past records and documents; in literature it includes biographical, textual and literary-historical study.", "Action research is done by practitioners (e.g. teachers) to improve their own practice; the term was coined by Kurt Lewin (1946).", "Case study research is an in-depth study of one unit (a person, text, institution or event).", "Longitudinal research studies the same subjects over a long period; cross-sectional research studies different subjects at one point in time.", "Inductive method moves from particular observations to a general conclusion (Francis Bacon, Novum Organum, 1620); deductive method moves from a general premise to particular conclusions (Aristotelian syllogism).", "Literary research is mostly qualitative, interpretive and library/archive based; it uses close reading, theory and historical contextualisation."]}, {"title": "Steps of the Research Process", "points": ["Usual sequence: identify area -> select and define problem -> review of literature -> formulate hypothesis/research questions -> research design -> data collection -> analysis -> interpretation -> report/thesis writing.", "A good research problem is specific, original, researchable, significant and manageable within time and resources.", "Research gap: the area not yet studied or insufficiently studied, identified through the literature review (jo kaam abhi tak nahi hua).", "Research questions state what the study will answer; objectives state what the study aims to achieve.", "Research design is the blueprint or plan of the whole study (data, method, analysis).", "Scope defines what the study covers; delimitations are boundaries the researcher chooses; limitations are constraints beyond the researcher's control.", "Synopsis (research proposal) is submitted before registration; it states title, problem, review, objectives, hypothesis, methodology, chapter plan and tentative bibliography.", "Pilot study is a small trial run to test tools (e.g. a questionnaire) before the main study.", "Interpretation means drawing meaning and generalisations from analysed data; it must not go beyond the evidence."]}, {"title": "Hypothesis", "points": ["A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement or educated guess about the relationship between variables (ek asthayi anumaan jise test kiya ja sake).", "Characteristics of a good hypothesis: clear, specific, testable/verifiable, consistent with known facts, simple (parsimonious) and related to the problem.", "Null hypothesis (H0) states no relationship or no difference; alternative hypothesis (H1 or Ha) states that a relationship or difference exists.", "Directional hypothesis predicts the direction of the effect; non-directional hypothesis only predicts that some difference exists.", "Type I error: rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive); Type II error: accepting a false null hypothesis (false negative).", "Independent variable is the presumed cause; dependent variable is the presumed effect; extraneous/confounding variables distort results.", "Karl Popper (The Logic of Scientific Discovery, 1934 German; English 1959): a scientific hypothesis must be falsifiable.", "In literary research a hypothesis is often replaced by a thesis statement or research questions, since interpretation is not statistically tested.", "Sources of hypothesis: theory, previous studies, observation, intuition and analogy."]}, {"title": "Primary, Secondary and Tertiary Sources", "points": ["Primary sources are original, first-hand materials: the literary text itself, manuscripts, letters, diaries, interviews, first editions, original documents.", "Secondary sources analyse or interpret primary sources: critical books, journal articles, reviews, biographies, commentaries.", "Tertiary sources compile or index primary and secondary sources: encyclopaedias, dictionaries, handbooks, bibliographies, indexes, databases.", "For a thesis on Hamlet, the play is primary; A. C. Bradley's Shakespearean Tragedy (1904) is secondary; an encyclopaedia entry on Shakespeare is tertiary.", "Whether a source is primary or secondary depends on the research question: a critic's essay becomes primary if the study is about that critic.", "Reference works NET asks: Oxford English Dictionary (OED, first complete 1928, originally A New English Dictionary on Historical Principles; first editor James Murray).", "Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (ODNB, 2004) succeeded the Dictionary of National Biography (DNB, ed. Leslie Stephen from 1885).", "Cambridge Bibliography of English Literature (CBEL, 1940) and New CBEL; MLA International Bibliography indexes literary scholarship worldwide.", "M. H. Abrams, A Glossary of Literary Terms (1957) and J. A. Cuddon, A Dictionary of Literary Terms (1977) are standard tertiary reference works.", "Grey literature: unpublished or non-commercial material such as theses, reports, conference papers, working papers."]}, {"title": "Review of Literature", "points": ["Literature review is a critical survey of existing scholarship on the topic; it is not a mere summary or list of books.", "Purposes: show familiarity with the field, find the research gap, avoid duplication, refine the problem, justify the study and locate it in a scholarly conversation.", "It should be organised thematically, chronologically or methodologically, and should compare, evaluate and synthesise sources.", "Narrative (traditional) review surveys literature selectively; systematic review follows a stated, replicable search and selection protocol.", "Meta-analysis statistically combines results of many quantitative studies (term coined by Gene V. Glass, 1976).", "In a thesis the review usually comes after the introduction and before methodology, or forms part of the introductory chapter.", "Sources for review in India: Shodhganga (full-text Indian theses, INFLIBNET), Shodhgangotri (synopses), JSTOR, Project MUSE, MLA International Bibliography, Google Scholar.", "Always cite every source reviewed; reproducing others' summaries without acknowledgement is plagiarism."]}, {"title": "MLA Handbook 9th Edition: Core Elements", "points": ["MLA Handbook, 9th edition, was published by the Modern Language Association in April 2021; 8th ed. 2016; 7th ed. 2009. The first MLA Handbook appeared in 1977.", "MLA 8 (2016) introduced the 'container' model with core elements; MLA 9 keeps it and adds detailed guidance, including a chapter on inclusive language.", "The nine core elements in order: 1 Author. 2 Title of source. 3 Title of container, 4 Other contributors, 5 Version, 6 Number, 7 Publisher, 8 Publication date, 9 Location.", "Punctuation: a period after Author and after Title of source; commas between elements inside a container; a period at the end of the container.", "A container is the larger whole that holds the source: a book holding an essay, a journal holding an article, a website holding a page, a TV series holding an episode. A source can have a second container (e.g. a database like JSTOR).", "Titles of self-contained works (books, journals, films, websites) are in italics; titles of parts (articles, poems, short stories, chapters, episodes) are in quotation marks.", "Other contributors use descriptive phrases written out: edited by, translated by, introduction by, directed by.", "Version: e.g. 2nd ed., revised ed., King James Version, director's cut. Number: vol. 12, no. 3 for journals; season and episode numbers.", "Publisher's city is normally omitted (since MLA 8); include it only for books published before 1900 or in special cases. Business words like Inc. and Company are dropped, and University Press is shortened to UP (e.g. Oxford UP).", "Location: page numbers (p. 15 / pp. 15-30), DOI (preferred over URL), URL (without http:// or https://), or a physical place for an artwork or lecture.", "Optional elements include date of original publication and date of access (Accessed 10 Jan. 2021.), recommended for undated web sources."]}, {"title": "MLA 9: Works Cited Format and Examples", "points": ["The list is titled Works Cited (centred, not bold, not italic); it includes only sources actually cited in the text. 'Works Consulted' includes others too.", "Entries are arranged alphabetically by author's surname (or by title if no author, ignoring A, An, The).", "Format: double-spaced throughout with a hanging indent of 0.5 inch (half inch) for every line after the first.", "One author: Morrison, Toni. Beloved. Alfred A. Knopf, 1987.", "Two authors: first author reversed, second in normal order joined by 'and': Gilbert, Sandra M., and Susan Gubar.", "Three or more authors: first author's name followed by et al. (Smith, John, et al.).", "Same author, more than one work: give the name in the first entry; later entries begin with three hyphens and a period (---.) and are alphabetised by title.", "Journal article: Author. \"Title of Article.\" Journal Name, vol. 5, no. 2, 2019, pp. 45-67. JSTOR, doi or URL.", "Essay in an edited book: Author. \"Essay.\" Book Title, edited by Editor Name, Publisher, Year, pp. 10-25.", "Months longer than four letters are abbreviated in Works Cited (Jan., Feb., Aug., Sept., Oct., Nov., Dec.); May, June, July are written in full.", "Titles use title case (major words capitalised) in MLA, unlike APA which uses sentence case for article and book titles."]}, {"title": "MLA 9: In-text Citation and Manuscript Format", "points": ["MLA uses parenthetical author-page citation: (Morrison 45) with no comma and no 'p.' between author and page.", "If the author is named in the sentence, give only the page: Morrison argues that ... (45).", "Two authors: (Gilbert and Gubar 78); three or more: (Smith et al. 23).", "No author: use a shortened title in the parenthesis, in italics or quotation marks as the full title would be.", "Two works by the same author: add a short title: (Eliot, Waste Land 12).", "Poetry is cited by line numbers (lines 5-8 in the first citation, then numbers only); verse plays by act, scene, line: (Hamlet 3.1.56).", "Block quotation: prose longer than four lines, or poetry longer than three lines, is set off, indented half an inch from the left margin, without quotation marks; the citation comes after the final punctuation.", "Ellipsis (...) shows omission from a quotation; square brackets [ ] show the researcher's insertion; [sic] marks an error in the original.", "Indirect source: cite with 'qtd. in' (quoted in): (qtd. in Brooks 42).", "Paper format: 1-inch margins, double spacing, readable 12-point font (e.g. Times New Roman), header with surname and page number at top right, no separate title page unless required.", "Notes: MLA allows footnotes or endnotes for bibliographic or content (explanatory) notes, marked by superscript numbers."]}, {"title": "APA, Chicago and Other Style Manuals", "points": ["APA (American Psychological Association) style, 7th edition, published October 2019; first APA guide 1929. Used in social sciences, education, psychology, linguistics.", "APA uses author-date in-text citation: (Smith, 2020, p. 15); ampersand (&) for two authors inside parentheses: (Smith & Jones, 2020).", "APA 7: et al. is used for three or more authors from the first citation; the reference list can include up to 20 authors.", "APA list is titled References; titles of books and articles use sentence case; date comes right after the author: Smith, J. (2020).", "Chicago Manual of Style (University of Chicago Press), first published 1906; 17th edition 2017; 18th edition 2024.", "Chicago has two systems: notes and bibliography (footnotes/endnotes, preferred in humanities, history, literature) and author-date (sciences and social sciences).", "Turabian: A Manual for Writers of Research Papers, Theses, and Dissertations by Kate L. Turabian, a student version of Chicago style (9th ed. 2018).", "MHRA Style Guide (Modern Humanities Research Association, UK) is a footnote-based style used in British humanities.", "Harvard referencing is a generic author-date system; Vancouver is a numbered system used in medicine; IEEE is numbered and used in engineering.", "Bibliography (Chicago) lists all sources consulted; Works Cited (MLA) lists sources cited; References (APA) lists sources cited."]}, {"title": "Latin Abbreviations and Notes", "points": ["Ibid. (ibidem): in the same place, refers to the immediately preceding note.", "Op. cit. (opere citato): in the work cited earlier; loc. cit. (loco citato): in the same place cited earlier (same page). MLA and Chicago now discourage op. cit. and loc. cit., preferring short-form notes.", "Et al. (et alii): and others; etc. (et cetera): and so on; cf. (confer): compare; viz. (videlicet): namely; i.e. (id est): that is; e.g. (exempli gratia): for example.", "Sic: thus, used in brackets to show an error is in the original; passim: here and there, throughout the work.", "Et seq. (et sequens): and the following; N.B. (nota bene): note well; q.v. (quod vide): which see; idem: the same author.", "Circa (c. or ca.): approximately, used with dates (c. 1340).", "Fl. (floruit): flourished, used when birth and death dates are unknown.", "n.d.: no date; n. pag.: no pagination (older MLA usage); MLA 9 simply omits unknown elements.", "Footnotes appear at the foot of the page; endnotes are gathered at the end of the chapter or thesis."]}, {"title": "Plagiarism and UGC Regulations", "points": ["Plagiarism: presenting another person's words, ideas, data or work as one's own without acknowledgement (Latin plagiarius, kidnapper).", "Types: direct/verbatim, paraphrase without credit, mosaic or patchwriting (term of Rebecca Moore Howard), self-plagiarism (reusing one's own published work), accidental plagiarism, and idea plagiarism.", "UGC (Promotion of Academic Integrity and Prevention of Plagiarism in Higher Educational Institutions) Regulations, 2018 define levels of similarity.", "Level 0: similarity up to 10% (minor, no penalty); Level 1: above 10% to 40%; Level 2: above 40% to 60%; Level 3: above 60%.", "Under these regulations, common knowledge, quoted work with citation, references/bibliography, abstract/summary, and generic terms are excluded from similarity checks.", "Penalties for students: Level 1 revise within 6 months; Level 2 revise within 12 months; Level 3 registration cancelled. Faculty penalties include withdrawal of manuscript and denial of increments.", "Software: Turnitin, iThenticate, Urkund (later Ouriginal), Grammarly; in India, INFLIBNET's ShodhShuddhi initiative (2019) provides plagiarism software to universities.", "Avoid plagiarism by quoting with quotation marks, paraphrasing in your own words with citation, keeping notes of sources and using reference managers.", "Fabrication (inventing data), falsification (manipulating data) and plagiarism together are called FFP, the three major forms of research misconduct."]}, {"title": "Research and Publication Ethics", "points": ["Core ethical principles: honesty, objectivity, integrity, openness, respect for intellectual property, confidentiality and responsible publication.", "Informed consent: participants must voluntarily agree after knowing the purpose, risks and their right to withdraw.", "Landmark codes: Nuremberg Code (1947), Declaration of Helsinki (1964, World Medical Association), Belmont Report (1979, USA: respect for persons, beneficence, justice).", "COPE (Committee on Publication Ethics), founded 1997 in the UK, gives guidelines on publication misconduct.", "UGC made a 2-credit course on Research and Publication Ethics (RPE) compulsory in PhD coursework from 2019-20.", "UGC-CARE (Consortium for Academic and Research Ethics) was set up by UGC in 2019 to maintain a reference list of quality journals.", "Predatory journals charge fees and publish without genuine peer review; Jeffrey Beall's list (Beall's List) popularised the term.", "Peer review types: single-blind (reviewer knows author), double-blind (neither knows the other), open review.", "Authorship ethics: gift/honorary authorship and ghost authorship are unethical; duplicate (redundant) publication and salami slicing are misconduct.", "Copyright protects the expression of ideas, not ideas themselves; fair use / fair dealing allows limited quotation for criticism and research. Indian Copyright Act, 1957."]}, {"title": "Bibliography and Textual Criticism", "points": ["Enumerative (systematic) bibliography lists books on a subject or by an author; analytical bibliography studies books as physical objects; descriptive bibliography gives a detailed physical description of a book; textual bibliography studies how transmission affected the text.", "Ronald B. McKerrow, An Introduction to Bibliography for Literary Students (1927); Fredson Bowers, Principles of Bibliographical Description (1949).", "W. W. Greg, 'The Rationale of Copy-Text' (1950-51): accidentals (spelling, punctuation) follow the copy-text, substantives may be emended. Developed further by Bowers and G. Thomas Tanselle (Greg-Bowers-Tanselle theory).", "Jerome McGann, A Critique of Modern Textual Criticism (1983) and D. F. McKenzie, Bibliography and the Sociology of Texts (1986) stressed the social production of texts.", "Karl Lachmann's method (19th century): build a stemma (family tree of manuscripts) to reconstruct the archetype.", "Textual criticism stages: recension (selecting the most reliable evidence), examination, emendation (correcting errors).", "Book formats by folding a sheet: folio (folded once, 2 leaves), quarto (twice, 4 leaves), octavo (three times, 8 leaves). Shakespeare's First Folio was published in 1623 by Heminges and Condell.", "Incunabula: books printed before 1501; William Caxton set up the first printing press in England at Westminster in 1476.", "Colophon: publication details at the end of a book; errata: list of errors; variorum edition: an edition recording variant readings and commentaries of many editors.", "Short-Title Catalogue by A. W. Pollard and G. R. Redgrave (1926) lists English books 1475-1640; Donald Wing's catalogue covers 1641-1700; ESTC covers 1473-1800."]}, {"title": "Annotation, Abstract and Note-taking", "points": ["Annotation is a brief note added to a source: in an annotated bibliography each citation is followed by a paragraph (about 100-200 words).", "Descriptive (informative) annotation summarises content; critical (evaluative) annotation also judges usefulness, reliability and relevance.", "Marginalia are notes written in the margins of a book (e.g. Coleridge's famous marginalia).", "An abstract is a concise summary of the whole work (purpose, method, findings, conclusion), usually 150-300 words, followed by 4-6 keywords.", "Types of abstract: descriptive (states what the paper covers, no results) and informative (includes findings and conclusions).", "An abstract is written in the past or present tense, contains no citations, and is written after the research is finished though it is placed at the beginning.", "Summary condenses the content in one's own words; precis is a short exact summary keeping the original order and tone; paraphrase restates a passage in one's own words at about the same length.", "Note-taking methods: index cards (bibliography cards and note cards), Cornell method, digital notes in Zotero or similar tools."]}, {"title": "Structure of Thesis and Research Paper", "points": ["Preliminary pages: title page, declaration, supervisor's certificate, acknowledgements, abstract, table of contents, list of abbreviations/tables.", "Main body: Introduction (background, problem, objectives, hypothesis, methodology, chapter plan), Review of literature, analytical chapters, Conclusion (findings, limitations, scope for further research).", "End matter: Works Cited/Bibliography, appendices, glossary, index; plagiarism report is now attached in Indian universities.", "IMRaD structure for scientific papers: Introduction, Methods, Results, and Discussion.", "Thesis statement: one or two sentences stating the central argument of the paper.", "UGC (Minimum Standards and Procedures for Award of Ph.D. Degree) Regulations, 2022 replaced the 2016 regulations; they removed the earlier compulsory requirement of journal publication before thesis submission.", "M.Phil. was discontinued under the National Education Policy 2020 and the UGC 2022 PhD regulations.", "Umberto Eco, How to Write a Thesis (Italian 1977; English translation 2015) is a classic guide; other guides: Richard Altick, The Art of Literary Research (1963) and George Watson, The Literary Thesis (1970).", "Wellek and Warren, Theory of Literature (1949) distinguished the extrinsic approach (biography, psychology, society) from the intrinsic approach (the work itself) to literary study.", "Viva voce: the oral defence of the thesis before examiners."]}, {"title": "Archives and Archival Research", "points": ["An archive is a collection of historical records (manuscripts, letters, diaries, official papers) preserved for their enduring value.", "Finding aids (catalogues, inventories) help locate materials; provenance is the history of ownership and custody of a document.", "Archival documents: holograph/autograph (handwritten by the author), typescript, foul papers (author's working draft), fair copy, palimpsest (manuscript written over an erased earlier text).", "Major libraries/archives: British Library (est. 1973), Bodleian Library, Oxford (opened 1602 by Thomas Bodley), Folger Shakespeare Library, Washington DC (1932), Harry Ransom Center (Texas), National Archives of India, New Delhi (founded 1891 as Imperial Record Department, Calcutta).", "Michel Foucault, The Archaeology of Knowledge (1969): the archive is the system governing what can be said in a period.", "Jacques Derrida, Archive Fever (Mal d'archive, 1995): the archive is shaped by power and the desire to preserve and forget.", "Carolyn Steedman, Dust (2001) and Ann Laura Stoler, Along the Archival Grain (2009) are key works on archival theory; Stoler reads colonial archives 'along the grain'.", "Oral history records spoken memories through interviews, important for subaltern, Dalit and Partition studies (e.g. Urvashi Butalia, The Other Side of Silence, 1998)."]}, {"title": "Digital Humanities and Research Tools", "points": ["Roberto Busa, an Italian Jesuit, began the Index Thomisticus (with IBM, from 1949), a concordance of Thomas Aquinas; he is called the father of humanities computing.", "Franco Moretti coined 'distant reading' in 'Conjectures on World Literature' (2000); also Graphs, Maps, Trees (2005) and Distant Reading (2013); co-founded Stanford Literary Lab (2010).", "Matthew Jockers, Macroanalysis (2013); Stephen Ramsay, Reading Machines (2011, algorithmic criticism); N. Katherine Hayles, How We Think (2012).", "A Companion to Digital Humanities (2004), ed. Susan Schreibman, Ray Siemens and John Unsworth, helped popularise the term 'digital humanities' over 'humanities computing'.", "TEI (Text Encoding Initiative, 1987) gives XML guidelines for encoding literary texts; stylometry uses statistics of style to settle authorship (Mosteller and Wallace on The Federalist Papers, 1964).", "Digital archives: Project Gutenberg (1971, Michael Hart), Internet Archive (1996, Brewster Kahle) and its Wayback Machine, HathiTrust (2008), Rossetti Archive (Jerome McGann), William Blake Archive, EEBO, ECCO.", "Tools: Voyant Tools (text analysis), Google Ngram Viewer (2010), AntConc (concordance), topic modelling (MALLET), GIS mapping.", "Databases: JSTOR (1995), Project MUSE (1995), Scopus (Elsevier, 2004), Web of Science (Clarivate), Google Scholar (2004), MLA International Bibliography, Shodhganga (INFLIBNET, Gandhinagar).", "Reference managers: Zotero (free, open source, 2006, George Mason University), Mendeley (2008, owned by Elsevier), EndNote (Clarivate).", "Identifiers: ISBN (13 digits since 2007, earlier 10), ISSN (8 digits, for serials), DOI (Digital Object Identifier, permanent link for articles), ORCID (unique researcher ID, launched 2012).", "Impact factor was devised by Eugene Garfield (Journal Citation Reports); h-index was proposed by physicist Jorge E. Hirsch (2005); i10-index is used by Google Scholar."]}, {"title": "Qualitative and Quantitative Methods", "points": ["Quantitative research uses numerical data, measurement, statistics and large samples; it is objective and seeks generalisation (rooted in positivism, Auguste Comte).", "Qualitative research uses words, meanings, texts and experiences; it is interpretive, small-sample and in-depth (rooted in interpretivism, hermeneutics, phenomenology).", "Mixed methods research combines both; triangulation uses multiple methods, sources or researchers to check findings (Norman Denzin, 1970).", "Qualitative tools: interviews (structured, semi-structured, unstructured), focus groups, participant observation, ethnography, content analysis, discourse analysis, narrative analysis.", "Grounded theory builds theory from data: Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, The Discovery of Grounded Theory (1967).", "Thick description: Clifford Geertz, The Interpretation of Cultures (1973), borrowing the term from philosopher Gilbert Ryle.", "Quantitative tools: questionnaire, survey, Likert scale (Rensis Likert, 1932, usually 5-point), experiments, statistical tests (t-test, chi-square, ANOVA), software SPSS; NVivo and ATLAS.ti are used for qualitative coding.", "Probability sampling: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster. Non-probability sampling: convenience, purposive (judgmental), quota, snowball.", "Reliability means consistency of results on repetition; validity means the tool measures what it claims to measure.", "Measures of central tendency: mean (average), median (middle value), mode (most frequent value).", "Thomas Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), introduced 'paradigm' and 'paradigm shift'; Hans-Georg Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960) is the key text of philosophical hermeneutics."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Research", "pron": "रि-सर्च (ri-SURCH)", "hindi": "किसी विषय पर व्यवस्थित तरीके से नई जानकारी खोजना।", "net": "A systematic, careful and objective investigation to discover, interpret or revise facts, theories or applications."}, {"term": "Hypothesis", "pron": "हाइ-पॉ-थि-सिस (hy-POTH-uh-sis)", "hindi": "जाँचने लायक एक अस्थायी अनुमान।", "net": "A tentative, testable proposition about the relationship between two or more variables, to be verified by research."}, {"term": "Null Hypothesis", "pron": "नल हाइ-पॉ-थि-सिस (NULL hy-POTH-uh-sis)", "hindi": "यह मान लेना कि कोई संबंध या अंतर नहीं है।", "net": "H0: the hypothesis that there is no significant relationship or difference between variables; it is tested statistically for rejection."}, {"term": "Inductive Method", "pron": "इन-डक-टिव (in-DUK-tiv)", "hindi": "छोटे-छोटे उदाहरणों से बड़ा नियम निकालना।", "net": "Reasoning from particular observations to general conclusions; associated with Francis Bacon's Novum Organum (1620)."}, {"term": "Deductive Method", "pron": "डि-डक-टिव (di-DUK-tiv)", "hindi": "बड़े नियम से छोटे निष्कर्ष तक पहुँचना।", "net": "Reasoning from a general premise or theory to specific conclusions, as in the Aristotelian syllogism."}, {"term": "Action Research", "pron": "ऐक्-शन रि-सर्च (AK-shun ri-SURCH)", "hindi": "अपने ही काम को सुधारने के लिए की गई खोज।", "net": "Practitioner-led cyclical research (plan, act, observe, reflect) aimed at improving practice; term coined by Kurt Lewin (1946)."}, {"term": "Primary Source", "pron": "प्राइ-म-री सोर्स (PRY-muh-ree SORSS)", "hindi": "मूल सामग्री, जैसे असली उपन्यास या पत्र।", "net": "Original first-hand material such as a literary text, manuscript, letter, diary or document studied directly by the researcher."}, {"term": "Secondary Source", "pron": "से-कन-ड-री सोर्स (SEK-un-dree SORSS)", "hindi": "मूल सामग्री पर लिखी गई आलोचना या व्याख्या।", "net": "A work that analyses, interprets or comments on primary sources, such as critical books, articles and biographies."}, {"term": "Tertiary Source", "pron": "टर-शि-अ-री सोर्स (TUR-shee-air-ee SORSS)", "hindi": "विश्वकोश या शब्दकोश जैसी संकलन सामग्री।", "net": "A reference work that compiles or indexes primary and secondary sources: encyclopaedias, dictionaries, bibliographies."}, {"term": "Literature Review", "pron": "लि-ट-रे-चर रि-व्यू (LIT-ruh-chur ri-VYOO)", "hindi": "विषय पर पहले हुए काम की आलोचनात्मक समीक्षा।", "net": "A critical, organised survey and synthesis of existing scholarship on a topic that identifies the research gap."}, {"term": "Research Gap", "pron": "रि-सर्च गैप (ri-SURCH GAP)", "hindi": "वह हिस्सा जिस पर अभी तक काम नहीं हुआ।", "net": "An unexplored or insufficiently explored area in existing scholarship that justifies a new study."}, {"term": "Synopsis", "pron": "सि-नॉप-सिस (si-NOP-sis)", "hindi": "शोध की योजना का छोटा खाका।", "net": "A brief research proposal outlining the topic, problem, objectives, review, methodology and chapter plan, submitted before registration."}, {"term": "Abstract", "pron": "ऐब्-स्ट्रैक्ट (AB-strakt)", "hindi": "पूरे शोध-पत्र का छोटा सार।", "net": "A concise summary of a research work's purpose, method, findings and conclusion, usually 150-300 words, placed at the beginning."}, {"term": "Annotation", "pron": "ऐ-नो-टे-शन (an-oh-TAY-shun)", "hindi": "किसी स्रोत के साथ लिखा गया छोटा टिप्पणी-नोट।", "net": "A brief descriptive or evaluative note accompanying a citation or written in a text's margin."}, {"term": "Annotated Bibliography", "pron": "ऐ-नो-टे-टिड बिब-लि-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (AN-oh-tay-tid bib-lee-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "स्रोतों की सूची जिसमें हर एक के साथ छोटा विवरण हो।", "net": "A list of citations each followed by a short paragraph that describes and/or evaluates the source."}, {"term": "Bibliography", "pron": "बिब-लि-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (bib-lee-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "किताबों और स्रोतों की सूची; किताब का अध्ययन भी।", "net": "A list of works on a subject or consulted for a study; also the study of books as physical objects (analytical/descriptive bibliography)."}, {"term": "Works Cited", "pron": "वर्क्स साइ-टिड (WURKS SY-tid)", "hindi": "केवल उन्हीं स्रोतों की सूची जिनका हवाला दिया गया।", "net": "MLA's title for the alphabetical list of all sources actually cited in a paper, double-spaced with hanging indent."}, {"term": "Hanging Indent", "pron": "हैंग-इंग इन-डेंट (HANG-ing IN-dent)", "hindi": "पहली लाइन बाहर, बाकी लाइनें अंदर खिसकी हुई।", "net": "Formatting in which the first line of an entry is flush left and subsequent lines are indented half an inch, used in MLA Works Cited."}, {"term": "Container", "pron": "कन-टे-नर (kun-TAY-nur)", "hindi": "वह बड़ा स्रोत जिसमें आपका लेख या भाग रखा है।", "net": "MLA 8/9 term for the larger whole that holds a source, e.g. a journal, anthology, website or database."}, {"term": "Parenthetical Citation", "pron": "पै-रन-थे-टि-कल साइ-टे-शन (pa-ren-THET-i-kul sy-TAY-shun)", "hindi": "वाक्य के अंदर कोष्ठक में लेखक और पेज लिखना।", "net": "A brief in-text reference in parentheses, e.g. (Morrison 45) in MLA or (Morrison, 1987, p. 45) in APA."}, {"term": "DOI", "pron": "डी-ओ-आई (DEE-oh-EYE)", "hindi": "किसी लेख का स्थायी ऑनलाइन पहचान नंबर।", "net": "Digital Object Identifier: a permanent unique alphanumeric string assigned to a digital publication; MLA 9 prefers it to a URL."}, {"term": "Plagiarism", "pron": "प्ले-ज-रि-ज़्म (PLAY-juh-riz-um)", "hindi": "दूसरे के शब्द या विचार बिना श्रेय दिए अपना बताना।", "net": "The use of another's words, ideas or work without acknowledgement, presented as one's own; from Latin plagiarius (kidnapper)."}, {"term": "Self-plagiarism", "pron": "सेल्फ़ प्ले-ज-रि-ज़्म (SELF PLAY-juh-riz-um)", "hindi": "अपना ही पहले छपा काम बिना बताए दोबारा इस्तेमाल करना।", "net": "Reusing one's own previously published text or data without acknowledgement, including duplicate publication."}, {"term": "Patchwriting", "pron": "पैच-राइ-टिंग (PATCH-ry-ting)", "hindi": "स्रोत के वाक्यों में थोड़े शब्द बदलकर जोड़-तोड़ करना।", "net": "Rebecca Moore Howard's term for copying from a source while deleting words or substituting synonyms; also called mosaic plagiarism."}, {"term": "Paraphrase", "pron": "पै-र-फ़्रेज़ (PAIR-uh-frayz)", "hindi": "किसी अंश को अपने शब्दों में फिर से कहना।", "net": "A restatement of a passage in one's own words and structure, roughly of the same length, which still requires citation."}, {"term": "Ibid.", "pron": "इ-बिड (IB-id)", "hindi": "वही स्रोत जो ठीक पिछले नोट में था।", "net": "Abbreviation of Latin ibidem, 'in the same place', referring to the source cited in the immediately preceding note."}, {"term": "Op. cit.", "pron": "ऑप सिट (OP SIT)", "hindi": "पहले बताई गई उसी किताब में।", "net": "Opere citato, 'in the work cited': refers to a work by the same author cited earlier but not immediately before."}, {"term": "Sic", "pron": "सिक (SIK)", "hindi": "मूल में ऐसा ही लिखा है, गलती मेरी नहीं।", "net": "Latin 'thus': inserted in square brackets in a quotation to show that an error or oddity is in the original."}, {"term": "Copy-text", "pron": "कॉ-पी टेक्स्ट (KOP-ee TEKST)", "hindi": "संपादन के लिए आधार बनाया गया मूल पाठ।", "net": "The early text chosen as the basis of a critical edition; W. W. Greg's 'The Rationale of Copy-Text' (1950) governs its use."}, {"term": "Textual Criticism", "pron": "टेक्-स्चु-अल क्रि-टि-सि-ज़्म (TEKS-choo-ul KRIT-i-siz-um)", "hindi": "किसी रचना का सबसे शुद्ध पाठ तय करना।", "net": "The scholarly discipline of establishing the most authoritative text of a work by comparing manuscripts and editions."}, {"term": "Stemma", "pron": "स्टे-मा (STEM-uh)", "hindi": "पांडुलिपियों का वंश-वृक्ष।", "net": "A family-tree diagram showing relationships among surviving manuscripts of a text, used in Lachmann's method."}, {"term": "Emendation", "pron": "ई-मेन-डे-शन (ee-men-DAY-shun)", "hindi": "पाठ की गलती का सुधार।", "net": "A correction introduced by an editor into a text where the transmitted reading is judged corrupt."}, {"term": "Variorum Edition", "pron": "वै-रि-ओ-रम (vair-ee-OR-um)", "hindi": "ऐसा संस्करण जिसमें कई संपादकों के पाठ और टिप्पणियाँ हों।", "net": "An edition that records variant readings and the notes of previous editors and commentators."}, {"term": "Incunabula", "pron": "इन-क्यू-नै-ब्यू-ला (in-kyoo-NAB-yoo-luh)", "hindi": "1501 से पहले छपी शुरुआती किताबें।", "net": "Books printed in Europe before 1501, in the infancy of printing; Latin for 'cradle' or 'swaddling clothes'."}, {"term": "Colophon", "pron": "कॉ-ल-फ़न (KOL-uh-fon)", "hindi": "किताब के अंत में छपाई की जानकारी।", "net": "A statement, usually at the end of a book, giving the printer, place and date of printing; also a publisher's emblem."}, {"term": "Palimpsest", "pron": "पै-लिम्प-सेस्ट (PAL-imp-sest)", "hindi": "मिटाकर दोबारा लिखी गई पांडुलिपि।", "net": "A manuscript page whose original writing was scraped off and written over, with traces of the earlier text still visible."}, {"term": "Provenance", "pron": "प्रॉ-व-नन्स (PROV-uh-nunss)", "hindi": "किसी दस्तावेज़ के मालिकाना हक़ का इतिहास।", "net": "The documented origin and chain of ownership or custody of a manuscript, book or archival record."}, {"term": "Archive", "pron": "आर-काइव (AR-kyv)", "hindi": "पुराने दस्तावेज़ों का संग्रह।", "net": "A collection of historical records preserved for their enduring value; in Foucault, the system governing what can be said."}, {"term": "Distant Reading", "pron": "डिस-टेंट री-डिंग (DIS-tunt REE-ding)", "hindi": "कंप्यूटर से हज़ारों किताबों का एक साथ पैटर्न देखना।", "net": "Franco Moretti's term (2000) for studying literature through computational analysis of large corpora rather than close reading of few texts."}, {"term": "Digital Humanities", "pron": "डि-जि-टल ह्यू-मै-नि-टीज़ (DIJ-i-tul hyoo-MAN-i-teez)", "hindi": "साहित्य और मानविकी में कंप्यूटर का प्रयोग।", "net": "A field applying computational tools and methods (encoding, text mining, mapping, archives) to humanities research; earlier 'humanities computing'."}, {"term": "Stylometry", "pron": "स्टाइ-लॉ-मि-ट्री (sty-LOM-uh-tree)", "hindi": "लेखन-शैली को आँकड़ों से मापना।", "net": "The statistical analysis of literary style, often used to determine authorship."}, {"term": "Triangulation", "pron": "ट्राइ-ऐंग-ग्यू-ले-शन (try-ang-gyoo-LAY-shun)", "hindi": "कई तरीकों से एक ही बात को जाँचना।", "net": "Use of multiple methods, data sources, theories or investigators to cross-check findings (Norman Denzin, 1970)."}, {"term": "Thick Description", "pron": "थिक डिस-क्रिप-शन (THIK di-SKRIP-shun)", "hindi": "किसी घटना का संदर्भ सहित गहरा वर्णन।", "net": "Clifford Geertz's term (The Interpretation of Cultures, 1973), borrowed from Gilbert Ryle, for describing behaviour along with its cultural context and meaning."}, {"term": "Grounded Theory", "pron": "ग्राउन्-डिड थि-अ-री (GROWN-did THEER-ee)", "hindi": "आँकड़ों से धीरे-धीरे सिद्धांत बनाना।", "net": "Qualitative method of generating theory inductively from data, introduced by Glaser and Strauss (1967)."}, {"term": "Likert Scale", "pron": "लि-कर्ट स्केल (LIK-urt SKAYL)", "hindi": "सहमत-असहमत के 5 स्तरों वाला पैमाना।", "net": "A rating scale (usually 5 points, strongly agree to strongly disagree) for measuring attitudes, devised by Rensis Likert (1932)."}, {"term": "Reliability", "pron": "रि-लाइ-अ-बि-लि-टी (ri-ly-uh-BIL-i-tee)", "hindi": "बार-बार करने पर एक जैसा परिणाम।", "net": "The consistency and stability of a research instrument's results over repeated applications."}, {"term": "Validity", "pron": "व-लि-डि-टी (vuh-LID-i-tee)", "hindi": "जो मापना है वही सही से मापना।", "net": "The extent to which a tool or study measures what it claims to measure and its conclusions are sound."}, {"term": "Peer Review", "pron": "पीयर रि-व्यू (PEER ri-VYOO)", "hindi": "छपने से पहले विशेषज्ञों द्वारा जाँच।", "net": "Evaluation of a manuscript by independent experts in the field before publication; may be single-blind, double-blind or open."}, {"term": "Predatory Journal", "pron": "प्रे-ड-टो-री जर-नल (PRED-uh-tor-ee JUR-nul)", "hindi": "पैसा लेकर बिना असली जाँच के छापने वाली पत्रिका।", "net": "An exploitative publication that charges fees without genuine peer review or editorial standards; term popularised by Jeffrey Beall."}, {"term": "h-index", "pron": "एच इन-डेक्स (AYCH IN-deks)", "hindi": "शोधकर्ता के लेखों और उनके हवालों का माप।", "net": "Jorge Hirsch's metric (2005): a researcher has index h if h of their papers have each been cited at least h times."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "The 9th edition of the MLA Handbook was published in:", "options": ["2009", "2016", "2021", "2024"], "answer": 2, "exp": "MLA Handbook 9th edition appeared in April 2021; the 8th was 2016."}, {"q": "Which is the correct order of MLA core elements?", "options": ["Author, Title of source, Title of container, Other contributors, Version, Number, Publisher, Publication date, Location", "Author, Title of container, Title of source, Publisher, Version, Number, Location, Publication date, Other contributors", "Title of source, Author, Publisher, Container, Date, Location, Version, Number, Contributors", "Author, Publication date, Title of source, Container, Publisher, Location, Version, Number, Contributors"], "answer": 0, "exp": "MLA 8/9 list nine core elements in this exact order."}, {"q": "In MLA style, how is an in-text citation of page 45 of Toni Morrison's Beloved written?", "options": ["(Morrison, 45)", "(Morrison p. 45)", "(Morrison, 1987, p. 45)", "(Morrison 45)"], "answer": 3, "exp": "MLA uses author and page with no comma and no 'p.'; (Morrison, 1987, p. 45) is APA style."}, {"q": "In MLA Works Cited, a source by three or more authors is listed as:", "options": ["All authors' names in full", "First author's name followed by et al.", "First two authors followed by et al.", "Only the title, without authors"], "answer": 1, "exp": "MLA 9: give the first author reversed, then et al."}, {"q": "In MLA format, the Works Cited entries use:", "options": ["Single spacing and first-line indent", "Double spacing and hanging indent of half an inch", "Double spacing and no indentation", "Single spacing and hanging indent of one inch"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Works Cited is double-spaced with a 0.5-inch hanging indent."}, {"q": "According to MLA, which of the following titles should be placed in quotation marks rather than italics?", "options": ["A novel", "A journal", "A film", "A poem in an anthology"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Parts of larger works (poems, articles, stories) take quotation marks; self-contained works are italicised."}, {"q": "In MLA, a prose quotation is set off as a block quotation when it runs more than:", "options": ["Two lines", "Three lines", "Four lines", "Ten lines"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Prose over four lines (poetry over three) becomes an indented block quotation."}, {"q": "Which month is NOT abbreviated in an MLA Works Cited entry?", "options": ["January", "September", "December", "June"], "answer": 3, "exp": "May, June and July are written in full; longer months are abbreviated."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): MLA 9 recommends a DOI over a URL when both are available.\nReason (R): A DOI is a permanent identifier that does not change even if the web address changes.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "MLA prefers the DOI precisely because it is stable."}, {"q": "Match the style manual with its discipline:\na. APA  b. MLA  c. Chicago author-date  d. Vancouver\ni. Medicine  ii. Psychology and social sciences  iii. Literature and languages  iv. Sciences and social sciences", "options": ["a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i", "a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"], "answer": 0, "exp": "APA: social sciences; MLA: languages and literature; Chicago author-date: sciences; Vancouver: medicine."}, {"q": "The 7th edition of the APA Publication Manual was published in:", "options": ["2009", "2017", "2019", "2021"], "answer": 2, "exp": "APA 7 was released in October 2019."}, {"q": "The Chicago Manual of Style's 'notes and bibliography' system is preferred in:", "options": ["Natural sciences", "Humanities such as literature and history", "Engineering", "Medicine"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Chicago offers notes-bibliography for humanities and author-date for sciences."}, {"q": "'Ibid.' in a footnote means:", "options": ["Compare", "Here and there", "In the work cited earlier", "In the same place as the preceding note"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Ibidem refers to the source of the immediately preceding note."}, {"q": "Match the abbreviation with its meaning:\na. cf.  b. viz.  c. passim  d. sic\ni. thus  ii. compare  iii. namely  iv. here and there", "options": ["a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i", "a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "cf. = confer, viz. = videlicet, passim = throughout, sic = thus."}, {"q": "Under the UGC Regulations 2018 on plagiarism, a similarity of up to 10% falls in:", "options": ["Level 0", "Level 1", "Level 2", "Level 3"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Up to 10% is Level 0, a minor similarity with no penalty."}, {"q": "Under the UGC Regulations 2018, a student's thesis with above 60% similarity attracts:", "options": ["Revision within six months", "Revision within one year", "Cancellation of registration", "No penalty"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Level 3 (above 60%) leads to cancellation of registration for the programme."}, {"q": "ShodhGanga, the repository of Indian theses, is maintained by:", "options": ["NAAC", "ICSSR", "NCERT", "INFLIBNET"], "answer": 3, "exp": "INFLIBNET Centre, Gandhinagar runs ShodhGanga."}, {"q": "Which of the following is a primary source for a study of Wordsworth's poetry?", "options": ["A. C. Bradley's lecture on Wordsworth", "The Prelude", "An encyclopaedia entry on Romanticism", "A journal article on The Prelude"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The literary text itself is the primary source."}, {"q": "An encyclopaedia or a bibliography is classified as a:", "options": ["Primary source", "Secondary source", "Tertiary source", "Grey literature"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Reference works that compile other sources are tertiary."}, {"q": "A hypothesis stating that there is no difference between two groups is called:", "options": ["Alternative hypothesis", "Directional hypothesis", "Research question", "Null hypothesis"], "answer": 3, "exp": "H0, the null hypothesis, assumes no relationship or difference."}, {"q": "Rejecting a null hypothesis when it is actually true is:", "options": ["Type I error", "Type II error", "Sampling error", "Random error"], "answer": 0, "exp": "A Type I error is a false positive."}, {"q": "Who coined the term 'distant reading'?", "options": ["Roberto Busa", "Franco Moretti", "Matthew Jockers", "N. Katherine Hayles"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Moretti introduced it in 'Conjectures on World Literature' (2000)."}, {"q": "The Index Thomisticus, regarded as the beginning of humanities computing, was started by:", "options": ["Franco Moretti", "Jerome McGann", "Tim Berners-Lee", "Roberto Busa"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Father Roberto Busa began it with IBM in 1949."}, {"q": "'The Rationale of Copy-Text' (1950) was written by:", "options": ["Fredson Bowers", "R. B. McKerrow", "W. W. Greg", "Jerome McGann"], "answer": 2, "exp": "W. W. Greg's essay distinguished substantives from accidentals."}, {"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (i) McKerrow, An Introduction to Bibliography for Literary Students (ii) Greg, 'The Rationale of Copy-Text' (iii) McGann, A Critique of Modern Textual Criticism (iv) Bowers, Principles of Bibliographical Description", "options": ["i, iv, ii, iii", "i, ii, iv, iii", "iv, i, ii, iii", "ii, i, iv, iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1927, 1949, 1950, 1983."}, {"q": "Books printed before 1501 are known as:", "options": ["Palimpsests", "Incunabula", "Folios", "Holographs"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Incunabula are books from the infancy of printing, before 1501."}, {"q": "'Thick description' as a method of interpretation is associated with:", "options": ["Clifford Geertz", "Thomas Kuhn", "Karl Popper", "Barney Glaser"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Geertz used it in The Interpretation of Cultures (1973), borrowing from Gilbert Ryle."}, {"q": "Which of the following is a non-probability sampling technique?", "options": ["Stratified sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Systematic sampling", "Snowball sampling"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Snowball sampling relies on referrals, not random selection."}, {"q": "Match the tool or concept with its originator:\na. h-index  b. Likert scale  c. Action research  d. Impact factor\ni. Rensis Likert  ii. Jorge Hirsch  iii. Eugene Garfield  iv. Kurt Lewin", "options": ["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii", "a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii", "a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Hirsch (2005), Likert (1932), Lewin (1946), Garfield (JCR)."}, {"q": "Which of the following is open-source reference management software?", "options": ["EndNote", "Turnitin", "Zotero", "iThenticate"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Zotero is free and open source; Turnitin and iThenticate check plagiarism."}]}, {"id": 2, "title": "Poetry", "overview": "Is unit mein Chaucer se lekar aaj tak ki English poetry aati hai: Old/Middle English, Renaissance, Metaphysical, Neoclassical, Romantic, Victorian, Modern, post-war, American, Indian English aur postcolonial poets, saath mein prosody, metre aur poetic forms. NET mein zyada sawaal chronology, match-the-following (poet-poem-quote-character), famous lines kisne likhi, aur stanza/metre ki pehchaan par aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Old English Poetry (c. 450-1066)", "points": ["Beowulf: longest Old English epic (3182 lines), composed c. 8th-10th century, survives in a single manuscript, Cotton Vitellius A.xv (c. 1000).", "Beowulf characters: hero Beowulf (a Geat), King Hrothgar of the Danes, mead-hall Heorot, monsters Grendel, Grendel's mother and the dragon; Wiglaf is the loyal thane at the end.", "Caedmon's Hymn is the earliest recorded Old English poem, preserved in Bede's Ecclesiastical History of the English People (731) (sabse purani English kavita).", "Cynewulf signed his poems in runes: Elene, Juliana, Christ II and The Fates of the Apostles.", "Exeter Book elegies: The Wanderer, The Seafarer, The Wife's Lament, The Ruin; the riddles are also in the Exeter Book.", "The Dream of the Rood (Vercelli Book) is a dream-vision in which the Cross speaks; some lines are carved on the Ruthwell Cross.", "The Battle of Maldon commemorates the battle of 991 against the Vikings; Brunanburh (937) appears in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.", "Four Old English poetic codices: Beowulf manuscript (Nowell Codex), Exeter Book, Vercelli Book, Junius Manuscript.", "Old English verse is accentual and alliterative: four stresses per line, split into two half-lines by a caesura; uses kennings (whale-road = sea) and litotes.", "Scop = Anglo-Saxon court poet; gleeman = wandering minstrel; Widsith and Deor are scop poems."]}, {"title": "Middle English Poetry (1066-c. 1500, excluding Chaucer)", "points": ["Alliterative Revival (14th century) in the north and west Midlands revived Old English alliterative metre.", "Sir Gawain and the Green Knight (late 14th c.) by the anonymous Pearl (Gawain) Poet; same manuscript (Cotton Nero A.x) has Pearl, Patience and Cleanness (Purity).", "Gawain uses alliterative long lines ending in a 'bob and wheel'; Gawain's pentangle symbolises five sets of five virtues; the Green Knight is Bertilak de Hautdesert.", "William Langland: Piers Plowman (A, B, C texts, c. 1360s-1390s), allegorical dream vision; the dreamer is Will; Field Full of Folk.", "John Gower wrote in three languages: Mirour de l'Omme (French), Vox Clamantis (Latin), Confessio Amantis (English, 1390); Chaucer called him 'moral Gower' in Troilus and Criseyde.", "English Chaucerians: John Lydgate (Troy Book, The Fall of Princes) and Thomas Hoccleve (The Regiment of Princes).", "Scottish Chaucerians (Makars): Robert Henryson (The Testament of Cresseid), William Dunbar (Lament for the Makaris, 'Timor mortis conturbat me'), Gavin Douglas (Eneados, Scots translation of the Aeneid), James I (The Kingis Quair).", "Middle English lyrics: 'Sumer is icumen in' (c. 1260, cuckoo song) and the Harley Lyrics (MS Harley 2253).", "John Skelton (c. 1460-1529) gave his name to Skeltonics, short breathless rhyming lines (Phyllyp Sparowe).", "Popular ballads such as Sir Patrick Spens, The Twa Corbies and Robin Hood ballads were collected later by Percy (Reliques of Ancient English Poetry, 1765) and Child."]}, {"title": "Geoffrey Chaucer (c. 1343-1400)", "points": ["Dryden called him 'the father of English poetry' and said of The Canterbury Tales 'here is God's plenty' (Preface to Fables, 1700).", "Three phases: French (The Book of the Duchess c. 1369, elegy on Blanche, wife of John of Gaunt; Romaunt of the Rose), Italian (The House of Fame, The Parliament of Fowls, Troilus and Criseyde), English (The Canterbury Tales).", "Troilus and Criseyde (c. 1385) is based on Boccaccio's Il Filostrato and written in rhyme royal; Pandarus is the go-between.", "The Parliament of Fowls is linked with St Valentine's Day and uses rhyme royal; The Legend of Good Women has the first heroic couplets in English.", "The Canterbury Tales (c. 1387-1400): General Prologue opens 'Whan that Aprill with his shoures soote'; about 29 pilgrims ('wel nyne and twenty') meet at the Tabard Inn, Southwark, to visit Becket's shrine at Canterbury.", "Host Harry Bailly proposes four tales per pilgrim; only 24 tales were written; the Knight's Tale comes first, the Parson's Tale (prose) last, followed by Chaucer's Retraction.", "Famous pilgrims: the Knight ('a verray, parfit gentil knyght'), the Prioress Madame Eglentyne (brooch 'Amor vincit omnia'), the Wife of Bath Alisoun (five husbands), the Pardoner, the Summoner, the Clerk of Oxenford, the Miller, the Reeve.", "Chaucer the pilgrim tells the Tale of Sir Thopas (stopped by the Host) and then the prose Tale of Melibee.", "Other works: Boece (translation of Boethius' Consolation of Philosophy) and A Treatise on the Astrolabe.", "Matthew Arnold in The Study of Poetry (1880) denied Chaucer 'high seriousness' though praised his 'liquid diction'."]}, {"title": "Renaissance and Elizabethan Poetry", "points": ["Sir Thomas Wyatt and Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey introduced the sonnet to English; their poems appeared in Tottel's Miscellany (Songes and Sonettes, 1557).", "Wyatt: 'Whoso list to hunt' (from Petrarch, said to allude to Anne Boleyn), 'They flee from me'. Surrey wrote the first English blank verse (Aeneid Books II and IV) and shaped the English sonnet (abab cdcd efef gg).", "Edmund Spenser: The Shepheardes Calender (1579, twelve eclogues, one per month, dedicated to Sidney, glossed by 'E.K.').", "The Faerie Queene: Books I-III (1590), IV-VI (1596), Mutabilitie Cantos (1609); Gloriana = Elizabeth I; explained in the Letter to Raleigh; written in the Spenserian stanza.", "Faerie Queene knights: I Redcrosse (Holiness), II Guyon (Temperance), III Britomart (Chastity), IV Cambell and Triamond (Friendship), V Artegall (Justice), VI Calidore (Courtesy).", "Spenser also wrote Amoretti and Epithalamion (1595) and Prothalamion (1596, refrain 'Sweet Thames, run softly, till I end my song'); Charles Lamb called him 'the poets' poet'.", "Sir Philip Sidney: Astrophel and Stella (published 1591, 108 sonnets; Stella = Penelope Devereux); opening sonnet ends 'Fool, said my Muse to me, look in thy heart and write'.", "Shakespeare's Sonnets (1609, 154 sonnets, published by Thomas Thorpe, dedicated to 'Mr. W.H.'): 1-126 Fair Youth, 127-152 Dark Lady; Sonnet 18 'Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?', 116 'Let me not to the marriage of true minds', 130 'My mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun'.", "Shakespeare's narrative poems: Venus and Adonis (1593) and The Rape of Lucrece (1594), both dedicated to the Earl of Southampton.", "Marlowe: 'The Passionate Shepherd to His Love' ('Come live with me and be my love'); Raleigh answered with 'The Nymph's Reply to the Shepherd'; Marlowe also wrote Hero and Leander.", "Michael Drayton: Idea sonnets ('Since there's no help, come let us kiss and part') and Poly-Olbion; Samuel Daniel: Delia."]}, {"title": "Metaphysical and Cavalier Poets", "points": ["Dryden said Donne 'affects the metaphysics' (Discourse concerning Satire, 1693); Samuel Johnson fixed the label in the Life of Cowley (Lives of the Poets, 1779-81): 'heterogeneous ideas are yoked by violence together'.", "T.S. Eliot revived them in 'The Metaphysical Poets' (1921), coining 'dissociation of sensibility' (said to set in after the 17th century).", "John Donne (1572-1631): Songs and Sonnets (pub. 1633): 'The Canonization' ('For God's sake hold your tongue, and let me love'), 'The Sun Rising' ('Busy old fool, unruly sun'), 'The Flea', 'The Good-Morrow', 'The Relic', 'A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning' (twin compasses conceit).", "Donne's Holy Sonnets: 'Death, be not proud' and 'Batter my heart, three-personed God'; prose Devotions upon Emergent Occasions (1624): 'No man is an island', 'for whom the bell tolls'.", "George Herbert: The Temple (1633); 'The Collar', 'The Pulley', 'Virtue', 'Love (III)'; 'Easter Wings' and 'The Altar' are pattern (shaped) poems.", "Andrew Marvell: 'To His Coy Mistress' ('Had we but world enough and time'; 'Time's winged chariot hurrying near'), 'The Garden', 'The Definition of Love', 'Bermudas', 'An Horatian Ode upon Cromwell's Return from Ireland' (1650).", "Henry Vaughan: Silex Scintillans (1650); 'The Retreat' anticipates Wordsworth's Immortality Ode; 'The World' opens 'I saw Eternity the other night'.", "Richard Crashaw: Steps to the Temple, 'The Flaming Heart' (on St Teresa); Abraham Cowley: Pindarique Odes, Davideis; Thomas Traherne: Centuries of Meditations.", "Ben Jonson: 'On My First Son', 'To Celia' ('Drink to me only with thine eyes'), 'To Penshurst' (country-house poem); called Shakespeare 'not of an age, but for all time'.", "Cavaliers / Sons of Ben: Herrick ('Gather ye rosebuds while ye may', To the Virgins, Hesperides 1648), Lovelace ('Stone walls do not a prison make', To Althea, from Prison; 'I could not love thee, dear, so much, / Loved I not honour more', To Lucasta), Suckling ('Why so pale and wan, fond lover?'), Thomas Carew."]}, {"title": "John Milton (1608-1674)", "points": ["Early poems: 'On the Morning of Christ's Nativity' (1629), the companion poems L'Allegro and Il Penseroso, and the masque Comus (performed 1634 at Ludlow Castle).", "Lycidas (1637): pastoral elegy on Edward King, drowned in the Irish Sea; printed in Justa Edouardo King (1638); 'Fame is the spur'; opens 'Yet once more, O ye laurels'.", "Sonnets: 'On His Blindness' ('They also serve who only stand and wait'), 'On the Late Massacre in Piedmont' ('Avenge, O Lord, thy slaughtered saints'), 'Methought I saw my late espoused saint'.", "Paradise Lost: first edition 1667 in 10 books, second edition 1674 in 12 books; written in blank verse, defended in the note 'The Verse'.", "Stated aim in Book I: to 'justify the ways of God to men'; Satan says 'Better to reign in Hell, than serve in Heaven' and 'The mind is its own place'.", "Pandemonium is the capital of Hell (Book I); the Fall occurs in Book IX; Urania is invoked in Book VII.", "Paradise Regained (1671, 4 books) treats Christ's temptation in the wilderness; Samson Agonistes (1671) is a closet tragedy on the Greek model ('Eyeless in Gaza').", "Prose: Areopagitica (1644) defends freedom of the press.", "Blake: Milton was 'of the Devil's party without knowing it' (The Marriage of Heaven and Hell); Johnson called Lycidas 'easy, vulgar, and therefore disgusting'.", "Wordsworth's sonnet 'London, 1802' opens 'Milton! thou shouldst be living at this hour'."]}, {"title": "Neoclassical (Restoration and Augustan) and Transitional Poetry", "points": ["John Dryden: Absalom and Achitophel (1681): David = Charles II, Absalom = Duke of Monmouth, Achitophel = Earl of Shaftesbury, Zimri = Duke of Buckingham.", "Dryden's Mac Flecknoe (1682) mocks Thomas Shadwell; other poems: Annus Mirabilis (1667), Religio Laici (1682), The Hind and the Panther (1687), 'Alexander's Feast' (1697). Johnson: Dryden found English 'brick' and left it 'marble'.", "Alexander Pope: An Essay on Criticism (1711): 'A little learning is a dangerous thing', 'To err is human, to forgive divine', 'Fools rush in where angels fear to tread'.", "The Rape of the Lock (2 cantos 1712; 5 cantos 1714): Belinda = Arabella Fermor, the Baron = Lord Petre; sylphs as epic machinery; card game of ombre.", "The Dunciad (1728, hero Lewis Theobald; four-book version 1743, hero Colley Cibber); An Essay on Man (1733-34): 'Know then thyself, presume not God to scan; / The proper study of mankind is man', 'Whatever is, is right'.", "Epistle to Dr Arbuthnot (1735): Atticus = Addison, Sporus = Lord Hervey; Pope also translated the Iliad and Odyssey and wrote Eloisa to Abelard.", "Samuel Johnson: London (1738) and The Vanity of Human Wishes (1749), both imitations of Juvenal.", "Graveyard School: Edward Young, Night Thoughts (1742-45); Robert Blair, The Grave (1743); Thomas Gray, 'Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard' (1751): 'The paths of glory lead but to the grave', 'Full many a flower is born to blush unseen'.", "Pre-Romantics: James Thomson, The Seasons (1726-30); William Collins, 'Ode to Evening'; Oliver Goldsmith, The Deserted Village (1770, 'Sweet Auburn'); William Cowper, The Task (1785); George Crabbe, The Village (1783).", "Thomas Chatterton (Rowley poems), James Macpherson (Ossian, 1760s) and Robert Burns (Poems, Chiefly in the Scottish Dialect, Kilmarnock 1786: 'To a Mouse', later 'A Red, Red Rose', 'Tam o' Shanter') prepare the Romantic revival."]}, {"title": "Romantic Poetry I: Blake, Wordsworth, Coleridge", "points": ["Blake: Songs of Innocence (1789); combined Songs of Innocence and of Experience (1794), 'shewing the two contrary states of the human soul'.", "Blake pairs: 'The Lamb'/'The Tyger' ('Tyger Tyger, burning bright'), two 'Chimney Sweeper' poems, 'Holy Thursday'; 'London' has 'mind-forg'd manacles'; 'Auguries of Innocence': 'To see a World in a Grain of Sand'.", "Blake's prophetic books: The Book of Thel, The Marriage of Heaven and Hell, Milton (preface lyric 'And did those feet in ancient time', i.e. 'Jerusalem'), Jerusalem.", "Lyrical Ballads (1798, anonymous, Wordsworth and Coleridge) opens with 'The Rime of the Ancyent Marinere' and ends with 'Tintern Abbey'; Preface added in 1800, enlarged 1802.", "Preface: poetry is 'the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings' which 'takes its origin from emotion recollected in tranquillity'; language 'really used by men'.", "Wordsworth: 'Michael', Lucy poems, 'Resolution and Independence' (leech-gatherer), 'The Solitary Reaper', 'I wandered lonely as a cloud', 'Ode: Intimations of Immortality' (1807), 'My heart leaps up' ('The Child is father of the Man').", "The Prelude ('Growth of a Poet's Mind', addressed to Coleridge): finished 1805, published posthumously 1850; The Excursion (1814); Wordsworth became Poet Laureate in 1843 after Southey.", "Coleridge: 'The Rime of the Ancient Mariner' (albatross; 'Water, water, every where, / Nor any drop to drink'), 'Kubla Khan' (pub. 1816, interrupted by 'a person from Porlock'), 'Christabel' (unfinished), 'Dejection: An Ode' (1802).", "Coleridge's conversation poems: 'The Eolian Harp', 'This Lime-Tree Bower My Prison', 'Frost at Midnight'.", "Biographia Literaria (1817): Fancy vs Imagination, primary and secondary imagination, 'willing suspension of disbelief'. Wordsworth, Coleridge and Southey are called the Lake Poets."]}, {"title": "Romantic Poetry II: Byron, Shelley, Keats", "points": ["Byron: Childe Harold's Pilgrimage (1812-18; after Cantos I-II he 'awoke and found himself famous'); English Bards and Scotch Reviewers (1809); 'She Walks in Beauty'; The Prisoner of Chillon; Manfred.", "Byron's Don Juan (1819-24, unfinished, ottava rima) and The Vision of Judgment (satire on Southey); the Byronic hero; died at Missolonghi (1824) in the Greek war of independence.", "Shelley: 'Ode to the West Wind' (1819; terza rima units closing in couplets; 'If Winter comes, can Spring be far behind?'), 'To a Skylark' ('Hail to thee, blithe Spirit!'; 'Our sweetest songs are those that tell of saddest thought'), 'Ozymandias' (1818).", "Shelley's longer works: Alastor (1816), Prometheus Unbound (1820, lyrical drama), The Mask of Anarchy (1819, on Peterloo), Epipsychidion, Adonais (1821, elegy on Keats in Spenserian stanzas).", "Shelley's A Defence of Poetry (written 1821, pub. 1840): 'Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world'; Arnold called him 'a beautiful and ineffectual angel'.", "Keats: 'On First Looking into Chapman's Homer' (1816, Petrarchan sonnet, 'stout Cortez'); Endymion (1818, 'A thing of beauty is a joy for ever'); Hyperion and The Fall of Hyperion (unfinished).", "Odes of 1819: 'Ode to a Nightingale', 'Ode on a Grecian Urn' ('Beauty is truth, truth beauty'), 'Ode on Melancholy', 'Ode to Psyche', 'Ode on Indolence', 'To Autumn' ('Season of mists and mellow fruitfulness').", "Keats's narrative poems: 'The Eve of St Agnes' (Spenserian stanza), 'Isabella', 'Lamia', and the ballad 'La Belle Dame sans Merci'.", "Keats's letters: 'Negative Capability' (to his brothers, December 1817), 'the vale of Soul-making', the 'camelion poet'; he was attacked as part of the 'Cockney School' by Blackwood's.", "Keats died in Rome (1821); epitaph 'Here lies one whose name was writ in water'. Minor Romantics: Leigh Hunt ('Abou Ben Adhem'), Thomas Moore, John Clare."]}, {"title": "Victorian Poetry", "points": ["Tennyson: Poet Laureate 1850; In Memoriam A.H.H. (1850, on Arthur Henry Hallam, abba tetrameter quatrains): ''Tis better to have loved and lost', 'Nature, red in tooth and claw'.", "Tennyson's other poems: 'The Lady of Shalott', 'The Lotos-Eaters', 'Ulysses' (1842, 'To strive, to seek, to find, and not to yield'), 'Tithonus', 'Break, Break, Break', 'The Charge of the Light Brigade' (1854), Maud (1855, monodrama), Idylls of the King, 'Crossing the Bar' (1889).", "Robert Browning, master of the dramatic monologue: 'My Last Duchess' (Dramatic Lyrics, 1842), 'Porphyria's Lover', 'The Bishop Orders His Tomb', 'Fra Lippo Lippi' and 'Andrea del Sarto' (Men and Women, 1855): 'a man's reach should exceed his grasp'.", "Browning also wrote 'Rabbi Ben Ezra' ('Grow old along with me! The best is yet to be'), 'A Grammarian's Funeral', 'Childe Roland to the Dark Tower Came', Pippa Passes ('God's in his heaven, / All's right with the world!'), The Ring and the Book (1868-69, 12 books).", "Elizabeth Barrett Browning: Sonnets from the Portuguese (1850; Sonnet 43 'How do I love thee? Let me count the ways'), Aurora Leigh (1856, verse novel), 'The Cry of the Children'.", "Matthew Arnold: 'Dover Beach' (pub. 1867, 'ignorant armies clash by night'), 'The Scholar-Gipsy', 'Thyrsis' (elegy on A.H. Clough), 'Sohrab and Rustum', 'Stanzas from the Grande Chartreuse' ('Wandering between two worlds, one dead, / The other powerless to be born').", "Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood (1848): D.G. Rossetti ('The Blessed Damozel', The House of Life), Christina Rossetti ('Goblin Market' 1862, 'Remember'), William Morris (The Defence of Guenevere, The Earthly Paradise), Swinburne (Atalanta in Calydon, Poems and Ballads 1866).", "Robert Buchanan attacked Rossetti and Swinburne in 'The Fleshly School of Poetry' (1871).", "G.M. Hopkins: 'The Wreck of the Deutschland' (1875), 'The Windhover' ('To Christ our Lord'), 'God's Grandeur', 'Pied Beauty', the 'terrible sonnets' ('Carrion Comfort'); sprung rhythm, inscape and instress; poems published by Robert Bridges in 1918.", "Edward FitzGerald, The Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam (1859); A.H. Clough, 'Say not the Struggle Nought Availeth'; Thomas Hardy: 'The Darkling Thrush' (dated 31 December 1900), 'The Convergence of the Twain' (Titanic), Poems of 1912-13, The Dynasts."]}, {"title": "Modern Poetry (1900-1939): Yeats, Eliot, Pound, Imagism, War Poets", "points": ["W.B. Yeats (Nobel 1923): 'The Lake Isle of Innisfree' (1890), 'The Wild Swans at Coole', 'Easter, 1916' ('A terrible beauty is born'), 'An Irish Airman Foresees His Death'.", "Yeats: 'The Second Coming' ('Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold'; 'rough beast' slouching towards Bethlehem), 'Sailing to Byzantium' (The Tower, 1928, ottava rima), 'Byzantium', 'Leda and the Swan', 'Among School Children' ('How can we know the dancer from the dance?'); A Vision (1925) explains the gyres.", "Yeats's 'Under Ben Bulben' ends with his epitaph 'Cast a cold eye / On life, on death. / Horseman, pass by!'.", "T.S. Eliot: 'The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock' (1915; 'I have measured out my life with coffee spoons'); The Waste Land (1922), dedicated to Pound 'il miglior fabbro', five parts: The Burial of the Dead, A Game of Chess, The Fire Sermon, Death by Water, What the Thunder Said.", "The Waste Land opens 'April is the cruellest month', ends 'Shantih shantih shantih'; Tiresias is the central consciousness; notes cite Jessie Weston's From Ritual to Romance and Frazer's The Golden Bough.", "Eliot's later poems: 'The Hollow Men' (1925, 'Not with a bang but a whimper'), Ash-Wednesday (1930), Four Quartets (Burnt Norton 1936, East Coker 1940, The Dry Salvages 1941, Little Gidding 1942); Nobel 1948.", "Imagism (c. 1912-17): Ezra Pound, H.D. ('Oread'), Richard Aldington, T.E. Hulme, later Amy Lowell; Pound's 'A Few Don'ts by an Imagiste' (1913) and anthology Des Imagistes (1914); 'In a Station of the Metro' (1913) is the model Imagist poem.", "Pound also wrote Cathay (1915), Hugh Selwyn Mauberley (1920) and The Cantos; slogan 'Make it new'.", "Georgian Poetry anthologies (1912-22) edited by Edward Marsh; A.E. Housman, A Shropshire Lad (1896); Walter de la Mare, 'The Listeners'; Edward Thomas, 'Adlestrop'.", "WWI poets: Rupert Brooke, 'The Soldier' ('some corner of a foreign field / That is for ever England'); Wilfred Owen, 'Dulce et Decorum Est', 'Anthem for Doomed Youth', 'Strange Meeting' (pararhyme); Owen's Preface: 'My subject is War, and the pity of War. The poetry is in the pity.'", "Siegfried Sassoon ('The General', 'Base Details'), Isaac Rosenberg ('Break of Day in the Trenches'), John McCrae ('In Flanders Fields', a rondeau)."]}, {"title": "Thirties and Post-War British Poetry", "points": ["The 1930s 'Auden Group' or Pylon Poets: W.H. Auden, Stephen Spender ('The Pylons'), C. Day-Lewis, Louis MacNeice ('Snow', Autumn Journal); anthology New Signatures (1932, ed. Michael Roberts).", "Auden: 'Musee des Beaux Arts' (1938, Brueghel's Icarus), 'In Memory of W. B. Yeats' (1939: 'poetry makes nothing happen'), 'September 1, 1939' ('a low dishonest decade'), 'The Unknown Citizen', 'Funeral Blues' ('Stop all the clocks'), The Age of Anxiety (1947).", "Dylan Thomas: 'Fern Hill', 'And death shall have no dominion', villanelle 'Do not go gentle into that good night' (1951, for his father), radio play Under Milk Wood (1954); linked with the 1940s New Apocalypse.", "The Movement: name from J.D. Scott in The Spectator (1954); anthologies Poets of the 1950s (D.J. Enright, 1955) and New Lines (Robert Conquest, 1956); members include Philip Larkin, Kingsley Amis, Donald Davie, John Wain, Elizabeth Jennings, D.J. Enright, Thom Gunn.", "Philip Larkin: The Less Deceived (1955, 'Church Going'), The Whitsun Weddings (1964), High Windows (1974: 'This Be The Verse', 'Annus Mirabilis' - 'Sexual intercourse began / In nineteen sixty-three'), 'Aubade' (1977).", "Ted Hughes: The Hawk in the Rain (1957, 'The Thought-Fox'), Lupercal (1960, 'Hawk Roosting', 'Pike'), Crow (1970), Birthday Letters (1998, on Sylvia Plath); Poet Laureate 1984.", "Seamus Heaney (Nobel 1995): Death of a Naturalist (1966, 'Digging': 'Between my finger and my thumb / The squat pen rests'), Wintering Out (1972, 'The Tollund Man'), North (1975, bog poems), Field Work, Station Island, translation of Beowulf (1999).", "Others: Stevie Smith ('Not Waving but Drowning'), R.S. Thomas, John Betjeman (Laureate 1972), Geoffrey Hill (Mercian Hymns), Tony Harrison ('v.'), Basil Bunting (Briggflatts).", "Carol Ann Duffy became the first woman Poet Laureate (2009); The World's Wife (1999). Simon Armitage became Laureate in 2019."]}, {"title": "American Poetry", "points": ["Anne Bradstreet, The Tenth Muse Lately Sprung Up in America (1650), first American published poet; Edward Taylor, Preparatory Meditations.", "Edgar Allan Poe: 'The Raven' (1845, 'Quoth the Raven, Nevermore'), 'Annabel Lee', 'To Helen'; essay 'The Philosophy of Composition'. Longfellow: The Song of Hiawatha (trochaic tetrameter), Evangeline (dactylic hexameter). Emerson: 'Concord Hymn' ('the shot heard round the world'), 'Brahma'.", "Walt Whitman: Leaves of Grass (1855; revised to the 'deathbed edition' 1891-92), free verse; 'Song of Myself' ('I celebrate myself, and sing myself'; 'I contain multitudes'), 'Crossing Brooklyn Ferry'; Lincoln elegies 'When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom'd' and 'O Captain! My Captain!'.", "Emily Dickinson (1830-86): nearly 1,800 poems, very few published in her lifetime; dashes, slant rhyme, hymn metre; 'Because I could not stop for Death', 'I heard a Fly buzz - when I died', 'Hope is the thing with feathers', 'Tell all the truth but tell it slant'.", "Robert Frost: 'Mending Wall' ('Good fences make good neighbors'), 'The Road Not Taken', 'Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening' ('miles to go before I sleep'), 'Birches', 'After Apple-Picking', 'Home Burial', 'Fire and Ice'; four Pulitzer Prizes; recited 'The Gift Outright' at Kennedy's inauguration (1961).", "Frost's essay 'The Figure a Poem Makes': a poem 'begins in delight and ends in wisdom'.", "Wallace Stevens: Harmonium (1923), 'Sunday Morning', 'Anecdote of the Jar', 'The Emperor of Ice-Cream', 'Thirteen Ways of Looking at a Blackbird', 'The Idea of Order at Key West'. W.C. Williams: 'The Red Wheelbarrow' ('so much depends'), Paterson, 'no ideas but in things'.", "Marianne Moore, 'Poetry' ('imaginary gardens with real toads in them'); E.E. Cummings; Hart Crane, The Bridge (1930); Langston Hughes (Harlem Renaissance): 'The Negro Speaks of Rivers', 'Harlem' ('What happens to a dream deferred?').", "Confessional poetry: term coined by M.L. Rosenthal reviewing Robert Lowell's Life Studies (1959); Sylvia Plath (Ariel 1965: 'Daddy', 'Lady Lazarus'), Anne Sexton, John Berryman (The Dream Songs), W.D. Snodgrass (Heart's Needle).", "Beat poets: Allen Ginsberg, Howl (1956: 'I saw the best minds of my generation destroyed by madness'), Lawrence Ferlinghetti (City Lights); Black Mountain poet Charles Olson, 'Projective Verse' (1950).", "Elizabeth Bishop ('One Art', villanelle; 'The Fish'), Adrienne Rich ('Diving into the Wreck', 1973), Maya Angelou ('Still I Rise')."]}, {"title": "Indian English Poetry", "points": ["Henry Louis Vivian Derozio (1809-31): 'To India - My Native Land', The Fakeer of Jungheera (1828); Michael Madhusudan Dutt: The Captive Ladie (1849).", "Toru Dutt: A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields (1876); Ancient Ballads and Legends of Hindustan (1882, posthumous), includes 'Our Casuarina Tree'.", "Sarojini Naidu, 'Nightingale of India': The Golden Threshold (1905), The Bird of Time (1912), The Broken Wing (1917); 'In the Bazaars of Hyderabad', 'Palanquin Bearers'.", "Rabindranath Tagore: Gitanjali (Song Offerings, 1912, introduction by Yeats); Nobel Prize 1913. Sri Aurobindo: Savitri: A Legend and a Symbol, epic in blank verse.", "Nissim Ezekiel, father of modern Indian English poetry: A Time to Change (1952), The Exact Name (1965), Latter-Day Psalms (Sahitya Akademi Award 1983); 'Night of the Scorpion', 'Enterprise', 'The Professor', 'Goodbye Party for Miss Pushpa T.S.', 'Poet, Lover, Birdwatcher'.", "A.K. Ramanujan: The Striders (1966), Relations (1971), Second Sight (1986); 'Small-Scale Reflections on a Great House', 'Obituary', 'A River'; translations The Interior Landscape (1967, Sangam poems) and Speaking of Siva (1973).", "Kamala Das: Summer in Calcutta (1965), The Descendants (1967), The Old Playhouse and Other Poems (1973); 'An Introduction' ('I am Indian, very brown, born in Malabar'); autobiography My Story.", "Jayanta Mahapatra: Relationship won the Sahitya Akademi Award (1981), first for Indian English poetry; 'Hunger', 'Indian Summer'. Keki N. Daruwalla: The Keeper of the Dead (Sahitya Akademi 1984).", "Arun Kolatkar: Jejuri (1976, Commonwealth Poetry Prize 1977); R. Parthasarathy: Rough Passage (1977); Dom Moraes: A Beginning (1957, Hawthornden Prize); A.K. Mehrotra, Gieve Patel, Adil Jussawalla, Eunice de Souza (Fix), Dilip Chitre.", "Diaspora and contemporary: Agha Shahid Ali (The Country Without a Post Office, English ghazals), Vikram Seth (The Golden Gate 1986, novel in Onegin stanzas), Jeet Thayil (These Errors Are Correct, Sahitya Akademi 2013), Meena Kandasamy, Arundhathi Subramaniam, Ranjit Hoskote."]}, {"title": "Postcolonial and Commonwealth Poetry", "points": ["Derek Walcott (St Lucia, Nobel 1992): In a Green Night (1962) with 'A Far Cry from Africa' ('I who am poisoned with the blood of both'); 'The Schooner Flight' ('either I'm nobody, or I'm a nation'); Omeros (1990), Caribbean epic echoing Homer, in a loose terza rima.", "Kamau Brathwaite (Barbados): The Arrivants trilogy - Rights of Passage, Masks, Islands; theory of 'nation language' (History of the Voice, 1984).", "Other Caribbean voices: Louise Bennett (Jamaican Creole), Grace Nichols (I is a Long Memoried Woman, 1983), Linton Kwesi Johnson (dub poetry), Lorna Goodison.", "Negritude movement: Leopold Sedar Senghor, Aime Cesaire, Leon Damas; David Diop's 'Africa'.", "Wole Soyinka (Nigeria, first African Nobel laureate, 1986): 'Telephone Conversation' satirises a racist landlady.", "Christopher Okigbo: 'Heavensgate', Labyrinths (1971, posthumous); killed in the Biafran war (1967). J.P. Clark: 'Night Rain'; Gabriel Okara: 'Piano and Drums', 'Once Upon a Time'.", "Okot p'Bitek (Uganda): Song of Lawino (1966); Dennis Brutus (South Africa): Letters to Martha; Kofi Awoonor (Ghana).", "Australia: A.D. Hope ('Australia'), Judith Wright, Les Murray; Oodgeroo Noonuccal (Kath Walker), We Are Going (1964), first Aboriginal woman to publish a book of verse.", "Canada: E.J. Pratt, A.M. Klein, Margaret Atwood (The Journals of Susanna Moodie, 1970); New Zealand: Allen Curnow.", "South Asia beyond India: Faiz Ahmed Faiz (Urdu, widely translated), Taufiq Rafat (Pakistan), Yasmine Gooneratne (Sri Lanka)."]}, {"title": "Prosody and Metre", "points": ["Metrical feet: iamb (u /, 'a-BOUT'), trochee (/ u, 'TI-ger'), anapaest (u u /, 'in-ter-VENE'), dactyl (/ u u, 'MUR-mur-ing'), spondee (/ /), pyrrhic (u u), amphibrach (u / u) (u = unstressed, / = stressed).", "Line lengths: monometer 1 foot, dimeter 2, trimeter 3, tetrameter 4, pentameter 5, hexameter 6, heptameter 7; iambic hexameter = alexandrine; iambic heptameter = fourteener.", "Iambic pentameter is the commonest English line: 'Shall I / com-PARE / thee TO / a SUM / mer's DAY'.", "Blank verse = unrhymed iambic pentameter: introduced by Surrey, Marlowe's 'mighty line' (Jonson's phrase), used by Shakespeare, Milton (Paradise Lost), Wordsworth (The Prelude).", "Heroic couplet = rhymed iambic pentameter couplets; first used by Chaucer, perfected as the closed couplet by Dryden and Pope.", "Metrical systems: accentual (Old English, stresses only), accentual-syllabic (most English verse), syllabic (Marianne Moore, haiku), quantitative (Greek and Latin, long and short syllables).", "Sprung rhythm (Hopkins): counts only stresses, each foot having one stress plus any number of slack syllables; uses 'outrides'.", "Caesura = pause inside a line (marked ||); enjambment = sense runs over the line-end; end-stopped line = sense pauses at line-end; catalexis = last foot lacks a syllable (Blake's 'Tyger' is catalectic trochaic tetrameter).", "Rhyme types: masculine (single stressed syllable: 'hill/still'), feminine ('motion/ocean'), internal rhyme, eye rhyme ('love/move'), slant or half rhyme (Dickinson), pararhyme ('escaped/scooped', Owen).", "Sound devices: alliteration (initial consonants), assonance (vowel sounds), consonance (consonant sounds), onomatopoeia, euphony and cacophony.", "Common or ballad metre: alternating iambic tetrameter and trimeter (8.6.8.6), rhymed abcb or abab; poulter's measure alternates alexandrine and fourteener.", "Famous metres: Byron's 'The Destruction of Sennacherib' (anapaestic tetrameter, 'The Assyrian came down like the wolf on the fold'); Longfellow's Hiawatha (trochaic tetrameter); Evangeline (dactylic hexameter); Whitman (free verse / vers libre)."]}, {"title": "Poetic Forms, Stanzas and Genres", "points": ["Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet: octave abbaabba + sestet cdecde or cdcdcd, volta at line 9; Shakespearean: abab cdcd efef gg; Spenserian sonnet: abab bcbc cdcd ee (Amoretti); Miltonic sonnet runs octave into sestet without a clear break.", "Curtal sonnet (Hopkins, 'Pied Beauty', 10 and a half lines); Meredith's Modern Love uses 16-line 'sonnets'; a sonnet sequence links many sonnets (Astrophel and Stella).", "Ode types: Pindaric (strophe, antistrophe, epode; Gray's 'The Progress of Poesy'), Horatian (uniform stanzas; Marvell's Horatian Ode, Keats's odes), Irregular or Cowleyan (Wordsworth's Immortality Ode).", "Pastoral elegy conventions: invocation of the muse, nature mourns, procession of mourners, flower passage, consolation; examples Lycidas, Adonais, Thyrsis; Whitman's 'Lilacs' and Tennyson's In Memoriam are also elegies.", "Epic conventions: invocation, in medias res, epic simile, catalogue, epic machinery; mock-epic or mock-heroic applies them to trivial matters (The Rape of the Lock, Mac Flecknoe, The Dunciad).", "Ballad: narrative song in ballad stanza with refrain and incremental repetition; folk ballad ('Sir Patrick Spens') vs literary ballad ('The Rime of the Ancient Mariner', 'La Belle Dame sans Merci').", "Dramatic monologue: a single speaker, a silent listener, a revealing situation (Browning's 'My Last Duchess', Tennyson's 'Ulysses', Eliot's 'Prufrock').", "Stanzas: terza rima aba bcb cdc (Dante; Shelley's 'West Wind'); ottava rima abababcc (Byron's Don Juan, Yeats's 'Sailing to Byzantium'); rhyme royal ababbcc (Chaucer's Troilus); Spenserian stanza ababbcbcc (eight pentameters + an alexandrine); In Memoriam stanza abba tetrameter; Burns stanza / Standard Habbie aaabab.", "Villanelle: 19 lines, five tercets and a quatrain, two refrains, two rhymes (Thomas's 'Do not go gentle', Bishop's 'One Art', Empson's 'Missing Dates').", "Sestina: six six-line stanzas plus a three-line envoi (39 lines), repeating six end-words (Sidney's double sestina 'Ye Goatherd Gods'); rondeau ('In Flanders Fields'); triolet (8 lines, 2 rhymes); ballade (three stanzas and envoi).", "Other forms: epithalamion (wedding song) and prothalamion (Spenser), aubade (dawn song: Donne's 'The Sun Rising', Larkin's 'Aubade'), eclogue/idyll (pastoral dialogue), haiku (5-7-5), ghazal (couplets with radif and qafia), limerick (Edward Lear), clerihew (E.C. Bentley), shaped/concrete poem ('Easter Wings').", "Elegiac stanza / heroic quatrain: abab iambic pentameter quatrains, used in Gray's 'Elegy' and Dryden's Annus Mirabilis."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Alliteration", "pron": "ए-लि-टे-रे-शन (uh-li-tuh-RAY-shun)", "hindi": "एक जैसी शुरुआती व्यंजन ध्वनि का दोहराव", "net": "Repetition of the same initial consonant sounds in nearby stressed syllables; the structural basis of Old English verse (e.g. 'Fair is foul, and foul is fair')."}, {"term": "Assonance", "pron": "ऐ-सो-नेंस (AS-uh-nuns)", "hindi": "पास-पास शब्दों में एक जैसी स्वर ध्वनि का दोहराव", "net": "Repetition of identical or similar vowel sounds in stressed syllables of neighbouring words whose consonants differ (e.g. 'lake' and 'fate')."}, {"term": "Consonance", "pron": "कॉन-सो-नेंस (KON-suh-nuns)", "hindi": "शब्दों में एक जैसी व्यंजन ध्वनि का दोहराव, स्वर अलग", "net": "Repetition of a sequence of consonants with a change in the intervening vowel (e.g. 'live/love', 'chitter/chatter'); also used loosely for repeated end consonants."}, {"term": "Kenning", "pron": "के-निंग (KEN-ing)", "hindi": "किसी चीज़ का दो शब्दों वाला चित्रात्मक नाम, जैसे समुद्र = व्हेल-मार्ग", "net": "A figurative compound phrase substituted for a common noun, characteristic of Old English and Old Norse poetry (e.g. 'whale-road' for sea, 'bone-house' for body)."}, {"term": "Caesura", "pron": "सी-ज़्यू-रा (si-ZYOOR-uh)", "hindi": "पंक्ति के बीच में आने वाला ठहराव", "net": "A pause or break within a line of verse, usually near the middle and marked by punctuation or sense; essential in Old English alliterative verse (marked ||)."}, {"term": "Enjambment", "pron": "इन-जैम-मेंट (in-JAM-ment)", "hindi": "वाक्य का एक पंक्ति से अगली पंक्ति में बिना रुके चले जाना", "net": "The running-on of the sense and grammar of one verse line into the next without a pause at the line-end; opposite of an end-stopped line (French enjambement)."}, {"term": "Iamb", "pron": "आय-ऐम (EYE-am)", "hindi": "एक हल्का और फिर एक ज़ोरदार अक्षर वाला छंद-पद (u /)", "net": "A metrical foot of an unstressed syllable followed by a stressed one (u /), as in 'de-LIGHT'; the commonest foot in English verse."}, {"term": "Trochee", "pron": "ट्रो-की (TROH-kee)", "hindi": "पहले ज़ोरदार फिर हल्का अक्षर वाला छंद-पद (/ u)", "net": "A metrical foot of a stressed syllable followed by an unstressed one (/ u), as in 'TY-ger'; Longfellow's Hiawatha is in trochaic tetrameter."}, {"term": "Anapaest", "pron": "ऐ-ना-पेस्ट (AN-uh-pest)", "hindi": "दो हल्के और फिर एक ज़ोरदार अक्षर वाला पद (u u /)", "net": "A metrical foot of two unstressed syllables followed by a stressed one (u u /), as in 'in-ter-VENE'; e.g. Byron's 'The Destruction of Sennacherib'."}, {"term": "Dactyl", "pron": "डैक-टिल (DAK-til)", "hindi": "एक ज़ोरदार फिर दो हल्के अक्षरों वाला पद (/ u u)", "net": "A metrical foot of one stressed syllable followed by two unstressed (/ u u), as in 'MUR-mur-ing'; dactylic hexameter is the metre of Homer and Virgil."}, {"term": "Spondee", "pron": "स्पॉन-डी (SPON-dee)", "hindi": "दो ज़ोरदार अक्षरों वाला पद (/ /)", "net": "A metrical foot of two stressed syllables (/ /), as in 'heart-break'; usually a substitute foot within an iambic or trochaic line."}, {"term": "Pentameter", "pron": "पेन-टै-मि-टर (pen-TAM-i-ter)", "hindi": "पाँच पदों (feet) वाली काव्य पंक्ति", "net": "A line of verse consisting of five metrical feet; iambic pentameter is the staple line of English poetry from Chaucer onward."}, {"term": "Alexandrine", "pron": "ऐ-लिग-ज़ैन-ड्रीन (al-ig-ZAN-dreen)", "hindi": "छह आयम्बिक पदों वाली लंबी पंक्ति", "net": "A line of six iambic feet (iambic hexameter), named after medieval French poems on Alexander the Great; the ninth line of the Spenserian stanza. Pope mocked it: 'A needless Alexandrine ends the song'."}, {"term": "Blank Verse", "pron": "ब्लैंक वर्स (blank VURS)", "hindi": "बिना तुक वाली आयम्बिक पेंटामीटर कविता", "net": "Unrhymed iambic pentameter; introduced into English by the Earl of Surrey (Aeneid translation), used in Elizabethan drama, Paradise Lost and The Prelude."}, {"term": "Heroic Couplet", "pron": "हि-रो-इक कप-लेट (hi-ROH-ik KUP-lit)", "hindi": "तुकबंद आयम्बिक पेंटामीटर पंक्तियों का जोड़ा", "net": "A pair of rhymed lines in iambic pentameter; introduced by Chaucer, perfected as the 'closed couplet' by Dryden and Pope in the Neoclassical age."}, {"term": "Free Verse (Vers Libre)", "pron": "फ्री वर्स / वेर ली-ब्र (free VURS / vair LEE-bruh)", "hindi": "नियमित छंद और तुक से मुक्त कविता", "net": "Verse without regular metre or rhyme, organised by cadence, line-breaks and repetition; French vers libre; pioneered in English by Whitman, adopted by the Imagists and Modernists."}, {"term": "Sprung Rhythm", "pron": "स्प्रंग रिदम (sprung RITH-um)", "hindi": "केवल ज़ोरदार अक्षर गिनने वाली लय, हॉपकिंस की", "net": "G.M. Hopkins's term for a rhythm measured by the number of stressed syllables only, each foot beginning with a stress followed by any number of unstressed syllables, as in 'The Windhover'."}, {"term": "Inscape", "pron": "इन-स्केप (IN-skayp)", "hindi": "हर वस्तु का अपना अनोखा भीतरी स्वरूप", "net": "Hopkins's term for the unique inner form or distinctive pattern that makes each natural thing itself; instress is the force that sustains it and impresses it on the beholder."}, {"term": "Conceit", "pron": "कन-सीट (kun-SEET)", "hindi": "दो बिल्कुल अलग चीज़ों की चौंकाने वाली, विस्तृत तुलना", "net": "An elaborate or far-fetched figure establishing a striking parallel between very dissimilar things; the Petrarchan conceit exaggerates the lover's suffering."}, {"term": "Metaphysical Conceit", "pron": "मे-टा-फि-ज़ि-कल कन-सीट (met-uh-FIZ-i-kul kun-SEET)", "hindi": "विद्वत्तापूर्ण, तर्क से भरी अनोखी तुलना (डन की कविता जैसी)", "net": "An intellectually ingenious conceit drawn from learning, science or theology, worked out with logic; e.g. Donne's twin compasses in 'A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning'. Johnson: 'heterogeneous ideas yoked by violence together'."}, {"term": "Dramatic Monologue", "pron": "ड्रा-मै-टिक मो-नो-लॉग (druh-MAT-ik MON-uh-log)", "hindi": "एक पात्र का किसी चुप श्रोता से नाटकीय भाषण, जिससे उसका चरित्र खुलता है", "net": "A lyric poem in which a single speaker, not the poet, addresses a silent auditor at a critical moment, unwittingly revealing his character; perfected by Robert Browning ('My Last Duchess')."}, {"term": "Ode", "pron": "ओड (OHD)", "hindi": "किसी विषय की प्रशंसा में गंभीर, लंबी गीति-कविता", "net": "A long, serious, elaborately structured lyric on an exalted theme; kinds are the Pindaric (strophe, antistrophe, epode), the Horatian (homostrophic) and the irregular (Cowleyan) ode."}, {"term": "Elegy", "pron": "ए-ली-जी (EL-uh-jee)", "hindi": "किसी की मृत्यु पर शोक-गीत", "net": "A formal poem of lament and meditation on the death of a particular person, or on mortality generally (Gray's 'Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard')."}, {"term": "Pastoral Elegy", "pron": "पास्-टो-रल ए-ली-जी (PAS-tuh-rul EL-uh-jee)", "hindi": "गड़रियों के परिवेश में लिखा शोक-गीत", "net": "An elegy in which poet and mourned friend are represented as shepherds, with conventions such as invocation, nature's mourning, procession of mourners, flower passage and consolation (Lycidas, Adonais, Thyrsis)."}, {"term": "Volta", "pron": "वोल-टा (VOHL-tuh)", "hindi": "सॉनेट में विचार का मोड़", "net": "The 'turn' in thought or argument in a sonnet, usually at line 9 of the Petrarchan sonnet (between octave and sestet) or at the final couplet of the Shakespearean sonnet."}, {"term": "Octave and Sestet", "pron": "ऑक-टेव / सेस-टेट (OK-tiv / ses-TET)", "hindi": "सॉनेट की पहली आठ और अंतिम छह पंक्तियाँ", "net": "The two divisions of the Petrarchan sonnet: the octave (first eight lines, abbaabba) states a problem; the sestet (last six lines) resolves or comments on it."}, {"term": "Villanelle", "pron": "वि-ला-नेल (vil-uh-NEL)", "hindi": "19 पंक्तियों और दो दोहराई जाने वाली पंक्तियों का फ़्रांसीसी रूप", "net": "A French fixed form of 19 lines: five tercets (aba) and a closing quatrain (abaa), using two rhymes and two refrains that alternate and join at the end (Dylan Thomas, 'Do not go gentle')."}, {"term": "Sestina", "pron": "सेस-टी-ना (ses-TEE-nuh)", "hindi": "छह अंत-शब्दों को बदलते क्रम में दोहराने वाली 39 पंक्तियों की कविता", "net": "A fixed form of six six-line stanzas and a three-line envoi in which the same six end-words recur in a rotating order; invented by the Provencal troubadour Arnaut Daniel."}, {"term": "Terza Rima", "pron": "टेर-त्सा री-मा (TAIR-tsuh REE-muh)", "hindi": "तीन-तीन पंक्तियों की जुड़ती तुक (aba bcb cdc)", "net": "An Italian form of linked tercets rhyming aba bcb cdc, used by Dante in the Divine Comedy; in English by Shelley ('Ode to the West Wind')."}, {"term": "Ottava Rima", "pron": "ओ-टा-वा री-मा (oh-TAH-vuh REE-muh)", "hindi": "आठ पंक्तियों का छंद, तुक abababcc", "net": "An Italian stanza of eight lines rhyming abababcc (in English, iambic pentameter), used by Ariosto and Tasso; in English by Byron (Don Juan) and Yeats ('Sailing to Byzantium')."}, {"term": "Rhyme Royal", "pron": "राइम रॉयल (rime ROY-ul)", "hindi": "सात पंक्तियों का छंद, तुक ababbcc", "net": "A seven-line iambic pentameter stanza rhyming ababbcc, introduced by Chaucer (Troilus and Criseyde, Parliament of Fowls); the name is often linked to James I of Scotland's The Kingis Quair."}, {"term": "Spenserian Stanza", "pron": "स्पेन-सी-रि-यन स्टैन-ज़ा (spen-SEER-ee-un STAN-zuh)", "hindi": "नौ पंक्तियों का छंद, आठ पेंटामीटर और अंत में एक अलेक्ज़ेंड्रीन", "net": "A nine-line stanza of eight iambic pentameters and a final alexandrine rhyming ababbcbcc, devised by Spenser for The Faerie Queene; used by Byron (Childe Harold), Keats (The Eve of St Agnes), Shelley (Adonais)."}, {"term": "Ballad", "pron": "बै-लड (BAL-ud)", "hindi": "लोक-गीत शैली में सरल कहानी कहने वाली कविता", "net": "A short narrative song transmitted orally, told impersonally with dialogue, refrain and incremental repetition, usually in ballad stanza; the literary ballad imitates it (The Rime of the Ancient Mariner)."}, {"term": "Mock-Epic (Mock-Heroic)", "pron": "मॉक ए-पिक (mok EP-ik)", "hindi": "छोटी बात को महाकाव्य की भव्य शैली में लिखकर व्यंग्य करना", "net": "A satiric form that applies the elevated style and conventions of epic to a trivial subject, as in Pope's The Rape of the Lock and Dryden's Mac Flecknoe."}, {"term": "Epic Simile (Homeric Simile)", "pron": "ए-पिक सि-मि-ली (EP-ik SIM-uh-lee)", "hindi": "महाकाव्य में आने वाली लंबी, विस्तार वाली उपमा", "net": "An extended, elaborate simile running over several lines, comparing a heroic action with a familiar one; used by Homer, Virgil and Milton (Satan's shield compared to the moon)."}, {"term": "Apostrophe", "pron": "अ-पॉस-ट्रो-फ़ी (uh-POS-truh-fee)", "hindi": "किसी अनुपस्थित व्यक्ति या निर्जीव वस्तु को सीधे संबोधित करना", "net": "A figure of speech in which the speaker directly addresses an absent person, an abstraction or an inanimate object ('O wild West Wind', 'Milton! thou shouldst be living at this hour')."}, {"term": "Pathetic Fallacy", "pron": "पै-थे-टिक फ़ै-ल-सी (puh-THET-ik FAL-uh-see)", "hindi": "प्रकृति पर मनुष्य की भावनाएँ आरोपित करना", "net": "John Ruskin's term (Modern Painters, vol. III, 1856) for attributing human feelings and responses to inanimate nature ('the cruel, crawling foam')."}, {"term": "Negative Capability", "pron": "नेगेटिव केपेबिलिटी (NEG-uh-tiv kay-puh-BIL-i-tee)", "hindi": "अनिश्चितता और रहस्य को बिना जल्दबाज़ी के तर्क के स्वीकार करने की क्षमता", "net": "Keats's phrase (letter to his brothers, December 1817) for the capacity of being 'in uncertainties, mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact and reason', which he found pre-eminently in Shakespeare."}, {"term": "Objective Correlative", "pron": "ऑब्-जेक्-टिव कॉ-रि-लेटिव (ob-JEK-tiv kuh-REL-uh-tiv)", "hindi": "भाव जगाने वाली वस्तुओं, स्थितियों या घटनाओं की श्रृंखला", "net": "T.S. Eliot's term in 'Hamlet and His Problems' (1919): 'a set of objects, a situation, a chain of events which shall be the formula of that particular emotion' and which evokes it in the reader."}, {"term": "Dissociation of Sensibility", "pron": "डि-सो-सि-ए-शन ऑफ़ सेन-सि-बि-लि-टी (di-soh-see-AY-shun of sen-suh-BIL-i-tee)", "hindi": "सोच और भावना का अलग-अलग हो जाना", "net": "Eliot's phrase in 'The Metaphysical Poets' (1921) for the split between thought and feeling that, he claimed, set in after the Metaphysical poets, aggravated by Milton and Dryden."}, {"term": "Imagism", "pron": "इ-मे-जि-ज़्म (IM-uh-jiz-um)", "hindi": "स्पष्ट, सटीक बिंब और कम शब्दों वाली आधुनिक काव्य-धारा", "net": "A movement in English and American poetry (c. 1912-17), led by Ezra Pound, H.D., Aldington and Amy Lowell, demanding direct treatment of the thing, no superfluous word, and composition in the sequence of the musical phrase."}, {"term": "Pararhyme", "pron": "पै-रा-राइम (PAIR-uh-rime)", "hindi": "व्यंजन एक जैसे पर स्वर अलग वाली अधूरी तुक", "net": "Half-rhyme in which the consonants before and after differing vowels are identical ('escaped/scooped', 'hall/hell'); characteristic of Wilfred Owen ('Strange Meeting')."}, {"term": "Feminine Rhyme", "pron": "फ़े-मि-निन राइम (FEM-uh-nin rime)", "hindi": "दो अक्षरों वाली तुक, जिसमें अंतिम अक्षर हल्का हो", "net": "A rhyme of two syllables, a stressed followed by an unstressed one ('motion/ocean'); opposed to masculine rhyme on a single stressed final syllable ('hill/still')."}, {"term": "Synaesthesia", "pron": "सि-नेस-थी-ज़िया (sin-is-THEE-zhuh)", "hindi": "एक इंद्रिय के अनुभव को दूसरी इंद्रिय की भाषा में कहना", "net": "The description of one kind of sensation in terms of another, e.g. colour as sound; frequent in Keats ('Tasting of Flora and the country green') and the Symbolists."}, {"term": "Zeugma", "pron": "ज़्यूग-मा (ZOOG-muh)", "hindi": "एक ही शब्द को दो चीज़ों से अलग-अलग अर्थ में जोड़ना", "net": "A figure in which one word governs two others in different senses, often for comic effect: Pope, 'Or stain her honour, or her new brocade' (The Rape of the Lock)."}, {"term": "Catalexis", "pron": "कै-टा-लेक-सिस (kat-uh-LEK-sis)", "hindi": "पंक्ति के अंतिम पद में एक अक्षर कम होना", "net": "The omission of one or more syllables from the final foot of a line; such a line is catalectic (Blake's 'Tyger Tyger, burning bright' is catalectic trochaic tetrameter)."}, {"term": "Common Metre", "pron": "कॉ-मन मी-टर (KOM-un MEE-ter)", "hindi": "चार और तीन पदों की पंक्तियों का बारी-बारी चलना (8.6.8.6)", "net": "Also ballad or hymn metre: quatrains alternating iambic tetrameter and iambic trimeter (8.6.8.6 syllables), rhyming abcb or abab; used widely by Emily Dickinson."}, {"term": "Confessional Poetry", "pron": "कन-फ़े-श-नल पो-ए-ट्री (kun-FESH-uh-nul POH-uh-tree)", "hindi": "कवि के निजी दुखों, मानसिक संघर्ष और जीवन को खुलकर कहने वाली कविता", "net": "Term coined by M.L. Rosenthal (1959) reviewing Robert Lowell's Life Studies, for post-war American poetry revealing intimate, often painful personal experience (Lowell, Plath, Sexton, Berryman, Snodgrass)."}, {"term": "Negritude", "pron": "ने-ग्री-ट्यूड (NEG-ri-tyood)", "hindi": "अश्वेत अफ़्रीकी पहचान और संस्कृति पर गर्व का आंदोलन", "net": "A literary and ideological movement of the 1930s onward, founded by Aime Cesaire, Leopold Sedar Senghor and Leon Damas, affirming black African identity and values against colonial assimilation."}, {"term": "Epithalamion", "pron": "ए-पि-था-ले-मि-यन (ep-uh-thuh-LAY-mee-un)", "hindi": "विवाह के अवसर का मंगल-गीत", "net": "A lyric poem celebrating a marriage, sung outside the bridal chamber; Spenser's Epithalamion (1595) celebrates his own marriage to Elizabeth Boyle."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Arrange in chronological order of publication: (i) The Waste Land (ii) Lyrical Ballads (iii) In Memoriam A.H.H. (iv) The Whitsun Weddings", "options": ["(ii), (iii), (i), (iv)", "(iii), (ii), (i), (iv)", "(ii), (i), (iii), (iv)", "(iii), (ii), (iv), (i)"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Lyrical Ballads 1798, In Memoriam 1850, The Waste Land 1922, The Whitsun Weddings 1964."}, {"q": "Match List I with List II: (a) Absalom and Achitophel (b) The Rape of the Lock (c) Mac Flecknoe (d) Epistle to Dr Arbuthnot -- (i) Thomas Shadwell (ii) Earl of Shaftesbury (iii) Arabella Fermor (iv) Lord Hervey (Sporus)", "options": ["a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii", "a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Achitophel = Shaftesbury; Belinda = Arabella Fermor; Mac Flecknoe mocks Shadwell; Sporus = Lord Hervey."}, {"q": "Who said that the metaphysical poets yoked 'heterogeneous ideas' 'by violence together'?", "options": ["John Dryden", "Samuel Johnson", "T.S. Eliot", "Alexander Pope"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Johnson, Life of Cowley in Lives of the Poets."}, {"q": "The phrase 'dissociation of sensibility' occurs in T.S. Eliot's essay:", "options": ["Tradition and the Individual Talent", "Hamlet and His Problems", "The Metaphysical Poets", "The Function of Criticism"], "answer": 2, "exp": "'The Metaphysical Poets' (1921)."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Lycidas is a pastoral elegy. Reason (R): Milton mourns Edward King in the guise of a shepherd lamenting a fellow shepherd.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "The pastoral disguise of mourner and mourned is exactly what makes it a pastoral elegy."}, {"q": "Which stanza form does Spenser use in The Faerie Queene?", "options": ["ababbcc in pentameter", "ababbcbcc, eight pentameters and an alexandrine", "abababcc in pentameter", "aba bcb cdc"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The Spenserian stanza: nine lines ending in an alexandrine."}, {"q": "Match the knight with the virtue in The Faerie Queene: (a) Redcrosse (b) Guyon (c) Britomart (d) Calidore -- (i) Courtesy (ii) Chastity (iii) Temperance (iv) Holiness", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Book I Holiness, II Temperance, III Chastity, VI Courtesy."}, {"q": "'Poetry is the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings' is found in:", "options": ["Biographia Literaria", "A Defence of Poetry", "Preface to Lyrical Ballads", "An Apology for Poetry"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Wordsworth's Preface (1800/1802)."}, {"q": "'Negative Capability' was defined by Keats in a letter to:", "options": ["Benjamin Bailey", "John Hamilton Reynolds", "his brothers George and Tom", "Fanny Brawne"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Letter of December 1817 to George and Tom Keats."}, {"q": "Which of the following poems is NOT written in terza rima or terza rima units?", "options": ["Ode to the West Wind", "The Divine Comedy", "Don Juan", "The Triumph of Life"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Don Juan is in ottava rima."}, {"q": "'Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world' was written by:", "options": ["Sidney", "Shelley", "Wordsworth", "Arnold"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Closing sentence of Shelley's A Defence of Poetry."}, {"q": "The term 'pathetic fallacy' was coined by:", "options": ["Matthew Arnold", "Walter Pater", "John Ruskin", "S.T. Coleridge"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Ruskin, Modern Painters, vol. III (1856)."}, {"q": "Which Browning poem contains the line 'Ah, but a man's reach should exceed his grasp'?", "options": ["Fra Lippo Lippi", "Andrea del Sarto", "Rabbi Ben Ezra", "My Last Duchess"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Andrea del Sarto speaks it in Men and Women (1855)."}, {"q": "Tennyson's In Memoriam A.H.H. is an elegy for:", "options": ["Edward King", "Arthur Henry Hallam", "Arthur Hugh Clough", "John Keats"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Hallam died in 1833; the poem appeared in 1850."}, {"q": "Thyrsis by Matthew Arnold is an elegy on:", "options": ["Arthur Hugh Clough", "Thomas Arnold", "Edward King", "Arthur Hallam"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Thyrsis (1865) mourns his friend Clough."}, {"q": "Sprung rhythm is associated with:", "options": ["Robert Bridges", "G.M. Hopkins", "Swinburne", "D.G. Rossetti"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Hopkins's metrical theory, poems edited by Bridges in 1918."}, {"q": "Arrange the sections of The Waste Land in order: (i) The Fire Sermon (ii) A Game of Chess (iii) What the Thunder Said (iv) Death by Water", "options": ["(ii), (i), (iv), (iii)", "(i), (ii), (iv), (iii)", "(ii), (iv), (i), (iii)", "(i), (iv), (ii), (iii)"], "answer": 0, "exp": "After The Burial of the Dead: A Game of Chess, The Fire Sermon, Death by Water, What the Thunder Said."}, {"q": "'Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold' is from:", "options": ["Easter, 1916", "The Second Coming", "Sailing to Byzantium", "Among School Children"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Yeats, 'The Second Coming' (1920)."}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT one of Eliot's Four Quartets?", "options": ["Burnt Norton", "East Coker", "Little Gidding", "Ash-Wednesday"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Ash-Wednesday (1930) is a separate poem; the fourth quartet is The Dry Salvages."}, {"q": "'My subject is War, and the pity of War. The poetry is in the pity.' Who wrote this?", "options": ["Siegfried Sassoon", "Rupert Brooke", "Wilfred Owen", "Isaac Rosenberg"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Owen's draft Preface to his poems."}, {"q": "The anthology New Lines (1956), associated with the Movement, was edited by:", "options": ["D.J. Enright", "Robert Conquest", "Michael Roberts", "Edward Marsh"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Conquest edited New Lines; Enright edited Poets of the 1950s."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Night of the Scorpion (b) Small-Scale Reflections on a Great House (c) An Introduction (d) Jejuri -- (i) Kamala Das (ii) Arun Kolatkar (iii) Nissim Ezekiel (iv) A.K. Ramanujan", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii", "a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Ezekiel, Ramanujan, Kamala Das, Kolatkar respectively."}, {"q": "Which Indian English poet was the first to receive the Sahitya Akademi Award for poetry in English, for Relationship?", "options": ["Nissim Ezekiel", "Jayanta Mahapatra", "A.K. Ramanujan", "Keki N. Daruwalla"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Mahapatra received it in 1981; Ezekiel followed in 1983."}, {"q": "The term 'confessional poetry' was first used by M.L. Rosenthal in reviewing:", "options": ["Ariel by Sylvia Plath", "Howl by Allen Ginsberg", "Life Studies by Robert Lowell", "The Dream Songs by John Berryman"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Rosenthal's 1959 review of Life Studies."}, {"q": "'Good fences make good neighbors' occurs in:", "options": ["Birches", "Mending Wall", "Home Burial", "After Apple-Picking"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Frost's 'Mending Wall', spoken by the neighbour."}, {"q": "Who wrote Omeros, a Caribbean reworking of Homeric epic?", "options": ["Kamau Brathwaite", "V.S. Naipaul", "Derek Walcott", "Wole Soyinka"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Walcott's Omeros (1990); he won the Nobel in 1992."}, {"q": "A villanelle has:", "options": ["14 lines with a volta", "19 lines with two refrains and two rhymes", "39 lines with six repeated end-words", "8 lines with two rhymes"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Five tercets and a quatrain; 39 lines describes the sestina, 8 lines the triolet."}, {"q": "The metrical foot in 'The Assyrian came down like the wolf on the fold' is predominantly:", "options": ["Iambic", "Trochaic", "Anapaestic", "Dactylic"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Byron's line is anapaestic tetrameter (u u /)."}, {"q": "Rhyme royal, first used in English by Chaucer, rhymes:", "options": ["ababbcc", "abababcc", "ababbcbcc", "abba"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Seven-line stanza of Troilus and Criseyde; abababcc is ottava rima."}, {"q": "Which poem was written by Marvell?", "options": ["The Retreat", "The Collar", "An Horatian Ode upon Cromwell's Return from Ireland", "The Flaming Heart"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The Retreat is Vaughan's, The Collar Herbert's, The Flaming Heart Crashaw's."}]}, {"id": 3, "title": "Fiction, Short Story", "overview": "Is unit mein novel ka safar hai - 18th century (Defoe, Richardson, Fielding) se Victorian, Modernist, post-war, American, Indian English aur African/Caribbean fiction tak, saath mein short story writers aur narrative theory. NET mein mostly author-work-year, characters, opening lines, chronology aur match-the-following poochhe jaate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Precursors and the Rise of the Novel", "points": ["Aphra Behn, Oroonoko (1688): early prose fiction about an enslaved African prince.", "John Bunyan, The Pilgrim's Progress (1678): allegory, a precursor of the novel (Christian, Vanity Fair, Slough of Despond).", "Daniel Defoe: Robinson Crusoe (1719, man Friday), A Journal of the Plague Year (1722), Moll Flanders (1722), Roxana (1724).", "Jonathan Swift, Gulliver's Travels (1726): Lilliput, Brobdingnag, Laputa, Houyhnhnms and Yahoos (satire, novel nahi).", "Samuel Richardson: Pamela, or Virtue Rewarded (1740), Clarissa (1747-48, one of the longest novels in English), Sir Charles Grandison (1753); all epistolary (chitthiyon ke roop mein kahani).", "Henry Fielding: Shamela (1741, parody of Pamela), Joseph Andrews (1742, Parson Adams), Tom Jones (1749, Squire Allworthy, Sophia Western), Amelia (1751).", "Fielding called Joseph Andrews a 'comic epic poem in prose' (Preface, 1742).", "Tobias Smollett: Roderick Random (1748), Peregrine Pickle (1751), Humphry Clinker (1771, epistolary); picaresque tradition.", "Laurence Sterne: Tristram Shandy (1759-67; Uncle Toby, Walter Shandy; black page, marbled page, digressions), A Sentimental Journey (1768).", "Ian Watt, The Rise of the Novel (1957): Defoe, Richardson, Fielding; 'formal realism' and the rise of the middle-class reading public."]}, {"title": "Later 18th Century: Gothic, Sentimental and Romantic-era Fiction", "points": ["Samuel Johnson, Rasselas (1759); Oliver Goldsmith, The Vicar of Wakefield (1766, Dr Primrose).", "Henry Mackenzie, The Man of Feeling (1771): sentimental novel (Harley).", "Horace Walpole, The Castle of Otranto (1764): first Gothic novel; subtitle 'A Gothic Story' in 2nd ed. (1765).", "Ann Radcliffe, The Mysteries of Udolpho (1794) - 'explained supernatural'; M.G. Lewis, The Monk (1796); William Beckford, Vathek (1786).", "William Godwin, Caleb Williams (1794); Fanny Burney, Evelina (1778), Cecilia (1782).", "Maria Edgeworth, Castle Rackrent (1800): early regional novel (Irish), narrator Thady Quirk.", "Jane Austen: Sense and Sensibility (1811), Pride and Prejudice (1813), Mansfield Park (1814), Emma (1815), Northanger Abbey and Persuasion (1817, posthumous).", "Austen characters: Elizabeth Bennet and Mr Darcy (P&P), Fanny Price (MP), Anne Elliot and Captain Wentworth (Persuasion), Catherine Morland (NA, parody of Gothic).", "Austen on her art: 'the little bit (two Inches wide) of Ivory' (letter, 1816) - 3 or 4 families in a country village.", "Walter Scott: Waverley (1814, first historical novel in English tradition), Rob Roy (1817), Ivanhoe (1819); 'Author of Waverley'.", "Mary Shelley, Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus (1818): frame narrative of Walton's letters; Victor and the Creature."]}, {"title": "Victorian Novel I: Dickens, Thackeray, Brontes, Gaskell", "points": ["Dickens: Pickwick Papers (1836-37), Oliver Twist (1837-39), A Christmas Carol (1843), David Copperfield (1849-50), Bleak House (1852-53), Hard Times (1854), Little Dorrit (1855-57), A Tale of Two Cities (1859), Great Expectations (1860-61), Our Mutual Friend (1864-65), Edwin Drood (unfinished, 1870).", "Dickens characters: Fagin, Artful Dodger (OT); Mr Micawber, Uriah Heep, Steerforth (DC); Pip, Miss Havisham, Magwitch, Estella (GE); Gradgrind, Coketown (HT); Jarndyce v Jarndyce, Lady Dedlock (BH).", "Dickens's favourite child: David Copperfield (semi-autobiographical); Hard Times is dedicated to Thomas Carlyle.", "W.M. Thackeray, Vanity Fair (1847-48): 'A Novel without a Hero'; Becky Sharp, Amelia Sedley; title from Bunyan.", "Charlotte Bronte (Currer Bell): Jane Eyre (1847; Rochester, Bertha Mason, Thornfield, Lowood), Shirley (1849), Villette (1853).", "Emily Bronte (Ellis Bell): Wuthering Heights (1847); Heathcliff, Catherine Earnshaw, narrators Lockwood and Nelly Dean.", "Anne Bronte (Acton Bell): Agnes Grey (1847), The Tenant of Wildfell Hall (1848).", "Elizabeth Gaskell: Mary Barton (1848), Cranford (1851-53), North and South (1854-55; Margaret Hale, John Thornton); Life of Charlotte Bronte (1857).", "Condition-of-England / industrial novels: Disraeli Sybil (1845), Gaskell Mary Barton, Dickens Hard Times, Kingsley Alton Locke (1850); phrase from Carlyle's Chartism (1839)."]}, {"title": "Victorian Novel II: Eliot, Trollope, Hardy and Late Victorians", "points": ["George Eliot (Mary Ann Evans): Adam Bede (1859), The Mill on the Floss (1860; Maggie and Tom Tulliver), Silas Marner (1861), Romola (1863), Middlemarch (1871-72), Daniel Deronda (1876).", "Middlemarch: 'A Study of Provincial Life'; Dorothea Brooke, Casaubon, Lydgate, Rosamond Vincy; Woolf: 'one of the few English novels written for grown-up people'.", "Anthony Trollope: Barsetshire novels - The Warden (1855), Barchester Towers (1857); Palliser novels.", "Thomas Hardy (Wessex novels): Far from the Madding Crowd (1874), The Return of the Native (1878), The Mayor of Casterbridge (1886), The Woodlanders (1887), Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891, 'A Pure Woman'), Jude the Obscure (1895).", "Hardy characters: Bathsheba Everdene, Gabriel Oak (FMC); Eustacia Vye, Egdon Heath (RN); Michael Henchard sells wife (MC); Tess, Angel Clare, Alec (Tess); Jude Fawley, Sue Bridehead.", "After hostile reviews of Jude (1895), Hardy gave up novels for poetry.", "Sensation novel: Wilkie Collins, The Woman in White (1859-60), The Moonstone (1868, T.S. Eliot: 'first, longest and best' English detective novel); M.E. Braddon, Lady Audley's Secret (1862).", "George Meredith, The Egoist (1879); Lewis Carroll, Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865).", "R.L. Stevenson: Treasure Island (1883), Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde (1886); Oscar Wilde, The Picture of Dorian Gray (1890-91).", "George Gissing, New Grub Street (1891); Bram Stoker, Dracula (1897, epistolary); H.G. Wells, The Time Machine (1895), The War of the Worlds (1898)."]}, {"title": "Transition: James, Conrad and the Edwardians", "points": ["Henry James: Daisy Miller (1878), The Portrait of a Lady (1881, Isabel Archer), The Turn of the Screw (1898), The Wings of the Dove (1902), The Ambassadors (1903, Strether), The Golden Bowl (1904).", "James's essay 'The Art of Fiction' (1884) - reply to Walter Besant; novel as 'a personal impression of life'.", "James called big Victorian novels 'loose baggy monsters' (Preface to The Tragic Muse); 'international theme' (American vs European).", "Joseph Conrad: Almayer's Folly (1895), The Nigger of the 'Narcissus' (1897, famous Preface: 'to make you see'), Heart of Darkness (1899, book 1902), Lord Jim (1900), Nostromo (1904), The Secret Agent (1907), Under Western Eyes (1911).", "Heart of Darkness: narrator Marlow, Kurtz's last words 'The horror! The horror!'; 'Mistah Kurtz - he dead' (epigraph to Eliot's The Hollow Men).", "Edwardian realists attacked by Woolf: H.G. Wells, Arnold Bennett (The Old Wives' Tale, 1908), John Galsworthy (The Forsyte Saga) - called 'materialists'.", "E.M. Forster: Where Angels Fear to Tread (1905), A Room with a View (1908), Howards End (1910; epigraph 'Only connect...'), A Passage to India (1924; Aziz, Fielding, Adela Quested, Mrs Moore, Marabar Caves), Maurice (posthumous 1971).", "Forster's Aspects of the Novel (1927, Clark Lectures): story vs plot, flat vs round characters, 'pattern and rhythm'.", "Ford Madox Ford, The Good Soldier (1915): 'This is the saddest story I have ever heard'; unreliable narrator John Dowell.", "W. Somerset Maugham, Of Human Bondage (1915); Kipling, Kim (1901)."]}, {"title": "Modernist Novel: Joyce, Woolf, Lawrence", "points": ["James Joyce: Dubliners (1914), A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man (1916, Stephen Dedalus), Ulysses (1922), Finnegans Wake (1939).", "Ulysses: set on 16 June 1904 (Bloomsday) in Dublin; Leopold Bloom, Molly Bloom, Stephen Dedalus; parallels Homer's Odyssey; ends with Molly's 'yes'.", "Stephen's credo: 'silence, exile, and cunning' (Portrait).", "Virginia Woolf: The Voyage Out (1915), Jacob's Room (1922), Mrs Dalloway (1925; Clarissa, Septimus Warren Smith), To the Lighthouse (1927; Mrs Ramsay, Lily Briscoe; 'Time Passes'), Orlando (1928), The Waves (1931), Between the Acts (1941).", "Woolf essays: 'Modern Fiction' (1919/1925) - 'Life is a luminous halo, a semi-transparent envelope'; 'Mr Bennett and Mrs Brown' (1924) - 'on or about December 1910 human character changed'; A Room of One's Own (1929).", "D.H. Lawrence: The White Peacock (1911), Sons and Lovers (1913; Paul Morel, Gertrude, Miriam), The Rainbow (1915, banned), Women in Love (1920; Ursula and Gudrun Brangwen), Lady Chatterley's Lover (1928; UK trial 1960).", "Lawrence: 'The novel is the one bright book of life' (essay 'Why the Novel Matters').", "Stream of consciousness: phrase from William James, Principles of Psychology (1890); applied to fiction by May Sinclair (1918) reviewing Dorothy Richardson's Pilgrimage.", "Others: Aldous Huxley - Crome Yellow (1921), Point Counter Point (1928), Brave New World (1932); Elizabeth Bowen, The Death of the Heart (1938)."]}, {"title": "1930s to Post-war British Fiction", "points": ["George Orwell: Burmese Days (1934), Animal Farm (1945; 'All animals are equal but some animals are more equal than others'), Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949; Winston Smith, Big Brother, Newspeak).", "Evelyn Waugh: Decline and Fall (1928), A Handful of Dust (1934), Brideshead Revisited (1945).", "Graham Greene: Brighton Rock (1938, Pinkie), The Power and the Glory (1940, whisky priest), The Heart of the Matter (1948, Scobie), The Quiet American (1955).", "William Golding: Lord of the Flies (1954; Ralph, Piggy, Jack, Simon), The Inheritors (1955), Rites of Passage (1980 Booker); Nobel 1983.", "Angry Young Men fiction: Kingsley Amis Lucky Jim (1954, Jim Dixon; campus novel), John Wain Hurry on Down (1953), John Braine Room at the Top (1957), Alan Sillitoe Saturday Night and Sunday Morning (1958).", "Iris Murdoch: Under the Net (1954), The Bell (1958), The Sea, the Sea (1978 Booker).", "Doris Lessing: The Grass Is Singing (1950), The Golden Notebook (1962; Anna Wulf); Nobel 2007.", "Muriel Spark, The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie (1961); Anthony Burgess, A Clockwork Orange (1962, Nadsat slang); John Fowles, The French Lieutenant's Woman (1969, double ending, metafiction).", "Jean Rhys, Wide Sargasso Sea (1966): prequel to Jane Eyre from Bertha's (Antoinette Cosway's) view.", "Angela Carter, The Bloody Chamber (1979, rewritten fairy tales); L.P. Hartley, The Go-Between (1953); Daphne du Maurier, Rebecca (1938)."]}, {"title": "Contemporary British Fiction (1980s onward)", "points": ["Kazuo Ishiguro: A Pale View of Hills (1982), The Remains of the Day (1989 Booker; butler Stevens), Never Let Me Go (2005; Kathy H.), Klara and the Sun (2021); Nobel 2017.", "Ian McEwan: Amsterdam (1998 Booker), Atonement (2001; Briony Tallis).", "Julian Barnes: Flaubert's Parrot (1984), A History of the World in 10 1/2 Chapters (1989), The Sense of an Ending (2011 Booker).", "Hilary Mantel: Wolf Hall (2009 Booker) and Bring Up the Bodies (2012 Booker) - Thomas Cromwell trilogy, ended by The Mirror and the Light (2020).", "A.S. Byatt, Possession (1990 Booker); Graham Swift, Waterland (1983), Last Orders (1996 Booker).", "Martin Amis, Money (1984), London Fields (1989), Time's Arrow (1991, backward narration).", "Salman Rushdie (British-Indian): Midnight's Children (1981 Booker), The Satanic Verses (1988, fatwa 1989).", "Zadie Smith, White Teeth (2000); Bernardine Evaristo, Girl, Woman, Other (2019 Booker, joint with Atwood's The Testaments).", "Postmodern features: metafiction, historiographic metafiction (Linda Hutcheon), intertextuality, pastiche, unreliable narration."]}, {"title": "American Novel: 19th Century to 1920", "points": ["James Fenimore Cooper: Leatherstocking Tales - The Last of the Mohicans (1826), Natty Bumppo/Hawkeye.", "Nathaniel Hawthorne: The Scarlet Letter (1850; Hester Prynne, Arthur Dimmesdale, Roger Chillingworth, Pearl; letter 'A'; preface 'The Custom-House'), The House of the Seven Gables (1851); called his works 'romances'.", "Herman Melville: Typee (1846), Moby-Dick (1851; 'Call me Ishmael', Captain Ahab, Pequod, Queequeg, Starbuck), 'Bartleby, the Scrivener' (1853), Billy Budd (posthumous 1924); Moby-Dick dedicated to Hawthorne.", "Harriet Beecher Stowe, Uncle Tom's Cabin (1852): anti-slavery novel.", "Mark Twain: The Adventures of Tom Sawyer (1876), Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (1884 UK/1885 US; Huck, Jim, vernacular first-person).", "Hemingway (Green Hills of Africa, 1935): 'All modern American literature comes from one book by Mark Twain called Huckleberry Finn.'", "Realism: W.D. Howells, The Rise of Silas Lapham (1885); Henry James.", "Naturalism: Stephen Crane, Maggie (1893), The Red Badge of Courage (1895; Henry Fleming); Frank Norris, McTeague (1899); Theodore Dreiser, Sister Carrie (1900), An American Tragedy (1925); Jack London.", "Kate Chopin, The Awakening (1899; Edna Pontellier); Edith Wharton, The House of Mirth (1905, Lily Bart), Ethan Frome (1911), The Age of Innocence (1920; first woman to win Pulitzer for fiction, 1921).", "Upton Sinclair, The Jungle (1906, Chicago meatpacking); Sinclair Lewis, Main Street (1920), Babbitt (1922), first American Nobel in Literature (1930)."]}, {"title": "American Novel: 20th Century to Contemporary", "points": ["F. Scott Fitzgerald: This Side of Paradise (1920), The Great Gatsby (1925; Nick Carraway narrator, Jay Gatsby, Daisy Buchanan, green light; 'So we beat on, boats against the current'), Tender Is the Night (1934); 'Jazz Age'.", "Ernest Hemingway: The Sun Also Rises (1926; epigraph 'You are all a lost generation' - Gertrude Stein; Jake Barnes), A Farewell to Arms (1929), For Whom the Bell Tolls (1940, title from Donne), The Old Man and the Sea (1952, Santiago); Nobel 1954; iceberg theory.", "William Faulkner: The Sound and the Fury (1929; Benjy, Quentin, Jason, Dilsey; title from Macbeth), As I Lay Dying (1930; Addie Bundren), Light in August (1932), Absalom, Absalom! (1936); Yoknapatawpha County; Nobel 1949.", "John Steinbeck: Of Mice and Men (1937; George and Lennie), The Grapes of Wrath (1939; Joad family); Nobel 1962.", "African-American fiction: Zora Neale Hurston, Their Eyes Were Watching God (1937, Janie); Richard Wright, Native Son (1940, Bigger Thomas); Ralph Ellison, Invisible Man (1952); James Baldwin, Go Tell It on the Mountain (1953).", "Toni Morrison: The Bluest Eye (1970, Pecola), Sula (1973), Song of Solomon (1977), Beloved (1987; Sethe; Pulitzer 1988); Nobel 1993. Alice Walker, The Color Purple (1982, Celie; Pulitzer 1983).", "J.D. Salinger, The Catcher in the Rye (1951; Holden Caulfield, 'phony'); Vladimir Nabokov, Lolita (1955; Humbert Humbert); Jack Kerouac, On the Road (1957, Beat).", "Joseph Heller, Catch-22 (1961, Yossarian); Harper Lee, To Kill a Mockingbird (1960; Atticus, Scout); Sylvia Plath, The Bell Jar (1963).", "Postmodern: Kurt Vonnegut, Slaughterhouse-Five (1969; Billy Pilgrim; 'So it goes'); Thomas Pynchon, The Crying of Lot 49 (1966), Gravity's Rainbow (1973); Don DeLillo, White Noise (1985).", "Saul Bellow, Herzog (1964), Nobel 1976; Philip Roth, American Pastoral (1997); Cormac McCarthy, The Road (2006)."]}, {"title": "Indian English Novel I: Beginnings to 1970s", "points": ["Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, Rajmohan's Wife (1864): first Indian novel in English.", "The 'Big Three': Mulk Raj Anand, R.K. Narayan, Raja Rao.", "Mulk Raj Anand: Untouchable (1935; Bakha, one day in a sweeper's life; preface by E.M. Forster), Coolie (1936, Munoo), Two Leaves and a Bud (1937), The Private Life of an Indian Prince (1953).", "R.K. Narayan: Swami and Friends (1935; Graham Greene helped publish), The Bachelor of Arts (1937), The English Teacher (1945), The Financial Expert (1952), The Guide (1958; Raju, Rosie; Sahitya Akademi 1960), The Man-Eater of Malgudi (1961); fictional town Malgudi.", "Raja Rao: Kanthapura (1938; narrator Achakka, hero Moorthy, Gandhian movement; Sthala-purana form), The Serpent and the Rope (1960, Sahitya Akademi 1964), The Cat and Shakespeare (1965).", "Raja Rao's Foreword to Kanthapura: 'One has to convey in a language that is not one's own the spirit that is one's own.'", "Ahmed Ali, Twilight in Delhi (1940); G.V. Desani, All About H. Hatterr (1948); Bhabani Bhattacharya, So Many Hungers! (1947), He Who Rides a Tiger (1952).", "Partition novels: Khushwant Singh, Train to Pakistan (1956; village Mano Majra, Juggut Singh, Iqbal, Hukum Chand); Manohar Malgonkar, A Bend in the Ganges (1964); Chaman Nahal, Azadi (1975); Bapsi Sidhwa, Ice-Candy-Man (1988).", "Kamala Markandaya, Nectar in a Sieve (1954; Rukmani, title from Coleridge); Nayantara Sahgal, Rich Like Us (1985).", "Anita Desai: Cry, the Peacock (1963, Maya), Fire on the Mountain (1977), Clear Light of Day (1980), In Custody (1984); Arun Joshi, The Strange Case of Billy Biswas (1971), The Last Labyrinth (1981, Sahitya Akademi)."]}, {"title": "Indian English Novel II: Rushdie and After", "points": ["Salman Rushdie, Midnight's Children (1981; Saleem Sinai born at midnight 15 Aug 1947; Booker 1981, 'Booker of Bookers' 1993, 'Best of the Booker' 2008); Shame (1983); The Satanic Verses (1988); The Moor's Last Sigh (1995).", "Amitav Ghosh: The Circle of Reason (1986), The Shadow Lines (1988; Tridib, Ila, Thamma; Sahitya Akademi 1989), The Calcutta Chromosome (1995), The Glass Palace (2000), The Hungry Tide (2004), Ibis trilogy - Sea of Poppies (2008), River of Smoke (2011), Flood of Fire (2015); Jnanpith 2018.", "Vikram Seth: The Golden Gate (1986, novel in verse - Onegin stanzas), A Suitable Boy (1993; Lata Mehra), An Equal Music (1999).", "Arundhati Roy, The God of Small Things (1997 Booker; twins Estha and Rahel, Ammu, Velutha; Ayemenem, Kerala; 'Love Laws'); The Ministry of Utmost Happiness (2017).", "Booker-winning Indians/Indian-origin: V.S. Naipaul (1971), Rushdie (1981), Roy (1997), Kiran Desai The Inheritance of Loss (2006), Aravind Adiga The White Tiger (2008).", "The White Tiger: Balram Halwai narrates in letters to Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao; 'Rooster Coop' metaphor.", "Upamanyu Chatterjee, English, August (1988; Agastya Sen); Shashi Tharoor, The Great Indian Novel (1989, Mahabharata retold); Allan Sealy, The Trotter-Nama (1988).", "Shashi Deshpande, That Long Silence (1988; Jaya; Sahitya Akademi 1990); Rohinton Mistry, Such a Long Journey (1991), A Fine Balance (1995).", "Jhumpa Lahiri: Interpreter of Maladies (1999, stories; Pulitzer 2000), The Namesake (2003, Gogol Ganguli), The Lowland (2013).", "Geetanjali Shree, Tomb of Sand (Ret Samadhi), translated by Daisy Rockwell: International Booker 2022 (first Hindi book)."]}, {"title": "African Fiction", "points": ["Chinua Achebe: Things Fall Apart (1958; Okonkwo, Umuofia, Ibo/Igbo; title from Yeats's 'The Second Coming'), No Longer at Ease (1960, Obi Okonkwo), Arrow of God (1964, Ezeulu), A Man of the People (1966), Anthills of the Savannah (1987).", "Achebe's lecture 'An Image of Africa: Racism in Conrad's Heart of Darkness' (1975, publ. 1977): called Conrad 'a thoroughgoing racist'.", "Ngugi wa Thiong'o: Weep Not, Child (1964), The River Between (1965), A Grain of Wheat (1967), Petals of Blood (1977); Devil on the Cross (in Gikuyu); Decolonising the Mind (1986) - farewell to English.", "Wole Soyinka: The Interpreters (1965), Season of Anomy (1973); first African Nobel laureate (1986).", "Nadine Gordimer: The Conservationist (1974, joint Booker), Burger's Daughter (1979), July's People (1981); Nobel 1991.", "J.M. Coetzee: Waiting for the Barbarians (1980), Life and Times of Michael K (1983 Booker), Foe (1986, rewrites Robinson Crusoe), Disgrace (1999 Booker; David Lurie); first writer to win the Booker twice (1983, 1999); Nobel 2003.", "Alan Paton, Cry, the Beloved Country (1948); Ayi Kwei Armah, The Beautyful Ones Are Not Yet Born (1968); Buchi Emecheta, The Joys of Motherhood (1979).", "Ben Okri, The Famished Road (1991 Booker; spirit-child Azaro, abiku); Tsitsi Dangarembga, Nervous Conditions (1988).", "Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie: Purple Hibiscus (2003), Half of a Yellow Sun (2006, Biafran war), Americanah (2013); TED talk 'The Danger of a Single Story' (2009).", "Abdulrazak Gurnah (Zanzibar-born): Paradise (1994), By the Sea (2001), Afterlives (2020); Nobel 2021."]}, {"title": "Caribbean, Canadian, Australian and Other Postcolonial Fiction", "points": ["V.S. Naipaul (Trinidad): Miguel Street (1959), A House for Mr Biswas (1961, Mohun Biswas), The Mimic Men (1967), In a Free State (1971 Booker), A Bend in the River (1979); Nobel 2001.", "George Lamming, In the Castle of My Skin (1953), The Pleasures of Exile (1960, Caliban reading); Sam Selvon, The Lonely Londoners (1956, creolised narration, Moses).", "Wilson Harris, Palace of the Peacock (1960); Jamaica Kincaid, Annie John (1985), A Small Place (1988); Caryl Phillips, Crossing the River (1993).", "Jean Rhys (Dominica-born), Wide Sargasso Sea (1966): postcolonial 'writing back' to Jane Eyre.", "Margaret Atwood (Canada): Surfacing (1972), The Handmaid's Tale (1985; Offred, Gilead), The Blind Assassin (2000 Booker), The Testaments (2019 joint Booker); Survival (1972, criticism).", "Michael Ondaatje, The English Patient (1992 Booker, joint with Barry Unsworth's Sacred Hunger); Yann Martel, Life of Pi (2002 Booker); Alice Munro (short story), Nobel 2013.", "Patrick White (Australia): Voss (1957), The Tree of Man (1955); Nobel 1973. Peter Carey: Oscar and Lucinda (1988 Booker), True History of the Kelly Gang (2001 Booker).", "Keri Hulme (New Zealand), The Bone People (1985 Booker).", "Gabriel Garcia Marquez, One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967; Macondo, Buendia family) - landmark of magic realism; Nobel 1982.", "Theory link: The Empire Writes Back (1989) by Ashcroft, Griffiths and Tiffin - title from Rushdie's phrase."]}, {"title": "Short Story: Theory and Western Masters", "points": ["Edgar Allan Poe defined the short story's 'single effect' / 'unity of effect' in his review of Hawthorne's Twice-Told Tales (1842).", "Poe: 'The Fall of the House of Usher' (1839), 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue' (1841, first modern detective story, C. Auguste Dupin), 'The Tell-Tale Heart' (1843), 'The Purloined Letter' (1844), 'The Cask of Amontillado' (1846).", "Washington Irving, The Sketch Book (1819-20): 'Rip Van Winkle', 'The Legend of Sleepy Hollow' (Ichabod Crane). Hawthorne: 'Young Goodman Brown' (1835), 'The Minister's Black Veil'.", "Nikolai Gogol, 'The Overcoat' (1842, Akaky Akakievich) - 'We all came out from under Gogol's Overcoat' (attributed to Dostoevsky).", "Guy de Maupassant, 'The Necklace' (1884, Mathilde Loisel) - twist ending; Anton Chekhov, 'The Lady with the Dog' (1899), 'Ward No. 6' - plotless, open-ended mood story.", "O. Henry (William Sydney Porter): 'The Gift of the Magi' (1905; Della and Jim), 'The Last Leaf' - surprise ending.", "Charlotte Perkins Gilman, 'The Yellow Wallpaper' (1892); Kate Chopin, 'The Story of an Hour' (1894, Louise Mallard).", "James Joyce, Dubliners (1914): 'Araby', 'Eveline', 'The Dead' (Gabriel Conroy, Gretta, Michael Furey) - theme of paralysis, epiphany. Franz Kafka, 'The Metamorphosis' (1915, Gregor Samsa).", "Katherine Mansfield (NZ): 'Bliss' (1920), 'The Garden Party' (1922, Laura Sheridan), 'Miss Brill'. D.H. Lawrence, 'The Rocking-Horse Winner' (1926). Saki (H.H. Munro), 'The Open Window'.", "Hemingway, 'Hills Like White Elephants' (1927), 'The Killers'; Faulkner, 'A Rose for Emily' (1930, Emily Grierson); Shirley Jackson, 'The Lottery' (1948); Flannery O'Connor, 'A Good Man Is Hard to Find' (1953).", "Raymond Carver - minimalism ('dirty realism'): What We Talk About When We Talk About Love (1981), 'Cathedral' (1983). Frank O'Connor, The Lonely Voice (1963): short story is about 'submerged population groups'."]}, {"title": "Indian Short Story (English and in Translation)", "points": ["Rabindranath Tagore: 'Kabuliwala', 'The Postmaster', 'Subha' - Bengali, widely read in translation.", "Premchand: 'Kafan' (The Shroud, 1936, Ghisu and Madhav), 'Idgah' (Hamid); Saadat Hasan Manto: 'Toba Tek Singh' (1955, Bishan Singh), 'Thanda Gosht'.", "Ismat Chughtai, 'Lihaaf' (The Quilt, 1942) - obscenity trial in Lahore.", "Mulk Raj Anand: 'The Barber's Trade Union', 'The Lost Child'. Raja Rao: 'The Cow of the Barricades' (1947), 'Javni'.", "R.K. Narayan: Malgudi Days (1943; 'An Astrologer's Day', 'The Doctor's Word'), Lawley Road (1956), Under the Banyan Tree (1985).", "Khushwant Singh: 'The Portrait of a Lady' (his grandmother), 'Karma' (Sir Mohan Lal).", "Ruskin Bond: 'The Night Train at Deoli', 'The Eyes Have It', 'The Blue Umbrella'; novel The Room on the Roof (1956, John Llewellyn Rhys Prize); Our Trees Still Grow in Dehra (Sahitya Akademi 1992).", "Mahasweta Devi: 'Draupadi' (Dopdi Mejhen) and 'Breast-Giver' (Stanadayini) translated by Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak (In Other Worlds, 1987).", "Anita Desai, Games at Twilight (1978); Shashi Deshpande, 'The Intrusion'; Jhumpa Lahiri, Interpreter of Maladies (1999; 'A Temporary Matter', 'Mrs Sen's').", "Manto, Premchand and Chughtai are often set in NET as translated Indian literature - know characters and themes (partition, poverty, female desire)."]}, {"title": "Narrative Theory and Criticism of the Novel", "points": ["E.M. Forster, Aspects of the Novel (1927): 'The king died and then the queen died' is story; 'The king died, and then the queen died of grief' is plot.", "Percy Lubbock, The Craft of Fiction (1921): point of view, 'showing' vs 'telling' (based on Henry James).", "F.R. Leavis, The Great Tradition (1948): 'The great English novelists are Jane Austen, George Eliot, Henry James and Joseph Conrad' (D.H. Lawrence as heir); Dickens later added in Dickens the Novelist (1970).", "Wayne C. Booth, The Rhetoric of Fiction (1961): coined 'implied author' and 'unreliable narrator'.", "Gerard Genette, Narrative Discourse (Figures III, 1972): order (analepsis, prolepsis), duration, frequency, mood (focalization), voice (homodiegetic, heterodiegetic).", "Mikhail Bakhtin: polyphony (Problems of Dostoevsky's Poetics, 1929), dialogism, heteroglossia and chronotope (The Dialogic Imagination, essays 1930s-40s, Eng. 1981); carnivalesque (Rabelais and His World).", "Georg Lukacs: The Theory of the Novel (1916, novel as 'epic of a world abandoned by God'), The Historical Novel (1937, on Scott).", "Russian Formalists: fabula vs syuzhet (Shklovsky, Tomashevsky); Vladimir Propp, Morphology of the Folktale (1928) - 31 functions, 7 spheres of action.", "Ian Watt, The Rise of the Novel (1957): 'formal realism'; Frank Kermode, The Sense of an Ending (1967); Edward Said, Culture and Imperialism (1993) - reading of Mansfield Park.", "Linda Hutcheon, A Poetics of Postmodernism (1988): 'historiographic metafiction'; Patricia Waugh, Metafiction (1984)."]}, {"title": "Famous Opening Lines of Novels", "points": ["'It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.' - Pride and Prejudice (Austen).", "'Emma Woodhouse, handsome, clever, and rich...' - Emma (Austen). 'There was no possibility of taking a walk that day.' - Jane Eyre.", "'It was the best of times, it was the worst of times...' - A Tale of Two Cities. 'Whether I shall turn out to be the hero of my own life...' - David Copperfield.", "'Call me Ishmael.' - Moby-Dick. 'In my younger and more vulnerable years my father gave me some advice...' - The Great Gatsby.", "'Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.' - Anna Karenina (Tolstoy).", "'Stately, plump Buck Mulligan...' - Ulysses. 'Once upon a time and a very good time it was there was a moocow...' - A Portrait of the Artist.", "'Mrs Dalloway said she would buy the flowers herself.' - Mrs Dalloway. 'This is the saddest story I have ever heard.' - The Good Soldier.", "'It was a bright cold day in April, and the clocks were striking thirteen.' - Nineteen Eighty-Four. 'Last night I dreamt I went to Manderley again.' - Rebecca.", "'The past is a foreign country: they do things differently there.' - The Go-Between (L.P. Hartley). 'I am an invisible man.' - Invisible Man (Ellison).", "'If you really want to hear about it...' - The Catcher in the Rye. 'Lolita, light of my life, fire of my loins.' - Lolita.", "'Many years later, as he faced the firing squad, Colonel Aureliano Buendia...' - One Hundred Years of Solitude. 'As Gregor Samsa awoke one morning from uneasy dreams...' - The Metamorphosis.", "'I was born in the city of Bombay... once upon a time.' - Midnight's Children. 'May in Ayemenem is a hot, brooding month.' - The God of Small Things. 'Okonkwo was well known throughout the nine villages and even beyond.' - Things Fall Apart."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Novel", "pron": "नॉ-वल (NOV-ul)", "hindi": "लम्बी गद्य कहानी जिसमें पात्र और घटनाएँ यथार्थ जैसी होती हैं", "net": "An extended fictional prose narrative; the term derives from Italian novella ('a little new thing'); its English rise is traced by Ian Watt to Defoe, Richardson and Fielding."}, {"term": "Picaresque Novel", "pron": "पि-का-रेस्क (pik-uh-RESK)", "hindi": "एक चालाक घुमक्कड़ नायक के कारनामों वाली कहानी", "net": "An episodic narrative of the adventures of a rogue (Spanish picaro) of low birth who lives by his wits; prototype Lazarillo de Tormes (1554); e.g. Moll Flanders, Roderick Random."}, {"term": "Epistolary Novel", "pron": "इ-पिस-टो-लरी (ih-PIS-tuh-ler-ee)", "hindi": "चिट्ठियों के रूप में लिखा उपन्यास", "net": "A novel told through letters (and sometimes diaries) written by one or more characters, e.g. Richardson's Pamela and Clarissa, Smollett's Humphry Clinker, Walker's The Color Purple."}, {"term": "Bildungsroman", "pron": "बिल-डुंग्स-रो-मान (BIL-doongs-roh-mahn)", "hindi": "नायक के बचपन से बड़े होने और परिपक्व होने की कहानी", "net": "German 'novel of formation/education': traces the protagonist's development from youth to maturity; prototype Goethe's Wilhelm Meister's Apprenticeship; term attributed to Karl Morgenstern, popularised by Dilthey; e.g. David Copperfield, Great Expectations."}, {"term": "Kunstlerroman", "pron": "कुन्स्ट-लर-रो-मान (KOONST-ler-roh-mahn)", "hindi": "एक कलाकार के बनने की कहानी", "net": "A subtype of the Bildungsroman tracing the growth of an artist to maturity and recognition of artistic vocation, e.g. Joyce's A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man."}, {"term": "Gothic Novel", "pron": "गॉ-थिक (GOTH-ik)", "hindi": "डर, रहस्य, पुराने महल और अलौकिक घटनाओं वाला उपन्यास", "net": "A type of fiction of terror and mystery set in gloomy castles or abbeys with supernatural or sensational events; inaugurated by Walpole's The Castle of Otranto (1764), developed by Radcliffe and Lewis."}, {"term": "Sentimental Novel", "pron": "सेन-टि-मेन-टल (sen-tuh-MEN-tl)", "hindi": "भावुकता और करुणा पर ज़ोर देने वाला उपन्यास", "net": "An 18th-century novel exploiting the reader's capacity for tender feeling and displaying the 'sensibility' of virtuous characters, e.g. Mackenzie's The Man of Feeling (1771), Sterne's A Sentimental Journey."}, {"term": "Novel of Manners", "pron": "मै-नर्स (MAN-erz)", "hindi": "समाज के रीति-रिवाज़ और व्यवहार पर आधारित उपन्यास", "net": "A novel dealing with the customs, conventions and social codes of a particular class, in which characters are shaped by social expectation, e.g. Austen's novels."}, {"term": "Historical Novel", "pron": "हिस-टॉ-रि-कल (his-TOR-i-kul)", "hindi": "अतीत के ऐतिहासिक समय में रची कहानी", "net": "A novel that sets its action among real historical events and persons; established by Walter Scott (Waverley, 1814); theorised by Georg Lukacs in The Historical Novel (1937)."}, {"term": "Regional Novel", "pron": "री-ज-नल (REE-juh-nul)", "hindi": "किसी खास इलाके की ज़िन्दगी और बोली पर केंद्रित उपन्यास", "net": "A novel emphasising the setting, speech, customs and social structure of a particular locality as shaping the characters, e.g. Hardy's Wessex, Edgeworth's Castle Rackrent, Narayan's Malgudi."}, {"term": "Condition-of-England Novel", "pron": "कन-डि-शन (kun-DISH-un)", "hindi": "औद्योगिक क्रांति से गरीबों की हालत पर उपन्यास", "net": "Also 'industrial' or 'social-problem' novel: Victorian fiction about the effects of industrialisation on the working class; phrase from Carlyle's Chartism (1839); e.g. Mary Barton, Sybil, Hard Times."}, {"term": "Sensation Novel", "pron": "सेन-से-शन (sen-SAY-shun)", "hindi": "अपराध, रहस्य और गुप्त बातों से भरा सनसनीखेज़ उपन्यास", "net": "A popular 1860s genre combining domestic realism with crime, bigamy, secrets and suspense, e.g. Collins's The Woman in White and Braddon's Lady Audley's Secret."}, {"term": "Roman a clef", "pron": "रो-मां आ क्ले (roh-MAHN ah KLAY)", "hindi": "असली लोगों को नकली नामों से दिखाने वाला उपन्यास", "net": "French 'novel with a key': a novel in which real persons appear under fictitious names, e.g. Huxley's Point Counter Point, Peacock's Nightmare Abbey."}, {"term": "Stream of Consciousness", "pron": "स्ट्रीम ऑफ कॉन-शस-नेस (streem uv KON-shus-nis)", "hindi": "पात्र के मन में लगातार बहते विचारों को जैसे का तैसा लिखना", "net": "Phrase coined by William James (Principles of Psychology, 1890) for the unbroken flow of perceptions, thoughts and feelings; applied to fiction by May Sinclair (1918) for Dorothy Richardson; used by Joyce, Woolf, Faulkner."}, {"term": "Interior Monologue", "pron": "इन-टी-रि-अर मॉ-नो-लॉग (in-TEER-ee-er MON-uh-log)", "hindi": "पात्र का मन ही मन खुद से बोलना, बिना लेखक के बीच में आए", "net": "A type of stream-of-consciousness writing presenting a character's thoughts directly without the narrator's intervention; Joyce credited Dujardin's Les Lauriers sont coupes (1887); e.g. Molly Bloom's soliloquy in Ulysses."}, {"term": "Free Indirect Discourse", "pron": "फ्री इन-डा-इ-रेक्ट (free in-duh-REKT)", "hindi": "लेखक की आवाज़ में पात्र के विचार घुले-मिले होना", "net": "Style indirect libre (term of Charles Bally, 1912): third-person narration that adopts a character's idiom and perspective without quotation or tag; perfected by Austen and Flaubert."}, {"term": "Omniscient Narrator", "pron": "ऑम-नि-शि-ऐंट (om-NISH-unt)", "hindi": "सब कुछ जानने वाला कथावाचक", "net": "A third-person narrator who knows everything about the characters, including their thoughts and motives, and may comment freely, e.g. in Tom Jones and Middlemarch."}, {"term": "Unreliable Narrator", "pron": "अन-रि-ला-य-बल (un-ri-LY-uh-bul)", "hindi": "ऐसा कथावाचक जिसकी बात पर पूरा भरोसा न किया जा सके", "net": "Term coined by Wayne C. Booth (The Rhetoric of Fiction, 1961) for a narrator whose perception, interpretation or evaluation does not coincide with the implied author's norms, e.g. Dowell (The Good Soldier), Stevens (The Remains of the Day)."}, {"term": "Implied Author", "pron": "इम-प्लाइड ऑ-थर (im-PLYD AW-ther)", "hindi": "रचना से झलकने वाली लेखक की छवि, असली लेखक नहीं", "net": "Booth's term (1961) for the authorial presence a reader infers from the work as a whole, distinct from the real author and the narrator."}, {"term": "Point of View", "pron": "पॉइंट ऑफ व्यू (point uv VYOO)", "hindi": "कहानी किसकी नज़र से कही जा रही है", "net": "The way a story is told - the perspective (first person, third-person limited, omniscient) through which characters and events are presented; central to Henry James and Percy Lubbock's The Craft of Fiction (1921)."}, {"term": "Focalization", "pron": "फो-क-लाइ-ज़े-शन (foh-kuh-ly-ZAY-shun)", "hindi": "किस पात्र की आँखों से घटनाएँ देखी जा रही हैं", "net": "Gerard Genette's term (Narrative Discourse, 1972) distinguishing who 'sees' (perspective) from who 'speaks' (voice); zero, internal and external focalization."}, {"term": "Analepsis", "pron": "ऐ-न-लेप-सिस (an-uh-LEP-sis)", "hindi": "पीछे की घटना दिखाना - फ्लैशबैक", "net": "Genette's term for a flashback: narration of an event that occurred earlier than the point the story has reached."}, {"term": "Prolepsis", "pron": "प्रो-लेप-सिस (proh-LEP-sis)", "hindi": "आगे होने वाली घटना पहले बता देना - फ्लैश-फॉरवर्ड", "net": "Genette's term for a flash-forward: anticipation or narration of a later event before its chronological place, e.g. the opening of One Hundred Years of Solitude."}, {"term": "Fabula and Syuzhet", "pron": "फा-बू-ला / स्यू-ज़े (FAB-yoo-luh / SYOO-zhet)", "hindi": "घटनाओं का असली क्रम बनाम कहानी में उनका प्रस्तुत क्रम", "net": "Russian Formalist terms (Shklovsky, Tomashevsky): fabula is the chronological sequence of events; syuzhet is their artistic arrangement in the narrative; cf. Forster's story vs plot."}, {"term": "Flat and Round Characters", "pron": "फ्लैट ऐंड राउंड (flat and round)", "hindi": "एक-जैसा सरल पात्र बनाम जटिल, बदलने वाला पात्र", "net": "E.M. Forster's distinction (Aspects of the Novel, 1927): a flat character is 'constructed round a single idea or quality'; a round one is 'capable of surprising in a convincing way'."}, {"term": "Foil", "pron": "फॉइल (foyl)", "hindi": "ऐसा पात्र जो मुख्य पात्र के गुणों को उभार कर दिखाए", "net": "A character whose contrasting qualities set off or highlight those of another, usually the protagonist."}, {"term": "Anti-hero", "pron": "ऐन-टी-ही-रो (AN-tee-heer-oh)", "hindi": "ऐसा नायक जिसमें पारंपरिक वीरता के गुण न हों", "net": "A protagonist who lacks conventional heroic qualities such as courage, dignity or idealism, e.g. Jim Dixon (Lucky Jim), Yossarian (Catch-22)."}, {"term": "Narratee", "pron": "नै-रे-टी (nar-ay-TEE)", "hindi": "कहानी जिसे सुनाई जा रही है, वो श्रोता", "net": "Gerald Prince's term for the person to whom the narrator addresses the narrative within the text, e.g. Wen Jiabao in The White Tiger."}, {"term": "Frame Narrative", "pron": "फ्रेम नै-र-टिव (fraym NAR-uh-tiv)", "hindi": "कहानी के अंदर कहानी", "net": "A story within which one or more other stories are told, e.g. Walton's letters in Frankenstein, Lockwood and Nelly Dean in Wuthering Heights, the deck of the Nellie in Heart of Darkness."}, {"term": "In Medias Res", "pron": "इन मे-डि-आस रेस (in MAY-dee-us RAYS)", "hindi": "कहानी को बीच से शुरू करना", "net": "Latin 'into the middle of things' (Horace, Ars Poetica): beginning a narrative in the midst of the action, filling in earlier events later."}, {"term": "Denouement", "pron": "डे-नू-मां (day-noo-MAHN)", "hindi": "कहानी की गुत्थी सुलझना, अंतिम परिणाम", "net": "French 'unknotting': the final unravelling of the plot after the climax, in which complications are resolved."}, {"term": "Epiphany", "pron": "इ-पि-फ-नी (ih-PIF-uh-nee)", "hindi": "अचानक सच्चाई का एहसास होने वाला पल", "net": "Joyce's term (in Stephen Hero) for 'a sudden spiritual manifestation' in which a commonplace object or moment reveals its inner meaning; central to Dubliners."}, {"term": "Verisimilitude", "pron": "वे-रि-सि-मि-लि-ट्यूड (ver-uh-si-MIL-i-tood)", "hindi": "सच जैसी लगने वाली बात", "net": "The quality of seeming true to life; the degree to which a fictional work creates the illusion of reality."}, {"term": "Formal Realism", "pron": "फॉर-मल री-अ-लिज़्म (FOR-mul REE-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "रोज़मर्रा ज़िन्दगी का बारीक, यथार्थ चित्रण", "net": "Ian Watt's term (The Rise of the Novel, 1957) for the novel's narrative method of particularised characters, specific time and place and circumstantial detail."}, {"term": "Naturalism", "pron": "नै-च-र-लिज़्म (NACH-er-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "मनुष्य को वंश और माहौल से बँधा दिखाने वाला यथार्थवाद", "net": "A stricter realism holding that human beings are determined by heredity and environment; theorised by Emile Zola (Le Roman experimental, 1880); e.g. Crane, Norris, Dreiser, Hardy in part."}, {"term": "Iceberg Theory", "pron": "आइस-बर्ग (EYES-berg)", "hindi": "कम शब्दों में कहना, असली अर्थ नीचे छुपा रखना", "net": "Hemingway's 'theory of omission' (Death in the Afternoon, 1932): the dignity of movement of an iceberg is due to only one-eighth of it being above water; the writer may omit what he knows."}, {"term": "Metafiction", "pron": "मे-टा-फिक-शन (MET-uh-fik-shun)", "hindi": "ऐसी कथा जो खुद अपने कथा होने की बात करे", "net": "Fiction that self-consciously draws attention to its own status as artifice; term coined by William H. Gass (1970); e.g. Tristram Shandy, The French Lieutenant's Woman."}, {"term": "Historiographic Metafiction", "pron": "हिस-टो-रि-यो-ग्रै-फिक (his-tor-ee-uh-GRAF-ik)", "hindi": "इतिहास पर लिखी कथा जो इतिहास लिखने पर ही सवाल उठाए", "net": "Linda Hutcheon's term (A Poetics of Postmodernism, 1988) for postmodern novels that are self-reflexive yet lay claim to historical events, e.g. Midnight's Children, Flaubert's Parrot."}, {"term": "Magic Realism", "pron": "मै-जिक री-अ-लिज़्म (MAJ-ik REE-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "यथार्थ कहानी में जादुई बातें सामान्य ढंग से आना", "net": "Term coined by German art critic Franz Roh (1925); in fiction, the matter-of-fact blending of the fantastic with realistic narration; Carpentier's 'lo real maravilloso'; e.g. Garcia Marquez, Rushdie, Okri."}, {"term": "Mise en Abyme", "pron": "मीज़ आं अ-बीम (meez ahn ah-BEEM)", "hindi": "कहानी के अंदर उसी कहानी का छोटा रूप", "net": "Term from heraldry used by Andre Gide (1893) for a work containing a smaller replica or mirror of itself, e.g. a play within a play, a novel within a novel."}, {"term": "Polyphony", "pron": "प-लि-फ-नी (puh-LIF-uh-nee)", "hindi": "उपन्यास में कई स्वतंत्र आवाज़ें, लेखक की एक आवाज़ हावी नहीं", "net": "Bakhtin's term (Problems of Dostoevsky's Poetics, 1929) for a novel with 'a plurality of independent and unmerged voices and consciousnesses', exemplified by Dostoevsky."}, {"term": "Heteroglossia", "pron": "हे-ट-रो-ग्लॉ-सि-आ (het-er-oh-GLOS-ee-uh)", "hindi": "एक ही भाषा के अंदर अलग-अलग बोलियाँ और अंदाज़", "net": "Bakhtin's term (Discourse in the Novel) for the coexistence of diverse social speech types, dialects and registers within a language and within the novel."}, {"term": "Chronotope", "pron": "क्रॉ-नो-टोप (KRON-uh-tohp)", "hindi": "कथा में समय और स्थान का आपसी जुड़ाव", "net": "Bakhtin's term ('time-space') for the intrinsic connectedness of temporal and spatial relationships in literature, e.g. the road, the castle, the threshold."}, {"term": "Novella", "pron": "नो-वे-ला (noh-VEL-uh)", "hindi": "छोटा उपन्यास - कहानी से लंबा, उपन्यास से छोटा", "net": "A prose fiction of intermediate length, longer than a short story and shorter than a novel, e.g. Heart of Darkness, The Metamorphosis, Death in Venice."}, {"term": "Campus Novel", "pron": "कैम-पस (KAM-pus)", "hindi": "विश्वविद्यालय के माहौल पर आधारित उपन्यास", "net": "A novel set in a university, usually satirising academic life, e.g. Amis's Lucky Jim (1954), Lodge's Changing Places (1975) and Small World (1984)."}, {"term": "Dystopia", "pron": "डिस-टो-पि-आ (dis-TOH-pee-uh)", "hindi": "भयानक, दमनकारी भविष्य वाला काल्पनिक समाज", "net": "An imagined society worse than the present, the opposite of More's Utopia (1516); term used by J.S. Mill (1868); e.g. Brave New World, Nineteen Eighty-Four, The Handmaid's Tale."}, {"term": "Subaltern", "pron": "सब-ऑल-टर्न (sub-AWL-tern)", "hindi": "समाज के सबसे दबे, बेआवाज़ लोग", "net": "Gramsci's term for groups outside hegemonic power; developed by the Subaltern Studies group and Spivak ('Can the Subaltern Speak?', 1988)."}, {"term": "Hybridity", "pron": "हाइ-ब्रि-डि-टी (hy-BRID-i-tee)", "hindi": "दो संस्कृतियों के मेल से बनी नई पहचान", "net": "Homi Bhabha's term (The Location of Culture, 1994) for the new transcultural forms produced in the 'Third Space' of colonial encounter."}, {"term": "Mimicry", "pron": "मि-मि-क्री (MIM-i-kree)", "hindi": "उपनिवेशित व्यक्ति का शासक की नकल करना", "net": "Bhabha's concept of the colonised subject's imitation of the coloniser, 'almost the same, but not quite', which also mocks colonial authority; cf. Naipaul's The Mimic Men."}, {"term": "Diaspora", "pron": "डा-य-स्पो-रा (dy-AS-per-uh)", "hindi": "अपने देश से दूर बसे लोगों का समुदाय", "net": "Greek 'scattering': a community living outside its homeland; diasporic fiction explores displacement and memory, e.g. Lahiri, Mistry, Rushdie, Selvon."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who described his novel as a 'comic epic poem in prose'?", "options": ["Samuel Richardson", "Henry Fielding", "Tobias Smollett", "Laurence Sterne"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Fielding used the phrase in the Preface to Joseph Andrews (1742)."}, {"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (a) Tristram Shandy (b) Pamela (c) Humphry Clinker (d) Tom Jones", "options": ["b, d, a, c", "d, b, a, c", "b, a, d, c", "a, b, d, c"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Pamela 1740, Tom Jones 1749, Tristram Shandy 1759-67, Humphry Clinker 1771."}, {"q": "Match List I with List II: (a) Becky Sharp (b) Dorothea Brooke (c) Michael Henchard (d) Uriah Heep -- (i) David Copperfield (ii) The Mayor of Casterbridge (iii) Vanity Fair (iv) Middlemarch", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Becky Sharp-Vanity Fair, Dorothea-Middlemarch, Henchard-Mayor, Uriah Heep-David Copperfield."}, {"q": "'On or about December 1910 human character changed' was written by:", "options": ["D.H. Lawrence", "Virginia Woolf", "E.M. Forster", "T.S. Eliot"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Woolf, 'Mr Bennett and Mrs Brown' (1924)."}, {"q": "Who first applied the term 'stream of consciousness' to literature?", "options": ["William James", "Henry James", "May Sinclair", "Dorothy Richardson"], "answer": 2, "exp": "May Sinclair used it in 1918 reviewing Dorothy Richardson's Pilgrimage; William James coined it in psychology."}, {"q": "The distinction between 'flat' and 'round' characters is made in:", "options": ["The Craft of Fiction", "The Rhetoric of Fiction", "Aspects of the Novel", "The Great Tradition"], "answer": 2, "exp": "E.M. Forster, Aspects of the Novel (1927)."}, {"q": "The terms 'unreliable narrator' and 'implied author' were introduced by:", "options": ["Gerard Genette", "Wayne C. Booth", "Percy Lubbock", "Mikhail Bakhtin"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Booth, The Rhetoric of Fiction (1961)."}, {"q": "The title of Achebe's Things Fall Apart is taken from:", "options": ["Eliot's The Waste Land", "Yeats's 'The Second Coming'", "Hopkins's 'The Windhover'", "Conrad's Heart of Darkness"], "answer": 1, "exp": "'Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold' - Yeats."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Chinua Achebe called Joseph Conrad 'a thoroughgoing racist'. Reason (R): In 'An Image of Africa' Achebe argued that Heart of Darkness denies Africans full humanity.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Achebe's 1975 lecture makes exactly this argument."}, {"q": "'The past is a foreign country: they do things differently there' is the opening line of:", "options": ["Rebecca", "The Go-Between", "Atonement", "The Remains of the Day"], "answer": 1, "exp": "L.P. Hartley, The Go-Between (1953)."}, {"q": "Who among the following won the Booker Prize in 1997?", "options": ["Kiran Desai", "Arundhati Roy", "Aravind Adiga", "Vikram Seth"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The God of Small Things won the 1997 Booker."}, {"q": "Who is the narrator of Raja Rao's Kanthapura?", "options": ["Moorthy", "Rangamma", "Achakka", "Bade Khan"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The old grandmother Achakka narrates in sthala-purana style."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Untouchable (b) The Guide (c) Train to Pakistan (d) Nectar in a Sieve -- (i) Rukmani (ii) Juggut Singh (iii) Raju (iv) Bakha", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Bakha-Untouchable, Raju-The Guide, Juggut Singh-Train to Pakistan, Rukmani-Nectar in a Sieve."}, {"q": "Which is regarded as the first Indian novel in English?", "options": ["Kanthapura", "Rajmohan's Wife", "Twilight in Delhi", "Swami and Friends"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's Rajmohan's Wife (1864)."}, {"q": "Poe formulated his theory of the 'single effect' of the short tale in a review of:", "options": ["Irving's The Sketch Book", "Hawthorne's Twice-Told Tales", "Melville's Piazza Tales", "Cooper's The Pioneers"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Poe's 1842 review of Hawthorne's Twice-Told Tales."}, {"q": "'I would prefer not to' is the refrain of a character in:", "options": ["The Scarlet Letter", "Bartleby, the Scrivener", "Billy Budd", "Young Goodman Brown"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Melville's Bartleby (1853)."}, {"q": "Which novelist is NOT among the four named in the opening of F.R. Leavis's The Great Tradition (1948)?", "options": ["Jane Austen", "George Eliot", "Charles Dickens", "Joseph Conrad"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Leavis named Austen, Eliot, James and Conrad; Dickens was admitted only later."}, {"q": "Jean Rhys's Wide Sargasso Sea rewrites which novel from the point of view of Antoinette Cosway?", "options": ["Wuthering Heights", "Villette", "Jane Eyre", "Great Expectations"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Antoinette is Bertha Mason, Rochester's first wife in Jane Eyre."}, {"q": "Arrange Hardy's novels chronologically: (a) Tess of the d'Urbervilles (b) The Return of the Native (c) Jude the Obscure (d) Far from the Madding Crowd", "options": ["d, b, a, c", "b, d, a, c", "d, a, b, c", "b, d, c, a"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1874, 1878, 1891, 1895."}, {"q": "Who called the large Victorian novels 'loose baggy monsters'?", "options": ["Virginia Woolf", "Henry James", "Ford Madox Ford", "F.R. Leavis"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Henry James, Preface to The Tragic Muse."}, {"q": "Joyce's Ulysses takes place on:", "options": ["16 June 1904", "16 June 1922", "2 February 1904", "1 April 1916"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Bloomsday, 16 June 1904, in Dublin."}, {"q": "The term 'magic realism' was originally coined by the art critic:", "options": ["Alejo Carpentier", "Franz Roh", "Gabriel Garcia Marquez", "Jorge Luis Borges"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Franz Roh used Magischer Realismus in 1925 for post-expressionist painting."}, {"q": "In The White Tiger, Balram Halwai's narrative takes the form of letters addressed to:", "options": ["Barack Obama", "Wen Jiabao", "Mr Ashok", "Pinky Madam"], "answer": 1, "exp": "He writes to Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao."}, {"q": "The 1992 Booker Prize was shared by The English Patient and:", "options": ["Possession", "Sacred Hunger", "The Famished Road", "The Remains of the Day"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Ondaatje and Barry Unsworth shared it in 1992."}, {"q": "Match short stories with authors: (a) The Necklace (b) The Overcoat (c) The Garden Party (d) The Gift of the Magi -- (i) O. Henry (ii) Katherine Mansfield (iii) Gogol (iv) Maupassant", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Maupassant, Gogol, Mansfield, O. Henry."}, {"q": "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak translated 'Draupadi' and 'Breast-Giver', stories by:", "options": ["Ismat Chughtai", "Mahasweta Devi", "Ashapurna Devi", "Amrita Pritam"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Both are Mahasweta Devi's Bengali stories."}, {"q": "The narrator of The Great Gatsby is:", "options": ["Jay Gatsby", "Tom Buchanan", "Nick Carraway", "Jordan Baker"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Nick Carraway narrates in the first person."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Tristram Shandy is often called a precursor of modernist and metafictional fiction. Reason (R): It disrupts chronology through digressions and uses typographical devices like the black and marbled pages.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Its self-conscious, non-linear form explains its status."}, {"q": "Which African writer, in Decolonising the Mind (1986), bid farewell to English as his medium of fiction?", "options": ["Chinua Achebe", "Wole Soyinka", "Ngugi wa Thiong'o", "Ben Okri"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Ngugi turned to Gikuyu."}, {"q": "Which of the following pairs is INCORRECTLY matched?", "options": ["The Shadow Lines - Amitav Ghosh", "A Fine Balance - Rohinton Mistry", "That Long Silence - Anita Desai", "English, August - Upamanyu Chatterjee"], "answer": 2, "exp": "That Long Silence (1988) is by Shashi Deshpande."}]}, {"id": 4, "title": "Non-Fictional Prose", "overview": "Is unit mein English essay aur gadya ka poora safar hai: Bacon aur 17th century prose, Milton ki Areopagitica, Addison-Steele ke periodicals, Johnson, Romantic essayists (Lamb, Hazlitt, De Quincey), Victorian sages (Carlyle, Ruskin, Arnold, Pater, Newman), modern essayists (Woolf, Orwell, Russell) aur Indian non-fiction (Gandhi, Nehru, Ambedkar, Tagore, Sen), saath mein biography, autobiography, travel writing aur periodicals. NET mein yahan se 'kisne likha/kis essay ka quote hai', match-the-following, chronology aur assertion-reason wale sawal aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "The Essay: Origins and Francis Bacon", "points": ["Michel de Montaigne's Essais (1580) gave the form its name: 'essai' = attempt, trial (koshish). Montaigne: 'I am myself the matter of my book.'", "Francis Bacon (1561-1626) is called the father of the English essay; his essays are aphoristic, impersonal and didactic, unlike Montaigne's personal ones.", "Bacon's Essays: 1597 (10 essays), 1612 (38 essays), 1625 (58 essays, titled Essays or Counsels, Civil and Moral).", "Of Studies: 'Some books are to be tasted, others to be swallowed, and some few to be chewed and digested.' Also: 'Reading maketh a full man; conference a ready man; and writing an exact man.'", "Of Truth (opening essay of 1625): 'What is truth? said jesting Pilate, and would not stay for an answer.'", "Of Marriage and Single Life: 'He that hath wife and children hath given hostages to fortune.' Of Revenge: 'Revenge is a kind of wild justice.'", "Of Adversity: 'Prosperity is the blessing of the Old Testament; adversity is the blessing of the New.' Of Gardens: 'God Almighty first planted a garden.'", "Other Bacon prose: The Advancement of Learning (1605), Novum Organum (1620, Latin), History of the Reign of King Henry VII (1622), New Atlantis (1627, posthumous utopia; Salomon's House).", "In Novum Organum Bacon names four Idols (false notions): Idols of the Tribe, the Cave, the Market-place and the Theatre.", "Bacon's style is Senecan/curt (short, pointed sentences), opposed to the long Ciceronian period."]}, {"title": "Seventeenth-Century Prose: Browne, Burton, Walton and Others", "points": ["Sir Thomas Browne (1605-1682), a Norwich physician: Religio Medici (pirated 1642, authorised 1643) - 'the religion of a doctor'.", "Browne's Pseudodoxia Epidemica (1646), also called Vulgar Errors, examines popular false beliefs.", "Browne's Hydriotaphia, Urn-Burial and The Garden of Cyrus appeared together in 1658; Christian Morals was posthumous (1716). His style is ornate, Latinate, baroque.", "Hydriotaphia: 'Man is a noble animal, splendid in ashes, and pompous in the grave.'", "Robert Burton, The Anatomy of Melancholy (1621), written under the pseudonym Democritus Junior.", "Izaak Walton, The Compleat Angler (1653), a dialogue on fishing (Piscator, Venator, Auceps); also Lives of Donne, Wotton, Hooker, Herbert and Sanderson.", "Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651): life in the state of nature is 'solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short'.", "Jeremy Taylor, Holy Living (1650) and Holy Dying (1651); Thomas Fuller, The History of the Worthies of England (1662).", "Character writing (Theophrastan): Joseph Hall, Characters of Vertues and Vices (1608); Sir Thomas Overbury, Characters (1614); John Earle, Micro-cosmographie (1628).", "Bunyan's Grace Abounding to the Chief of Sinners (1666) is a spiritual autobiography; Pepys's Diary covers 1660-1669 (deciphered and published 1825)."]}, {"title": "Milton's Prose", "points": ["Milton called his prose the work of his 'left hand' (in The Reason of Church-Government, 1642, where he also announces his epic ambition).", "Anti-prelatical tracts: Of Reformation (1641), The Reason of Church-Government Urged against Prelaty (1642).", "Divorce tracts: The Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce (1643), Tetrachordon and Colasterion (1645).", "Of Education (1644), addressed to Samuel Hartlib.", "Areopagitica (November 1644): 'A Speech of Mr. John Milton for the Liberty of Unlicenc'd Printing, to the Parliament of England', against the Licensing Order of 1643; title from Isocrates' Areopagiticus.", "Areopagitica quote: 'Who kills a man kills a reasonable creature, God's image; but he who destroys a good book, kills reason itself.'", "Areopagitica quote: 'I cannot praise a fugitive and cloistered virtue, unexercised and unbreathed.'", "The Tenure of Kings and Magistrates (1649) defends the right to depose a tyrant; Eikonoklastes (1649) answers Eikon Basilike.", "Defensio pro Populo Anglicano (1651, Latin, against Salmasius) and Defensio Secunda (1654); The Ready and Easy Way to Establish a Free Commonwealth (1660).", "De Doctrina Christiana (Latin theological treatise) was discovered in 1823 and published in 1825."]}, {"title": "The Periodical Essay: Addison and Steele", "points": ["Daniel Defoe's Review (1704-1713) is an early periodical; the periodical essay flowered in the Queen Anne (Augustan) age.", "The Tatler was started by Richard Steele on 12 April 1709 (ran till January 1711) under the persona Isaac Bickerstaff, a name borrowed from Swift.", "The Spectator (1 March 1711 - 6 December 1712, 555 numbers) by Joseph Addison and Steele; Addison revived it in 1714 (Nos. 556-635).", "The Spectator Club: Mr. Spectator (silent narrator), Sir Roger de Coverley (Tory country squire), Sir Andrew Freeport (Whig merchant), Captain Sentry, Will Honeycomb (ageing beau), the Templar and the Clergyman.", "Spectator No. 10 (Addison): aim 'to have brought philosophy out of closets and libraries, schools and colleges, to dwell in clubs and assemblies, at tea-tables and in coffee-houses.'", "Addison's 'Pleasures of the Imagination' series (Spectator Nos. 411-421, 1712) and his Saturday papers on Paradise Lost.", "Sir Roger de Coverley was created by Steele (Spectator No. 2) but developed mainly by Addison; Addison 'killed' him (No. 517).", "The Guardian (1713) followed; Addison wrote the tragedy Cato (1713), Steele the sentimental comedy The Conscious Lovers (1722).", "Johnson on Addison: 'Whoever wishes to attain an English style, familiar but not coarse, and elegant but not ostentatious, must give his days and nights to the volumes of Addison.'", "Swift wrote for The Examiner (Tory, 1710-11); Goldsmith's The Citizen of the World (1762, as book) presents Lien Chi Altangi, a Chinese philosopher in London."]}, {"title": "Samuel Johnson and Boswell", "points": ["Johnson's periodicals: The Rambler (1750-1752, 208 numbers, almost all by Johnson), contributions to The Adventurer, and The Idler (1758-1760, in the Universal Chronicle).", "A Dictionary of the English Language (1755); the Letter to Lord Chesterfield (1755) rebukes a late patron: 'Is not a patron, my Lord, one who looks with unconcern on a man struggling for life in the water...'", "Life of Mr Richard Savage (1744); Rasselas, Prince of Abyssinia (1759), written to pay for his mother's funeral.", "Preface to Shakespeare (1765): Shakespeare is 'the poet of nature' who holds up 'a faithful mirrour of manners and of life'; defends mixing tragedy and comedy and rejects unities of time and place.", "A Journey to the Western Islands of Scotland (1775), from the 1773 tour with Boswell.", "Lives of the Poets (1779-1781): 52 lives; the term 'metaphysical poets' is fixed in the Life of Cowley ('heterogeneous ideas are yoked by violence together').", "Johnson: 'Language is the dress of thought'; 'Patriotism is the last refuge of a scoundrel' (reported by Boswell).", "James Boswell, The Life of Samuel Johnson, LL.D. (1791), often called the greatest English biography; The Journal of a Tour to the Hebrides (1785).", "The Club (Literary Club, 1764) included Johnson, Reynolds, Burke, Goldsmith, Garrick and later Boswell.", "'Johnsonese' = heavy, Latinate, balanced periodic style."]}, {"title": "Charles Lamb (Elia)", "points": ["Charles Lamb (1775-1834), clerk at the East India House; the Elia essays appeared in the London Magazine from 1820.", "Essays of Elia (1823) and The Last Essays of Elia (1833). 'Elia' was the name of an Italian clerk at the South-Sea House.", "First Elia essay: 'The South-Sea House' (1820).", "'Dream-Children: A Reverie': Elia tells his imagined children Alice and John about grandmother Field and uncle John L.; ends 'We are not of Alice, nor of thee, nor are we children at all.'", "'A Dissertation upon Roast Pig': Ho-ti and his son Bo-bo in China discover roast pork by burning down a house (story from a Chinese manuscript, attributed to his friend Manning).", "Other Essays of Elia: 'Christ's Hospital Five and Thirty Years Ago', 'The Two Races of Men' (borrowers and lenders), 'Mrs. Battle's Opinions on Whist', 'Imperfect Sympathies', 'Mackery End, in Hertfordshire', 'New Year's Eve', 'The Praise of Chimney-Sweepers', 'A Chapter on Ears', 'Oxford in the Vacation', 'Witches and Other Night-Fears'.", "Last Essays of Elia: 'Old China', 'The Superannuated Man', 'Poor Relations', 'Detached Thoughts on Books and Reading', 'Sanity of True Genius'.", "Bridget Elia in the essays is his sister Mary Lamb; Tales from Shakespeare (1807) was written with Mary.", "Specimens of English Dramatic Poets (1808); 'On the Tragedies of Shakespeare' (1811) argues that King Lear cannot be acted on the stage.", "Lamb is the master of the familiar/personal essay: whimsical, nostalgic, archaic in diction (Browne and Burton are his models)."]}, {"title": "Romantic Essayists: Hazlitt, Leigh Hunt and De Quincey", "points": ["The Round Table (1817, with Leigh Hunt) includes 'On Gusto'.", "Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817); Lectures on the English Poets (1818); Lectures on the English Comic Writers (1819).", "Table-Talk (1821-22): 'On Going a Journey' ('One of the pleasantest things in the world is going a journey; but I like to go by myself'), 'On Familiar Style', 'On the Pleasure of Painting'.", "The Spirit of the Age (1825): portraits of contemporaries such as Bentham, Godwin, Coleridge, Wordsworth, Scott, Byron, Jeffrey, Lamb.", "The Plain Speaker (1826) includes 'On the Prose Style of Poets'; Liber Amoris (1823) records his passion for Sarah Walker.", "'My First Acquaintance with Poets' (1823, in The Liberal) recalls meeting Coleridge in 1798.", "Leigh Hunt edited The Examiner (from 1808; jailed 1813-15 for libelling the Prince Regent); his Autobiography appeared in 1850. Blackwood's attacked Hunt, Keats and Hazlitt as the 'Cockney School'.", "Confessions of an English Opium-Eater: London Magazine 1821, book 1822, enlarged revision 1856.", "'On the Knocking at the Gate in Macbeth' (1823, London Magazine): the knocking marks the return of the human world after the murder.", "'On Murder Considered as One of the Fine Arts' (1827, Blackwood's; later postscripts).", "Suspiria de Profundis (1845, Blackwood's) includes 'Levana and Our Ladies of Sorrow': Mater Lachrymarum (Our Lady of Tears), Mater Suspiriorum (Our Lady of Sighs), Mater Tenebrarum (Our Lady of Darkness).", "Distinction between 'literature of knowledge' (to teach) and 'literature of power' (to move): in 'Letters to a Young Man' (1823) and the essay on Pope (1848)."]}, {"title": "Thomas Carlyle", "points": ["Thomas Carlyle (1795-1881), Scottish 'Sage of Chelsea'.", "'Signs of the Times' (1829, Edinburgh Review) calls the age 'the Mechanical Age'; 'Characteristics' (1831).", "Sartor Resartus ('The Tailor Re-tailored'): Fraser's Magazine 1833-34, book USA 1836, UK 1838. Hero: Professor Diogenes Teufelsdrockh of Weissnichtwo, 'Professor of Things in General'; philosophy of clothes.", "Sartor's spiritual stages: 'The Everlasting No', 'Centre of Indifference', 'The Everlasting Yea'.", "The French Revolution: A History (1837); the first manuscript of vol. I was burnt by J. S. Mill's housemaid and rewritten.", "On Heroes, Hero-Worship and the Heroic in History (lectures 1840, book 1841): Hero as Divinity (Odin), Prophet (Mahomet), Poet (Dante, Shakespeare), Priest (Luther, Knox), Man of Letters (Johnson, Rousseau, Burns), King (Cromwell, Napoleon).", "'The history of the world is but the biography of great men' (On Heroes).", "Chartism (1839) raised the 'Condition-of-England question'; Past and Present (1843) contrasts Abbot Samson of medieval Bury St Edmunds with modern 'cash nexus' society.", "Latter-Day Pamphlets (1850); History of Frederick the Great (1858-65); he called economics 'the dismal science' (1849).", "'Carlylese': Germanic, capitalised, exclamatory, prophetic style; strongly influenced by Goethe and German idealism."]}, {"title": "John Ruskin", "points": ["Modern Painters (5 vols, 1843-1860) began as a defence of J. M. W. Turner.", "'Pathetic fallacy' coined in Modern Painters vol. III (1856), chapter 'Of the Pathetic Fallacy'.", "The Seven Lamps of Architecture (1849): Sacrifice, Truth, Power, Beauty, Life, Memory, Obedience.", "The Stones of Venice (1851-53) contains the chapter 'The Nature of Gothic', reprinted by William Morris's Kelmscott Press (1892).", "Unto This Last (four essays in the Cornhill Magazine 1860, book 1862) attacks classical political economy; title from the parable of the labourers in the vineyard (Matthew 20). Key line: 'There is no wealth but life.'", "Gandhi read Unto This Last in 1904 and paraphrased it in Gujarati as Sarvodaya (1908).", "Sesame and Lilies (1865): 'Of Kings' Treasuries' (on reading) and 'Of Queens' Gardens' (on women's role); The Crown of Wild Olive (1866); Munera Pulveris (1862-63, book 1872).", "Fors Clavigera (1871-84): monthly letters 'to the workmen and labourers of Great Britain'; founded the Guild of St George.", "First Slade Professor of Fine Art at Oxford (1869); Whistler sued him for libel in 1878 over the phrase 'flinging a pot of paint in the public's face'.", "Praeterita (1885-89) is his unfinished autobiography."]}, {"title": "Matthew Arnold's Prose Criticism", "points": ["Preface to Poems (1853) explains dropping Empedocles on Etna; On Translating Homer (1861): Homer is rapid, plain in thought and style, and noble.", "'The Function of Criticism at the Present Time' (1864): criticism is 'a disinterested endeavour to learn and propagate the best that is known and thought in the world.'", "Essays in Criticism, First Series (1865); Second Series (1888, posthumous) includes 'The Study of Poetry', 'Wordsworth', 'Byron', 'Shelley', 'Keats'.", "'The Study of Poetry' (1880): poetry is 'a criticism of life'; touchstone method; Chaucer lacks 'high seriousness'; Dryden and Pope are 'classics of our prose'.", "In 'Shelley' Arnold calls him 'a beautiful and ineffectual angel, beating in the void his luminous wings in vain'.", "Culture and Anarchy (1869): culture is 'the study of perfection'; 'sweetness and light' (phrase from Swift's The Battle of the Books).", "Culture and Anarchy divides society into Barbarians (aristocracy), Philistines (middle class) and Populace (working class); contrasts Hebraism (strictness of conscience) and Hellenism (spontaneity of consciousness).", "Other prose: On the Study of Celtic Literature (1867), Friendship's Garland (1871), Literature and Dogma (1873).", "Arnold was Oxford Professor of Poetry (1857-67) and an Inspector of Schools for 35 years."]}, {"title": "Walter Pater, Aestheticism and Wilde", "points": ["Pater's Studies in the History of the Renaissance (1873), retitled The Renaissance: Studies in Art and Poetry (1877).", "Conclusion: 'To burn always with this hard, gem-like flame, to maintain this ecstasy, is success in life.' It was dropped from the 2nd edition (1877) and restored in 1888.", "'All art constantly aspires towards the condition of music' - from 'The School of Giorgione' (added 1877).", "The Leonardo essay's Mona Lisa passage: 'She is older than the rocks among which she sits'; Yeats printed it as free verse in the Oxford Book of Modern Verse (1936).", "Marius the Epicurean (1885); Imaginary Portraits (1887); Appreciations, with an Essay on Style (1889); Plato and Platonism (1893).", "Aestheticism slogan 'art for art's sake' (l'art pour l'art) goes back to Gautier's preface to Mademoiselle de Maupin (1835).", "Oscar Wilde's Intentions (1891): 'The Decay of Lying' ('Life imitates Art far more than Art imitates Life') and 'The Critic as Artist'; also 'The Soul of Man under Socialism' (1891) and De Profundis (1905, letter written in Reading Gaol).", "The Yellow Book (1894-97) was the quarterly of the Decadents, art editor Aubrey Beardsley."]}, {"title": "Newman, Mill, Macaulay and Victorian Thought", "points": ["John Henry Newman led the Oxford (Tractarian) Movement through Tracts for the Times (1833-41); Keble's Assize Sermon (14 July 1833) began it; Tract 90 (1841) caused a storm.", "Newman converted to Roman Catholicism in 1845 and became a Cardinal in 1879; canonised in 2019.", "The Idea of a University (discourses 1852, final form 1873): knowledge is its own end; 'It is almost a definition of a gentleman to say he is one who never inflicts pain.'", "Apologia Pro Vita Sua (1864), a spiritual autobiography written in reply to Charles Kingsley's attack.", "Newman also wrote The Dream of Gerontius (1865), An Essay in Aid of a Grammar of Assent (1870) and the hymn 'Lead, Kindly Light' (1833).", "J. S. Mill: 'What is Poetry?' (1833: eloquence is heard, poetry is overheard), On Liberty (1859), Utilitarianism (1861), The Subjection of Women (1869), Autobiography (1873).", "T. B. Macaulay: essay on Milton (1825, Edinburgh Review); Minute on Indian Education (1835): 'a single shelf of a good European library was worth the whole native literature of India and Arabia'; History of England (1848-61).", "T. H. Huxley ('Darwin's bulldog'): 'Science and Culture' (1880) debated Arnold's 'Literature and Science' (1882).", "R. L. Stevenson's essays Virginibus Puerisque (1881): 'An Apology for Idlers', 'El Dorado' ('to travel hopefully is a better thing than to arrive')."]}, {"title": "Virginia Woolf as Essayist", "points": ["The Common Reader (1925) and The Common Reader: Second Series (1932); title from Johnson's Life of Gray ('I rejoice to concur with the common reader').", "'Modern Fiction' (1919 as 'Modern Novels', revised 1925): 'Life is not a series of gig lamps symmetrically arranged; life is a luminous halo'; attacks Bennett, Wells, Galsworthy as 'materialists'.", "'Mr. Bennett and Mrs. Brown' (1924): 'On or about December 1910 human character changed'; Edwardians vs Georgians.", "A Room of One's Own (1929), from 1928 lectures at Newnham and Girton, Cambridge: 'a woman must have money and a room of her own if she is to write fiction.'", "A Room of One's Own: Shakespeare's imagined sister Judith Shakespeare; narrator 'call me Mary Beton, Mary Seton, Mary Carmichael'; Oxbridge and Fernham; the androgynous mind (from Coleridge).", "'Professions for Women' (1931 speech): killing 'the Angel in the House' (phrase from Coventry Patmore's poem).", "Three Guineas (1938) links patriarchy and war; The Death of the Moth and Other Essays (1942, posthumous).", "'The New Biography' (1927): biography should join truth ('granite') and personality ('rainbow'). Biographical works: Orlando: A Biography (1928), Flush (1933), Roger Fry (1940).", "Moments of Being (1976, posthumous) contains 'A Sketch of the Past'; Woolf and Leonard founded the Hogarth Press (1917); member of the Bloomsbury Group."]}, {"title": "Orwell, Russell and Other Modern Essayists", "points": ["Orwell = Eric Arthur Blair, born 1903 at Motihari, Bihar; served in the Indian Imperial Police in Burma (1922-27).", "'A Hanging' (1931, The Adelphi) and 'Shooting an Elephant' (1936, New Writing) are set in Burma and expose imperialism.", "Documentary books: Down and Out in Paris and London (1933), The Road to Wigan Pier (1937), Homage to Catalonia (1938, Spanish Civil War, POUM militia).", "'Politics and the English Language' (1946, Horizon): six rules, e.g. 'Never use a long word where a short one will do'; 'If it is possible to cut a word out, always cut it out'; political language makes 'lies sound truthful and murder respectable'.", "'Why I Write' (1946): four motives - sheer egoism, aesthetic enthusiasm, historical impulse, political purpose; 'Good prose is like a window pane.'", "'Reflections on Gandhi' (1949): 'Saints should always be judged guilty until they are proved innocent.'", "Bertrand Russell won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1950.", "Russell's works: Principia Mathematica (with Whitehead, 1910-13), The Problems of Philosophy (1912), Mysticism and Logic (1918, includes 'A Free Man's Worship', 1903).", "Why I Am Not a Christian (lecture 1927), Marriage and Morals (1929), The Conquest of Happiness (1930), In Praise of Idleness (1935), A History of Western Philosophy (1945), Unpopular Essays (1950), Portraits from Memory (1956, includes 'Knowledge and Wisdom').", "Prologue to Russell's Autobiography (1967-69), 'What I Have Lived For': 'Three passions, simple but overwhelmingly strong... the longing for love, the search for knowledge, and unbearable pity for the suffering of mankind.'", "G. K. Chesterton: Heretics (1905), Orthodoxy (1908), The Victorian Age in Literature (1913); master of paradox. Hilaire Belloc: On Nothing (1908).", "E. M. Forster: 'What I Believe' (1938: 'if I had to choose between betraying my country and betraying my friend, I hope I should have the guts to betray my country'), Two Cheers for Democracy (1951)."]}, {"title": "Indian Non-Fiction: Gandhi, Nehru and Ambedkar", "points": ["Gandhi's Hind Swaraj (1909) was written in Gujarati aboard the Kildonan Castle (London to South Africa); English translation 1910; a dialogue between Reader and Editor attacking modern civilisation.", "The Story of My Experiments with Truth: Gujarati 'Satyana Prayogo', serialised in Navajivan 1925-29; English translation by Mahadev Desai; in five parts.", "Gandhi's other works: Satyagraha in South Africa (1928), Constructive Programme (1941); journals Indian Opinion (1903), Young India, Navajivan, Harijan (1933). Influences: Ruskin, Tolstoy (The Kingdom of God Is Within You), Thoreau.", "Nehru: Letters from a Father to His Daughter (1929), Glimpses of World History (1934, prison letters to Indira 1930-33), An Autobiography (1936; US title Toward Freedom), The Discovery of India (1946, written in Ahmednagar Fort prison 1944).", "Nehru's 'Tryst with Destiny' speech: midnight 14-15 August 1947, Constituent Assembly.", "Ambedkar: Castes in India: Their Mechanism, Genesis and Development (Columbia seminar paper 1916, published 1917).", "Annihilation of Caste (1936): an undelivered address for the Jat-Pat-Todak Mandal of Lahore, published by Ambedkar; Gandhi replied in Harijan and Ambedkar answered in later editions.", "Other Ambedkar works: The Problem of the Rupee (1923), Pakistan or the Partition of India (1945), Who Were the Shudras? (1946), The Untouchables (1948), Thoughts on Linguistic States (1955), The Buddha and His Dhamma (1957, posthumous), Waiting for a Visa (autobiographical notes, c. 1935-36).", "Ambedkar's journals: Mooknayak (1920), Bahishkrit Bharat (1927), Janata, Prabuddha Bharat."]}, {"title": "Indian Non-Fiction: Tagore, Sen and Other Voices", "points": ["Tagore's English prose: Sadhana (1913), Nationalism (1917, lectures in Japan and the USA, critical of the Western nation-state), Personality (1917), Creative Unity (1922), The Religion of Man (1931, Hibbert Lectures at Oxford 1930).", "Tagore's last essay Crisis in Civilization (1941, read on his 80th birthday); My Reminiscences (Jiban Smriti, English 1917); Letters from Russia (1931).", "Amartya Sen (Nobel in Economics, 1998): Poverty and Famines (1981), Development as Freedom (1999), The Argumentative Indian (2005), Identity and Violence: The Illusion of Destiny (2006), The Idea of Justice (2009; niti vs nyaya), An Uncertain Glory (2013, with Jean Dreze), Home in the World (2021, memoir).", "Nirad C. Chaudhuri: The Autobiography of an Unknown Indian (1951), dedicated to the memory of the British Empire; sequel Thy Hand, Great Anarch! (1987).", "Sake Dean Mahomet's The Travels of Dean Mahomet (1794) is the first book in English by an Indian.", "Sri Aurobindo: The Renaissance in India and The Foundations of Indian Culture; Swami Vivekananda's Chicago address (1893).", "V. S. Naipaul's India trilogy: An Area of Darkness (1964), India: A Wounded Civilization (1977), India: A Million Mutinies Now (1990).", "Other Indian life-writing: Kamala Das, My Story (1976); A. P. J. Abdul Kalam, Wings of Fire (1999); Vikram Seth, From Heaven Lake (1983, travel); Amitav Ghosh, In an Antique Land (1992).", "Dalit autobiographies: Daya Pawar, Baluta (Marathi 1978); Sharankumar Limbale, Akkarmashi (1984, tr. The Outcaste); Bama, Karukku (Tamil 1992); Omprakash Valmiki, Joothan (Hindi 1997)."]}, {"title": "Biography, Autobiography and Diary", "points": ["Early English biography: William Roper's Life of Sir Thomas More, George Cavendish's Life of Wolsey; Walton's Lives (1640-78).", "Johnson's Lives of the Poets (1779-81) and Boswell's Life of Johnson (1791) set the model of critical and intimate biography.", "Nineteenth-century biographies: Southey's Life of Nelson (1813), Lockhart's Life of Scott (1837-38), Elizabeth Gaskell's The Life of Charlotte Bronte (1857), John Forster's Life of Charles Dickens (1872-74).", "Lytton Strachey's Eminent Victorians (1918) and Queen Victoria (1921) began the ironic 'new biography'.", "Spiritual autobiography: Augustine's Confessions (c. 400), Bunyan's Grace Abounding (1666); Rousseau's Confessions (1782-89) is the model of secular confession.", "English autobiographies: Gibbon's Memoirs (1796), Mill's Autobiography (1873), Newman's Apologia (1864), Ruskin's Praeterita, Edmund Gosse's Father and Son (1907).", "The word 'autobiography' entered English around 1797-1809 (Southey used it in 1809).", "Diaries: Samuel Pepys (1660-69, in shorthand, published 1825), John Evelyn (published 1818), Fanny Burney.", "Hagiography = saints' lives; memoir focuses on events and others, autobiography on the self's whole life."]}, {"title": "Travel Writing and Periodicals", "points": ["The Travels of Sir John Mandeville (c. 1357), largely fictional; Hakluyt's The Principal Navigations (1589, enlarged 1598-1600); Purchas his Pilgrimes (1625).", "Thomas Coryat, Coryat's Crudities (1611); he later walked to India and died at Surat (1617).", "Sterne's A Sentimental Journey through France and Italy (1768), narrator Yorick.", "Lady Mary Wortley Montagu's Turkish Embassy Letters (1763); Mary Wollstonecraft's Letters Written during a Short Residence in Sweden, Norway, and Denmark (1796).", "Victorian travel: A. W. Kinglake's Eothen (1844); Richard Burton's Pilgrimage to Al-Madinah and Meccah (1855-56); C. M. Doughty's Travels in Arabia Deserta (1888); R. L. Stevenson's Travels with a Donkey in the Cevennes (1879).", "Modern travel: D. H. Lawrence's Sea and Sardinia (1921), Robert Byron's The Road to Oxiana (1937), Graham Greene's The Lawless Roads (1939), Paul Theroux's The Great Railway Bazaar (1975), Bruce Chatwin's In Patagonia (1977).", "The Edinburgh Review (1802; Francis Jeffrey, Sydney Smith, Henry Brougham), Whig; Jeffrey's review of The Excursion began 'This will never do.'", "The Quarterly Review (1809, William Gifford), Tory; J. W. Croker's harsh review of Keats's Endymion (1818).", "Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine (1817); J. G. Lockhart as 'Z' attacked the 'Cockney School of Poetry'. The London Magazine (1820, John Scott) published Lamb, Hazlitt and De Quincey.", "The Westminster Review (1824), founded by Bentham for the Philosophical Radicals; Fraser's Magazine (1830) serialised Sartor Resartus.", "Dickens's Household Words (1850) and All the Year Round (1859); Punch (1841); The Cornhill Magazine (1860, first editor Thackeray) serialised Unto This Last; The Fortnightly Review (1865).", "Twentieth century: The Egoist (1914), T. S. Eliot's The Criterion (1922-39), F. R. Leavis's Scrutiny (1932-53), Cyril Connolly's Horizon (1940-49)."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Essay", "pron": "एस्से (ES-ay)", "hindi": "किसी एक विषय पर छोटा गद्य लेख, लेखक के विचारों के साथ", "net": "A short prose composition on a single subject, from French essai ('attempt'), named by Montaigne's Essais (1580) and introduced into English by Bacon (1597)."}, {"term": "Aphorism", "pron": "ऐफ़-अ-रिज़्म (AF-uh-riz-um)", "hindi": "छोटी, चुभती हुई सूक्ति जिसमें गहरी बात हो", "net": "A concise, pointed statement of a general truth or principle; Bacon's Essays are celebrated for aphoristic sentences."}, {"term": "Familiar Essay", "pron": "फ़-मिल-यर एस्से (fuh-MIL-yer ES-ay)", "hindi": "हल्का-फुल्का, बातचीत जैसा निजी निबंध", "net": "An informal, personal, conversational essay revealing the writer's personality and moods; perfected by Lamb, Hazlitt and Leigh Hunt."}, {"term": "Periodical Essay", "pron": "पी-रि-ऑ-डि-कल एस्से (peer-ee-OD-i-kul ES-ay)", "hindi": "अख़बार/पत्रिका में नियमित छपने वाला निबंध", "net": "A short essay published in a regular periodical issue, on manners, morals and taste, typical of The Tatler (1709) and The Spectator (1711)."}, {"term": "Persona", "pron": "पर-सो-ना (per-SOH-nuh)", "hindi": "लेखक का गढ़ा हुआ नकली नाम/रूप जो बोलता है", "net": "The 'mask' or assumed character through whom an author speaks, e.g. Isaac Bickerstaff, Mr. Spectator, Elia."}, {"term": "Pseudonym", "pron": "स्यू-डो-निम (SOO-duh-nim)", "hindi": "उपनाम, लेखक का बदला हुआ नाम", "net": "A fictitious name adopted by an author, e.g. Elia (Lamb), Democritus Junior (Burton), George Orwell (Eric Blair)."}, {"term": "Euphuism", "pron": "यू-फ़्यू-इज़्म (YOO-fyoo-iz-um)", "hindi": "बहुत सजी-धजी, अलंकारों से भरी गद्य शैली", "net": "An elaborate prose style of antithesis, alliteration and learned similes, from John Lyly's Euphues: The Anatomy of Wit (1578)."}, {"term": "Ciceronian Style", "pron": "सि-स-रो-नि-यन स्टाइल (sis-uh-ROH-nee-un)", "hindi": "लंबे, संतुलित, घुमावदार वाक्यों वाली शैली", "net": "A prose style modelled on Cicero, using long, balanced periodic sentences; contrasted with the Senecan or Attic style."}, {"term": "Senecan Style", "pron": "से-ने-कन स्टाइल (SEN-uh-kun)", "hindi": "छोटे, कसे हुए, चुटीले वाक्यों वाली शैली", "net": "A terse, pointed, 'curt' prose style modelled on Seneca, favoured by Bacon and Jonson; also called Attic style."}, {"term": "Baroque Prose", "pron": "ब-रोक प्रोज़ (buh-ROHK prohz)", "hindi": "भव्य, अलंकृत, लय-भरी 17वीं सदी की गद्य शैली", "net": "Ornate, rhythmic, Latinate seventeenth-century prose rich in conceits and digressions, as in Browne and Burton."}, {"term": "Character (Theophrastan)", "pron": "थी-ओ-फ़्रास-टन कैरेक्टर (thee-oh-FRAS-tun)", "hindi": "किसी एक प्रकार के इंसान का छोटा शब्द-चित्र", "net": "A brief prose sketch of a human type, after Theophrastus' Characters; English examples by Hall (1608), Overbury (1614) and Earle (1628)."}, {"term": "Polemic", "pron": "प-ले-मिक (puh-LEM-ik)", "hindi": "किसी मत के ख़िलाफ़ तीखा वाद-विवादी लेखन", "net": "A forceful written attack on, or controversy over, an opinion or doctrine, e.g. Milton's anti-prelatical tracts."}, {"term": "Tract", "pron": "ट्रैक्ट (trakt)", "hindi": "धार्मिक/राजनीतिक विषय पर छोटी पुस्तिका", "net": "A short treatise or pamphlet, usually religious or political, as in Milton's prose and the Tracts for the Times (1833-41)."}, {"term": "Pamphlet", "pron": "पैम्फ़-लेट (PAMF-lit)", "hindi": "बिना जिल्द की छोटी प्रचार-पुस्तिका", "net": "A short unbound publication on a topical issue, used for political and religious argument (Milton, Swift, Defoe, Carlyle's Latter-Day Pamphlets)."}, {"term": "Licensing (Censorship)", "pron": "लाइ-सन-सिंग (LY-sun-sing)", "hindi": "छपने से पहले सरकारी अनुमति की पाबंदी", "net": "Pre-publication censorship requiring official approval; the Licensing Order of 1643 provoked Milton's Areopagitica (1644)."}, {"term": "Coffee-house", "pron": "कॉफ़ी हाउस (KOF-ee hows)", "hindi": "18वीं सदी के बैठक-स्थल जहाँ लोग अख़बार पढ़ते और बहस करते", "net": "Eighteenth-century London meeting places (Will's, Button's) central to the public sphere and to the Tatler and Spectator readership."}, {"term": "Augustan Age", "pron": "ऑ-गस-टन एज (aw-GUS-tun)", "hindi": "अंग्रेज़ी साहित्य का क्लासिकी, नियम-प्रिय दौर (लगभग 1700-1745)", "net": "The period of Pope, Swift, Addison and Steele, named after Augustus' Rome, valuing reason, decorum and classical correctness."}, {"term": "Johnsonese", "pron": "जॉन-सन-ईज़ (jon-suh-NEEZ)", "hindi": "जॉनसन जैसी भारी, लैटिन-शब्दों वाली संतुलित शैली", "net": "A heavy, Latinate, antithetical and balanced style associated with Samuel Johnson's Rambler essays."}, {"term": "Impassioned Prose", "pron": "इम-पै-शन्ड प्रोज़ (im-PASH-und prohz)", "hindi": "कविता जैसी भावुक, संगीतमय गद्य शैली", "net": "De Quincey's term for his poetic, rhythmic, dream-laden prose, as in Suspiria de Profundis (1845)."}, {"term": "Literature of Knowledge / Power", "pron": "लिट-रे-चर ऑफ़ पावर (LIT-ruh-chur uv POW-er)", "hindi": "ज्ञान देने वाला साहित्य बनाम दिल को हिलाने वाला साहित्य", "net": "De Quincey's distinction: the function of the first is to teach, of the second to move; set out in 'Letters to a Young Man' (1823) and his essay on Pope (1848)."}, {"term": "Gusto", "pron": "गस-टो (GUS-toh)", "hindi": "किसी चीज़ में भरी हुई जोशीली जान/तीव्रता", "net": "Hazlitt's term (essay 'On Gusto', 1816, in The Round Table) for 'power or passion defining any object' in art."}, {"term": "Sage Writing", "pron": "सेज राइटिंग (sayj)", "hindi": "समाज को नैतिक उपदेश देने वाला विक्टोरियन गद्य", "net": "Victorian prose of moral and social prophecy (Carlyle, Ruskin, Arnold, Newman); term popularised by John Holloway, The Victorian Sage (1953)."}, {"term": "Carlylese", "pron": "कार-लाइल-ईज़ (kar-ly-LEEZ)", "hindi": "कार्लाइल की जर्मन-प्रभावित, गरजती हुई शैली", "net": "Carlyle's idiosyncratic style: Germanic compounds, capitalised abstractions, exclamations and prophetic rhetoric, as in Sartor Resartus."}, {"term": "Cash Nexus", "pron": "कैश नेक्सस (kash NEK-sus)", "hindi": "इंसानी रिश्तों को सिर्फ़ पैसे से जोड़ना", "net": "Carlyle's phrase (Chartism 1839; Past and Present 1843) for relations reduced to monetary payment."}, {"term": "Pathetic Fallacy", "pron": "प-थे-टिक फ़ै-ल-सी (puh-THET-ik FAL-uh-see)", "hindi": "प्रकृति पर इंसानी भावनाएँ थोपना", "net": "Ruskin's term (Modern Painters, vol. III, 1856) for attributing human emotions to nature, e.g. 'the cruel, crawling foam'."}, {"term": "Philistine", "pron": "फ़ि-लि-स्टाइन (FIL-uh-styn)", "hindi": "संस्कृति से कटा, केवल पैसा-मुनाफ़ा देखने वाला", "net": "Arnold's term in Culture and Anarchy (1869) for the materialistic, uncultured middle class (borrowed from German Philister)."}, {"term": "Hebraism and Hellenism", "pron": "ही-ब्रे-इज़्म, हे-ले-निज़्म (HEE-bray-iz-um, HEL-uh-niz-um)", "hindi": "नैतिक कठोरता बनाम स्वच्छंद बौद्धिक सौंदर्य", "net": "Arnold's two forces in Culture and Anarchy: Hebraism = 'strictness of conscience', Hellenism = 'spontaneity of consciousness'."}, {"term": "Sweetness and Light", "pron": "स्वीट-नेस एंड लाइट (SWEET-nis and lyt)", "hindi": "सौंदर्य और बुद्धि, संस्कृति के दो गुण", "net": "Beauty and intelligence as the aims of culture; phrase from Swift's The Battle of the Books (1704), used by Arnold in Culture and Anarchy."}, {"term": "Touchstone", "pron": "टच-स्टोन (TUCH-stohn)", "hindi": "कसौटी: महान पंक्तियाँ जिनसे दूसरी कविता परखी जाए", "net": "Arnold's method in 'The Study of Poetry' (1880) of judging poetry against short passages from the great masters (Homer, Dante, Shakespeare, Milton)."}, {"term": "High Seriousness", "pron": "हाई सी-रि-यस-नेस (hy SEER-ee-us-nis)", "hindi": "गहरी, गंभीर नैतिक सच्चाई का भाव", "net": "Arnold's criterion (from Aristotle's spoudaiotes) in 'The Study of Poetry'; he found Chaucer and Burns lacking in it."}, {"term": "Disinterestedness", "pron": "डिस-इन-ट्र-स्टेड-नेस (dis-IN-tris-tid-nis)", "hindi": "निष्पक्षता, निजी या दलगत स्वार्थ से ऊपर उठना", "net": "Arnold's ideal for criticism in 'The Function of Criticism at the Present Time' (1864): free from practical, political or sectarian ends."}, {"term": "Aestheticism", "pron": "एस-थे-टि-सिज़्म (es-THET-i-siz-um)", "hindi": "कला सिर्फ़ सुंदरता के लिए, उपदेश के लिए नहीं", "net": "The late-nineteenth-century movement holding that art needs no moral or social justification; associated with Pater and Wilde."}, {"term": "Art for Art's Sake", "pron": "आर्ट फ़ॉर आर्ट्स सेक (l'art pour l'art)", "hindi": "कला का मक़सद ख़ुद कला है", "net": "Slogan of aestheticism; from French 'l'art pour l'art', popularised by Gautier (preface to Mademoiselle de Maupin, 1835)."}, {"term": "Decadence", "pron": "डे-क-डेंस (DEK-uh-duns)", "hindi": "1890s का पतनशील, कृत्रिमता-प्रेमी साहित्यिक रुझान", "net": "The 1890s movement prizing artifice, sensation and refinement (Wilde, Beardsley, The Yellow Book), following Huysmans and Baudelaire."}, {"term": "Tractarianism", "pron": "ट्रैक-टे-रि-य-निज़्म (trak-TAIR-ee-uh-niz-um)", "hindi": "ऑक्सफ़र्ड मूवमेंट, चर्च में कैथलिक परंपरा लौटाने का आंदोलन", "net": "The Oxford Movement (1833-45) of Newman, Keble and Pusey, named from the Tracts for the Times."}, {"term": "Apologia", "pron": "ऐ-पो-लो-जिया (ap-uh-LOH-jee-uh)", "hindi": "माफ़ी नहीं, अपने विचारों/जीवन का बचाव", "net": "A formal written defence of one's opinions or conduct, e.g. Newman's Apologia Pro Vita Sua (1864)."}, {"term": "Utilitarianism", "pron": "यू-टि-लि-टे-रि-य-निज़्म (yoo-til-i-TAIR-ee-uh-niz-um)", "hindi": "अधिकतम लोगों का अधिकतम सुख वाला सिद्धांत", "net": "The doctrine of Bentham and J. S. Mill that right action produces 'the greatest happiness of the greatest number'; criticised by Carlyle, Ruskin and Dickens."}, {"term": "Biography", "pron": "बाइ-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (by-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "किसी दूसरे व्यक्ति की जीवन-कथा", "net": "A written account of another person's life; the word appears in Dryden's Life of Plutarch (1683), defined as 'the history of particular men's lives'."}, {"term": "Autobiography", "pron": "ऑ-टो-बाइ-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (aw-toh-by-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "अपनी जीवन-कथा ख़ुद लिखना, आत्मकथा", "net": "A person's account of his or her own life; the English word came into use c. 1797-1809 (Southey, 1809)."}, {"term": "Memoir", "pron": "मेम-वार (MEM-wahr)", "hindi": "संस्मरण: जीवन की कुछ घटनाओं/लोगों की यादें", "net": "An account of part of one's life focused on events and people witnessed rather than on the full inner development of the self."}, {"term": "Hagiography", "pron": "है-गी-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (hag-ee-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "संतों की जीवनी; या किसी की अंधी तारीफ़ वाली जीवनी", "net": "Writing on the lives of saints; by extension, an uncritically adulatory biography."}, {"term": "Travelogue", "pron": "ट्रै-व-लॉग (TRAV-uh-log)", "hindi": "यात्रा-वृत्तांत", "net": "A narrative of travel and of places and peoples encountered, e.g. Sterne's A Sentimental Journey (1768) or Naipaul's An Area of Darkness (1964)."}, {"term": "New Biography", "pron": "न्यू बाइ-ऑ-ग्र-फ़ी (nyoo by-OG-ruh-fee)", "hindi": "व्यंग्यपूर्ण, चुनिंदा, व्यक्तित्व पर ज़ोर देने वाली आधुनिक जीवनी", "net": "Early twentieth-century ironic, selective biography begun by Strachey's Eminent Victorians (1918); named in Woolf's essay 'The New Biography' (1927)."}, {"term": "Angel in the House", "pron": "एंजल इन द हाउस (AYN-jul in the hows)", "hindi": "आदर्श, त्यागी, घरेलू औरत की छवि", "net": "The self-sacrificing Victorian ideal of womanhood, from Coventry Patmore's poem (1854-62), which Woolf urges women writers to kill in 'Professions for Women' (1931)."}, {"term": "Androgynous Mind", "pron": "ऐन-ड्रॉ-जि-नस माइंड (an-DROJ-uh-nus)", "hindi": "ऐसा मन जिसमें स्त्री और पुरुष दोनों गुण मिले हों", "net": "Woolf's ideal, taken from Coleridge, in A Room of One's Own (1929): the great mind is 'man-womanly' and 'woman-manly'."}, {"term": "Satyagraha", "pron": "सत्याग्रह (sut-YAA-gruh-huh)", "hindi": "सत्य और अहिंसा पर टिका प्रतिरोध", "net": "Gandhi's term ('holding firmly to truth') for non-violent resistance, coined in South Africa (1906-08); see Satyagraha in South Africa (1928)."}, {"term": "Sarvodaya", "pron": "सर्वोदय (sur-VOH-duh-yuh)", "hindi": "सबका उदय, सबका कल्याण", "net": "'Welfare of all': Gandhi's title for his Gujarati paraphrase (1908) of Ruskin's Unto This Last."}, {"term": "Swaraj", "pron": "स्वराज (swuh-RAAJ)", "hindi": "अपना राज, आत्म-शासन", "net": "Self-rule; in Gandhi's Hind Swaraj (1909) it means moral self-rule as well as political independence."}, {"term": "Dalit Autobiography", "pron": "दलित आत्मकथा (DUH-lit)", "hindi": "जाति-उत्पीड़न का अनुभव बताने वाली आत्मकथा", "net": "Life-writing by Dalit authors testifying to caste oppression, e.g. Baluta (1978), Akkarmashi (1984), Karukku (1992), Joothan (1997); often read as testimonio."}, {"term": "Bloomsbury Group", "pron": "ब्लूम्ज़-बरी ग्रुप (BLOOMZ-buh-ree)", "hindi": "लंदन के बुद्धिजीवियों-कलाकारों का समूह", "net": "An early twentieth-century circle of writers and intellectuals including Virginia and Leonard Woolf, Lytton Strachey, E. M. Forster, Roger Fry and J. M. Keynes."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "'Some books are to be tasted, others to be swallowed, and some few to be chewed and digested' occurs in Bacon's essay:", "options": ["Of Truth", "Of Studies", "Of Friendship", "Of Adversity"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The line is from Of Studies."}, {"q": "Arrange the editions of Bacon's Essays in chronological order with the number of essays: (i) 58 essays (ii) 10 essays (iii) 38 essays", "options": ["(ii), (iii), (i)", "(i), (ii), (iii)", "(iii), (ii), (i)", "(ii), (i), (iii)"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1597 had 10, 1612 had 38, 1625 had 58 essays."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of Bacon's four Idols in Novum Organum?", "options": ["Idols of the Tribe", "Idols of the Cave", "Idols of the Market-place", "Idols of the Temple"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The four are Tribe, Cave, Market-place and Theatre."}, {"q": "Religio Medici, Pseudodoxia Epidemica and Hydriotaphia were written by:", "options": ["Robert Burton", "Jeremy Taylor", "Sir Thomas Browne", "Thomas Fuller"], "answer": 2, "exp": "All three are by Sir Thomas Browne of Norwich."}, {"q": "'Who kills a man kills a reasonable creature, God's image; but he who destroys a good book, kills reason itself.' This is from:", "options": ["Of Education", "Areopagitica", "Eikonoklastes", "The Tenure of Kings and Magistrates"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Milton's Areopagitica (1644) argues against pre-publication licensing."}, {"q": "Milton's Eikonoklastes (1649) was a reply to:", "options": ["Eikon Basilike", "Leviathan", "Defensio Regia", "The Anatomy of Melancholy"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Eikon Basilike was the royalist book attributed to Charles I."}, {"q": "Match the persona with the periodical: (a) Isaac Bickerstaff (b) Mr. Spectator (c) Lien Chi Altangi (d) Elia || (i) The Spectator (ii) The Citizen of the World (iii) The Tatler (iv) London Magazine", "options": ["a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv", "a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii", "a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Bickerstaff - Tatler; Mr. Spectator - Spectator; Lien Chi Altangi - Goldsmith; Elia - London Magazine."}, {"q": "Which member of the Spectator Club represents the Whig commercial interest?", "options": ["Sir Roger de Coverley", "Will Honeycomb", "Sir Andrew Freeport", "Captain Sentry"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Sir Andrew Freeport is the London merchant; Sir Roger is the Tory squire."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Addison wanted to bring philosophy 'out of closets and libraries' to 'tea-tables and coffee-houses'. Reason (R): The Spectator aimed at the moral and cultural education of the middle-class reading public.", "options": ["Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Spectator No. 10 states the aim, which follows from its educative purpose."}, {"q": "Johnson's letter to Lord Chesterfield (1755) concerns:", "options": ["A Dictionary of the English Language", "Rasselas", "The Rambler", "Lives of the Poets"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Chesterfield offered patronage only when the Dictionary was about to appear."}, {"q": "In which work does Johnson describe metaphysical wit as 'heterogeneous ideas yoked by violence together'?", "options": ["Preface to Shakespeare", "Life of Savage", "The Idler", "Life of Cowley"], "answer": 3, "exp": "It is in the Life of Cowley in Lives of the Poets (1779-81)."}, {"q": "'Dream-Children: A Reverie' and 'A Dissertation upon Roast Pig' are essays by:", "options": ["Hazlitt", "Leigh Hunt", "Charles Lamb", "De Quincey"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Both are Essays of Elia by Lamb (1823)."}, {"q": "Which of the following essays belongs to The Last Essays of Elia (1833)?", "options": ["The South-Sea House", "Old China", "Dream-Children", "Christ's Hospital Five and Thirty Years Ago"], "answer": 1, "exp": "'Old China' is in the 1833 collection; the others are in Essays of Elia (1823)."}, {"q": "The Spirit of the Age (1825), a series of portraits of contemporaries, was written by:", "options": ["William Hazlitt", "Thomas Carlyle", "Thomas De Quincey", "J. S. Mill"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Hazlitt's The Spirit of the Age profiles Bentham, Coleridge, Wordsworth and others."}, {"q": "The 'Ladies of Sorrow' - Mater Lachrymarum, Mater Suspiriorum and Mater Tenebrarum - appear in De Quincey's:", "options": ["The English Mail-Coach", "On Murder Considered as One of the Fine Arts", "Confessions of an English Opium-Eater", "Suspiria de Profundis"], "answer": 3, "exp": "'Levana and Our Ladies of Sorrow' is part of Suspiria de Profundis (1845)."}, {"q": "The distinction between 'literature of knowledge' and 'literature of power' was made by:", "options": ["Hazlitt", "Arnold", "De Quincey", "Pater"], "answer": 2, "exp": "De Quincey: the first teaches, the second moves."}, {"q": "Diogenes Teufelsdrockh, 'Professor of Things in General', is the hero of:", "options": ["Past and Present", "Sartor Resartus", "Heroes and Hero-Worship", "Chartism"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Sartor Resartus (1833-34) presents his philosophy of clothes."}, {"q": "In On Heroes, Hero-Worship and the Heroic in History, Carlyle presents Johnson, Rousseau and Burns as the Hero as:", "options": ["Man of Letters", "Priest", "Poet", "King"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Dante and Shakespeare are Poets; Johnson, Rousseau and Burns are Men of Letters."}, {"q": "The term 'pathetic fallacy' was coined by Ruskin in:", "options": ["The Stones of Venice", "Sesame and Lilies", "Unto This Last", "Modern Painters, vol. III"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Ruskin coined it in Modern Painters III (1856)."}, {"q": "Arrange Ruskin's works chronologically: (i) Unto This Last (ii) The Seven Lamps of Architecture (iii) Sesame and Lilies (iv) Praeterita", "options": ["(ii), (iii), (i), (iv)", "(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)", "(ii), (i), (iii), (iv)", "(iii), (ii), (i), (iv)"], "answer": 2, "exp": "1849, 1860-62, 1865, 1885-89."}, {"q": "Which work of Ruskin did Gandhi paraphrase in Gujarati as Sarvodaya?", "options": ["Fors Clavigera", "Unto This Last", "The Crown of Wild Olive", "Munera Pulveris"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Gandhi's Sarvodaya (1908) is based on Unto This Last."}, {"q": "In Culture and Anarchy, Arnold calls the middle class:", "options": ["Philistines", "Barbarians", "Populace", "Hellenes"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Barbarians = aristocracy, Philistines = middle class, Populace = working class."}, {"q": "Arnold borrowed the phrase 'sweetness and light' from:", "options": ["Carlyle's Sartor Resartus", "Pope's Essay on Criticism", "Addison's Spectator", "Swift's The Battle of the Books"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Swift used it in the bees-and-spider fable of The Battle of the Books."}, {"q": "'To burn always with this hard, gem-like flame, to maintain this ecstasy, is success in life.' This is from the Conclusion to:", "options": ["Wilde's Intentions", "Ruskin's Modern Painters", "Pater's The Renaissance", "Symons' The Symbolist Movement in Literature"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Pater's Studies in the History of the Renaissance (1873)."}, {"q": "Newman's Apologia Pro Vita Sua (1864) was written in response to an attack by:", "options": ["Thomas Arnold", "Charles Kingsley", "John Keble", "T. H. Huxley"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Kingsley questioned Newman's truthfulness, provoking the Apologia."}, {"q": "'On or about December 1910 human character changed' is from Woolf's:", "options": ["Mr. Bennett and Mrs. Brown", "A Room of One's Own", "Modern Fiction", "Three Guineas"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Mr. Bennett and Mrs. Brown (1924) contrasts Edwardians and Georgians."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of Orwell's four motives in 'Why I Write'?", "options": ["Sheer egoism", "Aesthetic enthusiasm", "Historical impulse", "Religious conviction"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The fourth motive is political purpose."}, {"q": "Match the work with the author: (a) Hind Swaraj (b) The Discovery of India (c) Annihilation of Caste (d) Nationalism || (i) Ambedkar (ii) Tagore (iii) Gandhi (iv) Nehru", "options": ["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Gandhi 1909, Nehru 1946, Ambedkar 1936, Tagore 1917."}, {"q": "Amartya Sen's distinction between 'niti' and 'nyaya' is developed in:", "options": ["Development as Freedom", "The Idea of Justice", "The Argumentative Indian", "Identity and Violence"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The Idea of Justice (2009) contrasts niti (institutions) and nyaya (realised justice)."}, {"q": "Lytton Strachey's Eminent Victorians (1918) contains biographical essays on all EXCEPT:", "options": ["John Henry Newman", "Florence Nightingale", "Dr Thomas Arnold", "Cardinal Manning"], "answer": 0, "exp": "The four subjects are Manning, Nightingale, Dr Arnold and General Gordon."}]}, {"id": 5, "title": "Language: Basic concepts, theories and pedagogy. English in Use.", "overview": "Is unit mein linguistics ke basic concepts (Saussure, Chomsky, Halliday, speech acts, Grice), phonetics se semantics tak, sociolinguistics, history of English, ELT methods aur testing, aur grammar-usage aate hain. NET mein yahan se zyadatar 'kisne kaha/kis book mein', match-the-following, chronology aur sahi-galat sentence wale direct sawal aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Ferdinand de Saussure and Structuralism", "points": ["Saussure (1857-1913), Swiss linguist, is called the father of modern linguistics and of structuralism.", "Course in General Linguistics (Cours de linguistique generale, 1916) was published posthumously, compiled from students' lecture notes by Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye.", "Langue = the abstract, shared system of a language; Parole = the individual, concrete act of speaking (langue = niyam-vyavastha, parole = asli bolna). Langage = the general human faculty of language.", "The linguistic sign has two sides: signifier (sound-image) and signified (concept); their relation is arbitrary.", "Language is a system of differences: 'In language there are only differences without positive terms.'", "Synchronic study = language at one point in time; diachronic study = language change over time. Saussure gave priority to synchronic study.", "Syntagmatic relations = linear, chain relations (horizontal); paradigmatic relations (Saussure called them associative) = relations of substitution (vertical).", "Saussure's comparison: language is like a game of chess - value of a piece depends on its position in the system.", "He proposed 'semiology' (semiologie), a science of signs; C. S. Peirce independently developed 'semiotics' (icon, index, symbol).", "American structuralism: Edward Sapir, Language (1921); Leonard Bloomfield, Language (1933), behaviourist, immediate constituent analysis; Prague School (Trubetzkoy, Jakobson) developed functional phonology."]}, {"title": "Noam Chomsky and Generative Grammar", "points": ["Syntactic Structures (1957) launched Transformational Generative Grammar (TGG) - the 'Chomskyan revolution'.", "Aspects of the Theory of Syntax (1965) gave the 'Standard Theory': competence vs performance, deep structure vs surface structure.", "Competence = speaker's unconscious knowledge of the language; Performance = actual use in concrete situations (compare Saussure's langue/parole).", "Example sentence 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously' shows a sentence can be grammatical yet meaningless (Syntactic Structures, 1957).", "'A Review of B. F. Skinner's Verbal Behavior' (1959, in Language) attacked behaviourist accounts of language learning.", "Innateness hypothesis: children have a Language Acquisition Device (LAD) and Universal Grammar (UG); 'poverty of the stimulus' argument.", "Later models: Extended Standard Theory (1970s), Government and Binding Theory - Lectures on Government and Binding (1981), Principles and Parameters, The Minimalist Program (1995).", "Other works: Cartesian Linguistics (1966), Language and Mind (1968), The Sound Pattern of English (1968, with Morris Halle).", "Chomsky's grammar is formal and mentalist; it is criticised by Dell Hymes, who proposed 'communicative competence' (1972).", "Three levels of adequacy: observational, descriptive and explanatory adequacy."]}, {"title": "M. A. K. Halliday and Functional Linguistics", "points": ["Halliday (1925-2018), pupil of J. R. Firth, founded Systemic Functional Linguistics (SFL); language is a 'social semiotic'.", "Works: Learning How to Mean (1975), Language as Social Semiotic (1978), An Introduction to Functional Grammar (1985), Cohesion in English (1976, with Ruqaiya Hasan).", "Three metafunctions: ideational (experiential + logical), interpersonal, textual.", "Register variables: Field (what is happening), Tenor (who is taking part), Mode (role of language, spoken/written).", "Seven functions of child language (Halliday): instrumental, regulatory, interactional, personal, heuristic, imaginative, representational/informative.", "Cohesion in English names five cohesive devices: reference, substitution, ellipsis, conjunction, lexical cohesion.", "Theme-Rheme: Theme is the starting point of a clause; Rheme is the rest (Prague School, Mathesius, developed by Halliday).", "J. R. Firth (London School) - 'You shall know a word by the company it keeps' (1957); context of situation (from Malinowski).", "Bronislaw Malinowski coined 'phatic communion' (1923) - talk for social contact, e.g. 'Nice weather!'.", "Roman Jakobson's six functions of language ('Linguistics and Poetics', 1960): emotive, conative, referential, poetic, phatic, metalingual."]}, {"title": "Speech Act Theory: Austin and Searle", "points": ["J. L. Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962), from William James Lectures at Harvard (1955).", "Austin first distinguished constatives (true/false statements) and performatives (utterances that do an act, e.g. 'I name this ship...').", "Three acts: locutionary (saying with meaning), illocutionary (force - promising, warning), perlocutionary (effect on hearer - persuading, frightening).", "Felicity conditions: conditions that must be met for a performative to succeed; failures are 'infelicities' (misfires and abuses).", "Austin's five classes of illocutionary verbs: verdictives, exercitives, commissives, behabitives, expositives.", "John R. Searle, Speech Acts: An Essay in the Philosophy of Language (1969); 'Indirect Speech Acts' (1975); Expression and Meaning (1979).", "Searle's taxonomy: representatives (assertives), directives, commissives, expressives, declarations.", "Indirect speech act: 'Can you pass the salt?' - question form, request function.", "Searle's felicity conditions: propositional content, preparatory, sincerity and essential conditions.", "Derrida criticised Austin in 'Signature Event Context' (1972), leading to the Derrida-Searle debate."]}, {"title": "Grice, Politeness and Pragmatics", "points": ["Charles Morris (Foundations of the Theory of Signs, 1938) divided semiotics into syntax, semantics and pragmatics.", "H. P. Grice, 'Logic and Conversation' (1975; William James Lectures, Harvard, 1967); earlier essay 'Meaning' (1957).", "Cooperative Principle: 'Make your conversational contribution such as is required, at the stage at which it occurs...'", "Four maxims: Quantity (be as informative as required), Quality (be truthful), Relation (be relevant), Manner (be clear, brief, orderly).", "Flouting a maxim openly creates conversational implicature; violating secretly misleads (jhooth bolna = violation).", "Conventional implicature is tied to words like 'but', 'even'; conversational implicature depends on context.", "Geoffrey Leech, Principles of Pragmatics (1983): Politeness Principle with maxims of tact, generosity, approbation, modesty, agreement, sympathy.", "Brown and Levinson, Politeness: Some Universals in Language Usage (1978/1987): positive face, negative face, face-threatening acts (FTAs); 'face' from Erving Goffman.", "Deixis: person (I, you), place (here, there), time (now, then), social and discourse deixis.", "Relevance Theory: Dan Sperber and Deirdre Wilson, Relevance: Communication and Cognition (1986)."]}, {"title": "Phonetics", "points": ["Branches: articulatory (production), acoustic (physical sound waves), auditory (perception).", "International Phonetic Association founded in Paris (1886); the IPA chart is used to transcribe sounds.", "Received Pronunciation (RP) has 44 phonemes: 24 consonants and 20 vowels (12 pure vowels/monophthongs + 8 diphthongs).", "RP has 7 short vowels and 5 long vowels; 8 diphthongs: /eɪ, aɪ, ɔɪ, aʊ, əʊ, ɪə, eə, ʊə/ (centring and closing).", "Places of articulation: bilabial (p, b, m), labiodental (f, v), dental (th sounds), alveolar (t, d, s, z, n, l), post-alveolar, palatal (j), velar (k, g, ng), glottal (h).", "Manners of articulation: plosive/stop, fricative, affricate (ch, j as in church, judge), nasal, lateral (l), approximant (r, w, j).", "Voiced sounds vibrate the vocal cords (b, d, g, v, z); voiceless do not (p, t, k, f, s).", "Schwa /ə/ is the most frequent vowel in English, found in weak/unstressed syllables (e.g. first vowel of 'about').", "Daniel Jones devised the Cardinal Vowels and wrote English Pronouncing Dictionary (1917) and An Outline of English Phonetics (1918); A. C. Gimson wrote An Introduction to the Pronunciation of English (1962).", "English is a stress-timed language (stress comes at regular intervals); French and most Indian languages are syllable-timed.", "Syllable structure: onset + rhyme (nucleus + coda); nucleus is usually a vowel; English allows up to 3 consonants in onset (e.g. 'strengths' has a complex coda)."]}, {"title": "Phonology", "points": ["Phonology studies the sound system/patterns of a language; phonetics studies speech sounds physically.", "Phoneme = smallest contrastive sound unit that changes meaning; written between slashes /p/.", "Allophones = variants of one phoneme, in square brackets: aspirated [ph] in 'pin' vs unaspirated [p] in 'spin'.", "Minimal pair = two words differing in one sound only: pin/bin, ship/sheep.", "Complementary distribution = allophones never occur in the same environment; free variation = interchangeable without meaning change.", "Connected speech processes: assimilation ('ten boys' -> tem boys), elision (dropping sounds, 'next day'), linking r and intrusive r, juncture.", "Suprasegmental features: stress, pitch, intonation, rhythm, tone, length, juncture.", "N. S. Trubetzkoy, Principles of Phonology (Grundzuge der Phonologie, 1939) - Prague School; Roman Jakobson developed distinctive features.", "Chomsky and Halle, The Sound Pattern of English (1968) founded generative phonology.", "Phonotactics = rules about permissible sound sequences (English words cannot begin with /ng/)."]}, {"title": "Morphology and Word Formation", "points": ["Morphology studies word structure; the term was introduced into linguistics in the 19th century (from Goethe's biological use).", "Morpheme = smallest meaningful unit; free morphemes stand alone (book), bound morphemes cannot (-s, un-).", "Derivational morphemes create new words/change class (happy -> happiness); inflectional morphemes mark grammar only.", "English has 8 inflectional morphemes: plural -s, possessive -'s, 3rd person singular -s, past -ed, past participle -en, progressive -ing, comparative -er, superlative -est.", "Allomorphs = variants of a morpheme: plural /s/ (cats), /z/ (dogs), /iz/ (buses); zero morpheme in 'sheep'.", "Blending: smog (smoke + fog), brunch, motel; Lewis Carroll called such words 'portmanteau' words in Through the Looking-Glass (1871).", "Clipping: ad (advertisement), flu (influenza), exam; Back-formation: edit (from editor), televise (from television), babysit.", "Acronym is read as a word (NATO, laser, scuba); initialism is read letter by letter (BBC, FBI).", "Conversion (zero derivation/functional shift): noun to verb, e.g. 'to google', 'to bottle'.", "Coinage/eponym: Kleenex, xerox, sandwich (from Earl of Sandwich), boycott; compounding: blackboard, greenhouse."]}, {"title": "Syntax", "points": ["Syntax studies how words combine into phrases, clauses and sentences.", "Immediate Constituent (IC) analysis: Bloomfield (1933) and Rulon Wells ('Immediate Constituents', 1947).", "Phrase Structure (PS) rules: S -> NP + VP; NP -> Det + N; VP -> V + NP (Chomsky, 1957).", "X-bar theory: Chomsky ('Remarks on Nominalization', 1970), developed by Ray Jackendoff (1977).", "Structural ambiguity: 'Flying planes can be dangerous'; 'Old men and women'; 'Visiting relatives can be boring'.", "Quirk et al., A Comprehensive Grammar of the English Language (1985): clause elements S, V, O, C, A and seven basic clause patterns (SV, SVO, SVC, SVA, SVOO, SVOC, SVOA).", "Sentence types by structure: simple, compound (coordination), complex (subordination), compound-complex.", "Sentence types by function: declarative, interrogative, imperative, exclamatory.", "English basic word order is SVO; Hindi is SOV.", "Recursion = a rule can apply repeatedly, producing infinitely long sentences ('the house that Jack built')."]}, {"title": "Semantics", "points": ["Michel Breal coined 'semantique' (1883); Essai de semantique (1897).", "C. K. Ogden and I. A. Richards, The Meaning of Meaning (1923): semiotic/semantic triangle - symbol, thought/reference, referent.", "Geoffrey Leech, Semantics (1974): seven types of meaning - conceptual, connotative, social, affective, reflected, collocative, thematic.", "Sense vs reference: Gottlob Frege, 'On Sense and Reference' (Uber Sinn und Bedeutung, 1892); 'morning star' and 'evening star' have one referent.", "Lexical relations: synonymy, antonymy, hyponymy (rose is hyponym of flower; flower is hypernym/superordinate), meronymy (part-whole), homonymy, polysemy.", "Antonym types: gradable (hot/cold), complementary/binary (alive/dead), converse/relational (buy/sell, husband/wife).", "Homonyms: same form, unrelated meanings (bank of river / bank for money); polysemy: one word, related meanings (head of body / head of department).", "Homophones sound alike (meet/meat); homographs are spelt alike (lead metal / lead verb).", "Entailment: 'He killed the bug' entails 'The bug died'; presupposition survives negation ('His wife is ill' presupposes he has a wife).", "Componential analysis: man = [+HUMAN, +ADULT, +MALE]; Prototype theory: Eleanor Rosch (1970s) - robin is a better example of 'bird' than penguin."]}, {"title": "Sociolinguistics", "points": ["Sociolinguistics studies language in relation to society: class, region, gender, ethnicity, context.", "Dialect = regional/social variety; idiolect = an individual's own speech; sociolect = class variety; register = variety according to use/situation.", "Diglossia: Charles Ferguson, 'Diglossia' (1959) - High (H) and Low (L) varieties in one community (e.g. Arabic, Swiss German); extended by Joshua Fishman (1967).", "Pidgin = contact language with no native speakers; creole = pidgin that becomes a mother tongue of a community (creolisation).", "Code-switching = moving between languages across sentences; code-mixing = mixing within a sentence (Hinglish).", "William Labov, The Social Stratification of English in New York City (1966) - department store study of post-vocalic /r/ (Saks, Macy's, S. Klein).", "Basil Bernstein: restricted code and elaborated code (Class, Codes and Control, 1971).", "Sapir-Whorf hypothesis (linguistic relativity): language shapes thought; strong version = linguistic determinism.", "Braj B. Kachru's Three Concentric Circles of English (1985): Inner (UK, USA), Outer (India, Nigeria), Expanding (China, Japan); The Alchemy of English (1986).", "Peter Trudgill studied Norwich English (1974); Howard Giles proposed Communication Accommodation Theory (convergence/divergence); Robin Lakoff, Language and Woman's Place (1975)."]}, {"title": "History of the English Language", "points": ["English belongs to the West Germanic branch of the Indo-European family; Sir William Jones (1786, Calcutta) noted Sanskrit's kinship with Greek and Latin.", "Periods: Old English (c. 450-1100/1150), Middle English (c. 1100/1150-1500), Early Modern English (1500-1700/1800), Modern English.", "Angles, Saxons and Jutes invaded Britain c. 449 AD (Bede). OE dialects: Northumbrian, Mercian, West Saxon (literary standard under King Alfred), Kentish.", "Scandinavian (Viking) influence gave 'they, them, their', sky, skin, egg, law; Danelaw. Norman Conquest (1066) brought huge French influence.", "English replaced French in law courts by the Statute of Pleading (1362); Chancery Standard shaped written English in the 15th century.", "William Caxton set up the first printing press in England at Westminster (1476).", "Great Vowel Shift (c. 1400-1700): long vowels were raised; term coined by Otto Jespersen.", "Grimm's Law (Jacob Grimm, 1822; first noted by Rasmus Rask, 1818) - First Germanic Consonant Shift: p>f, t>th, k>h (pater/father); Verner's Law (1875) explained exceptions.", "Milestones: King James (Authorised) Version of the Bible (1611); Samuel Johnson's A Dictionary of the English Language (1755); OED first published as A New English Dictionary (1884-1928).", "Key books: Otto Jespersen, Growth and Structure of the English Language (1905); A. C. Baugh, A History of the English Language (1935, later with Thomas Cable).", "Renaissance 'inkhorn terms' controversy: over-use of Latinate words; Sir Thomas Elyot and others defended borrowing."]}, {"title": "ELT Methods: GTM, Direct Method, ALM and Humanistic Methods", "points": ["Grammar Translation Method (GTM, also 'Prussian Method'): rules + translation, mother tongue used, reading/writing focus; associated with Karl Plotz, Seidenstucker, Ollendorf.", "Reform Movement (1880s): Henry Sweet, Wilhelm Vietor ('Language teaching must start afresh', 1882), Paul Passy - priority of speech and phonetics.", "Direct Method (Natural Method): no translation, target language only, inductive grammar; Maximilian Berlitz (Berlitz schools, 1878), Francois Gouin (Series Method), L. Sauveur.", "Oral Approach / Situational Language Teaching (1930s-60s): Harold Palmer, A. S. Hornby; Michael West's New Method Readers and General Service List (1953).", "Audio-Lingual Method (ALM, 'Army Method', WWII): based on structural linguistics and Skinner's behaviourism; habit formation, mim-mem, pattern drills; Charles Fries (Michigan).", "Contrastive Analysis Hypothesis: Robert Lado, Linguistics Across Cultures (1957) - L1-L2 differences predict errors.", "Silent Way: Caleb Gattegno (1963) - teacher silent, Cuisenaire rods, Fidel charts.", "Community Language Learning (CLL): Charles A. Curran - counselling learning; teacher as 'knower', learner as 'client'.", "Suggestopedia: Georgi Lozanov (Bulgaria, 1970s) - relaxation, baroque music, 'desuggestion'.", "Total Physical Response (TPR): James Asher (1960s-77) - commands with body actions.", "Bilingual Method: C. J. Dodson (1967); Structural Approach was popular in Indian schools (Madras 'Snowball' scheme).", "Indian context: Macaulay's Minute on Indian Education (1835); Kothari Commission (1964-66) and the Three-Language Formula (NPE 1968)."]}, {"title": "CLT, TBLT, Krashen and SLA Theories", "points": ["Communicative Language Teaching (CLT, 1970s): fluency over accuracy, meaning-focused; D. A. Wilkins, Notional Syllabuses (1976); H. G. Widdowson, Teaching Language as Communication (1978).", "Dell Hymes coined 'communicative competence' ('On Communicative Competence', 1972); Canale and Swain (1980): grammatical, sociolinguistic, strategic competence; Canale (1983) added discourse competence.", "Task-Based Language Teaching (TBLT): N. S. Prabhu's Bangalore (Communicational Teaching) Project, 1979-84; Second Language Pedagogy (1987) - information-gap, reasoning-gap, opinion-gap tasks.", "Jane Willis, A Framework for Task-Based Learning (1996): pre-task, task cycle (task, planning, report), language focus; David Nunan, Rod Ellis also central to TBLT.", "Stephen Krashen's Monitor Model - five hypotheses: Acquisition-Learning, Monitor, Natural Order, Input (i+1), Affective Filter (Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition, 1982).", "Natural Approach: Krashen and Tracy Terrell (1983).", "Interlanguage: Larry Selinker (1972); fossilization = errors that become permanent.", "Error Analysis: S. Pit Corder, 'The Significance of Learners' Errors' (1967) - errors (competence) vs mistakes (performance).", "Interaction Hypothesis: Michael Long (1981/1996); Output Hypothesis: Merrill Swain (1985); Noticing Hypothesis: Richard Schmidt (1990).", "Vygotsky: Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD); 'scaffolding' term by Wood, Bruner and Ross (1976).", "Lexical Approach: Michael Lewis (1993); Postmethod condition/pedagogy: B. Kumaravadivelu (1994) - particularity, practicality, possibility.", "PPP = Presentation, Practice, Production; ESA = Engage, Study, Activate (Jeremy Harmer)."]}, {"title": "Language Testing and Evaluation", "points": ["Validity = test measures what it claims to measure; types: content, construct, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive), face validity.", "Reliability = consistency of results; measured by test-retest, parallel forms, split-half, inter-rater/intra-rater reliability.", "Practicality (feasibility) and washback/backwash (effect of a test on teaching and learning - positive or negative).", "Test types by purpose: proficiency (TOEFL, IELTS), achievement, diagnostic, placement, aptitude (MLAT by John Carroll and Stanley Sapon, 1959).", "Formative evaluation = during the course for improvement; summative = at the end for grading; CCE = Continuous and Comprehensive Evaluation (CBSE, 2009).", "Norm-referenced tests compare a learner with others; criterion-referenced tests measure against fixed standards.", "Discrete-point testing (Robert Lado, Language Testing, 1961) tests one item at a time; integrative tests (cloze, dictation) test many skills together.", "Cloze procedure: Wilson L. Taylor (1953) - every nth word deleted; John Oller's Unitary Competence Hypothesis supported integrative testing.", "Item analysis: facility value (difficulty index) and discrimination index.", "Key texts: Arthur Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers (1989); Lyle Bachman, Fundamental Considerations in Language Testing (1990); Bloom's Taxonomy (1956)."]}, {"title": "Grammar and Usage (English in Use)", "points": ["Prescriptive grammar tells how one should speak (Robert Lowth, A Short Introduction to English Grammar, 1762; Lindley Murray, 1795); descriptive grammar records actual use.", "Traditional grammar has eight parts of speech: noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction, interjection.", "'Neither...nor' / 'either...or': verb agrees with the nearer subject - 'Neither the teacher nor the students were present.'", "'The number of students is...' (singular) but 'A number of students are...' (plural); 'Each/Every' takes a singular verb.", "'One of the best books that have been written' - verb agrees with plural antecedent 'books'.", "Correlatives: 'No sooner...than', 'Hardly/Scarcely...when', 'Lest...should' (never 'lest...not').", "Inversion after negative adverbials: 'Never have I seen...'; 'Seldom does he come.'", "Conditionals: zero (If you heat ice, it melts), first (If it rains, I will stay), second (If I were rich, I would...), third (If I had known, I would have...).", "Dangling modifier: 'Walking down the road, the tree fell' (wrong); split infinitive: 'to boldly go'.", "Reference works: H. W. Fowler, A Dictionary of Modern English Usage (1926); Quirk, Greenbaum, Leech, Svartvik, A Comprehensive Grammar of the English Language (1985).", "Common errors: 'cousin brother' (Indian English), 'discuss about' (discuss takes no 'about'), 'return back', 'I am having a car' (stative verb in progressive)."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Langue", "pron": "लॉन्ग (LONG, nasal)", "hindi": "किसी भाषा की पूरी नियम-व्यवस्था जो सबके दिमाग में साझी होती है", "net": "The abstract, social system of rules and conventions shared by a speech community; contrasted with parole by Saussure in Course in General Linguistics (1916)."}, {"term": "Parole", "pron": "पै-रोल (pa-ROLL)", "hindi": "किसी व्यक्ति का असल में बोला या लिखा गया वाक्य", "net": "The individual, concrete utterance or act of speech produced by a speaker using the system of langue (Saussure)."}, {"term": "Signifier", "pron": "सिग-नि-फ़ाय-अर (SIG-ni-fy-er)", "hindi": "शब्द की ध्वनि या लिखित रूप", "net": "The sound-image or material form of a linguistic sign; paired with the signified (concept) by Saussure."}, {"term": "Signified", "pron": "सिग-नि-फ़ाइड (SIG-ni-fyd)", "hindi": "शब्द सुनकर मन में आने वाला विचार", "net": "The concept or mental idea to which a signifier refers; together they form the sign, whose relation Saussure called arbitrary."}, {"term": "Arbitrariness", "pron": "आर-बि-ट्रे-री-नेस (AAR-bi-trer-ee-ness)", "hindi": "शब्द और उसके अर्थ के बीच कोई प्राकृतिक रिश्ता न होना", "net": "The principle that there is no natural or necessary link between signifier and signified; a basic design feature of language (Saussure; Hockett)."}, {"term": "Synchronic", "pron": "सिन-क्रॉ-निक (sin-KRON-ik)", "hindi": "किसी एक समय पर भाषा का अध्ययन", "net": "Study of a language as a system at a particular point in time, without reference to its history (Saussure)."}, {"term": "Diachronic", "pron": "डाय-अ-क्रॉ-निक (dy-uh-KRON-ik)", "hindi": "समय के साथ भाषा में आए बदलाव का अध्ययन", "net": "Historical study of how a language changes over time; historical linguistics (Saussure)."}, {"term": "Syntagmatic", "pron": "सिन-टैग-मै-टिक (sin-tag-MAT-ik)", "hindi": "वाक्य में शब्दों का एक के बाद एक जुड़ना", "net": "Linear relations between units that co-occur in a sequence or chain (horizontal axis of language)."}, {"term": "Paradigmatic", "pron": "पै-र-डिग-मै-टिक (pa-ruh-dig-MAT-ik)", "hindi": "एक जगह पर बदले जा सकने वाले शब्दों का संबंध", "net": "Relations of substitution between units that can replace each other in the same position (vertical axis); Saussure's 'associative' relations."}, {"term": "Competence", "pron": "कॉम-पि-टेंस (KOM-pi-tens)", "hindi": "भाषा के नियमों का अंदरूनी, अनजाना ज्ञान", "net": "A native speaker's tacit, internalised knowledge of the grammar of the language; contrasted with performance by Chomsky in Aspects of the Theory of Syntax (1965)."}, {"term": "Performance", "pron": "पर-फ़ॉर-मेंस (per-FOR-mens)", "hindi": "असल स्थिति में भाषा का प्रयोग", "net": "The actual use of language in concrete situations, affected by memory limits, distractions and errors (Chomsky)."}, {"term": "Deep Structure", "pron": "डीप स्ट्रक-चर (deep STRUK-cher)", "hindi": "वाक्य का अंदरूनी, मूल अर्थ वाला ढाँचा", "net": "The underlying abstract syntactic representation of a sentence from which surface structure is derived by transformations (Chomsky, 1965)."}, {"term": "Language Acquisition Device (LAD)", "pron": "लैंग-ग्विज अक्वि-ज़ि-शन डि-वाइस", "hindi": "बच्चे के दिमाग में भाषा सीखने की जन्मजात क्षमता", "net": "A hypothesised innate mental faculty that enables children to acquire language rapidly from limited input (Chomsky)."}, {"term": "Universal Grammar", "pron": "यू-नि-वर-सल ग्रै-मर (yoo-ni-VER-sal GRAM-er)", "hindi": "सभी भाषाओं में पाए जाने वाले साझा मूल नियम", "net": "The set of innate principles and parameters common to all human languages, proposed by Chomsky."}, {"term": "Metafunction", "pron": "मे-टा-फंक-शन (MET-uh-funk-shun)", "hindi": "भाषा के तीन मुख्य काम - अनुभव, संबंध और पाठ बनाना", "net": "In Halliday's Systemic Functional Linguistics, the three broad functions of language: ideational, interpersonal and textual."}, {"term": "Register", "pron": "रे-जिस-टर (REJ-is-ter)", "hindi": "स्थिति के हिसाब से बदली हुई भाषा, जैसे क़ानूनी या डॉक्टरी भाषा", "net": "A variety of language defined by its use in a particular situation, analysed by Halliday in terms of field, tenor and mode."}, {"term": "Cohesion", "pron": "को-ही-ज़न (koh-HEE-zhun)", "hindi": "पाठ के वाक्यों को आपस में जोड़ने वाले शब्दिक साधन", "net": "The grammatical and lexical linking within a text (reference, substitution, ellipsis, conjunction, lexical cohesion); Halliday and Hasan, Cohesion in English (1976)."}, {"term": "Phatic Communion", "pron": "फ़ै-टिक क-म्यू-नि-यन (FAT-ik kuh-MYOO-nyun)", "hindi": "सिर्फ़ रिश्ता बनाए रखने के लिए की गई बातचीत, जैसे 'कैसे हो?'", "net": "Language used to establish social contact rather than convey information; term coined by Bronislaw Malinowski (1923)."}, {"term": "Performative", "pron": "पर-फ़ॉर-म-टिव (per-FOR-muh-tiv)", "hindi": "ऐसा कथन जो बोलते ही कोई काम कर देता है, जैसे 'मैं वादा करता हूँ'", "net": "An utterance that performs an action rather than describes a state of affairs, e.g. 'I promise'; contrasted with constative by J. L. Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962)."}, {"term": "Illocutionary Act", "pron": "इल-लो-क्यू-श-न-री ऐक्ट (il-oh-KYOO-shuh-nuh-ree)", "hindi": "कथन के पीछे का इरादा, जैसे चेतावनी देना या वादा करना", "net": "The act performed in saying something, carrying a particular force such as promising, ordering or warning (Austin)."}, {"term": "Perlocutionary Act", "pron": "पर-लो-क्यू-श-न-री ऐक्ट (per-loh-KYOO-shuh-nuh-ree)", "hindi": "कथन का सुनने वाले पर पड़ने वाला असर", "net": "The consequential effect of an utterance on the hearer, such as persuading, frightening or convincing (Austin)."}, {"term": "Felicity Conditions", "pron": "फ़ि-लि-सि-टी कन-डि-शन्स (fi-LIS-i-tee)", "hindi": "किसी कथन के सफल होने के लिए ज़रूरी शर्तें", "net": "The conditions that must be satisfied for a speech act to be successfully performed (Austin; refined by Searle)."}, {"term": "Cooperative Principle", "pron": "को-ऑप-र-टिव प्रिन-सि-पल (koh-OP-er-uh-tiv)", "hindi": "बातचीत में आपसी सहयोग का नियम", "net": "Grice's principle that participants make their contribution such as is required by the accepted purpose of the talk; realised through the maxims of Quantity, Quality, Relation and Manner ('Logic and Conversation', 1975)."}, {"term": "Implicature", "pron": "इम-प्लि-का-चर (IM-pli-kuh-cher)", "hindi": "जो सीधे कहा नहीं गया पर इशारे से समझा गया अर्थ", "net": "Meaning that is implied or suggested by an utterance beyond its literal content; term coined by H. P. Grice."}, {"term": "Deixis", "pron": "डाइक-सिस (DYK-sis)", "hindi": "ऐसे शब्द जिनका अर्थ संदर्भ पर निर्भर हो, जैसे 'यहाँ', 'मैं', 'अब'", "net": "Words and expressions (I, here, now, this) whose reference depends on the context of utterance; types: person, place, time, social, discourse."}, {"term": "Presupposition", "pron": "प्री-स-पो-ज़ि-शन (pree-sup-uh-ZISH-un)", "hindi": "कथन में पहले से मान ली गई बात", "net": "An assumption taken for granted by an utterance, which remains true even when the sentence is negated."}, {"term": "Phoneme", "pron": "फ़ो-नीम (FOH-neem)", "hindi": "भाषा की सबसे छोटी ध्वनि इकाई जो अर्थ बदल दे", "net": "The smallest contrastive unit in the sound system of a language that can distinguish meaning, e.g. /p/ and /b/ in pin/bin."}, {"term": "Allophone", "pron": "ऐ-लो-फ़ोन (AL-uh-fohn)", "hindi": "एक ही ध्वनि के अलग-अलग उच्चारण रूप", "net": "A predictable phonetic variant of a phoneme that does not change meaning, e.g. aspirated [ph] in 'pin' vs [p] in 'spin'."}, {"term": "Minimal Pair", "pron": "मि-नि-मल पेयर (MIN-i-mul pair)", "hindi": "ऐसे दो शब्द जिनमें सिर्फ़ एक ध्वनि का फ़र्क हो", "net": "Two words that differ in only one sound segment and have different meanings, used to establish phonemes (ship/sheep)."}, {"term": "Schwa", "pron": "श्वा (SHWAA)", "hindi": "अंग्रेज़ी का सबसे कमज़ोर, बिना ज़ोर वाला 'अ' स्वर", "net": "The mid-central unstressed vowel /ə/, the most frequent vowel in English, e.g. the first vowel of 'about'."}, {"term": "Diphthong", "pron": "डिफ़-थॉन्ग (DIF-thong)", "hindi": "एक अक्षर में दो स्वरों का मिला हुआ स्वर, जैसे 'आइ'", "net": "A vowel sound involving a glide from one vowel quality to another within one syllable; RP has eight."}, {"term": "Assimilation", "pron": "अ-सि-मि-ले-शन (uh-sim-i-LAY-shun)", "hindi": "पास वाली ध्वनि के असर से किसी ध्वनि का बदल जाना", "net": "A connected-speech process in which a sound becomes more like a neighbouring sound, e.g. 'ten boys' pronounced 'tem boys'."}, {"term": "Suprasegmental", "pron": "सू-प्रा-सेग-मेन-टल (soo-pruh-seg-MEN-tul)", "hindi": "शब्द-ध्वनियों से ऊपर के गुण जैसे ज़ोर, सुर और लय", "net": "Phonological features extending over more than one segment: stress, pitch, intonation, rhythm and tone."}, {"term": "Morpheme", "pron": "मॉर-फ़ीम (MOR-feem)", "hindi": "शब्द का सबसे छोटा अर्थपूर्ण हिस्सा", "net": "The smallest meaningful or grammatical unit of language; may be free (book) or bound (-s, un-)."}, {"term": "Allomorph", "pron": "ऐ-लो-मॉर्फ़ (AL-uh-morf)", "hindi": "एक ही रूपिम के अलग-अलग रूप", "net": "A variant form of a morpheme, e.g. the English plural realised as /s/, /z/ or /iz/."}, {"term": "Back-formation", "pron": "बैक फ़ॉर-मे-शन (BAK for-MAY-shun)", "hindi": "किसी लंबे शब्द से गलती से छोटा नया शब्द बना लेना", "net": "Word formation by removing a supposed affix from an existing word, e.g. 'edit' from 'editor', 'televise' from 'television'."}, {"term": "Portmanteau Word", "pron": "पोर्ट-मैन-टो वर्ड (port-MAN-toh)", "hindi": "दो शब्दों के टुकड़े जोड़कर बना शब्द, जैसे smog", "net": "A blend formed by combining parts of two words (smog, brunch); term used by Lewis Carroll's Humpty Dumpty in Through the Looking-Glass (1871)."}, {"term": "Conversion", "pron": "कन-वर-शन (kun-VER-shun)", "hindi": "बिना बदलाव के शब्द की श्रेणी बदल देना, जैसे संज्ञा से क्रिया", "net": "Change of a word's grammatical class without any affix, e.g. noun 'bottle' to verb 'to bottle'; also called zero derivation or functional shift."}, {"term": "Hyponymy", "pron": "हाय-पॉ-नि-मी (hy-PON-i-mee)", "hindi": "छोटे वर्ग का बड़े वर्ग में शामिल होना, जैसे गुलाब फूल का प्रकार है", "net": "The sense relation of inclusion between a specific term (hyponym, rose) and a general term (hypernym/superordinate, flower)."}, {"term": "Polysemy", "pron": "पॉ-लि-सी-मी (po-LIS-i-mee)", "hindi": "एक शब्द के कई जुड़े हुए अर्थ", "net": "The existence of several related meanings for a single word, e.g. 'head' of body and 'head' of department; contrasted with homonymy."}, {"term": "Diglossia", "pron": "डाय-ग्लॉ-सि-आ (dy-GLOS-ee-uh)", "hindi": "एक समाज में भाषा के दो रूप - एक औपचारिक, एक बोलचाल का", "net": "A situation in which two varieties of a language, a High and a Low, are used for different social functions in one community; Charles Ferguson (1959)."}, {"term": "Pidgin", "pron": "पि-जिन (PIJ-in)", "hindi": "व्यापार आदि के लिए बनी मिली-जुली सरल भाषा, जिसका कोई मूल वक्ता न हो", "net": "A simplified contact language that develops between groups with no common language and has no native speakers."}, {"term": "Creole", "pron": "क्री-ओल (KREE-ohl)", "hindi": "ऐसी पिजिन भाषा जो किसी समुदाय की मातृभाषा बन गई हो", "net": "A language that develops from a pidgin when it becomes the native language of a community, acquiring full grammatical complexity."}, {"term": "Code-switching", "pron": "कोड स्विचिंग (KOHD swich-ing)", "hindi": "बात करते समय एक भाषा से दूसरी भाषा में चले जाना", "net": "The alternation between two or more languages or varieties within a conversation by bilingual speakers."}, {"term": "Interlanguage", "pron": "इन-टर-लैंग-ग्विज (IN-ter-lang-gwij)", "hindi": "सीखने वाले की बीच की भाषा - न मातृभाषा, न पूरी लक्ष्य भाषा", "net": "The learner's developing, systematic second-language system, intermediate between L1 and L2; term coined by Larry Selinker (1972)."}, {"term": "Fossilization", "pron": "फ़ॉ-सि-ला-ज़े-शन (fos-i-ly-ZAY-shun)", "hindi": "गलतियों का पक्का होकर स्थायी बन जाना", "net": "The process by which incorrect L2 forms become permanent in a learner's interlanguage (Selinker)."}, {"term": "Affective Filter", "pron": "अ-फ़ेक-टिव फ़िल-टर (uh-FEK-tiv FIL-ter)", "hindi": "डर, चिंता जैसी भावनाएँ जो भाषा सीखने में रुकावट डालें", "net": "Krashen's hypothesis that negative emotions such as anxiety and low motivation block comprehensible input from being acquired."}, {"term": "Comprehensible Input (i+1)", "pron": "कॉम-प्रि-हेन-सि-बल इन-पुट", "hindi": "ऐसी भाषा जो सीखने वाले के स्तर से थोड़ी ही ऊपर हो", "net": "Krashen's Input Hypothesis: acquisition occurs when learners understand input slightly beyond their current level (i+1)."}, {"term": "Washback", "pron": "वॉश-बैक (WOSH-bak)", "hindi": "परीक्षा का पढ़ाने और पढ़ने के तरीके पर पड़ने वाला असर", "net": "The effect, positive or negative, of a test on teaching and learning; also called backwash."}, {"term": "Cloze Test", "pron": "क्लोज़ टेस्ट (KLOHZ test)", "hindi": "पाठ में से हर कुछ शब्द हटाकर खाली जगह भरवाने वाली परीक्षा", "net": "An integrative test in which every nth word of a passage is deleted for the learner to restore; devised by Wilson L. Taylor (1953)."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who edited Saussure's Course in General Linguistics (1916) from his students' notes?", "options": ["Bally and Sechehaye", "Jakobson and Trubetzkoy", "Sapir and Whorf", "Ogden and Richards"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye compiled the Cours posthumously."}, {"q": "Saussure's distinction between language as a system and individual speech is:", "options": ["Competence and performance", "Langue and parole", "Signifier and signified", "Synchronic and diachronic"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Langue is the system; parole is the individual utterance."}, {"q": "'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously' was used by Chomsky to show that:", "options": ["Meaning determines grammar", "English is SOV", "A sentence can be grammatical but meaningless", "Semantics is independent of phonology"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Chomsky (1957) separated grammaticality from meaningfulness."}, {"q": "Match the works with the authors: (a) Syntactic Structures (b) How to Do Things with Words (c) Language as Social Semiotic (d) Logic and Conversation | (i) Grice (ii) Halliday (iii) Austin (iv) Chomsky", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Chomsky 1957; Austin 1962; Halliday 1978; Grice 1975."}, {"q": "Which is NOT one of Grice's conversational maxims?", "options": ["Politeness", "Quantity", "Relation", "Manner"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Grice's maxims are Quantity, Quality, Relation and Manner; politeness is Leech's principle."}, {"q": "In Austin's theory, the effect of an utterance on the listener is the:", "options": ["Locutionary act", "Perlocutionary act", "Illocutionary act", "Constative act"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Perlocution refers to consequential effects like persuading or frightening."}, {"q": "Searle's taxonomy of illocutionary acts includes all EXCEPT:", "options": ["Directives", "Commissives", "Behabitives", "Declarations"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Behabitives belong to Austin's classification, not Searle's."}, {"q": "Halliday's three metafunctions of language are:", "options": ["Emotive, conative, poetic", "Field, tenor, mode", "Locutionary, illocutionary, perlocutionary", "Ideational, interpersonal, textual"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Field, tenor, mode are register variables; the metafunctions are ideational, interpersonal, textual."}, {"q": "The term 'phatic communion' was coined by:", "options": ["Bronislaw Malinowski", "Roman Jakobson", "J. R. Firth", "Dell Hymes"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Malinowski used it in 1923 in a supplement to Ogden and Richards' The Meaning of Meaning."}, {"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (i) Grimm's Law (ii) Caxton's press (iii) Johnson's Dictionary (iv) Norman Conquest", "options": ["iv, ii, i, iii", "iv, ii, iii, i", "ii, iv, iii, i", "iv, iii, ii, i"], "answer": 1, "exp": "1066, 1476, 1755, 1822."}, {"q": "The term 'Great Vowel Shift' was coined by:", "options": ["Henry Sweet", "Jacob Grimm", "Otto Jespersen", "Daniel Jones"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Jespersen named the shift of Middle English long vowels."}, {"q": "The words 'they, them, their' entered English from:", "options": ["Latin", "Norman French", "Celtic", "Old Norse"], "answer": 3, "exp": "These pronouns came from Scandinavian (Old Norse) influence."}, {"q": "'Smog' and 'brunch' are examples of:", "options": ["Blending", "Clipping", "Back-formation", "Acronymy"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Blends combine parts of two words (smoke + fog)."}, {"q": "'Edit' derived from 'editor' is an example of:", "options": ["Conversion", "Back-formation", "Coinage", "Compounding"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Removing a supposed suffix from an existing word is back-formation."}, {"q": "Aspirated [ph] in 'pin' and unaspirated [p] in 'spin' are:", "options": ["Two phonemes", "Minimal pairs", "Allophones of /p/", "Free morphemes"], "answer": 2, "exp": "They are predictable variants of one phoneme in complementary distribution."}, {"q": "How many phonemes does RP English have?", "options": ["40", "42", "46", "44"], "answer": 3, "exp": "24 consonants and 20 vowels = 44."}, {"q": "Which of the following is a voiced bilabial plosive?", "options": ["/b/", "/p/", "/m/", "/v/"], "answer": 0, "exp": "/b/ is voiced, bilabial and plosive; /m/ is a nasal."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): English is a stress-timed language. Reason (R): In English, stressed syllables tend to occur at roughly regular intervals.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R explains A", "Both A and R are true but R does not explain A", "A is true, R is false", "A is false, R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Stress-timing is defined by regular intervals between stressed syllables."}, {"q": "Leech's 'seven types of meaning' appear in his book:", "options": ["Principles of Pragmatics", "A Linguistic Guide to English Poetry", "Semantics", "The Meaning of Meaning"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Leech, Semantics (1974) lists conceptual, connotative, social, affective, reflected, collocative and thematic meaning."}, {"q": "'Buy/sell' and 'husband/wife' are examples of:", "options": ["Gradable antonyms", "Complementary antonyms", "Hyponyms", "Converse antonyms"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Converse (relational) antonyms describe one relation from opposite viewpoints."}, {"q": "The concept of 'diglossia' was introduced by:", "options": ["Charles Ferguson", "William Labov", "Basil Bernstein", "Peter Trudgill"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Ferguson's 'Diglossia' (1959) described High and Low varieties."}, {"q": "Basil Bernstein distinguished between:", "options": ["Pidgin and creole", "Elaborated and restricted codes", "Langue and parole", "Dialect and register"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Bernstein linked social class to elaborated and restricted codes."}, {"q": "In Kachru's model, India belongs to the:", "options": ["Inner Circle", "Expanding Circle", "Outer Circle", "Core Circle"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Countries where English is a second, institutionalised language form the Outer Circle."}, {"q": "Which method is based on behaviourist psychology and structural linguistics?", "options": ["Communicative Language Teaching", "Silent Way", "Task-Based Language Teaching", "Audio-Lingual Method"], "answer": 3, "exp": "ALM uses habit formation through drills, from Skinner and structuralism."}, {"q": "Match method and founder: (a) Silent Way (b) Suggestopedia (c) TPR (d) Community Language Learning | (i) Asher (ii) Curran (iii) Gattegno (iv) Lozanov", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Gattegno, Lozanov, Asher and Curran respectively."}, {"q": "The Bangalore Project associated with Task-Based Language Teaching was led by:", "options": ["H. G. Widdowson", "N. S. Prabhu", "D. A. Wilkins", "Michael West"], "answer": 1, "exp": "N. S. Prabhu ran the Communicational Teaching Project (1979-84)."}, {"q": "Which is NOT one of Krashen's five hypotheses?", "options": ["Monitor Hypothesis", "Natural Order Hypothesis", "Output Hypothesis", "Affective Filter Hypothesis"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The Output Hypothesis was proposed by Merrill Swain (1985)."}, {"q": "The term 'interlanguage' was coined by:", "options": ["S. P. Corder", "Robert Lado", "Stephen Krashen", "Larry Selinker"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Selinker introduced 'interlanguage' in 1972."}, {"q": "The effect of a test on teaching and learning is called:", "options": ["Washback", "Reliability", "Validity", "Practicality"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Washback (backwash) can be positive or negative."}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence:", "options": ["Neither the teacher nor the students was present.", "Neither the teacher nor the students were present.", "Neither the teacher or the students were present.", "Neither the teacher nor the students is present."], "answer": 1, "exp": "With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject 'students'."}]}, {"id": 6, "title": "English in India: History, Evolution and Futures", "overview": "Is unit mein India mein English ka itihaas (Macaulay's Minute 1835, Wood's Despatch 1854), Indian English as a variety, Kachru ke circles, Derozio se aaj tak ke Indian English writers, translation aur awards aate hain. NET mein zyada tar match-the-following, chronology (saal) aur 'kisne kaha/likha' wale sawal aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Colonial Language Policy: Charter Act 1813 to Macaulay's Minute 1835", "points": ["Charter Act of 1813 set aside not less than one lakh rupees a year for the 'revival and improvement of literature' and education of Indians (pehli baar education ke liye paisa).", "Orientalist-Anglicist controversy: Orientalists (H.T. Prinsep, H.H. Wilson) favoured Sanskrit/Arabic/Persian; Anglicists (Macaulay, Trevelyan) favoured English and Western learning.", "Raja Ram Mohan Roy's letter to Lord Amherst (11 December 1823) opposed the Sanskrit College at Calcutta and pleaded for Western science and English education.", "Thomas Babington Macaulay, Law Member of the Governor-General's Council, wrote the 'Minute on Indian Education' dated 2 February 1835.", "Macaulay: 'A single shelf of a good European library was worth the whole native literature of India and Arabia.'", "Macaulay's aim: 'a class of persons, Indian in blood and colour, but English in taste, in opinions, in morals and in intellect' to act as interpreters between rulers and ruled.", "Lord William Bentinck's English Education Act (Resolution of 7 March 1835) made English the medium of higher education and directed funds to English education.", "Downward Filtration Theory: educate a small upper class in English and knowledge will 'filter down' to the masses.", "English replaced Persian as the language of courts and administration in 1837; Lord Hardinge (1844) gave government jobs preference to English-educated Indians.", "Gauri Viswanathan's 'Masks of Conquest: Literary Study and British Rule in India' (1989) argues English literary studies began in India as an instrument of colonial control.", "Homi K. Bhabha's essay 'Of Mimicry and Man' (1984) reads Macaulay's 'interpreter' class as colonial mimicry: 'almost the same, but not quite'."]}, {"title": "Wood's Despatch 1854 and Later Commissions", "points": ["Wood's Despatch (19 July 1854) was sent by Sir Charles Wood, President of the Board of Control, during Lord Dalhousie's governor-generalship.", "It is called the 'Magna Carta of English Education in India'.", "It proposed universities at Calcutta, Bombay and Madras on the model of London University; all three were set up in 1857.", "It recommended English for higher education and vernacular languages at the primary level, a Department of Public Instruction in each province, grants-in-aid, teacher training and women's education.", "It formally abandoned the Downward Filtration Theory and spoke of mass education.", "Hunter Commission (1882) under Lord Ripon, chaired by W.W. Hunter, reviewed primary and secondary education.", "Raleigh Commission (1902) led to the Indian Universities Act 1904 under Lord Curzon.", "Sadler Commission (Calcutta University Commission, 1917-19) chaired by Michael Sadler; Hartog Committee (1929); Sargent Plan (1944).", "Chronology to remember: Charter Act 1813 - Roy's letter 1823 - Macaulay's Minute 1835 - Wood's Despatch 1854 - Universities 1857 - Hunter 1882 - Universities Act 1904."]}, {"title": "English in Independent India: Language Policy", "points": ["University Education Commission (1948-49) chaired by Dr S. Radhakrishnan advised gradual replacement of English in higher education but its continued study.", "Article 343: Hindi in Devanagari script is the official language of the Union; English to continue for 15 years from 1950.", "Article 348: English is the language of the Supreme Court, High Courts and of Bills and Acts unless Parliament provides otherwise; Article 351: directive for spread of Hindi.", "Official Languages Act 1963 (amended 1967) allowed English to continue as an associate official language indefinitely, after anti-Hindi agitations (Tamil Nadu, 1965).", "Three-Language Formula: evolved by the Central Advisory Board of Education (1956), endorsed by the Kothari Commission (1964-66) and adopted in the National Policy on Education 1968.", "The Eighth Schedule of the Constitution now lists 22 languages; English is not in it.", "Central Institute of English (Hyderabad, 1958) became CIEFL and in 2007 the English and Foreign Languages University (EFLU).", "National Knowledge Commission (2006, Sam Pitroda) recommended English teaching from Class I; NEP 2020 emphasises mother tongue as medium till at least Class 5.", "David Graddol's 'English Next India' (British Council, 2010) discusses the future of English in India (aspiration, Hinglish, divide)."]}, {"title": "Indian English as a Variety", "points": ["Indian English (IndE) is a nativised, institutionalised second-language variety used in education, law, administration and media (apni alag pehchan wali English).", "Braj B. Kachru: 'The Indianization of English: The English Language in India' (1983) and 'The Alchemy of English' (1986); edited 'The Other Tongue' (1982).", "Kachru's key ideas: nativization, bilingual creativity, contact literature, and the bilingual's creativity as legitimate, not 'deviant'.", "Hobson-Jobson (1886), a glossary of Anglo-Indian words, was compiled by Henry Yule and A.C. Burnell.", "Raja Rao, Foreword to Kanthapura (1938): 'One has to convey in a language that is not one's own the spirit that is one's own.'", "Raja Rao also wrote: 'We cannot write like the English. We should not. We cannot write only as Indians.'", "Kamala Das, 'An Introduction': 'The language I speak / Becomes mine, its distortions, its queernesses / All mine, mine alone.'", "Salman Rushdie, 'Imaginary Homelands' (essay 1982; collection 1991): English must be remade 'for our own purposes'; his hybrid style is called 'chutnification'.", "Rushdie's introduction to 'The Vintage Book of Indian Writing 1947-1997' (with Elizabeth West) claimed Indian writing in English is 'stronger and more important' than work in the 16 official languages - a much debated claim.", "Nissim Ezekiel's 'Very Indian Poems in Indian English' (e.g. 'Goodbye Party for Miss Pushpa T.S.', 'The Patriot') parody Indian English usage.", "Nihalani, Tongue and Hosali, 'Indian and British English: A Handbook of Usage and Pronunciation' (1979) documents IndE usage."]}, {"title": "Kachru's Three Concentric Circles and Models of World Englishes", "points": ["Proposed by Braj Kachru in 'Standards, Codification and Sociolinguistic Realism: The English Language in the Outer Circle' (1985), in Quirk and Widdowson (eds.), 'English in the World'.", "Inner Circle (norm-providing): USA, UK, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Ireland - English as native language (ENL).", "Outer Circle (norm-developing): India, Singapore, Nigeria, Kenya, Philippines, Pakistan - English as second/official language (ESL), result of colonisation.", "Expanding Circle (norm-dependent): China, Japan, Russia, Korea, Egypt, Brazil - English as foreign language (EFL).", "India falls in the Outer Circle (yaad rakho: India = Outer, norm-developing).", "Kachru and Larry E. Smith edited the journal 'World Englishes' (from 1985); the term 'World Englishes' (plural) stresses pluricentricity.", "Tom McArthur's 'Circle of World English' (1987) puts 'World Standard English' at the centre; Manfred Gorlach's circle model (1990).", "Edgar W. Schneider's Dynamic Model (2003; book 'Postcolonial English', 2007) has five phases: Foundation, Exonormative Stabilization, Nativization, Endonormative Stabilization, Differentiation.", "Criticism of Kachru's model: based on nation-states and history, ignores proficiency, diaspora and multilingual countries; still the most cited model in NET."]}, {"title": "Features of Indian English: Sounds, Grammar and Vocabulary", "points": ["Phonology: retroflex /t/ and /d/ for English alveolars; dental t and d for /θ/ and /ð/ ('thin' as 'tin').", "No clear /v/-/w/ distinction (both as a labiodental approximant); monophthongs /e:/ and /o:/ for /eɪ/ and /əʊ/ ('gate', 'goat').", "IndE is largely rhotic, syllable-timed (not stress-timed), and shows spelling pronunciation and aspiration differences.", "Grammar: invariant tag question 'isn't it?' ('You are coming, isn't it?'); progressive with stative verbs ('I am knowing him').", "Emphatic 'itself' and 'only' ('I will come today itself', 'He is like that only'); reduplication ('small small things', 'hot hot tea').", "Indianisms: 'prepone', 'cousin-brother', 'co-brother', 'do the needful', 'revert back', 'passed out' (graduated), 'out of station', 'eve-teasing', 'airdash', 'tiffin'.", "Hybrid compounds mixing Indian and English elements: 'lathi charge', 'policewala', 'tiffin box', 'brahminhood'.", "Loanwords from Indian languages in English: bungalow, jungle, pyjama, shampoo, juggernaut, thug, guru, pundit, avatar, karma, loot, cot.", "Hinglish: code-mixing and code-switching between Hindi and English, common in media and advertising ('Yeh Dil Maange More').", "Discourse features: elaborate politeness ('kindly', 'your good self', 'with due respect') and formal, bookish style."]}, {"title": "Beginnings of Indian Writing in English (1794-1900)", "points": ["Sake Dean Mahomet's 'The Travels of Dean Mahomet' (1794) is regarded as the first book written by an Indian in English.", "Henry Louis Vivian Derozio (1809-1831), teacher at Hindu College, inspired 'Young Bengal'; 'Poems' (1827), 'The Fakeer of Jungheera' (1828); sonnet 'To India - My Native Land'.", "Kashiprasad Ghosh, 'The Shair and Other Poems' (1830) - early volume of verse by a Hindu Bengali in English.", "Michael Madhusudan Dutt wrote 'The Captive Ladie' (1849) in English before turning to Bengali ('Meghnad Badh Kavya', 1861).", "Toru Dutt (1856-1877): 'A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields' (1876); 'Ancient Ballads and Legends of Hindustan' (1882, posthumous, introduction by Edmund Gosse); poem 'Our Casuarina Tree'.", "Kylas Chunder Dutt, 'A Journal of Forty-Eight Hours of the Year 1945' (1835) and Shoshee Chunder Dutt, 'The Republic of Orissa' (1845) are early prose fictions.", "Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's 'Rajmohan's Wife' (serialised 1864) is considered the first Indian novel in English.", "Krupabai Satthianadhan: 'Kamala' (1894) and 'Saguna' (1895), early novels by an Indian woman in English.", "Krishna Mohan Banerjee's 'The Persecuted' (1831) is considered the first Indian play in English.", "Manmohan Ghose: 'Love Songs and Elegies' (1898); brother of Sri Aurobindo."]}, {"title": "Poetry before Independence: Tagore, Naidu, Aurobindo", "points": ["Rabindranath Tagore's 'Gitanjali: Song Offerings' (English, 1912, introduction by W.B. Yeats) won him the Nobel Prize in 1913 - first Asian Nobel laureate.", "Other Tagore English books: 'The Gardener' (1913), 'The Crescent Moon' (1913), 'Fruit-Gathering' (1916), 'Stray Birds' (1916); lectures 'Nationalism' (1917).", "Sarojini Naidu, 'The Nightingale of India': 'The Golden Threshold' (1905), 'The Bird of Time' (1912), 'The Broken Wing' (1917); 'The Feather of the Dawn' (1961, posthumous).", "Famous Naidu poems: 'Palanquin Bearers', 'In the Bazaars of Hyderabad', 'Indian Weavers', 'Coromandel Fishers'.", "Sri Aurobindo's 'Savitri: A Legend and a Symbol' (1950-51) is an epic of about 24,000 lines in blank verse, based on the Mahabharata story.", "Aurobindo's critical work 'The Future Poetry' discusses 'mantric' poetry; other poems: 'Love and Death', 'Baji Prabhou'.", "Harindranath Chattopadhyaya (brother of Sarojini Naidu): 'The Feast of Youth' (1918).", "Edmund Gosse encouraged Sarojini Naidu to write about Indian themes rather than imitate English poets."]}, {"title": "Modern Indian English Poetry (1950 onwards)", "points": ["Nissim Ezekiel's 'A Time to Change' (1952) is taken as the beginning of modern Indian English poetry; later 'The Exact Name' (1965), 'Hymns in Darkness' (1976), 'Latter-Day Psalms' (1982, Sahitya Akademi 1983).", "Ezekiel poems: 'Night of the Scorpion', 'Enterprise', 'Background, Casually', 'Poet, Lover, Birdwatcher', 'Philosophy'.", "A.K. Ramanujan: 'The Striders' (1966), 'Relations' (1971), 'Second Sight' (1986); 'Collected Poems' won Sahitya Akademi (1999, posthumous); poems 'Obituary', 'Small-Scale Reflections on a Great House'.", "Kamala Das: 'Summer in Calcutta' (1965), 'The Descendants' (1967), 'The Old Playhouse and Other Poems' (1973); autobiography 'My Story' (1976).", "Jayanta Mahapatra: 'Relationship' (1980) won the Sahitya Akademi Award (1981) - first for an Indian English poet; also 'Close the Sky, Ten by Ten' (1971).", "Arun Kolatkar: 'Jejuri' (1976, Commonwealth Poetry Prize 1977), 'Kala Ghoda Poems' and 'Sarpa Satra' (2004).", "Dom Moraes: 'A Beginning' (1957) won the Hawthornden Prize (1958); Keki N. Daruwalla: 'Under Orion' (1970), 'The Keeper of the Dead' (Sahitya Akademi 1984).", "R. Parthasarathy: 'Rough Passage' (1977); edited 'Ten Twentieth-Century Indian Poets' (1976); A.K. Mehrotra edited 'The Oxford India Anthology of Twelve Modern Indian Poets' (1992).", "P. Lal founded the Writers Workshop, Calcutta, in 1958; Adil Jussawalla 'Missing Person' (1976); Eunice de Souza 'Fix' (1979); Gieve Patel 'Poems' (1966).", "Agha Shahid Ali: 'The Country Without a Post Office' (1997), English ghazals 'Call Me Ishmael Tonight' (2003).", "Contemporary: Jeet Thayil 'These Errors Are Correct' (Sahitya Akademi 2012), Meena Kandasamy 'Touch' (2006), Ranjit Hoskote, Sudeep Sen, Arundhathi Subramaniam.", "Vikram Seth's 'The Golden Gate' (1986) is a novel in verse of 590 tetrameter sonnets (Onegin stanzas)."]}, {"title": "Fiction: The Big Three - Anand, Narayan, Raja Rao", "points": ["Mulk Raj Anand: 'Untouchable' (1935, preface by E.M. Forster; Bakha the sweeper boy, one day's action), 'Coolie' (1936, Munoo), 'Two Leaves and a Bud' (1937, Gangu).", "Anand also: 'The Village' (1939), 'Across the Black Waters' (1940), 'The Sword and the Sickle' (1942) - Lalu trilogy; 'The Private Life of an Indian Prince' (1953); 'Morning Face' (Sahitya Akademi 1971).", "R.K. Narayan created the fictional town Malgudi; 'Swami and Friends' (1935) was published with Graham Greene's help.", "Narayan: 'The Bachelor of Arts' (1937), 'The Dark Room' (1938), 'The English Teacher' (1945), 'Mr. Sampath' (1949), 'The Financial Expert' (1952, Margayya), 'Waiting for the Mahatma' (1955).", "Narayan's 'The Guide' (1958; Raju, Rosie, Marco, Velan) won the Sahitya Akademi Award 1960 - first English novel to receive it.", "Narayan: 'The Man-Eater of Malgudi' (1961, Nataraj and Vasu), 'The Vendor of Sweets' (1967, Jagan), 'The Painter of Signs' (1976), 'A Tiger for Malgudi' (1983).", "Raja Rao: 'Kanthapura' (1938) - Gandhian movement in a South Indian village, narrated by the old woman Achakka, hero Moorthy, in sthala-purana form.", "Raja Rao: 'The Serpent and the Rope' (1960; Ramaswamy and Madeleine; Sahitya Akademi 1964), 'The Cat and Shakespeare' (1965), 'Comrade Kirillov' (1976), 'The Chessmaster and His Moves' (1988).", "Raja Rao won the Neustadt International Prize for Literature in 1988.", "Anand = social realism/humanism; Narayan = gentle irony and comedy of middle-class life; Raja Rao = metaphysical/Vedantic vision."]}, {"title": "Fiction 1940s-1980s", "points": ["Ahmed Ali, 'Twilight in Delhi' (1940) - decline of Muslim culture in old Delhi (Mir Nihal).", "G.V. Desani, 'All About H. Hatterr' (1948) - linguistically playful, a precursor of Rushdie.", "Bhabani Bhattacharya: 'So Many Hungers!' (1947, Bengal famine), 'He Who Rides a Tiger' (1954), 'Shadow from Ladakh' (Sahitya Akademi 1967).", "Kamala Markandaya, 'Nectar in a Sieve' (1954) - peasant woman Rukmani and husband Nathan; title from Coleridge.", "Khushwant Singh, 'Train to Pakistan' (1956) - Partition in the village Mano Majra; Juggut Singh (Jugga), Iqbal, Hukum Chand, Nooran.", "Manohar Malgonkar, 'A Bend in the Ganges' (1964); Attia Hosain, 'Sunlight on a Broken Column' (1961).", "Anita Desai: 'Cry, the Peacock' (1963), 'Fire on the Mountain' (1977, Sahitya Akademi 1978), 'Clear Light of Day' (1980), 'In Custody' (1984).", "Arun Joshi: 'The Strange Case of Billy Biswas' (1971), 'The Last Labyrinth' (1981, Sahitya Akademi 1982).", "Nayantara Sahgal, 'Rich Like Us' (1985, Sahitya Akademi 1986) - set during the Emergency.", "Ruth Prawer Jhabvala won the Booker Prize 1975 for 'Heat and Dust'."]}, {"title": "Contemporary Fiction: Rushdie and After", "points": ["Salman Rushdie, 'Midnight's Children' (1981): Booker 1981, 'Booker of Bookers' 1993, 'Best of the Booker' 2008; narrator Saleem Sinai, born at midnight, 15 August 1947.", "Rushdie: 'Shame' (1983), 'The Satanic Verses' (1988; fatwa by Ayatollah Khomeini, 14 February 1989), 'The Moor's Last Sigh' (1995).", "Vikram Seth: 'A Suitable Boy' (1993; Mrs Rupa Mehra seeks a husband for Lata), 'The Golden Gate' (1986, Sahitya Akademi 1988).", "Amitav Ghosh: 'The Circle of Reason' (1986), 'The Shadow Lines' (1988, Sahitya Akademi 1989), 'The Calcutta Chromosome' (1995), 'The Glass Palace' (2000), 'The Hungry Tide' (2004).", "Ghosh's Ibis Trilogy: 'Sea of Poppies' (2008), 'River of Smoke' (2011), 'Flood of Fire' (2015); non-fiction 'The Great Derangement' (2016); Jnanpith 2018 - first English writer.", "Arundhati Roy, 'The God of Small Things' (1997 Booker) - twins Estha and Rahel, Ammu, Velutha, Ayemenem; 'The Ministry of Utmost Happiness' (2017).", "Kiran Desai, 'The Inheritance of Loss' (Booker 2006); Aravind Adiga, 'The White Tiger' (Booker 2008) - Balram Halwai writes letters to Wen Jiabao.", "Upamanyu Chatterjee, 'English, August' (1988, Agastya Sen); Shashi Tharoor, 'The Great Indian Novel' (1989, Mahabharata retold as Indian politics).", "Rohinton Mistry: 'Such a Long Journey' (1991), 'A Fine Balance' (1995); Jhumpa Lahiri: 'Interpreter of Maladies' (Pulitzer 2000), 'The Namesake' (2003).", "Popular fiction: Chetan Bhagat 'Five Point Someone' (2004); Amish Tripathi 'The Immortals of Meluha' (2010) - mass-market Indian English."]}, {"title": "Indian English and Indian Drama in Translation", "points": ["First Indian English play: Krishna Mohan Banerjee, 'The Persecuted' (1831).", "Tagore's plays in English: 'Chitra' (1913), 'The Post Office' (Dak Ghar; English 1914), 'Red Oleanders' (Raktakarabi).", "Sri Aurobindo's verse plays: 'Perseus the Deliverer', 'Vasavadutta', 'Rodogune'; Harindranath Chattopadhyaya, Asif Currimbhoy ('The Doldrummers', 'Goa', 'Inquilab', 'The Refugee').", "Gurcharan Das, 'Larins Sahib' (1970); Partap Sharma, 'A Touch of Brightness'.", "Girish Karnad (Kannada, self-translated): 'Yayati' (1961), 'Tughlaq' (1964), 'Hayavadana' (1971), 'Naga-Mandala' (1988), 'Tale-Danda' (1990), 'The Fire and the Rain' (Agni Mattu Male, 1995); Jnanpith 1998.", "Hayavadana draws on the Kathasaritsagara via Thomas Mann's 'The Transposed Heads'.", "Vijay Tendulkar (Marathi): 'Silence! The Court Is in Session' (Shantata! Court Chalu Aahe, 1967), 'Ghashiram Kotwal' (1972), 'Sakharam Binder' (1972), 'Kanyadaan'.", "Badal Sircar (Bengali): 'Evam Indrajit' (1963); founder of 'Third Theatre'; Mohan Rakesh (Hindi): 'Ashadh Ka Ek Din' (1958), 'Adhe Adhure' (1969).", "Mahesh Dattani: 'Dance Like a Man' (1989), 'Tara' (1990), 'Bravely Fought the Queen' (1991), 'Final Solutions' (1993), 'On a Muggy Night in Mumbai' (1998); first English playwright to win Sahitya Akademi (1998).", "Manjula Padmanabhan, 'Harvest' (1997) - organ trade; won the Onassis Prize (1997)."]}, {"title": "Translation in India: History and Theory", "points": ["Panchatantra was translated into Pahlavi (6th century) and travelled West as the Fables of Bidpai.", "Mughal translations: Akbar's Maktab Khana rendered the Mahabharata into Persian as 'Razmnama'; Dara Shikoh translated 50 Upanishads as 'Sirr-i-Akbar' (1657).", "Sir William Jones founded the Asiatic Society (Calcutta, 1784) and translated Kalidasa's 'Sakuntala' (1789).", "Charles Wilkins's 'Bhagavad Gita' (1785), with a preface by Warren Hastings, was the first direct English translation from Sanskrit.", "Max Muller edited 'The Sacred Books of the East' (50 vols., 1879-1910); Edwin Arnold's 'The Light of Asia' (1879) on the Buddha.", "Indian terms: anuvad (speaking after/repetition), rupantar (change of form), bhashantar (change of language), chhaya (shadow).", "Sujit Mukherjee, 'Translation as Discovery' (1981); G.N. Devy, 'After Amnesia' (1992) and 'In Another Tongue' (1993) - Indian literary culture as 'translating consciousness'.", "Tejaswini Niranjana, 'Siting Translation: History, Post-Structuralism and the Colonial Context' (1992) - translation as a site of colonial power.", "Susan Bassnett and Harish Trivedi (eds.), 'Post-Colonial Translation: Theory and Practice' (1999).", "A.K. Ramanujan translated 'The Interior Landscape' (1967, Tamil Sangam), 'Speaking of Siva' (1973, Kannada vachanas), 'Hymns for the Drowning' (1981, Nammalvar) and Ananthamurthy's 'Samskara' (1976).", "Ramanujan's essay 'Three Hundred Ramayanas: Five Examples and Three Thoughts on Translation' (pub. 1991) was dropped from the Delhi University syllabus in 2011.", "Sahitya Akademi Translation Prize since 1989; National Translation Mission (2008) at CIIL, Mysore."]}, {"title": "Indian Writing in Translation: Key Texts", "points": ["Tagore, 'Ghare Baire' as 'The Home and the World' (tr. Surendranath Tagore, 1919); Premchand, 'Godaan' (1936) - Hori and Dhania.", "U.R. Ananthamurthy, 'Samskara' (Kannada 1965; tr. A.K. Ramanujan 1976) - Praneshacharya and the corpse of Naranappa.", "Mahasweta Devi: 'Draupadi' and 'Breast-Giver' (tr. Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak), 'Imaginary Maps' (1995, Spivak), 'Mother of 1084' (Hajar Churashir Maa).", "Amrita Pritam, 'Pinjar' (1950; tr. Khushwant Singh as 'The Skeleton'); Qurratulain Hyder, 'Aag Ka Darya' (1959; self-transcreated as 'River of Fire', 1998).", "Saadat Hasan Manto, 'Toba Tek Singh' (Partition story); Ismat Chughtai, 'Lihaaf' (The Quilt).", "Thakazhi Sivasankara Pillai, 'Chemmeen' (1956); O.V. Vijayan, 'The Legends of Khasak'; Benyamin, 'Goat Days' (Aadujeevitham).", "Dalit writing: Bama, 'Karukku' (Tamil 1992; tr. Lakshmi Holmstrom 2000); Omprakash Valmiki, 'Joothan' (Hindi 1997; tr. Arun Prabha Mukherjee 2003); Sharankumar Limbale, 'Akkarmashi' (The Outcaste).", "Daya Pawar, 'Baluta'; Urmila Pawar, 'The Weave of My Life' (Aaydan); Bhalchandra Nemade, 'Kosla' (Cocoon).", "Perumal Murugan, 'One Part Woman' (Madhorubhagan, tr. Aniruddhan Vasudevan); Vivek Shanbhag, 'Ghachar Ghochar' (tr. Srinath Perur).", "Geetanjali Shree, 'Tomb of Sand' (Ret Samadhi, tr. Daisy Rockwell) - International Booker 2022, first Hindi book to win.", "Banu Mushtaq, 'Heart Lamp' (Kannada stories, tr. Deepa Bhasthi) - International Booker 2025."]}, {"title": "Sahitya Akademi and Literary Awards", "points": ["Sahitya Akademi (National Academy of Letters) was set up by a Government of India resolution of 15 December 1952 and inaugurated on 12 March 1954; Jawaharlal Nehru was its first President.", "It recognises 24 languages: the 22 Eighth Schedule languages plus English and Rajasthani.", "Sahitya Akademi Award given since 1955; first English winner R.K. Narayan ('The Guide', 1960).", "Other Akademi honours: Fellowship (highest), Bhasha Samman (1996), Translation Prize (1989), Bal Sahitya Puraskar (2010), Yuva Puraskar (2011); journals 'Indian Literature' (English) and 'Samkaleen Bharatiya Sahitya' (Hindi).", "Jnanpith Award by Bharatiya Jnanpith (trust founded 1944); first award 1965 to G. Sankara Kurup (Malayalam) for 'Odakkuzhal'; Amitav Ghosh (2018) first English writer.", "Saraswati Samman (K.K. Birla Foundation, 1991) - first to Harivansh Rai Bachchan; Vyas Samman (1991) for Hindi.", "Booker winners of Indian origin/subject: Rushdie 1981, Arundhati Roy 1997, Kiran Desai 2006, Aravind Adiga 2008 (also Jhabvala 1975 for 'Heat and Dust').", "DSC Prize for South Asian Literature (first 2011, H.M. Naqvi 'Home Boy'); JCB Prize for Literature (first 2018, Benyamin 'Jasmine Days', tr. Shahnaz Habib); Crossword Book Award (1998).", "Tagore - Nobel 1913; Raja Rao - Neustadt Prize 1988; Vikram Seth - Commonwealth Writers' Prize for 'A Suitable Boy' (1994)."]}, {"title": "Criticism, Histories and Futures of Indian English", "points": ["K.R. Srinivasa Iyengar: 'Indo-Anglian Literature' (1943) and 'Indian Writing in English' (1962) - pioneering history.", "M.K. Naik, 'A History of Indian English Literature' (1982); C.D. Narasimhaiah, 'The Swan and the Eagle' (1969), founder-editor of 'The Literary Criterion'.", "Meenakshi Mukherjee: 'The Twice Born Fiction' (1971), 'The Perishable Empire' (2000); essay 'The Anxiety of Indianness'.", "Bruce King, 'Modern Indian Poetry in English' (1987); A.K. Mehrotra (ed.), 'An Illustrated History of Indian Literature in English' (2003); William Walsh, 'Indian Literature in English' (1990).", "Terminology shift: 'Indo-Anglian' (Iyengar) - 'Indo-English' - 'Indian Writing in English' - 'Indian English Literature' (Sahitya Akademi usage).", "Postcolonial readings: Bhabha (mimicry, hybridity), Viswanathan (English studies as control), Aijaz Ahmad 'In Theory' (1992), Ngugi's 'Decolonising the Mind' (1986) on language choice.", "Debate: English as 'link language' and 'window to the world' (Nehru) vs English as elite, colonial language widening social divide.", "Futures: rise of Hinglish, mass-market fiction, digital English, English-medium demand, and growth of translation (JCB, International Booker) bringing bhasha literature to English readers."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Minute", "pron": "मि-निट (MIN-it)", "hindi": "सरकारी नोट या लिखित राय", "net": "An official memorandum recording a policy opinion; esp. Macaulay's 'Minute on Indian Education' (2 February 1835) advocating English education in India."}, {"term": "Despatch", "pron": "डिस-पैच (dis-PATCH)", "hindi": "सरकारी आदेश-पत्र", "net": "An official message from authorities; Wood's Despatch (1854) by Sir Charles Wood, called the Magna Carta of English education in India."}, {"term": "Anglicism / Anglicists", "pron": "एंग-ली-सिज़्म (ANG-gli-siz-um)", "hindi": "अंग्रेज़ी शिक्षा के समर्थक", "net": "The 1830s party (Macaulay, Trevelyan) that wanted Western knowledge taught through English, opposed to the Orientalists."}, {"term": "Orientalism", "pron": "ओ-री-एन-टल-इज़्म (or-ee-EN-tl-iz-um)", "hindi": "पूरब की भाषाओं/संस्कृति का अध्ययन; सईद के अनुसार पश्चिम का पूरब पर बना-बनाया नज़रिया", "net": "In the Indian education debate, support for Sanskrit, Arabic and Persian learning; in Edward Said's 'Orientalism' (1978), the Western discourse constructing the East as Other."}, {"term": "Downward Filtration Theory", "pron": "डाउन-वर्ड फ़िल-ट्रे-शन (DOWN-werd fil-TRAY-shun)", "hindi": "ऊपर के वर्ग को पढ़ाओ, ज्ञान नीचे तक अपने-आप पहुँचेगा", "net": "Colonial policy of educating a small elite in English so that knowledge would filter down to the masses; abandoned by Wood's Despatch (1854)."}, {"term": "Grants-in-aid", "pron": "ग्रांट्स-इन-एड (GRANTS-in-AYD)", "hindi": "निजी स्कूलों को सरकारी आर्थिक सहायता", "net": "System of government financial support to private educational institutions, recommended by Wood's Despatch (1854)."}, {"term": "Three-Language Formula", "pron": "थ्री-लैंग्वेज फ़ॉर्मूला (three-LANG-gwij FOR-myu-luh)", "hindi": "स्कूल में तीन भाषाएँ पढ़ाने की नीति", "net": "Policy (CABE 1956, Kothari Commission 1964-66, NPE 1968) requiring study of the regional language, Hindi and English (or another modern Indian language in Hindi areas)."}, {"term": "Indian English", "pron": "इन-डि-यन इंग-लिश (IN-dee-un ING-glish)", "hindi": "भारत में बोली-लिखी जाने वाली अंग्रेज़ी की अपनी किस्म", "net": "The institutionalised, nativised variety of English used in India, with its own phonological, grammatical and lexical features (Kachru)."}, {"term": "Indo-Anglian", "pron": "इन-डो-एंग-ली-यन (IN-doh-ANG-glee-un)", "hindi": "भारतीयों द्वारा अंग्रेज़ी में लिखा साहित्य (पुराना नाम)", "net": "Older term for original creative writing in English by Indians, popularised by K.R. Srinivasa Iyengar's 'Indo-Anglian Literature' (1943)."}, {"term": "Variety", "pron": "वै-राय-टी (vuh-RY-uh-tee)", "hindi": "भाषा का कोई खास रूप", "net": "A neutral sociolinguistic term for any distinguishable form of a language (regional, social or national), e.g. Indian English."}, {"term": "Nativization", "pron": "नेटि-वाइ-ज़े-शन (nay-ti-vy-ZAY-shun)", "hindi": "विदेशी भाषा का स्थानीय रंग में ढल जाना", "net": "Kachru's term for the process by which a transplanted language acquires local linguistic and cultural features; also Phase 3 of Schneider's Dynamic Model."}, {"term": "Indianization", "pron": "इन-डि-य-नाइ-ज़े-शन (in-dee-uh-ny-ZAY-shun)", "hindi": "अंग्रेज़ी का भारतीय बन जाना", "net": "The acculturation of English in Indian contexts; title of Braj Kachru's 'The Indianization of English' (1983)."}, {"term": "World Englishes", "pron": "वर्ल्ड इंग-लि-शिज़ (WURLD ING-gli-shiz)", "hindi": "दुनिया भर की अंग्रेज़ी की अलग-अलग किस्में", "net": "Pluricentric view that recognises many legitimate Englishes; associated with Braj Kachru and the journal 'World Englishes' (Kachru and Larry Smith)."}, {"term": "Concentric Circles (Kachru)", "pron": "कॉन-सेन-ट्रिक सर्-कल्स (kon-SEN-trik SUR-kulz)", "hindi": "इनर, आउटर, एक्सपैंडिंग - अंग्रेज़ी के तीन घेरे", "net": "Kachru's 1985 model: Inner Circle (norm-providing, ENL), Outer Circle (norm-developing, ESL, e.g. India), Expanding Circle (norm-dependent, EFL)."}, {"term": "Norm-developing", "pron": "नॉर्म-डि-वे-लपिंग (NORM-di-VEL-up-ing)", "hindi": "अपने नियम खुद बना रही अंग्रेज़ी", "net": "Kachru's description of Outer Circle varieties such as Indian English, which are developing their own standards."}, {"term": "Exonormative / Endonormative", "pron": "एक्सो-नॉर्-मे-टिव / एन्डो-नॉर्-मे-टिव (ek-soh-NOR-muh-tiv / en-doh-)", "hindi": "बाहरी मानक मानना / अपने अंदर के मानक मानना", "net": "Exonormative: following an external (e.g. British) standard; endonormative: following locally developed norms (Schneider's phases 2 and 4)."}, {"term": "Dynamic Model", "pron": "डाइ-ने-मिक मॉ-डल (dy-NAM-ik MOD-l)", "hindi": "उत्तर-औपनिवेशिक अंग्रेज़ी के विकास के पाँच चरण", "net": "Edgar Schneider's (2003) model of postcolonial Englishes: foundation, exonormative stabilization, nativization, endonormative stabilization, differentiation."}, {"term": "Lingua franca", "pron": "लिंग-ग्वा फ़्रैंका (LING-gwuh FRANG-kuh)", "hindi": "अलग भाषा वालों के बीच संपर्क की भाषा", "net": "A language used for communication between speakers of different mother tongues; English functions as India's link language."}, {"term": "Code-mixing", "pron": "कोड-मिक्सिंग (KOHD-mik-sing)", "hindi": "एक वाक्य में दो भाषाएँ मिलाना", "net": "Insertion of words or phrases from one language within a sentence of another, e.g. Hindi-English mixing (intra-sentential)."}, {"term": "Code-switching", "pron": "कोड-स्विचिंग (KOHD-swich-ing)", "hindi": "बात करते-करते एक भाषा से दूसरी पर जाना", "net": "Alternation between two languages across sentences or turns in discourse by bilingual speakers."}, {"term": "Hinglish", "pron": "हिंग-लिश (HING-glish)", "hindi": "हिंदी और अंग्रेज़ी का मिला-जुला रूप", "net": "Hybrid of Hindi and English marked by code-mixing, widespread in Indian media, advertising and youth speech."}, {"term": "Retroflex", "pron": "रे-ट्रो-फ़्लेक्स (RET-roh-fleks)", "hindi": "जीभ मोड़कर बोली जाने वाली ध्वनि (ट, ड)", "net": "Consonant articulated with the tongue tip curled back; Indian English replaces English alveolar /t d/ with retroflex sounds."}, {"term": "Syllable-timed", "pron": "सि-ला-बल टाइम्ड (SIL-uh-bl TYMD)", "hindi": "हर अक्षर को लगभग बराबर समय देना", "net": "Rhythm in which syllables occur at roughly equal intervals; Indian English is syllable-timed, unlike stress-timed British English."}, {"term": "Rhotic", "pron": "रो-टिक (ROH-tik)", "hindi": "शब्द में 'r' को हर जगह बोलना", "net": "Of an accent that pronounces /r/ after vowels (e.g. 'car'); Indian English is generally rhotic, RP is non-rhotic."}, {"term": "Invariant tag", "pron": "इन-वे-री-यन्ट टैग (in-VAIR-ee-unt TAG)", "hindi": "हर वाक्य के अंत में 'isn't it?' लगाना", "net": "A question tag that does not change with the subject or verb, e.g. Indian English 'isn't it?' or 'no?'."}, {"term": "Neologism", "pron": "नी-ओ-लॉ-जिज़्म (nee-OL-uh-jiz-um)", "hindi": "नया गढ़ा गया शब्द", "net": "A newly coined word; Indian English examples include 'prepone' and 'airdash'."}, {"term": "Loanword / Borrowing", "pron": "लोन-वर्ड (LOHN-wurd)", "hindi": "दूसरी भाषा से लिया गया शब्द", "net": "A word adopted from another language, e.g. 'bungalow', 'jungle', 'pyjama', 'shampoo' entered English from Indian languages."}, {"term": "Calque", "pron": "कॉल्क (KALK)", "hindi": "शब्दशः अनुवाद करके बना शब्द", "net": "A loan translation: a phrase translated element by element from another language, e.g. 'cousin-brother'."}, {"term": "Hybrid compound", "pron": "हाइ-ब्रिड कॉम्-पाउन्ड (HY-brid KOM-pownd)", "hindi": "भारतीय और अंग्रेज़ी शब्द जोड़कर बना शब्द", "net": "Compound combining elements from two languages, e.g. 'lathi charge', 'policewala' (Kachru's hybridization)."}, {"term": "Chutnification", "pron": "चट-नि-फ़ि-के-शन (chut-ni-fi-KAY-shun)", "hindi": "भाषा और इतिहास को चटनी की तरह मिला देना", "net": "Rushdie's metaphor (Midnight's Children) for his hybrid, spiced, Indianised English and the 'pickling' of history."}, {"term": "Bilingual creativity", "pron": "बाइ-लिंग-ग्वल क्री-ए-टि-विटी (by-LING-gwul kree-ay-TIV-i-tee)", "hindi": "दो भाषाओं के मेल से रचनात्मक लेखन", "net": "Kachru's term for creative writing produced by bilinguals using two or more linguistic and cultural codes."}, {"term": "Diglossia", "pron": "डाइ-ग्लॉ-सिया (dy-GLOS-ee-uh)", "hindi": "एक ही समाज में भाषा के ऊँचे और साधारण दो रूप", "net": "Charles Ferguson's (1959) term for a situation where a 'high' and 'low' variety of a language serve different functions."}, {"term": "Mimicry", "pron": "मि-मि-क्री (MIM-ik-ree)", "hindi": "नकल - शासित का शासक जैसा बनने की कोशिश", "net": "Homi Bhabha's concept ('Of Mimicry and Man', 1984): the colonised subject as 'almost the same, but not quite', which also unsettles colonial authority."}, {"term": "Hybridity", "pron": "हाइ-ब्रि-डि-टी (hy-BRID-i-tee)", "hindi": "दो संस्कृतियों का मेल", "net": "Bhabha's term for the mixed cultural forms produced in the colonial 'Third Space' of enunciation."}, {"term": "Postcolonialism", "pron": "पोस्ट-को-लो-नि-य-लिज़्म (pohst-kuh-LOH-nee-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "उपनिवेशवाद के बाद/विरुद्ध साहित्य और सिद्धांत", "net": "Critical study of the cultural legacy of colonialism (Said, Spivak, Bhabha; Ashcroft et al., 'The Empire Writes Back', 1989)."}, {"term": "Subaltern", "pron": "सब-ऑल-टर्न (sub-AWL-turn)", "hindi": "समाज का दबा-कुचला, जिसकी आवाज़ नहीं सुनी जाती", "net": "Gramsci's term for subordinated groups; used by the Subaltern Studies group (Ranajit Guha) and Spivak ('Can the Subaltern Speak?', 1988)."}, {"term": "Diaspora", "pron": "डाइ-ऐस-पो-रा (dy-AS-puh-ruh)", "hindi": "अपने देश से बाहर बसे लोग", "net": "Dispersed communities living outside the homeland; Indian diasporic writers include Rushdie, Mistry, Lahiri."}, {"term": "Sthala-purana", "pron": "स्थल-पुराण (STHUH-luh poo-RAA-nuh)", "hindi": "किसी गाँव/स्थान की पौराणिक कथा", "net": "Legendary history of a place; Raja Rao uses this form in 'Kanthapura' (1938)."}, {"term": "Transcreation", "pron": "ट्रांस-क्रि-ए-शन (trans-kree-AY-shun)", "hindi": "अनुवाद करते हुए नया रच देना", "net": "Free, creative translation adapting a text to the target culture; P. Lal's term for his Mahabharata renderings."}, {"term": "Anuvad", "pron": "अनुवाद (a-nu-VAAD)", "hindi": "अनुवाद - बाद में कहना", "net": "Sanskrit-derived Indian term for translation, literally 'saying after' or repetition; contrasted with rupantar and bhashantar."}, {"term": "Translating consciousness", "pron": "ट्रांस-ले-टिंग कॉन-शस-नेस (trans-LAY-ting KON-shus-nis)", "hindi": "भारतीय मन का स्वभाव से बहुभाषी/अनुवादी होना", "net": "G.N. Devy's idea ('In Another Tongue', 1993) that Indian literary culture is inherently multilingual and translational."}, {"term": "Dalit literature", "pron": "दलित साहित्य (DUH-lit)", "hindi": "दलितों द्वारा अपने अनुभव पर लिखा साहित्य", "net": "Writing by and about Dalits protesting caste oppression, e.g. Bama's 'Karukku', Valmiki's 'Joothan', Limbale's 'Akkarmashi'."}, {"term": "Testimonio", "pron": "टेस-टि-मो-नियो (tes-ti-MOH-nee-oh)", "hindi": "पूरे समुदाय की ओर से दी गई आत्मकथात्मक गवाही", "net": "First-person narrative bearing witness to collective oppression; applied to Dalit autobiographies like 'Karukku'."}, {"term": "Vachana", "pron": "वचन (VUH-chuh-nuh)", "hindi": "कन्नड़ भक्ति कविता के छोटे पद", "net": "Free-verse religious lyrics of 12th-century Kannada Virashaiva saints (Basavanna, Akka Mahadevi), translated by Ramanujan in 'Speaking of Siva' (1973)."}, {"term": "Akam and Puram", "pron": "अकम-पुरम (UH-kum, POO-rum)", "hindi": "तमिल संगम काव्य - अंदर (प्रेम) और बाहर (युद्ध/समाज)", "net": "Two categories of classical Tamil Sangam poetry: akam (interior, love) and puram (exterior, war, public life); Ramanujan's 'The Interior Landscape' (1967)."}, {"term": "Third Theatre", "pron": "थर्ड थिएटर (THURD THEE-uh-ter)", "hindi": "मंच-सज्जा बिना, दर्शकों के बीच किया जाने वाला नाटक", "net": "Badal Sircar's minimalist, flexible-space theatre fusing folk and proscenium traditions."}, {"term": "Onegin stanza", "pron": "वन्-गिन स्टैन्ज़ा (on-YAY-gin STAN-zuh)", "hindi": "पुश्किन जैसी 14 पंक्तियों की छंद-इकाई", "net": "Fourteen-line iambic tetrameter stanza from Pushkin's 'Eugene Onegin', used by Vikram Seth in 'The Golden Gate' (1986)."}, {"term": "Magic realism", "pron": "मै-जिक री-य-लिज़्म (MAJ-ik REE-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "यथार्थ में जादुई घटनाओं का सहज मेल", "net": "Narrative mode blending fantastic elements into realistic fiction, e.g. Rushdie's 'Midnight's Children'."}, {"term": "Malgudi", "pron": "मालगुडी (maal-GOO-dee)", "hindi": "आर.के. नारायण का काल्पनिक कस्बा", "net": "Fictional South Indian town in R.K. Narayan's novels and stories, from 'Swami and Friends' (1935) onward."}, {"term": "Hobson-Jobson", "pron": "हॉब-सन जॉब-सन (HOB-sun JOB-sun)", "hindi": "एंग्लो-इंडियन शब्दों का प्रसिद्ध शब्दकोश", "net": "Glossary of Anglo-Indian words and phrases by Henry Yule and A.C. Burnell (1886)."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who wrote that 'a single shelf of a good European library was worth the whole native literature of India and Arabia'?", "options": ["William Bentinck", "T.B. Macaulay", "Charles Wood", "H.H. Wilson"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The line is from Macaulay's Minute on Indian Education, 2 February 1835."}, {"q": "Macaulay's Minute on Indian Education is dated:", "options": ["7 March 1835", "2 February 1835", "19 July 1854", "11 December 1823"], "answer": 1, "exp": "2 Feb 1835 is the Minute; 7 March 1835 is Bentinck's resolution."}, {"q": "Which document is called the 'Magna Carta of English Education in India'?", "options": ["Charter Act 1813", "Macaulay's Minute", "Wood's Despatch", "Hunter Commission Report"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Wood's Despatch of 1854 is given this title."}, {"q": "Arrange chronologically: I. Wood's Despatch II. Charter Act providing one lakh rupees III. Macaulay's Minute IV. Indian Universities Act", "options": ["II, III, I, IV", "III, II, I, IV", "II, I, III, IV", "I, II, III, IV"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1813, 1835, 1854, 1904."}, {"q": "The universities of Calcutta, Bombay and Madras were established in 1857 on the model of:", "options": ["Oxford University", "Cambridge University", "London University", "Edinburgh University"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Wood's Despatch recommended the London University model."}, {"q": "In Kachru's model, India belongs to the:", "options": ["Inner Circle", "Outer Circle", "Expanding Circle", "Core Circle"], "answer": 1, "exp": "India is an Outer Circle, norm-developing, ESL country."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Inner Circle (b) Outer Circle (c) Expanding Circle with (i) norm-dependent (ii) norm-providing (iii) norm-developing", "options": ["a-ii, b-iii, c-i", "a-i, b-ii, c-iii", "a-iii, b-i, c-ii", "a-ii, b-i, c-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Inner provides, Outer develops, Expanding depends on norms."}, {"q": "'The Indianization of English' (1983) was written by:", "options": ["Raja Rao", "Braj B. Kachru", "R.K. Narayan", "Edgar Schneider"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Braj Kachru's landmark study of Indian English."}, {"q": "Which is NOT a phase of Schneider's Dynamic Model?", "options": ["Foundation", "Nativization", "Differentiation", "Fossilization"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Fossilization is an SLA concept (Selinker), not a Schneider phase."}, {"q": "'One has to convey in a language that is not one's own the spirit that is one's own.' This is from:", "options": ["Rushdie's 'Imaginary Homelands'", "Raja Rao's Foreword to 'Kanthapura'", "Kamala Das's 'An Introduction'", "Tagore's 'Nationalism'"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Raja Rao, Foreword to Kanthapura (1938)."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Indian English is syllable-timed. Reason (R): Indian languages generally give syllables roughly equal duration.", "options": ["Both A and R true; R explains A", "Both true; R does not explain A", "A true, R false", "A false, R true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "The syllable-timed rhythm of Indian English is attributed to L1 transfer."}, {"q": "Which of the following is an Indian English coinage?", "options": ["Postpone", "Prepone", "Delay", "Defer"], "answer": 1, "exp": "'Prepone' (to bring forward) is a typical IndE neologism."}, {"q": "The first book written by an Indian in English is generally taken to be:", "options": ["The Travels of Dean Mahomet", "Rajmohan's Wife", "The Fakeer of Jungheera", "The Captive Ladie"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Sake Dean Mahomet, 1794."}, {"q": "'Rajmohan's Wife', regarded as the first Indian novel in English, was written by:", "options": ["Toru Dutt", "Bankim Chandra Chatterjee", "Michael Madhusudan Dutt", "Kylas Chunder Dutt"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, serialised 1864."}, {"q": "'The Fakeer of Jungheera' (1828) is a work of:", "options": ["Kashiprasad Ghosh", "H.L.V. Derozio", "Toru Dutt", "Manmohan Ghose"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Derozio, teacher at Hindu College and inspirer of Young Bengal."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Toru Dutt (b) Sarojini Naidu (c) Sri Aurobindo (d) Tagore with (i) Savitri (ii) Gitanjali (iii) The Golden Threshold (iv) A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii", "a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Toru Dutt-Sheaf (1876), Naidu-Golden Threshold (1905), Aurobindo-Savitri, Tagore-Gitanjali."}, {"q": "The first Indian English poet to win the Sahitya Akademi Award was:", "options": ["Nissim Ezekiel", "A.K. Ramanujan", "Jayanta Mahapatra", "Kamala Das"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Mahapatra for 'Relationship' in 1981."}, {"q": "'Jejuri', which won the Commonwealth Poetry Prize, was written by:", "options": ["Arun Kolatkar", "Dilip Chitre", "Adil Jussawalla", "Gieve Patel"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Arun Kolatkar, 'Jejuri' (1976)."}, {"q": "Which collection is considered the beginning of modern Indian English poetry?", "options": ["The Exact Name", "A Time to Change", "The Striders", "Summer in Calcutta"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Nissim Ezekiel's 'A Time to Change' (1952)."}, {"q": "Arrange R.K. Narayan's novels chronologically: I. The Guide II. Swami and Friends III. The English Teacher IV. The Vendor of Sweets", "options": ["II, III, I, IV", "II, I, III, IV", "III, II, I, IV", "II, III, IV, I"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1935, 1945, 1958, 1967."}, {"q": "Which novel has Bakha as protagonist and a preface by E.M. Forster?", "options": ["Coolie", "Untouchable", "Kanthapura", "The Village"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Mulk Raj Anand's 'Untouchable' (1935)."}, {"q": "In 'Kanthapura' the story is narrated by:", "options": ["Moorthy", "Achakka", "Range Gowda", "Ratna"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The old woman Achakka narrates in sthala-purana style."}, {"q": "The first novel in English to win the Sahitya Akademi Award was:", "options": ["The Serpent and the Rope", "The Guide", "Untouchable", "Train to Pakistan"], "answer": 1, "exp": "R.K. Narayan's 'The Guide', award in 1960."}, {"q": "Match the Booker winners: (a) 1981 (b) 1997 (c) 2006 (d) 2008 with (i) The White Tiger (ii) The Inheritance of Loss (iii) Midnight's Children (iv) The God of Small Things", "options": ["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii", "a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i", "a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Rushdie 1981, Roy 1997, Kiran Desai 2006, Adiga 2008."}, {"q": "The first writer in English to receive the Jnanpith Award was:", "options": ["Vikram Seth", "Amitav Ghosh", "Salman Rushdie", "Ruskin Bond"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Amitav Ghosh, 54th Jnanpith (2018)."}, {"q": "'Hayavadana' by Girish Karnad is partly based on:", "options": ["Thomas Mann's 'The Transposed Heads'", "Kalidasa's 'Shakuntalam'", "Bhasa's 'Urubhangam'", "Tagore's 'Chitra'"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Karnad drew on the Kathasaritsagara tale via Mann's novella."}, {"q": "The first Indian playwright in English to receive the Sahitya Akademi Award is:", "options": ["Asif Currimbhoy", "Mahesh Dattani", "Gurcharan Das", "Manjula Padmanabhan"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Dattani, for 'Final Solutions and Other Plays' (1998)."}, {"q": "'Siting Translation: History, Post-Structuralism and the Colonial Context' (1992) is by:", "options": ["G.N. Devy", "Tejaswini Niranjana", "Sujit Mukherjee", "Harish Trivedi"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Tejaswini Niranjana's critique of translation under colonialism."}, {"q": "Which pair is INCORRECTLY matched (work - translator)?", "options": ["Samskara - A.K. Ramanujan", "Imaginary Maps - G.C. Spivak", "Karukku - Lakshmi Holmstrom", "Tomb of Sand - Deepa Bhasthi"], "answer": 3, "exp": "'Tomb of Sand' was translated by Daisy Rockwell; Bhasthi translated 'Heart Lamp'."}, {"q": "The Sahitya Akademi was formally inaugurated on:", "options": ["15 August 1947", "15 December 1952", "12 March 1954", "26 January 1950"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Set up by resolution of 15 Dec 1952 and inaugurated 12 March 1954."}]}, {"id": 7, "title": "Cultural Studies", "overview": "Is unit me Birmingham CCCS (Hoggart, Williams, Hall, Thompson), Frankfurt School, Benjamin, Gramsci, Althusser, Barthes, Baudrillard, Bourdieu, Fiske se lekar subaltern, gender-queer, ecocriticism, digital aur Indian cultural studies tak sab aata hai. NET mostly author-work matching, coined terms, famous quotes, chronology aur assertion-reason poochta hai.", "topics": [{"title": "Background: From Arnold and Leavis to Cultural Studies", "points": ["Matthew Arnold, Culture and Anarchy (1869): culture is 'the best which has been thought and said in the world' (sweetness and light vs Philistines, Barbarians, Populace).", "F. R. Leavis, Mass Civilisation and Minority Culture (1930): culture is kept alive by a small minority; mass culture means 'levelling down'.", "Q. D. Leavis, Fiction and the Reading Public (1932): attack on popular fiction and best-sellers.", "F. R. Leavis and Denys Thompson, Culture and Environment (1933): training critical awareness against advertising and mass media; nostalgia for the 'organic community'.", "This 'Culture and Civilisation' tradition (Leavisism) saw mass culture as a threat; Cultural Studies began by both learning from and rejecting it.", "Cultural Studies treats culture as 'a whole way of life' (Williams), not only high art; studies everyday practices, media, youth, class, race and gender.", "Founding texts (often called 'culturalism'): Hoggart's The Uses of Literacy (1957), Williams's Culture and Society 1780-1950 (1958) and The Long Revolution (1961), E. P. Thompson's The Making of the English Working Class (1963).", "Stuart Hall, 'Cultural Studies: Two Paradigms' (1980) divides the field into culturalism (Hoggart, Williams, Thompson) and structuralism (Levi-Strauss, Barthes, Althusser).", "Popular textbook: John Storey, Cultural Theory and Popular Culture: An Introduction (first ed. 1993) gives six definitions of popular culture."]}, {"title": "Birmingham CCCS (Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies)", "points": ["Founded in 1964 at the University of Birmingham by Richard Hoggart, its first director.", "Stuart Hall joined in 1964, became acting director in 1968 and director (1972-1979); then moved to the Open University (1979).", "Later directors: Richard Johnson (1979-), Jorge Lorrain; the department was closed by the university in 2002.", "Method: interdisciplinary, Marxist (Gramsci, Althusser), semiotic and ethnographic; 'Working Papers in Cultural Studies' (from 1971).", "Resistance Through Rituals: Youth Subcultures in Post-War Britain (1975), ed. Stuart Hall and Tony Jefferson.", "Policing the Crisis: Mugging, the State, and Law and Order (1978), Hall et al.: moral panic over 'mugging' and race.", "Women Take Issue (1978) by the CCCS Women's Studies Group: feminist intervention into the Centre.", "The Empire Strikes Back: Race and Racism in 70s Britain (1982), CCCS collective incl. Paul Gilroy.", "Paul Willis, Learning to Labour: How Working Class Kids Get Working Class Jobs (1977): ethnography of 'the lads'.", "David Morley, The 'Nationwide' Audience (1980, with Charlotte Brunsdon): audience study testing Hall's encoding/decoding."]}, {"title": "Richard Hoggart and E. P. Thompson", "points": ["Richard Hoggart (1918-2014), The Uses of Literacy: Aspects of Working-Class Life with Special Reference to Publications and Entertainments (1957).", "Part one: the older 'full rich life' of working-class Leeds (Hunslet); part two: 'Yielding Place to New' - criticises Americanised mass entertainment.", "Famous phrases: 'candy-floss world', 'juke-box boys', 'shiny barbarism'; the anxious 'scholarship boy' uprooted by education.", "Hoggart was a defence witness in the Lady Chatterley's Lover obscenity trial (1960).", "E. P. Thompson (1924-1993), The Making of the English Working Class (1963): 'history from below'.", "Opening claim: 'The working class did not rise like the sun at an appointed time. It was present at its own making.'", "Quote: 'I am seeking to rescue the poor stockinger, the Luddite cropper, the obsolete hand-loom weaver ... from the enormous condescension of posterity.'", "Class for Thompson is a 'historical relationship' and 'happening', not a static thing or category.", "Other works: William Morris: Romantic to Revolutionary (1955), Whigs and Hunters (1975), The Poverty of Theory (1978, attack on Althusser's structuralism), Customs in Common (1991, 'moral economy')."]}, {"title": "Raymond Williams and Cultural Materialism", "points": ["Raymond Williams (1921-1988), Welsh critic; 'Culture is Ordinary' (essay, 1958).", "Culture and Society 1780-1950 (1958): traces 'culture' through Burke, Coleridge, Arnold, Carlyle, Eliot, Leavis; five keywords: industry, democracy, class, art, culture.", "The Long Revolution (1961): three definitions of culture - ideal, documentary, social ('a particular way of life').", "'Structure of feeling': lived experience of a period (first in Preface to Film, 1954; developed in The Long Revolution).", "Marxism and Literature (1977): dominant, residual and emergent cultures; rethinks base and superstructure; coins 'cultural materialism'.", "Keywords: A Vocabulary of Culture and Society (1976): 'Culture is one of the two or three most complicated words in the English language.'", "The Country and the City (1973): critique of pastoral myths of a lost 'organic' rural England.", "Television: Technology and Cultural Form (1974): concept of 'flow' in TV programming.", "Other works: Drama from Ibsen to Brecht (1968), Modern Tragedy (1966), Problems in Materialism and Culture (1980), Culture (1981); novel Border Country (1960).", "'Cultural materialism' was later used by Jonathan Dollimore and Alan Sinfield in Political Shakespeare (1985); its US cousin is New Historicism (Greenblatt)."]}, {"title": "Stuart Hall", "points": ["Stuart Hall (1932-2014), born in Kingston, Jamaica; Rhodes Scholar at Oxford; founding editor of New Left Review (1960).", "'Encoding and Decoding in the Television Discourse' (CCCS paper, 1973; revised as 'Encoding/Decoding', 1980).", "Three reading positions: dominant-hegemonic (preferred), negotiated, and oppositional.", "Meaning is not fixed in the text: the moments of encoding and decoding are 'relatively autonomous'.", "'Cultural Studies: Two Paradigms' (1980): culturalism vs structuralism.", "'Notes on Deconstructing the Popular' (1981): popular culture is a site of struggle for and against the culture of the powerful.", "'The Great Moving Right Show' (1979) and The Hard Road to Renewal (1988): Thatcherism as 'authoritarian populism'.", "'Cultural Identity and Diaspora' (1990): identity is 'a production which is never complete'; identity as 'being' and as 'becoming'.", "'New Ethnicities' (1988): end of the 'innocent notion of the essential black subject'.", "Representation: Cultural Representations and Signifying Practices (1997): reflective, intentional and constructionist approaches; theory of 'articulation'; 'the West and the Rest' (1992)."]}, {"title": "Youth Subcultures, Style and Moral Panics", "points": ["Stanley Cohen, Folk Devils and Moral Panics (1972): Mods and Rockers clashes (Clacton, 1964); coined the sociological use of 'moral panic'.", "Dick Hebdige, Subculture: The Meaning of Style (1979): punk, skinheads, Rastafarians; style as 'intentional communication'.", "Hebdige borrows 'bricolage' from Claude Levi-Strauss (The Savage Mind, 1962): objects re-used to make new meanings (e.g. punk safety pin).", "Hebdige: subcultures are 'incorporated' by two forms - the commodity form and the ideological form.", "Hebdige also uses 'homology' (Willis) and semiotic guerrilla warfare (Umberto Eco).", "Phil Cohen (1972): subcultures 'magically resolve' contradictions of the parent working-class culture.", "Angela McRobbie: 'Jackie: An Ideology of Adolescent Femininity' (1978); with Jenny Garber 'Girls and Subcultures' (1975).", "Post-subcultural theory (1990s-2000s): 'neo-tribes' (Michel Maffesoli), club cultures and 'subcultural capital' (Sarah Thornton, 1995)."]}, {"title": "Frankfurt School and the Culture Industry", "points": ["Institute for Social Research founded in Frankfurt in 1923; Max Horkheimer became director in 1930; exiled to New York during Nazism.", "Members: Max Horkheimer, Theodor W. Adorno, Herbert Marcuse, Walter Benjamin (associate), Leo Lowenthal, Erich Fromm; second generation: Jurgen Habermas.", "Horkheimer, 'Traditional and Critical Theory' (1937) defines 'critical theory'.", "Adorno and Horkheimer, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944 as Philosophical Fragments; 1947 Amsterdam).", "Key chapter: 'The Culture Industry: Enlightenment as Mass Deception' - culture produced like factory goods.", "Features of the culture industry: standardisation, pseudo-individuality, repetition, passive consumers, amusement as 'prolongation of work'.", "Adorno, 'On Popular Music' (1941): standardised hit songs; 'Culture Industry Reconsidered' (1963): 'the masses are not primary but secondary'.", "Adorno: 'To write poetry after Auschwitz is barbaric' ('Cultural Criticism and Society', 1949/1951); other works Minima Moralia (1951), Negative Dialectics (1966), Aesthetic Theory (1970).", "Herbert Marcuse, One-Dimensional Man (1964): 'repressive desublimation', false needs; Eros and Civilization (1955).", "Jurgen Habermas, The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962): bourgeois 'public sphere' of coffee houses and salons."]}, {"title": "Walter Benjamin", "points": ["Walter Benjamin (1892-1940), German-Jewish critic; died at Portbou on the French-Spanish border fleeing the Nazis.", "'The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction' (1935 draft; published 1936).", "Mechanical reproduction (photography, film) destroys the 'aura' of the original - its 'here and now', unique existence.", "Shift from 'cult value' to 'exhibition value'; art's basis shifts from ritual to politics.", "Conclusion: fascism 'aestheticizes politics'; communism responds by 'politicizing art'.", "'Theses on the Philosophy of History' (1940): angel of history (Paul Klee's Angelus Novus) faces the past as 'one single catastrophe'.", "Quote: 'There is no document of civilization which is not at the same time a document of barbarism.'", "The Arcades Project (Passagen-Werk, unfinished, 1927-1940): Paris arcades, the flaneur (from Baudelaire).", "Other essays: 'The Task of the Translator' (1923), 'The Author as Producer' (1934), 'The Storyteller' (1936); book The Origin of German Tragic Drama (1928, Trauerspiel).", "Unlike Adorno, Benjamin saw democratic potential in film and mass reproduction."]}, {"title": "Antonio Gramsci and Hegemony", "points": ["Antonio Gramsci (1891-1937), Italian Marxist, co-founder of the Italian Communist Party; imprisoned by Mussolini in 1926.", "Prison Notebooks (Quaderni del carcere), written 1929-1935; selections in English 1971 (Hoare and Nowell-Smith).", "Hegemony: rule by the dominant class through consent ('spontaneous consent' of the masses) backed by coercion.", "Civil society (church, schools, media - consent) vs political society/State (army, police, courts - coercion).", "Organic intellectuals (arise from a class and organise it) vs traditional intellectuals (clergy, professors who see themselves as autonomous).", "Quote: 'All men are intellectuals, but not all men have in society the function of intellectuals.'", "War of position (cultural trench warfare in civil society) vs war of manoeuvre (frontal attack on the state).", "Other terms: historic bloc, common sense vs good sense, passive revolution, counter-hegemony.", "Gramsci used 'subaltern' for subordinated groups (peasants of southern Italy) - later taken up by Subaltern Studies.", "The 'turn to Gramsci' at CCCS in the 1970s: popular culture seen as a site of negotiation between resistance and incorporation."]}, {"title": "Louis Althusser and Ideological State Apparatuses", "points": ["Louis Althusser (1918-1990), French structuralist Marxist.", "'Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses (Notes towards an Investigation)' (written 1969, published 1970; in Lenin and Philosophy, 1971).", "Repressive State Apparatus (RSA): government, army, police, courts, prisons - functions 'by violence'.", "Ideological State Apparatuses (ISAs): religious, educational, family, legal, political, trade-union, communications, cultural - function 'by ideology'.", "In mature capitalism the educational ISA (school) is the dominant ISA, replacing the Church.", "Definition: 'Ideology represents the imaginary relationship of individuals to their real conditions of existence.'", "'Ideology has a material existence' and 'Ideology has no history'.", "Interpellation (hailing): 'Hey, you there!' - ideology interpellates individuals as subjects.", "Other works: For Marx (1965), Reading Capital (1965, with Balibar and others).", "Key concepts: epistemological break (young vs mature Marx), overdetermination, problematic, symptomatic reading, relative autonomy of the superstructure; influenced Pierre Macherey, A Theory of Literary Production (1966)."]}, {"title": "Roland Barthes: Mythologies and Semiology", "points": ["Roland Barthes (1915-1980), French semiotician; Mythologies (1957; English selection 1972, trans. Annette Lavers).", "Short essays on everyday French culture: 'The World of Wrestling', 'Wine and Milk', 'Steak and Chips', 'Soap-powders and Detergents', 'The Face of Garbo', 'The New Citroen', 'Einstein's Brain', 'Toys', 'The Great Family of Man'.", "Theoretical essay 'Myth Today': myth is a 'type of speech' and a 'second-order semiological system'.", "First order: signifier + signified = sign; in myth this sign becomes the 'form', joined to a 'concept' to give 'signification'.", "Example: Paris-Match cover of a young Black soldier in French uniform saluting the tricolour - myth of French imperiality.", "Myth 'transforms history into nature'; myth is 'depoliticized speech'; it serves bourgeois ideology ('ex-nomination' of the bourgeoisie).", "Elements of Semiology (1964): denotation and connotation (borrowed from Hjelmslev).", "'The Death of the Author' (1967): 'the birth of the reader must be at the cost of the death of the Author'.", "S/Z (1970): readerly (lisible) vs writerly (scriptible) texts; five codes (hermeneutic, proairetic, semic, symbolic, cultural).", "The Pleasure of the Text (1973): plaisir vs jouissance; Camera Lucida (1980): studium vs punctum; Empire of Signs (1970) on Japan."]}, {"title": "Baudrillard, Simulation and Postmodern Media Theory", "points": ["Jean Baudrillard (1929-2007), French theorist; Simulacra and Simulation (1981; English 1994).", "Hyperreality: 'the generation by models of a real without origin or reality'; the map precedes the territory ('precession of simulacra').", "Four phases of the image: reflects a basic reality; masks and perverts reality; masks the absence of reality; bears no relation to any reality - its own pure simulacrum.", "Three orders of simulacra (Symbolic Exchange and Death, 1976): counterfeit (Renaissance), production (industrial), simulation (code-governed present).", "Disneyland is presented as imaginary 'in order to make us believe that the rest is real'.", "The Gulf War Did Not Take Place (1991): war as media spectacle; other works The System of Objects (1968), The Consumer Society (1970), For a Critique of the Political Economy of the Sign (1972).", "Guy Debord, The Society of the Spectacle (1967): 'All that once was directly lived has become mere representation.'", "Marshall McLuhan: 'the medium is the message' (Understanding Media, 1964); 'global village' (The Gutenberg Galaxy, 1962); hot vs cool media.", "Fredric Jameson, Postmodernism, or, the Cultural Logic of Late Capitalism (1991; essay 1984): pastiche, depthlessness, 'waning of affect'.", "Jean-Francois Lyotard, The Postmodern Condition: A Report on Knowledge (1979): 'incredulity toward metanarratives'."]}, {"title": "Pierre Bourdieu: Taste, Capital and Field", "points": ["Pierre Bourdieu (1930-2002), French sociologist.", "Distinction: A Social Critique of the Judgement of Taste (1979; English 1984): taste is socially produced and marks class.", "Quote: 'Taste classifies, and it classifies the classifier.'", "Forms of capital ('The Forms of Capital', 1986): economic, cultural, social, and symbolic capital.", "Cultural capital has three states: embodied, objectified, institutionalised (degrees).", "Habitus: durable, internalised dispositions shaping how people perceive and act (Outline of a Theory of Practice, 1972/1977).", "Field (champ): a structured social arena of struggle, e.g. The Field of Cultural Production (1993), The Rules of Art (1992).", "Symbolic violence: domination accepted as natural by the dominated; Reproduction in Education, Society and Culture (1970, with Jean-Claude Passeron).", "Taste types in Distinction: legitimate (high), middle-brow, popular; 'pure gaze' vs 'popular aesthetic'."]}, {"title": "John Fiske and Popular Culture Studies", "points": ["John Fiske (1939-2021): Reading Television (1978, with John Hartley), Television Culture (1987), Understanding Popular Culture (1989), Reading the Popular (1989).", "Fiske: popular culture is made by the people from the products of the culture industry, 'not imposed upon them'.", "Key ideas: polysemy of TV texts, 'semiotic democracy', 'producerly text' (between Barthes's readerly and writerly).", "Two economies of TV: financial economy (wealth) and cultural economy (meanings and pleasures).", "Michel de Certeau, The Practice of Everyday Life (1980; English 1984): strategies (of the powerful) vs tactics (of the weak); reading as 'poaching'.", "Henry Jenkins, Textual Poachers: Television Fans and Participatory Culture (1992); Convergence Culture (2006).", "Janice Radway, Reading the Romance (1984): ethnography of women romance readers in 'Smithton'.", "Ien Ang, Watching Dallas (1982; English 1985): 'ideology of mass culture' and viewers' pleasure.", "Mass-culture critics: Dwight Macdonald, 'A Theory of Mass Culture' (1953) - masscult and midcult; Clement Greenberg, 'Avant-Garde and Kitsch' (1939).", "Leslie Fiedler, 'Cross the Border - Close the Gap' (1969) urged closing the gap between high and popular culture."]}, {"title": "Subaltern Studies", "points": ["Term 'subaltern' taken from Gramsci; Subaltern Studies collective founded by Ranajit Guha; first volume 1982 (Oxford University Press, Delhi).", "Guha's manifesto 'On Some Aspects of the Historiography of Colonial India' (1982) attacks colonialist and bourgeois-nationalist elitism.", "Guha: Elementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency in Colonial India (1983); Dominance without Hegemony (1997).", "Members: Partha Chatterjee, Dipesh Chakrabarty, Shahid Amin, Gyanendra Pandey, David Hardiman, David Arnold, Gautam Bhadra.", "Shahid Amin, 'Gandhi as Mahatma' (1984); Event, Metaphor, Memory: Chauri Chaura 1922-1992 (1995).", "Partha Chatterjee: Nationalist Thought and the Colonial World: A Derivative Discourse? (1986); The Nation and Its Fragments (1993) - inner/spiritual vs outer/material domain.", "Dipesh Chakrabarty, Provincializing Europe (2000): Europe as 'hyperreal' and the 'waiting room of history'.", "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, 'Can the Subaltern Speak?' (1983 lecture; published 1988 in Marxism and the Interpretation of Culture): sati debate; 'White men are saving brown women from brown men'; answer: 'the subaltern cannot speak'.", "Spivak's 'strategic essentialism' ('Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography', 1985); Selected Subaltern Studies (1988), ed. Guha and Spivak, foreword by Edward Said."]}, {"title": "Gender, Sexuality and Queer Theory in Cultural Studies", "points": ["Laura Mulvey, 'Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema' (Screen, 1975): the 'male gaze', scopophilia; woman as 'to-be-looked-at-ness'.", "bell hooks, 'The Oppositional Gaze: Black Female Spectators' (1992).", "Michel Foucault, The History of Sexuality, Vol. 1 (1976): rejects the 'repressive hypothesis'; sexuality produced by discourse.", "Adrienne Rich, 'Compulsory Heterosexuality and Lesbian Existence' (1980): 'lesbian continuum'.", "Monique Wittig, 'One Is Not Born a Woman' (1981) and The Straight Mind (1992).", "Teresa de Lauretis coined 'queer theory' (1990 conference; 'Queer Theory: Lesbian and Gay Sexualities', differences, 1991).", "Judith Butler, Gender Trouble (1990): gender is 'performative' - 'a stylized repetition of acts'; Bodies That Matter (1993).", "Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick: Between Men (1985) - 'male homosocial desire'; Epistemology of the Closet (1990).", "Michael Warner popularised 'heteronormativity' (Fear of a Queer Planet, 1993); Lee Edelman, No Future (2004) - 'reproductive futurism'.", "Donna Haraway, 'A Cyborg Manifesto' (1985): 'I would rather be a cyborg than a goddess.'"]}, {"title": "Ecocriticism", "points": ["Term 'ecocriticism' coined by William Rueckert in 'Literature and Ecology: An Experiment in Ecocriticism' (1978).", "Cheryll Glotfelty and Harold Fromm (eds.), The Ecocriticism Reader (1996): 'ecocriticism is the study of the relationship between literature and the physical environment' (Glotfelty).", "ASLE (Association for the Study of Literature and Environment) founded in 1992; journal ISLE from 1993.", "Lawrence Buell, The Environmental Imagination (1995) on Thoreau; The Future of Environmental Criticism (2005) - 'first wave' and 'second wave'.", "British 'green studies': Jonathan Bate, Romantic Ecology (1991) and The Song of the Earth (2000); Greg Garrard, Ecocriticism (2004, New Critical Idiom).", "Rachel Carson, Silent Spring (1962); Leo Marx, The Machine in the Garden (1964); Arne Naess coined 'deep ecology' (1973).", "Francoise d'Eaubonne coined 'ecofeminism' (1974); Vandana Shiva, Staying Alive (1988).", "Ramachandra Guha and Joan Martinez-Alier, Varieties of Environmentalism (1997): 'environmentalism of the poor'.", "Rob Nixon, Slow Violence and the Environmentalism of the Poor (2011); Amitav Ghosh, The Great Derangement (2016) on climate change and the novel.", "Timothy Morton: Ecology without Nature (2007), Hyperobjects (2013); Anthropocene term popularised by Paul Crutzen (2000)."]}, {"title": "Digital Culture, New Media and Indian Cultural Studies", "points": ["Ted Nelson coined 'hypertext' (1965); George Landow, Hypertext (1992); Espen Aarseth, Cybertext (1997) - 'ergodic literature'.", "William Gibson coined 'cyberspace' ('Burning Chrome', 1982; Neuromancer, 1984); Richard Dawkins coined 'meme' (The Selfish Gene, 1976).", "Lev Manovich, The Language of New Media (2001); Bolter and Grusin, Remediation (1999); Manuel Castells, The Rise of the Network Society (1996).", "N. Katherine Hayles, How We Became Posthuman (1999); Sherry Turkle, Alone Together (2011); Shoshana Zuboff, The Age of Surveillance Capitalism (2019).", "Arjun Appadurai, Modernity at Large (1996): five 'scapes' - ethnoscapes, mediascapes, technoscapes, financescapes, ideoscapes.", "Homi K. Bhabha, The Location of Culture (1994): hybridity, mimicry ('almost the same, but not quite'), ambivalence, Third Space.", "Ashis Nandy, The Intimate Enemy: Loss and Recovery of Self under Colonialism (1983).", "Tejaswini Niranjana, Siting Translation (1992); Susie Tharu and K. Lalita (eds.), Women Writing in India (1991-93); G. N. Devy, After Amnesia (1992).", "Kancha Ilaiah, Why I Am Not a Hindu (1996); A. K. Ramanujan, 'Three Hundred Ramayanas' (1991); Arvind Rajagopal, Politics after Television (2001) on the TV Ramayan.", "Centre for the Study of Culture and Society (CSCS), Bangalore, founded 1996; Ashish Rajadhyaksha, 'The Bollywoodization of the Indian Cinema' (2003)."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Cultural Studies", "pron": "कल्चरल स्टडीज़ (KUL-chuh-rul STUH-deez)", "hindi": "संस्कृति, मीडिया और रोज़मर्रा की ज़िंदगी में सत्ता का अध्ययन", "net": "An interdisciplinary field, institutionalised at the Birmingham CCCS (1964), that studies culture as 'a whole way of life' and as a site of power, ideology and resistance."}, {"term": "Culturalism", "pron": "कल्चरलिज़्म (KUL-chuh-ruh-liz-um)", "hindi": "आम लोगों के जीवित अनुभव को संस्कृति का केंद्र मानना", "net": "Stuart Hall's term (1980) for the humanist, experience-centred paradigm of Hoggart, Williams and Thompson, opposed to structuralism."}, {"term": "Structure of Feeling", "pron": "स्ट्रक्चर ऑफ़ फ़ीलिंग (STRUK-chur uv FEE-ling)", "hindi": "किसी दौर की साझा भावना या जिया गया अनुभव", "net": "Raymond Williams's term (Preface to Film, 1954; The Long Revolution, 1961) for the lived, felt sense of life of a particular generation or period."}, {"term": "Cultural Materialism", "pron": "कल्चरल मटीरियलिज़्म (KUL-chur-ul muh-TEER-ee-uh-liz-um)", "hindi": "संस्कृति को भौतिक-आर्थिक हालात से जोड़कर पढ़ना", "net": "Williams's term (Marxism and Literature, 1977) for analysing culture as material production; later applied to Renaissance texts by Dollimore and Sinfield (Political Shakespeare, 1985)."}, {"term": "Dominant, Residual, Emergent", "pron": "डॉमिनेंट, रेज़िड्युअल, इमर्जेंट (DOM-i-nunt, ri-ZID-yoo-ul, i-MUR-junt)", "hindi": "हावी, पुरानी बची हुई, और नई उभरती संस्कृति", "net": "Williams's threefold model (Marxism and Literature, 1977) of cultural elements in any period: the ruling, the survivals of the past, and new meanings and practices."}, {"term": "Culture Industry", "pron": "कल्चर इंडस्ट्री (KUL-chur IN-dus-tree)", "hindi": "फ़ैक्ट्री की तरह बनाई-बेची जाने वाली मनोरंजन संस्कृति", "net": "Adorno and Horkheimer's term in Dialectic of Enlightenment (1947) for the standardised, mass-produced culture of capitalism that produces passive conformity."}, {"term": "Pseudo-individuality", "pron": "स्यूडो-इंडिविजुअलिटी (SOO-doh in-di-vi-joo-AL-i-tee)", "hindi": "नकली अनोखापन, असल में सब एक जैसा", "net": "Adorno's term ('On Popular Music', 1941) for the superficial differences that disguise the standardisation of mass-cultural products."}, {"term": "Aura", "pron": "ऑरा (AW-ruh)", "hindi": "मूल कलाकृति की अनोखी, पवित्र चमक", "net": "Walter Benjamin's term ('The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction', 1936) for the unique presence in time and space of the original work, which mechanical reproduction destroys."}, {"term": "Flaneur", "pron": "फ़्लानर (flah-NUR)", "hindi": "शहर की गलियों में बेमक़सद घूमने-देखने वाला", "net": "The idle urban stroller-observer of modern Paris, from Baudelaire, theorised by Walter Benjamin in The Arcades Project."}, {"term": "Hegemony", "pron": "हेजेमनी (hi-JEM-uh-nee)", "hindi": "ज़बरदस्ती से नहीं, सहमति से चलने वाला वर्चस्व", "net": "Gramsci's concept (Prison Notebooks, 1929-35) of the leadership of a dominant class secured chiefly through the consent of subordinate groups, backed by coercion."}, {"term": "Organic Intellectual", "pron": "ऑर्गैनिक इंटेलेक्चुअल (or-GAN-ik in-tuh-LEK-choo-ul)", "hindi": "अपने वर्ग से निकला और उसी के लिए सोचने वाला बुद्धिजीवी", "net": "Gramsci's term for intellectuals who emerge from and articulate the interests of a social class, as against 'traditional intellectuals'."}, {"term": "War of Position", "pron": "वॉर ऑफ़ पोज़ीशन (WOR uv puh-ZI-shun)", "hindi": "संस्कृति और समाज में धीरे-धीरे लड़ी जाने वाली लड़ाई", "net": "Gramsci's term for long-term cultural and ideological struggle within civil society, contrasted with the 'war of manoeuvre' (direct assault on the state)."}, {"term": "Ideological State Apparatus", "pron": "आइडियोलॉजिकल स्टेट एपरेटस (eye-dee-uh-LOJ-i-kul STAYT ap-uh-RAY-tus)", "hindi": "स्कूल, परिवार, धर्म, मीडिया जैसी संस्थाएँ जो विचारधारा फैलाती हैं", "net": "Althusser's term ('Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses', 1970) for institutions that function 'by ideology' (school, church, family, media), as against the Repressive State Apparatus."}, {"term": "Repressive State Apparatus", "pron": "रिप्रेसिव स्टेट एपरेटस (ri-PRES-iv STAYT ap-uh-RAY-tus)", "hindi": "पुलिस, सेना, अदालत जैसी दबाने वाली ताक़तें", "net": "Althusser's term for the government, army, police, courts and prisons, which function predominantly 'by violence'."}, {"term": "Interpellation", "pron": "इंटरपेलेशन (in-tur-puh-LAY-shun)", "hindi": "विचारधारा का हमें 'ऐ, तुम!' कहकर पुकारना और हमें 'subject' बनाना", "net": "Althusser's term for the process by which ideology 'hails' ('Hey, you there!') concrete individuals and constitutes them as subjects."}, {"term": "Overdetermination", "pron": "ओवरडिटर्मिनेशन (oh-vur-di-tur-mi-NAY-shun)", "hindi": "एक घटना के पीछे कई कारण एक साथ होना", "net": "Althusser's term (For Marx, 1965), from Freud, for the multiple, interacting causes of any social contradiction, rejecting simple economic determinism."}, {"term": "Symptomatic Reading", "pron": "सिम्प्टोमैटिक रीडिंग (simp-tuh-MAT-ik REE-ding)", "hindi": "पाठ की चुप्पियों और कमियों को पढ़ना", "net": "Althusser's method (Reading Capital, 1965) of reading a text for its absences, silences and gaps, which reveal its hidden problematic."}, {"term": "Myth", "pron": "मिथ (MITH)", "hindi": "ऐसा अर्थ जो इतिहास को 'स्वाभाविक' बताकर पेश करे", "net": "For Barthes ('Myth Today', Mythologies, 1957) a second-order semiological system and 'depoliticized speech' that turns history into nature."}, {"term": "Connotation", "pron": "कनोटेशन (kon-uh-TAY-shun)", "hindi": "शब्द या चित्र का छिपा, सांस्कृतिक अर्थ", "net": "The secondary, culturally coded meaning of a sign beyond its literal denotation; developed by Barthes (Elements of Semiology, 1964) from Hjelmslev."}, {"term": "Punctum", "pron": "पंक्टम (PUNK-tum)", "hindi": "फ़ोटो की वो बारीक चीज़ जो दिल को चुभ जाए", "net": "Barthes's term (Camera Lucida, 1980) for the poignant, personal detail in a photograph that 'pricks' the viewer, as against the studium (general cultural interest)."}, {"term": "Jouissance", "pron": "ज़ुइसाँस (zhwee-SAHNS)", "hindi": "चरम, तोड़-फोड़ करने वाला आनंद", "net": "Barthes's term (The Pleasure of the Text, 1973) for disruptive bliss in reading, as opposed to comfortable 'plaisir'."}, {"term": "Simulacrum", "pron": "सिम्युलेक्रम (sim-yoo-LAY-krum)", "hindi": "असली के बिना बनी नकल", "net": "For Baudrillard (Simulacra and Simulation, 1981) a copy without an original; in the final phase the image 'bears no relation to any reality'."}, {"term": "Hyperreality", "pron": "हाइपररियलिटी (hy-pur-ree-AL-i-tee)", "hindi": "नकल जो असली से भी ज़्यादा असली लगे", "net": "Baudrillard's term for 'the generation by models of a real without origin or reality', where simulation replaces the real (e.g. Disneyland)."}, {"term": "Spectacle", "pron": "स्पेक्टेकल (SPEK-tuh-kul)", "hindi": "तस्वीरों के ज़रिए बना सामाजिक रिश्ता, दिखावे का समाज", "net": "Guy Debord's term (The Society of the Spectacle, 1967) for 'a social relation among people, mediated by images'."}, {"term": "Cultural Capital", "pron": "कल्चरल कैपिटल (KUL-chur-ul KAP-i-tul)", "hindi": "शिक्षा, रुचि, ज्ञान जैसी सांस्कृतिक पूँजी", "net": "Bourdieu's term (Distinction, 1979; 'The Forms of Capital', 1986) for non-economic assets (education, tastes, credentials) existing in embodied, objectified and institutionalised states."}, {"term": "Habitus", "pron": "हैबिटस (HAB-i-tus)", "hindi": "समाज से सीखी आदतें और सोचने का ढंग", "net": "Bourdieu's term for a system of durable, internalised dispositions that generate perceptions and practices (Outline of a Theory of Practice, 1972)."}, {"term": "Symbolic Violence", "pron": "सिम्बॉलिक वायलेंस (sim-BOL-ik VY-uh-luns)", "hindi": "ऐसा दबदबा जिसे दबे हुए लोग भी स्वाभाविक मान लें", "net": "Bourdieu's term for domination exercised through culture and accepted as legitimate by those subjected to it."}, {"term": "Encoding/Decoding", "pron": "एनकोडिंग/डीकोडिंग (en-KOH-ding / dee-KOH-ding)", "hindi": "संदेश बनाना और दर्शक का उसे अपने ढंग से समझना", "net": "Stuart Hall's model (1973/1980) of mass communication in which audiences may decode a text in dominant-hegemonic, negotiated or oppositional ways."}, {"term": "Preferred Reading", "pron": "प्रेफ़र्ड रीडिंग (pri-FURD REE-ding)", "hindi": "वो अर्थ जो बनाने वाला चाहता है कि दर्शक समझे", "net": "In Hall's encoding/decoding model, the dominant meaning inscribed in a media text, which audiences may accept, negotiate or oppose."}, {"term": "Articulation", "pron": "आर्टिक्युलेशन (ar-tik-yoo-LAY-shun)", "hindi": "अलग-अलग चीज़ों का किसी समय विशेष पर जुड़ जाना", "net": "Hall's (from Laclau) concept of a contingent, non-necessary linkage between different elements that forms a unity under specific conditions."}, {"term": "Moral Panic", "pron": "मॉरल पैनिक (MOR-ul PAN-ik)", "hindi": "किसी समूह को समाज के लिए ख़तरा बताकर फैलाया गया डर", "net": "Stanley Cohen's term (Folk Devils and Moral Panics, 1972) for the media-amplified reaction defining a group (e.g. Mods and Rockers) as a threat to social values."}, {"term": "Subculture", "pron": "सबकल्चर (SUB-kul-chur)", "hindi": "मुख्य संस्कृति के भीतर अलग पहचान वाला छोटा समूह", "net": "A group within a larger culture marked by distinctive style and values; theorised at CCCS (Resistance Through Rituals, 1975) and by Hebdige (1979) as symbolic resistance."}, {"term": "Bricolage", "pron": "ब्रिकोलाज़ (bree-koh-LAHZH)", "hindi": "पुरानी चीज़ों को जोड़कर नया अर्थ बनाना", "net": "Levi-Strauss's term (The Savage Mind, 1962), used by Hebdige (Subculture, 1979) for subcultures' re-use of objects to create new, oppositional meanings."}, {"term": "Semiotic Democracy", "pron": "सेमियोटिक डेमोक्रेसी (sem-ee-OT-ik di-MOK-ruh-see)", "hindi": "दर्शकों को टीवी के अर्थ ख़ुद बनाने की आज़ादी", "net": "John Fiske's term (Television Culture, 1987) for audiences' freedom to make their own meanings from polysemic television texts."}, {"term": "Producerly Text", "pron": "प्रोड्यूसरली टेक्स्ट (pruh-DOO-sur-lee TEKST)", "hindi": "आसान पर कई अर्थ देने वाला लोकप्रिय पाठ", "net": "Fiske's term for a popular text that is accessible like Barthes's readerly text yet open, like the writerly, to the reader's own meanings."}, {"term": "Tactics", "pron": "टैक्टिक्स (TAK-tiks)", "hindi": "कमज़ोर लोगों की रोज़ की चालाक जुगत", "net": "Michel de Certeau's term (The Practice of Everyday Life, 1980) for the improvised practices of the weak, as against the 'strategies' of institutions."}, {"term": "Subaltern", "pron": "सबाल्टर्न (suh-BAWL-turn)", "hindi": "समाज के हाशिए पर पड़े, दबे हुए लोग", "net": "Gramsci's term for subordinated social groups, adopted by Ranajit Guha's Subaltern Studies (1982-) and problematised in Spivak's 'Can the Subaltern Speak?' (1988)."}, {"term": "Strategic Essentialism", "pron": "स्ट्रैटेजिक एसेंशियलिज़्म (struh-TEE-jik i-SEN-shuh-liz-um)", "hindi": "राजनीतिक मक़सद के लिए अस्थायी रूप से एक साझा पहचान अपनाना", "net": "Spivak's term (1985) for the provisional, tactical use of a shared group identity by marginalised groups for political ends."}, {"term": "Epistemic Violence", "pron": "एपिस्टेमिक वायलेंस (ep-i-STEE-mik VY-uh-luns)", "hindi": "ज्ञान के नाम पर दूसरों की आवाज़ मिटा देना", "net": "Spivak's term ('Can the Subaltern Speak?') for the violence done by colonial and Western knowledge systems in constituting and silencing the colonised Other."}, {"term": "Male Gaze", "pron": "मेल गेज़ (MAYL GAYZ)", "hindi": "फ़िल्म में औरत को मर्द की नज़र से भोग की वस्तु दिखाना", "net": "Laura Mulvey's term ('Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema', 1975) for the way classical cinema positions woman as image and man as bearer of the look."}, {"term": "Performativity", "pron": "परफ़ॉर्मेटिविटी (pur-for-muh-TIV-i-tee)", "hindi": "जेंडर का बार-बार दोहराए गए व्यवहार से बनना", "net": "Judith Butler's concept (Gender Trouble, 1990) that gender is produced through 'the repeated stylization of the body', not an inner essence."}, {"term": "Heteronormativity", "pron": "हेटरोनॉर्मेटिविटी (het-uh-roh-nor-muh-TIV-i-tee)", "hindi": "विषमलैंगिकता को ही 'सामान्य' मान लेना", "net": "Michael Warner's term (Fear of a Queer Planet, 1993) for the institutions and assumptions that make heterosexuality seem natural and normative."}, {"term": "Homosocial Desire", "pron": "होमोसोशल डिज़ायर (hoh-moh-SOH-shul di-ZY-ur)", "hindi": "पुरुषों के आपसी सामाजिक बंधन जिनमें छिपी चाह हो", "net": "Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick's term (Between Men, 1985) for the continuum of male bonds, often triangulated through a woman."}, {"term": "Queer Theory", "pron": "क्वीयर थ्योरी (KWEER THEE-uh-ree)", "hindi": "लैंगिक और यौन पहचानों की तयशुदा श्रेणियों पर सवाल", "net": "A theory, so named by Teresa de Lauretis (1990-91), that deconstructs fixed categories of sex, gender and sexuality; key figures Butler and Sedgwick."}, {"term": "Ecocriticism", "pron": "इकोक्रिटिसिज़्म (EE-koh-KRIT-i-siz-um)", "hindi": "साहित्य और पर्यावरण के रिश्ते का अध्ययन", "net": "Term coined by William Rueckert (1978); defined by Cheryll Glotfelty (1996) as 'the study of the relationship between literature and the physical environment'."}, {"term": "Deep Ecology", "pron": "डीप इकोलॉजी (DEEP ee-KOL-uh-jee)", "hindi": "प्रकृति का अपना मूल्य मानने वाली पर्यावरण सोच", "net": "Arne Naess's term (1973) for a biocentric philosophy asserting the intrinsic value of all life, against 'shallow', human-centred environmentalism."}, {"term": "Ecofeminism", "pron": "इकोफ़ेमिनिज़्म (ee-koh-FEM-i-niz-um)", "hindi": "औरत और प्रकृति के शोषण को जोड़कर देखने वाला नारीवाद", "net": "Term coined by Francoise d'Eaubonne (1974) linking the domination of women and of nature; Indian voice Vandana Shiva (Staying Alive, 1988)."}, {"term": "Slow Violence", "pron": "स्लो वायलेंस (SLOH VY-uh-luns)", "hindi": "धीरे-धीरे, बिना दिखे होने वाली पर्यावरणीय तबाही", "net": "Rob Nixon's term (2011) for gradual, often invisible environmental harm that falls mostly on the poor."}, {"term": "Hybridity", "pron": "हाइब्रिडिटी (hy-BRID-i-tee)", "hindi": "दो संस्कृतियों के मिलने से बनी नई मिश्रित पहचान", "net": "Homi Bhabha's term (The Location of Culture, 1994) for new transcultural forms produced in the 'Third Space' of colonial encounter."}, {"term": "Mediascape", "pron": "मीडियास्केप (MEE-dee-uh-skayp)", "hindi": "दुनिया भर में फैले मीडिया चित्रों का जाल", "net": "One of Arjun Appadurai's five 'scapes' of global cultural flow (Modernity at Large, 1996), with ethnoscapes, technoscapes, financescapes and ideoscapes."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who founded the Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies at Birmingham in 1964?", "options": ["Richard Hoggart", "Stuart Hall", "Raymond Williams", "E. P. Thompson"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Richard Hoggart founded CCCS in 1964 and was its first director."}, {"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (a) The Uses of Literacy (b) The Making of the English Working Class (c) Culture and Society 1780-1950 (d) Keywords", "options": ["c, a, b, d", "a, c, b, d", "a, b, c, d", "c, b, a, d"], "answer": 1, "exp": "1957, 1958, 1963, 1976."}, {"q": "'Structure of feeling' is a concept associated with:", "options": ["Richard Hoggart", "Terry Eagleton", "Raymond Williams", "Louis Althusser"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Williams developed it in Preface to Film (1954) and The Long Revolution (1961)."}, {"q": "Match the List I with List II:\nI. Dominant, residual, emergent  II. Interpellation  III. Aura  IV. Hegemony\n1. Benjamin  2. Gramsci  3. Williams  4. Althusser", "options": ["I-4, II-3, III-2, IV-1", "I-3, II-1, III-4, IV-2", "I-2, II-4, III-1, IV-3", "I-3, II-4, III-1, IV-2"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Williams-dominant/residual/emergent; Althusser-interpellation; Benjamin-aura; Gramsci-hegemony."}, {"q": "In Stuart Hall's 'Encoding/Decoding', which is NOT one of the three decoding positions?", "options": ["Aberrant-hermeneutic", "Dominant-hegemonic", "Negotiated", "Oppositional"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Hall names dominant-hegemonic, negotiated and oppositional readings."}, {"q": "'The Culture Industry: Enlightenment as Mass Deception' is a chapter of:", "options": ["One-Dimensional Man", "Dialectic of Enlightenment", "Minima Moralia", "The Society of the Spectacle"], "answer": 1, "exp": "It appears in Adorno and Horkheimer's Dialectic of Enlightenment (1947)."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Benjamin argues that mechanical reproduction withers the aura of a work of art.\nReason (R): Reproduction substitutes a plurality of copies for a unique existence.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Benjamin explicitly links loss of aura to replacing unique existence with mass copies."}, {"q": "Who said 'There is no document of civilization which is not at the same time a document of barbarism'?", "options": ["Theodor Adorno", "Herbert Marcuse", "Antonio Gramsci", "Walter Benjamin"], "answer": 3, "exp": "From Benjamin's 'Theses on the Philosophy of History' (1940)."}, {"q": "According to Althusser, the dominant Ideological State Apparatus in mature capitalist societies is the:", "options": ["Educational ISA", "Religious ISA", "Family ISA", "Communications ISA"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Althusser says the school replaced the Church as the dominant ISA."}, {"q": "Which of the following belongs to the Repressive State Apparatus in Althusser's scheme?", "options": ["The family", "The courts and prisons", "The trade unions", "The press"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The RSA includes government, army, police, courts and prisons."}, {"q": "'Ideology represents the imaginary relationship of individuals to their real conditions of existence.' This definition is given by:", "options": ["Karl Marx", "Antonio Gramsci", "Louis Althusser", "Pierre Macherey"], "answer": 2, "exp": "From Althusser's 'Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses' (1970)."}, {"q": "Gramsci's distinction between 'war of position' and 'war of manoeuvre' appears in:", "options": ["For Marx", "The German Ideology", "History and Class Consciousness", "Prison Notebooks"], "answer": 3, "exp": "These concepts belong to Gramsci's Prison Notebooks (1929-35)."}, {"q": "In Barthes's 'Myth Today', the photograph of a young Black soldier saluting the French flag appears on the cover of:", "options": ["Paris-Match", "Le Monde", "Elle", "Les Temps Modernes"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Barthes's famous example is the Paris-Match cover."}, {"q": "Which essay is NOT part of Barthes's Mythologies?", "options": ["The World of Wrestling", "The Death of the Author", "Wine and Milk", "The Face of Garbo"], "answer": 1, "exp": "'The Death of the Author' is a separate essay of 1967."}, {"q": "Barthes describes myth as:", "options": ["A first-order linguistic system", "A purely denotative sign", "A second-order semiological system", "An archetypal pattern of the collective unconscious"], "answer": 2, "exp": "In 'Myth Today' myth is a second-order semiological system."}, {"q": "Baudrillard's essays claiming that a certain war 'did not take place' concern:", "options": ["The Vietnam War", "The Falklands War", "The Second World War", "The Gulf War"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The Gulf War Did Not Take Place (1991)."}, {"q": "Arrange Baudrillard's phases of the image in correct order:\n(a) It masks the absence of a basic reality (b) It is the reflection of a basic reality (c) It bears no relation to any reality (d) It masks and perverts a basic reality", "options": ["b, d, a, c", "b, a, d, c", "d, b, a, c", "a, b, d, c"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Reflection, perversion, masking absence, pure simulacrum."}, {"q": "'Taste classifies, and it classifies the classifier' is from:", "options": ["Fiske's Understanding Popular Culture", "Bourdieu's Distinction", "Williams's Keywords", "Hebdige's Subculture"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Bourdieu, Distinction (1979)."}, {"q": "Which is NOT one of the three states of cultural capital in Bourdieu?", "options": ["Embodied", "Objectified", "Commodified", "Institutionalised"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Bourdieu lists embodied, objectified and institutionalised states."}, {"q": "The concept of 'semiotic democracy' is associated with:", "options": ["Dwight Macdonald", "F. R. Leavis", "Jurgen Habermas", "John Fiske"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Fiske, Television Culture (1987)."}, {"q": "Match the authors with their works:\nI. Dick Hebdige  II. Stanley Cohen  III. Paul Willis  IV. Janice Radway\n1. Learning to Labour  2. Reading the Romance  3. Subculture: The Meaning of Style  4. Folk Devils and Moral Panics", "options": ["I-3, II-4, III-1, IV-2", "I-4, II-3, III-2, IV-1", "I-3, II-1, III-4, IV-2", "I-1, II-4, III-3, IV-2"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Hebdige 1979, Cohen 1972, Willis 1977, Radway 1984."}, {"q": "Hebdige borrowed the term 'bricolage' from:", "options": ["Roland Barthes", "Claude Levi-Strauss", "Jacques Derrida", "Umberto Eco"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Levi-Strauss used it in The Savage Mind (1962)."}, {"q": "The first volume of Subaltern Studies was published in 1982 under the editorship of:", "options": ["Partha Chatterjee", "Dipesh Chakrabarty", "Ranajit Guha", "Gayatri Spivak"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Ranajit Guha founded and edited the series."}, {"q": "'White men are saving brown women from brown men' is a sentence analysed in:", "options": ["Orientalism", "The Location of Culture", "Provincializing Europe", "Can the Subaltern Speak?"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Spivak uses it to discuss the British abolition of sati."}, {"q": "Who coined the term 'ecocriticism'?", "options": ["William Rueckert", "Cheryll Glotfelty", "Lawrence Buell", "Jonathan Bate"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Rueckert, 'Literature and Ecology: An Experiment in Ecocriticism' (1978)."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Laura Mulvey argues that classical Hollywood cinema is structured by a male gaze.\nReason (R): In it, woman is displayed as spectacle, connoting 'to-be-looked-at-ness'.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Mulvey's 1975 essay links the male gaze to woman as passive spectacle."}, {"q": "Gender as 'performative' is a key idea in:", "options": ["Sedgwick's Between Men", "Rich's 'Compulsory Heterosexuality'", "Butler's Gender Trouble", "Foucault's The History of Sexuality"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Butler, Gender Trouble (1990)."}, {"q": "Which is NOT one of Arjun Appadurai's five 'scapes'?", "options": ["Ethnoscapes", "Ideoscapes", "Financescapes", "Landscapes"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The five are ethnoscapes, mediascapes, technoscapes, financescapes and ideoscapes."}, {"q": "'The medium is the message' was said by:", "options": ["Marshall McLuhan", "Jean Baudrillard", "Guy Debord", "Raymond Williams"], "answer": 0, "exp": "McLuhan, Understanding Media (1964)."}, {"q": "Arrange chronologically: (a) Dialectic of Enlightenment (b) Mythologies (c) Simulacra and Simulation (d) Distinction", "options": ["b, a, d, c", "a, b, d, c", "a, b, c, d", "a, d, b, c"], "answer": 1, "exp": "1947, 1957, 1979, 1981."}]}, {"id": 8, "title": "Literary Criticism", "overview": "Is unit mein Plato se lekar Russian Formalism tak Western criticism aur Bharata ki Rasa se Kshemendra ke Auchitya tak Indian poetics aate hain. NET mein zyada sawaal famous quotes (kisne kaha), works ke saal, terms ke coiner, match-the-following aur chronology par aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Plato (c. 428-348 BCE): Poetry as Imitation", "points": ["Key works for criticism: Ion, Republic (Books II, III and X), Phaedrus, Symposium; all written as dialogues with Socrates as speaker.", "Mimesis: art imitates the world of appearances, which itself copies the world of Ideas/Forms, so art is 'thrice removed from the truth' (Republic X) [satya se teen guna door].", "Bed/couch analogy (Republic X): God makes the Idea of bed, the carpenter makes a bed, the painter only paints its appearance.", "Republic Book X speaks of 'the ancient quarrel between philosophy and poetry' and banishes imitative poets from the ideal state; only hymns to gods and praises of good men allowed.", "Moral charge: poetry 'feeds and waters the passions' instead of drying them up, and shows gods and heroes behaving badly (Books II-III).", "Book III distinguishes diegesis (simple narration in the poet's own voice) from mimesis (imitation through characters' speech); dithyramb = pure narration, drama = pure imitation, epic = mixed.", "Ion: the rhapsode Ion recites Homer; poetry comes from divine inspiration/madness, not art or knowledge; the magnet and iron-rings image shows inspiration passing from Muse to poet to rhapsode to audience.", "Phaedrus calls poetic madness a gift of the Muses; Plato himself is called a poet-philosopher who attacked poetry poetically.", "Plato's attack is answered by Aristotle (Poetics) and, in English, by Sidney's Apology for Poetry."]}, {"title": "Aristotle (384-322 BCE): Poetics", "points": ["Poetics (c. 335 BCE), 26 chapters, survives incomplete; the section on comedy is lost. Aristotle was Plato's pupil and tutor of Alexander.", "Answers Plato: imitation is natural to man and a source of learning and pleasure; poetry is 'more philosophical and a higher thing than history' because it expresses the universal, history the particular (Ch. 9).", "Definition of tragedy (Ch. 6): 'an imitation of an action that is serious, complete, and of a certain magnitude ... through pity and fear effecting the proper purgation (catharsis) of these emotions.'", "Six parts of tragedy in order of importance: Plot (mythos), Character (ethos), Thought (dianoia), Diction (lexis), Song (melos), Spectacle (opsis).", "'Plot is the first principle and, as it were, the soul of a tragedy'; a tragedy is possible without character but not without action.", "Plot must have a beginning, middle and end; complex plot contains peripeteia (reversal) and anagnorisis (recognition); third element is pathos (scene of suffering).", "The ideal tragic hero is neither wholly good nor wholly bad, of high fortune, who falls through hamartia (error of judgement), e.g. Oedipus; Sophocles' Oedipus Rex is his model tragedy.", "Aristotle insists only on unity of action; he notes tragedy tries to keep within 'a single revolution of the sun'; unity of place was added by Castelvetro (1570), making the 'three unities' of neoclassicism.", "Comedy imitates men 'worse than the average'; tragedy men better than average; epic differs from tragedy in length and narrative form; tragedy is judged superior to epic (Ch. 26).", "Other works: Rhetoric, Politics (catharsis also discussed there), Nicomachean Ethics."]}, {"title": "Horace (65-8 BCE) and Longinus (1st century CE)", "points": ["Horace's Ars Poetica (also Epistula ad Pisones), verse letter c. 19-18 BCE, addressed to the Piso family; Roman, Latin.", "Aim of poetry: to teach and to delight (prodesse et delectare); 'he wins every vote who mixes the useful with the sweet' (utile dulci).", "Horatian rules: decorum (fitness of style to character and subject), begin in medias res, five acts, no more than three speaking actors, avoid deus ex machina, no violent deeds like Medea killing her children on stage.", "Famous Horatian phrases: 'purple patches' (purpureus pannus), 'ut pictura poesis' (as is painting so is poetry), 'Even good Homer nods', 'the mountains will labour and a ridiculous mouse will be born', keep a work 'nine years' before publishing.", "Longinus, Peri Hupsous (On the Sublime), Greek treatise of the 1st century CE, addressed to Postumius Terentianus; author's identity uncertain (Pseudo-Longinus).", "Sublimity aims not at persuasion but at transport (ekstasis); 'Sublimity is the echo of a great soul.'", "Five sources of the sublime: (1) grandeur of thought, (2) strong and inspired emotion - both innate; (3) proper use of figures, (4) noble diction, (5) dignified and elevated composition (word arrangement) - both acquired by art.", "Longinus quotes Sappho's ode ('Phainetai moi') and Genesis 'Let there be light'; compares the Iliad to the midday sun and the Odyssey to the setting sun (Homer in old age).", "Boileau's French translation (1674) spread the idea; Burke's Philosophical Enquiry into the Sublime and Beautiful (1757) developed it; Longinus is seen as the first romantic critic."]}, {"title": "Bharata and the Rasa Theory", "points": ["Bharata Muni's Natyashastra (c. 200 BCE-200 CE), 36 chapters (37 in some recensions), called the 'fifth Veda' (Natyaveda); earliest Indian treatise on drama, dance and music.", "Rasa Sutra: 'vibhavanubhava-vyabhichari-samyogad rasa-nishpattih' - rasa arises from the union of vibhava (determinants), anubhava (consequents) and vyabhicharibhava (transitory states) [rasa = kavya ka aanand/swad].", "Vibhava has two kinds: alambana (the person/object on whom emotion rests) and uddipana (exciting circumstances like moonlight, garden).", "Bharata names 8 rasas with 8 sthayibhavas: Shringara-rati, Hasya-hasa, Karuna-shoka, Raudra-krodha, Vira-utsaha, Bhayanaka-bhaya, Bibhatsa-jugupsa, Adbhuta-vismaya.", "Ninth rasa, Shanta (sthayibhava nirveda/shama), was accepted later and firmly defended by Abhinavagupta; Vatsalya and Bhakti rasa were added still later (Bhakti by Rupa Goswami).", "Bharata lists 33 vyabhicharibhavas (sancharibhavas) and 8 sattvikabhavas (stambha, sveda, romancha, etc.).", "Four interpreters of the rasa sutra: Bhatta Lollata - utpattivada (rasa produced in the character); Shri Shankuka - anumitivada (rasa inferred); Bhatta Nayaka - bhuktivada (rasa enjoyed through sadharanikarana); Abhinavagupta - abhivyaktivada (rasa manifested in the spectator).", "Abhinavagupta's commentary on the Natyashastra is the Abhinavabharati; the ideal reader is the sahridaya (one of similar heart).", "Vishwanatha's Sahityadarpana (14th century) defines poetry as 'vakyam rasatmakam kavyam' (a sentence whose soul is rasa).", "Natyashastra also gives four abhinayas (angika, vachika, aharya, sattvika) and four vrittis (kaishiki, sattvati, arabhati, bharati)."]}, {"title": "Dhvani: Anandavardhana and Abhinavagupta", "points": ["Anandavardhana (9th century, Kashmir, court of Avantivarman) wrote the Dhvanyaloka; key claim 'kavyasyatma dhvanih' - suggestion is the soul of poetry.", "Three powers (shaktis) of words: abhidha (denotation, literal meaning), lakshana (indication, secondary meaning), vyanjana (suggestion); dhvani works through vyanjana.", "Three kinds of dhvani: vastu dhvani (suggestion of a fact/idea), alankara dhvani (suggestion of a figure), rasa dhvani (suggestion of emotion) - rasa dhvani is the highest.", "Three grades of poetry: dhvani kavya (uttama, best - suggested sense dominates), gunibhuta vyangya (madhyama - suggested sense subordinate), chitra kavya (adhama - only verbal/figurative ornament).", "Anandavardhana united the rasa and dhvani ideas: rasa can never be stated directly, it can only be suggested.", "Abhinavagupta (c. 950-1016) wrote Lochana (Dhvanyalokalochana), the commentary on Dhvanyaloka, and Abhinavabharati on the Natyashastra.", "Opponent Mahimabhatta (Vyaktiviveka, 11th century) argued dhvani is only inference (anumana); Kuntaka tried to include it in vakrokti.", "Mammata's Kavyaprakasha (11th century) firmly established the dhvani school; Jagannatha's Rasagangadhara (17th century) is the last major work.", "Dhvani is often compared with Empson's ambiguity and with the idea of suggestion/implicature in Western criticism."]}, {"title": "Alankara, Riti, Vakrokti and Auchitya Schools", "points": ["Six schools (sampradayas) of Indian poetics: Rasa, Alankara, Riti, Dhvani, Vakrokti, Auchitya.", "Alankara school: Bhamaha (Kavyalankara, c. 6th-7th century) - earliest extant alankara text; defines poetry 'shabdarthau sahitau kavyam' (word and meaning together); considers vakrokti the basis of all figures.", "Dandin (Kavyadarsha, 7th century) - alankara as 'kavya-shobha-karan dharma' (elements that beautify poetry); also discusses two margas, Vaidarbha and Gauda; Udbhata and Rudrata are later alankarikas.", "Alankaras divide into shabdalankara (sound figures: anuprasa, yamaka, shlesha) and arthalankara (sense figures: upama, rupaka, utpreksha).", "Riti school: Vamana (Kavyalankarasutravritti, 8th century, court of Jayapida of Kashmir) - 'ritir atma kavyasya' (style is the soul of poetry); riti = 'vishishta padarachana' (special arrangement of words).", "Vamana's three ritis: Vaidarbhi (best, has all ten gunas), Gaudi (ojas and kanti, heavy compounds), Panchali (madhurya and saukumarya); Rudrata added Lati.", "Vakrokti school: Kuntaka (Vakroktijivita, c. 10th-11th century) - 'vakroktih kavyajivitam' (striking/oblique expression is the life of poetry).", "Kuntaka's six levels of vakrata: varna-vinyasa (phoneme), pada-purvardha (word base), pada-parardha (suffix), vakya (sentence), prakarana (episode), prabandha (whole composition); often compared to Russian Formalist defamiliarization.", "Auchitya school: Kshemendra (Auchitya-vichara-charcha, 11th century, Kashmir) - 'auchityam rasasiddhasya sthiram kavyasya jivitam' (propriety is the abiding life of poetry full of rasa); comparable to Horace's decorum.", "Rough chronology: Bharata - Bhamaha - Dandin - Vamana - Anandavardhana - Abhinavagupta - Kuntaka - Kshemendra - Mammata - Vishwanatha - Jagannatha."]}, {"title": "Sir Philip Sidney: An Apology for Poetry", "points": ["Written c. 1579-80, published posthumously 1595 in two editions: An Apology for Poetry (Olney) and The Defence of Poesy (Ponsonby).", "Occasioned by Stephen Gosson's puritan attack The School of Abuse (1579), which Gosson dedicated to Sidney.", "Poet as 'maker' (from Greek poiein) and vates (prophet); the poet is not tied to nature but creates 'another nature'.", "'Nature never set forth the earth in so rich tapestry as divers poets have done ... Her world is brazen, the poets only deliver a golden.'", "Poetry is 'an art of imitation ... a speaking picture, with this end, to teach and delight' (Horatian echo).", "The poet is the 'monarch' of all sciences, superior to the philosopher (precept only) and the historian (example only), since he joins precept and example.", "Answer to the charge of lying: 'the poet, he nothing affirms, and therefore never lieth.'", "Criticises contemporary English drama for breaking the unities and for 'mongrel tragi-comedy'; praises Gorboduc (1561) for its style but faults it on unity of time and place.", "Sidney's other works: Astrophel and Stella (sonnet sequence), Arcadia (prose romance); he is the first major English critic of the Renaissance."]}, {"title": "John Dryden: Father of English Criticism", "points": ["Dr Johnson called Dryden 'the father of English criticism'.", "An Essay of Dramatic Poesy (1668), a dialogue set on a barge on the Thames on 3 June 1665 during the naval battle between the English and the Dutch.", "Four speakers: Crites (Sir Robert Howard) defends the Ancients; Eugenius (Lord Buckhurst, Charles Sackville) defends the Moderns; Lisideius (Sir Charles Sedley) praises French drama; Neander (Dryden himself) defends English drama and rhyme.", "Lisideius's definition of a play: 'a just and lively image of human nature, representing its passions and humours, and the changes of fortune to which it is subject, for the delight and instruction of mankind.'", "On Shakespeare: 'the man who of all modern, and perhaps ancient poets, had the largest and most comprehensive soul'; on Jonson: 'I admire him, but I love Shakespeare.'", "Neander examines Jonson's The Silent Woman (Epicoene) as a model of the regular play; defends English tragicomedy and subplots.", "Preface to the Fables, Ancient and Modern (1700): calls Chaucer 'the father of English poetry' and says of The Canterbury Tales 'here is God's plenty.'", "Other critical prose: Of Heroic Plays (1672), Preface to All for Love (1678), A Discourse Concerning the Original and Progress of Satire (1693) - where he says Donne 'affects the metaphysics'."]}, {"title": "Alexander Pope and Samuel Johnson", "points": ["Pope's An Essay on Criticism (1711), in heroic couplets, three parts; written when Pope was about 21; neoclassical creed of following Nature and the Ancients.", "Pope quotes: 'First follow Nature'; 'A little learning is a dangerous thing; / Drink deep, or taste not the Pierian spring'; 'To err is human, to forgive divine'; 'For fools rush in where angels fear to tread.'", "'True wit is Nature to advantage dressed, / What oft was thought, but ne'er so well expressed'; 'The sound must seem an echo to the sense'; 'Those rules of old discovered, not devised, / Are Nature still, but Nature methodized.'", "Pope also wrote the Preface to his edition of Shakespeare (1725) and Peri Bathous, or the Art of Sinking in Poetry (1728), a parody of Longinus.", "Johnson's Preface to Shakespeare (1765): 'Shakespeare is above all writers, at least above all modern writers, the poet of nature; the poet that holds up to his readers a faithful mirror of manners and of life.'", "Johnson: Shakespeare's characters are 'commonly a species', not individuals; he defends the mingling of tragic and comic and rejects the unities of time and place, keeping only unity of action.", "Johnson's faults list: Shakespeare 'sacrifices virtue to convenience'; 'A quibble is to Shakespeare what luminous vapours are to the traveller ... the fatal Cleopatra for which he lost the world.'", "Lives of the Poets (1779-81): in the Life of Cowley Johnson describes the metaphysical poets: 'the most heterogeneous ideas are yoked by violence together'; he popularised the label 'metaphysical poets'.", "Rasselas (1759), Imlac: 'The business of a poet is to examine, not the individual, but the species ... he does not number the streaks of the tulip.' Johnson's Dictionary appeared in 1755."]}, {"title": "William Wordsworth and S. T. Coleridge", "points": ["Lyrical Ballads (1798) by Wordsworth and Coleridge marks the start of English Romanticism; the famous Preface was added in the 1800 edition and enlarged in 1802.", "Wordsworth: 'all good poetry is the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings: it takes its origin from emotion recollected in tranquillity.'", "Aim: to choose 'incidents and situations from common life' and describe them 'in a selection of language really used by men', throwing over them 'a certain colouring of imagination'; rustic life preferred.", "Poet is 'a man speaking to men'; poetry is 'the breath and finer spirit of all knowledge'; he attacks 18th-century 'poetic diction', citing Gray's Sonnet on the Death of Richard West.", "Wordsworth says there is no essential difference between the language of prose and metrical composition.", "Coleridge's Biographia Literaria (1817): Chapter XIII distinguishes primary imagination, secondary imagination and fancy; Chapter XIV gives the origin of Lyrical Ballads and 'that willing suspension of disbelief for the moment, which constitutes poetic faith.'", "Primary imagination: 'the living power and prime agent of all human perception ... a repetition in the finite mind of the eternal act of creation in the infinite I AM'; secondary imagination 'dissolves, diffuses, dissipates, in order to re-create'; fancy deals with 'fixities and definites'.", "Coleridge coined 'esemplastic' (shaping into one) for imagination; imagination 'reveals itself in the balance or reconciliation of opposite or discordant qualities'.", "Chapters XVII-XXII of Biographia criticise Wordsworth's theory of poetic language; Table Talk (1827): prose = 'words in their best order', poetry = 'the best words in the best order'.", "Coleridge's Shakespeare lectures distinguish organic form (grows from within) from mechanic form (imposed from outside), drawing on A.W. Schlegel."]}, {"title": "P. B. Shelley, Matthew Arnold and Walter Pater", "points": ["Shelley's A Defence of Poetry, written 1821, published posthumously 1840, a reply to Thomas Love Peacock's The Four Ages of Poetry (1820).", "Shelley quotes: 'Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world'; poetry is 'the record of the best and happiest moments of the happiest and best minds'; 'the mind in creation is as a fading coal'; 'Reason is to imagination as the instrument to the agent.'", "Arnold, 'The Function of Criticism at the Present Time' (1864; Essays in Criticism, First Series, 1865): criticism is 'a disinterested endeavour to learn and propagate the best that is known and thought in the world'.", "Arnold, 'The Study of Poetry' (1880, introduction to T.H. Ward's The English Poets; reprinted in Essays in Criticism, Second Series, 1888): poetry is 'a criticism of life'; he rejects the historical and personal estimates in favour of the real estimate.", "Touchstone method: short lines from Homer, Dante, Shakespeare and Milton used to test poetry; Chaucer lacks 'high seriousness' (Aristotle's spoudaiotes); Dryden and Pope are 'classics of our prose'; Burns also lacks high seriousness.", "Arnold's Culture and Anarchy (1869): culture as 'sweetness and light', Hebraism vs Hellenism, classes as Barbarians, Philistines and Populace; he called Shelley 'a beautiful and ineffectual angel'.", "Walter Pater, Studies in the History of the Renaissance (1873); Conclusion: 'To burn always with this hard, gem-like flame, to maintain this ecstasy, is success in life.'", "Pater, 'The School of Giorgione' (added 1877): 'All art constantly aspires towards the condition of music'; famous Mona Lisa passage: 'She is older than the rocks among which she sits.'", "Pater's aesthetic/impressionistic criticism asks 'What is this song or picture ... to me?'; father of English Aestheticism ('art for art's sake'); other works Marius the Epicurean (1885), Appreciations (1889, with the essay 'Style')."]}, {"title": "T. S. Eliot as Critic", "points": ["'Tradition and the Individual Talent' (1919, The Egoist; collected in The Sacred Wood, 1920): tradition requires the 'historical sense' of 'not only the pastness of the past, but of its presence'.", "Eliot's impersonal theory: 'Poetry is not a turning loose of emotion, but an escape from emotion; it is not the expression of personality, but an escape from personality.'", "Catalyst analogy: the poet's mind is like a filament of platinum which, introduced into oxygen and sulphur dioxide, forms sulphurous acid while itself remaining unchanged.", "'The progress of an artist is a continual self-sacrifice, a continual extinction of personality'; 'Honest criticism and sensitive appreciation is directed not upon the poet but upon the poetry.'", "'Hamlet and His Problems' (1919): coins/popularises the 'objective correlative'; calls Hamlet 'most certainly an artistic failure' and 'the Mona Lisa of literature'.", "'The Metaphysical Poets' (1921, review of Grierson's anthology): 'dissociation of sensibility' set in during the 17th century, after Donne and the metaphysicals; for Donne 'a thought ... was an experience; it modified his sensibility.'", "Other critical works: 'The Function of Criticism' (1923), Selected Essays (1932), The Use of Poetry and the Use of Criticism (1933, includes 'auditory imagination'), After Strange Gods (1934), Notes Towards the Definition of Culture (1948), 'The Frontiers of Criticism' (1956).", "Eliot's essays helped revalue the metaphysical poets and Jacobean dramatists and dislodge Milton; he deeply influenced Leavis and the New Critics.", "Eliot called himself 'classicist in literature, royalist in politics, and Anglo-Catholic in religion' (preface to For Lancelot Andrewes, 1928)."]}, {"title": "I. A. Richards and William Empson", "points": ["I.A. Richards (1893-1979), Cambridge: The Meaning of Meaning (1923, with C.K. Ogden), Principles of Literary Criticism (1924), Science and Poetry (1926), Practical Criticism (1929), The Philosophy of Rhetoric (1936).", "Principles opens: 'A book is a machine to think with'; poetry's value lies in organising and balancing impulses (synaesthesis); poetry of inclusion vs poetry of exclusion.", "Two uses of language: scientific/referential and emotive; poetic statements are 'pseudo-statements' which are not true or false like science.", "Practical Criticism (1929): based on 'protocols' written by Cambridge students on 13 unsigned poems; lists reading difficulties like stock responses, sentimentality, doctrinal adhesions.", "Four kinds of meaning in Practical Criticism: sense, feeling, tone and intention.", "The Philosophy of Rhetoric (1936) introduces 'tenor' and 'vehicle' as the two parts of a metaphor.", "William Empson (1906-1984), Richards's student at Cambridge: Seven Types of Ambiguity (1930), Some Versions of Pastoral (1935), The Structure of Complex Words (1951), Milton's God (1961).", "Empson defines ambiguity as 'any verbal nuance, however slight, which gives room for alternative reactions to the same piece of language'; the first type is metaphor, the seventh is full contradiction showing a division in the author's mind.", "Richards and Empson laid the ground for close reading and American New Criticism."]}, {"title": "F. R. Leavis", "points": ["F.R. Leavis (1895-1978), Cambridge (Downing College); with wife Q.D. Leavis founded and edited Scrutiny (1932-1953).", "Mass Civilization and Minority Culture (1930); Culture and Environment (1933, with Denys Thompson); Q.D. Leavis wrote Fiction and the Reading Public (1932).", "New Bearings in English Poetry (1932): praises Eliot, Pound and Hopkins as the new direction of poetry.", "Revaluation: Tradition and Development in English Poetry (1936): traces a line of wit from Donne; dislodges Milton.", "The Great Tradition (1948) opens: 'The great English novelists are Jane Austen, George Eliot, Henry James and Joseph Conrad'; D.H. Lawrence added as heir; Dickens's Hard Times treated as a 'moral fable'.", "Other works: The Common Pursuit (1952; title from Eliot's phrase 'the common pursuit of true judgment'), D.H. Lawrence: Novelist (1955), Two Cultures? The Significance of C.P. Snow (1962), The Living Principle (1975).", "Key values: moral seriousness, 'life', concreteness, 'felt life', 'reverent openness before life'; literature as the centre of humane education.", "Leavis's debate with René Wellek (1937) on whether a critic must state his philosophical norms; Leavis refused, insisting on 'Yes, but...' collaborative judgement."]}, {"title": "New Criticism: Ransom, Brooks, Tate, Wimsatt and Beardsley", "points": ["American movement of the 1930s-50s, with roots in the Fugitives/Agrarians at Vanderbilt University; the name comes from John Crowe Ransom's book The New Criticism (1941).", "Ransom: The World's Body (1938), essay 'Criticism, Inc.' (1937) calling for professional, objective criticism; poem has a logical 'structure' and local 'texture'; founded The Kenyon Review (1939).", "Cleanth Brooks: Modern Poetry and the Tradition (1939); The Well Wrought Urn (1947) - essays 'The Language of Paradox' ('the language of poetry is the language of paradox') and 'The Heresy of Paraphrase'.", "Brooks and Robert Penn Warren: Understanding Poetry (1938) and Understanding Fiction (1943), textbooks that spread close reading; they edited The Southern Review (1935).", "Allen Tate, 'Tension in Poetry' (1938): good poetry has tension, formed by cutting the prefixes from 'extension' (denotation) and 'intension' (connotation).", "W.K. Wimsatt and Monroe C. Beardsley, 'The Intentional Fallacy' (1946): 'the design or intention of the author is neither available nor desirable as a standard for judging the success of a work of literary art.'", "Wimsatt and Beardsley, 'The Affective Fallacy' (1949): confusing the poem with its results (what it does to the reader); both essays collected in Wimsatt's The Verbal Icon (1954).", "Key tenets: the poem as an autonomous, organic 'verbal icon'; close reading of irony, paradox, ambiguity and tension; exclusion of biography, history and reader response.", "Other New Critics: R.P. Blackmur, Yvor Winters, Robert Penn Warren; René Wellek and Austin Warren's Theory of Literature (1949) distinguishes 'intrinsic' and 'extrinsic' approaches."]}, {"title": "Northrop Frye: Archetypal Criticism", "points": ["Canadian critic Northrop Frye (1912-1991): Fearful Symmetry (1947, on Blake), Anatomy of Criticism (1957), The Educated Imagination (1963), The Great Code: The Bible and Literature (1982).", "Anatomy of Criticism has a 'Polemical Introduction' and four essays: Historical Criticism - Theory of Modes; Ethical Criticism - Theory of Symbols; Archetypal Criticism - Theory of Myths; Rhetorical Criticism - Theory of Genres.", "Five fictional modes based on the hero's power of action: myth, romance, high mimetic, low mimetic and ironic.", "Four mythoi linked with seasons: comedy - spring, romance - summer, tragedy - autumn, irony and satire - winter.", "Four genres by 'radical of presentation': drama (acted), epos (recited), fiction/prose (read), lyric (overheard/sung).", "Five phases of symbols: literal, descriptive, formal, mythical (archetypal) and anagogic.", "Archetype: a typical or recurring image that connects one poem with another; 'displacement' adapts myth towards plausibility.", "Frye wanted criticism to be a systematic 'science' and rejected 'value judgements' as part of the history of taste; drew on Jung and Frazer's The Golden Bough."]}, {"title": "Russian Formalism", "points": ["Two groups: the Moscow Linguistic Circle (1915, led by Roman Jakobson) and OPOJAZ - Society for the Study of Poetic Language (Petrograd, 1916: Viktor Shklovsky, Boris Eichenbaum, Yury Tynyanov).", "Viktor Shklovsky, 'Art as Technique' / 'Art as Device' (1917): art exists 'to make the stone stony'; ostranenie (defamiliarization, making strange) counters automatization; example from Tolstoy's 'Kholstomer' (story told by a horse).", "Shklovsky: art is 'a way of experiencing the artfulness of an object; the object is not important'; Tristram Shandy called 'the most typical novel in world literature'.", "Fabula (raw chronological story) vs syuzhet (plot as artistically arranged) - distinction developed by Shklovsky and Tomashevsky; Tomashevsky also distinguished bound and free motifs.", "Jakobson coined 'literariness' (literaturnost, 1921): the object of literary science is not literature but what makes a given work literary.", "Jakobson's 'Linguistics and Poetics' (1960): six factors and functions of communication; the poetic function 'projects the principle of equivalence from the axis of selection into the axis of combination'.", "Tynyanov's 'On Literary Evolution' (1927) and the concept of the 'dominant'; Eichenbaum's 'The Theory of the Formal Method' (1926).", "Vladimir Propp, Morphology of the Folktale (1928): 31 functions and 7 spheres of action in Russian fairy tales; foundation of narratology.", "Suppressed under Stalin around 1930; ideas continued in the Prague Linguistic Circle (1926; Jan Mukarovsky's 'foregrounding'); Bakhtin/Medvedev criticised it in The Formal Method in Literary Scholarship (1928); Victor Erlich's Russian Formalism: History-Doctrine (1955) introduced it to the West."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Mimesis", "pron": "मि-मी-सिस (mi-MEE-sis)", "hindi": "नकल या अनुकरण; कला द्वारा दुनिया की नकल।", "net": "Imitation or representation of reality in art; for Plato a copy of a copy 'thrice removed from truth', for Aristotle the natural basis of all poetry (Poetics)."}, {"term": "Catharsis", "pron": "कै-थार-सिस (kuh-THAAR-sis)", "hindi": "दर्शक के मन से करुणा और भय का शुद्धिकरण/विरेचन।", "net": "Aristotle's term in the definition of tragedy (Poetics, Ch. 6) for the purgation or purification of pity and fear aroused in the audience."}, {"term": "Hamartia", "pron": "हा-मार-टी-आ (haa-maar-TEE-uh)", "hindi": "नायक की गलती या निर्णय की भूल जिससे उसका पतन होता है।", "net": "Aristotle's 'error of judgement' or tragic flaw by which a hero of high fortune falls into misery (Poetics, Ch. 13)."}, {"term": "Peripeteia", "pron": "पे-री-पे-टी-आ (pe-ree-pe-TEE-uh)", "hindi": "भाग्य का अचानक उलट जाना।", "net": "Reversal of the situation: a change by which the action veers round to its opposite (Aristotle, Poetics Ch. 11), as when the messenger's news in Oedipus Rex produces the opposite effect."}, {"term": "Anagnorisis", "pron": "ऐ-नैग-नो-री-सिस (an-ag-NOR-i-sis)", "hindi": "सच्चाई की पहचान या आत्म-बोध का क्षण।", "net": "Recognition or discovery: 'a change from ignorance to knowledge' (Aristotle, Poetics Ch. 11), best when it coincides with peripeteia."}, {"term": "Three Unities", "pron": "थ्री यू-नि-टीज़ (three YOO-ni-teez)", "hindi": "नाटक में एक कथा, एक दिन और एक स्थान का नियम।", "net": "Neoclassical rules of action, time and place; Aristotle stressed only unity of action, the unity of place being added by Castelvetro (1570)."}, {"term": "Decorum", "pron": "डि-को-रम (di-KOR-um)", "hindi": "औचित्य; विषय और पात्र के अनुरूप भाषा-शैली।", "net": "Propriety or fitness of style, diction and action to the character, subject and genre; a central principle of Horace's Ars Poetica and neoclassical criticism."}, {"term": "In medias res", "pron": "इन मी-डि-आस रेज़ (in MAY-dee-us rays)", "hindi": "कहानी को बीच से शुरू करना।", "net": "Latin 'into the middle of things'; Horace's advice (Ars Poetica) that an epic should begin in the middle of the action, as Homer does."}, {"term": "Dulce et utile", "pron": "डुल-के एत ऊ-टि-ले (DUL-keh et OO-ti-leh)", "hindi": "मनोरंजन और उपदेश दोनों।", "net": "Horace's formula that poetry should be both sweet (pleasing) and useful (instructive); echoed by Sidney's 'to teach and delight'."}, {"term": "Sublime", "pron": "सब-लाइम (suh-BLIME)", "hindi": "उदात्त; ऐसी भव्यता जो मन को ऊपर उठा दे।", "net": "Loftiness of thought and expression that produces transport (ekstasis) rather than persuasion; from Longinus's Peri Hupsous, 'the echo of a great soul'."}, {"term": "Rasa", "pron": "र-स (RUH-suh)", "hindi": "काव्य या नाटक से मिलने वाला भावात्मक आनंद/रस।", "net": "The aesthetic relish or emotional flavour evoked in the spectator, produced by the union of vibhava, anubhava and vyabhicharibhava (Bharata, Natyashastra)."}, {"term": "Sthayibhava", "pron": "स्था-यी-भाव (STHAA-yee-bhaav)", "hindi": "मन का स्थायी भाव, जैसे प्रेम, शोक, क्रोध।", "net": "The permanent or dominant emotion latent in every person (rati, hasa, shoka etc.) which, when developed by vibhava and other bhavas, becomes rasa."}, {"term": "Vibhava", "pron": "वि-भाव (vi-BHAAV)", "hindi": "भाव जगाने वाला कारण, जैसे पात्र या चाँदनी रात।", "net": "Determinant or cause of emotion; divided into alambana (the object or person) and uddipana (the stimulating circumstances)."}, {"term": "Anubhava", "pron": "अनु-भाव (a-nu-BHAAV)", "hindi": "भाव के बाहरी लक्षण, जैसे आँसू, मुस्कान।", "net": "Consequent: the outward physical expression of an emotion, such as tears, glances or gestures, in Bharata's rasa sutra."}, {"term": "Vyabhicharibhava", "pron": "व्य-भि-चा-री-भाव (vya-bhi-CHAA-ree-bhaav)", "hindi": "क्षणिक या संचारी भाव जो आते-जाते रहते हैं।", "net": "Transitory or fleeting emotional states (also sancharibhava) that strengthen the sthayibhava; Bharata lists 33 of them."}, {"term": "Sahridaya", "pron": "स-हृ-द-य (sa-HRI-da-ya)", "hindi": "समान हृदय वाला रसिक पाठक/दर्शक।", "net": "The sensitive, cultivated reader or spectator 'of similar heart' who can relish rasa; a key idea of Anandavardhana and Abhinavagupta."}, {"term": "Sadharanikarana", "pron": "सा-धा-र-णी-क-र-ण (saa-dhaa-ra-nee-KA-ra-na)", "hindi": "भावों का सामान्य या सार्वभौमिक बन जाना।", "net": "Generalisation or universalisation of emotions, freed from personal ties, so that the spectator enjoys rasa; theory of Bhatta Nayaka, developed by Abhinavagupta."}, {"term": "Dhvani", "pron": "ध्व-नि (DHVUH-ni)", "hindi": "व्यंजना; शब्दों से निकलने वाला छिपा अर्थ।", "net": "Suggestion: the implied meaning conveyed through vyanjana beyond literal and secondary sense; declared the soul of poetry by Anandavardhana in Dhvanyaloka."}, {"term": "Abhidha", "pron": "अ-भि-धा (a-bhi-DHAA)", "hindi": "शब्द का सीधा, शब्दकोश वाला अर्थ।", "net": "Denotation: the primary power of a word to convey its literal, conventional meaning."}, {"term": "Lakshana", "pron": "लक्ष-णा (luk-SHUH-naa)", "hindi": "लक्षणा; सीधा अर्थ न बैठने पर दूसरा अर्थ लेना।", "net": "Indication or secondary meaning used when the literal sense is blocked, e.g. 'a village on the Ganga' meaning on its bank."}, {"term": "Vyanjana", "pron": "व्यं-ज-ना (VYUN-ja-naa)", "hindi": "शब्द की वह शक्ति जो संकेत से अर्थ देती है।", "net": "Suggestive power of words, beyond abhidha and lakshana, through which dhvani and rasa are conveyed."}, {"term": "Gunibhuta vyangya", "pron": "गु-णी-भू-त व्यंग्य (gu-nee-BHOO-ta VYUNG-ya)", "hindi": "ऐसा काव्य जिसमें छिपा अर्थ गौण हो।", "net": "Anandavardhana's 'middle' poetry, in which the suggested meaning is subordinate to the expressed meaning."}, {"term": "Alankara", "pron": "अ-लं-का-र (a-LUN-kaa-ra)", "hindi": "काव्य का आभूषण; उपमा, रूपक आदि।", "net": "Figure of speech or poetic ornament (shabdalankara and arthalankara); the Alankara school of Bhamaha and Dandin made it the essence of poetry."}, {"term": "Riti", "pron": "री-ति (REE-ti)", "hindi": "काव्य की शैली या शब्द-रचना का ढंग।", "net": "Style, defined by Vamana as 'vishishta padarachana' (special arrangement of words) and declared the soul of poetry; three kinds: Vaidarbhi, Gaudi, Panchali."}, {"term": "Vakrokti", "pron": "व-क्रो-क्ति (va-KROK-ti)", "hindi": "टेढ़ी या चमत्कारपूर्ण उक्ति।", "net": "Oblique, striking or deviant expression; Kuntaka in Vakroktijivita called it the life of poetry and analysed six levels of vakrata."}, {"term": "Auchitya", "pron": "औ-चि-त्य (au-CHIT-ya)", "hindi": "उचितता; हर चीज़ का सही जगह, सही ढंग से होना।", "net": "Propriety or appropriateness; Kshemendra in Auchitya-vichara-charcha called it the abiding life of poetry full of rasa."}, {"term": "Poetic diction", "pron": "पो-ए-टिक डिक-शन (po-ET-ik DIK-shun)", "hindi": "कविता के लिए बनावटी, बोलचाल से अलग भाषा।", "net": "Language and phrasing held proper to poetry and distinct from ordinary speech, typical of 18th-century verse; attacked by Wordsworth in the Preface to Lyrical Ballads."}, {"term": "Fancy", "pron": "फ़ैन-सी (FAN-see)", "hindi": "कल्पना का निचला, यांत्रिक रूप जो चीज़ों को जोड़ता भर है।", "net": "Coleridge's term (Biographia Literaria, Ch. XIII) for the associative faculty that plays with 'fixities and definites', inferior to imagination."}, {"term": "Esemplastic", "pron": "ए-सेम-प्लास-टिक (es-em-PLAS-tik)", "hindi": "अनेक चीज़ों को एक रूप में ढालने वाली।", "net": "Word coined by Coleridge (Biographia Literaria) for the unifying, shaping power of the imagination, 'to shape into one'."}, {"term": "Willing suspension of disbelief", "pron": "विलिंग सस्पेंशन ऑफ़ डिसबिलीफ़", "hindi": "कहानी को सच मानने के लिए अविश्वास को कुछ देर छोड़ देना।", "net": "Coleridge's phrase (Biographia Literaria, Ch. XIV): 'that willing suspension of disbelief for the moment, which constitutes poetic faith.'"}, {"term": "Touchstone", "pron": "टच-स्टोन (TUCH-stohn)", "hindi": "कसौटी; अच्छी कविता परखने का पैमाना।", "net": "Arnold's method in 'The Study of Poetry' (1880) of judging poetry by comparing it with short passages from the great masters such as Homer, Dante, Shakespeare and Milton."}, {"term": "High seriousness", "pron": "हाई सी-रि-अस-नेस", "hindi": "गहरी गंभीरता जो महान कविता में होती है।", "net": "Arnold's quality of great poetry, after Aristotle's spoudaiotes; he denied it to Chaucer and Burns in 'The Study of Poetry'."}, {"term": "Aestheticism", "pron": "एस-थे-टि-सिज़्म (es-THET-i-sizm)", "hindi": "कला केवल कला के लिए, नैतिक उपदेश के लिए नहीं।", "net": "Late-19th-century doctrine of 'art for art's sake', associated in England with Walter Pater and Oscar Wilde, valuing beauty and intense experience above morality."}, {"term": "Objective correlative", "pron": "ऑब-जेक-टिव कॉ-रि-लेटिव", "hindi": "भावना जगाने वाली ठोस घटनाएँ या वस्तुएँ।", "net": "Eliot's term ('Hamlet and His Problems', 1919): 'a set of objects, a situation, a chain of events which shall be the formula of that particular emotion.'"}, {"term": "Dissociation of sensibility", "pron": "डि-सो-सि-ए-शन ऑफ़ सेन-सि-बि-लि-टी", "hindi": "विचार और भावना का अलग-अलग हो जाना।", "net": "Eliot's phrase ('The Metaphysical Poets', 1921) for the split between thought and feeling that set in in English poetry in the 17th century, after the metaphysicals."}, {"term": "Impersonality", "pron": "इम-पर-स-ना-लि-टी", "hindi": "कवि का अपने निजी भावों से दूर रहना।", "net": "Eliot's doctrine ('Tradition and the Individual Talent', 1919) that poetry is 'an escape from personality', the poet's mind being like a catalyst."}, {"term": "Pseudo-statement", "pron": "स्यू-डो स्टेट-मेंट (SOO-doh)", "hindi": "ऐसा कथन जो सच-झूठ नहीं, भावना के लिए होता है।", "net": "I.A. Richards's term (Science and Poetry, 1926) for poetic statements valued for their emotional effect, not for scientific truth."}, {"term": "Tenor and vehicle", "pron": "टे-नर एंड वी-इ-कल", "hindi": "रूपक का मूल विषय और जिस चीज़ से तुलना की जाती है।", "net": "Richards's terms (The Philosophy of Rhetoric, 1936) for the subject of a metaphor (tenor) and the image that carries it (vehicle)."}, {"term": "Ambiguity", "pron": "ऐम-बि-ग्यू-इ-टी (am-bi-GYOO-i-tee)", "hindi": "एक ही शब्द/पंक्ति के कई अर्थ होना।", "net": "Empson's 'any verbal nuance, however slight, which gives room for alternative reactions to the same piece of language' (Seven Types of Ambiguity, 1930)."}, {"term": "Close reading", "pron": "क्लोज़ री-डिंग", "hindi": "पाठ के हर शब्द को ध्यान से पढ़कर अर्थ निकालना।", "net": "Detailed analysis of the words, images and structure of a text in isolation from biography and history; method of Richards, Empson and the New Critics."}, {"term": "Intentional fallacy", "pron": "इन-टेन-श-नल फ़ै-ल-सी", "hindi": "लेखक के इरादे से रचना को परखने की भूल।", "net": "Wimsatt and Beardsley (1946): the error of judging a work by the author's intention, which is 'neither available nor desirable' as a standard."}, {"term": "Affective fallacy", "pron": "अ-फ़ेक-टिव फ़ै-ल-सी", "hindi": "पाठक पर प्रभाव से रचना को परखने की भूल।", "net": "Wimsatt and Beardsley (1949): 'a confusion between the poem and its results', judging it by its emotional effect on the reader."}, {"term": "Heresy of paraphrase", "pron": "हे-रे-सी ऑफ़ पै-रा-फ़्रेज़", "hindi": "कविता का सार अपने शब्दों में कहकर उसे समझ लेने की भूल।", "net": "Cleanth Brooks's term (The Well Wrought Urn, 1947) for the mistaken belief that a poem's meaning can be reduced to a prose statement."}, {"term": "Paradox", "pron": "पै-रा-डॉक्स (PA-ra-doks)", "hindi": "विरोधाभास; ऊपर से उल्टा लगने वाला पर सच्चा कथन।", "net": "An apparently self-contradictory statement that reveals a truth; for Brooks 'the language of poetry is the language of paradox'."}, {"term": "Tension", "pron": "टेन-शन (TEN-shun)", "hindi": "कविता में शाब्दिक और भावार्थ के बीच का संतुलित खिंचाव।", "net": "Allen Tate's term ('Tension in Poetry', 1938) made by removing the prefixes of extension and intension: the organised whole of a poem's meanings."}, {"term": "Archetype", "pron": "आर-की-टाइप (AAR-kee-type)", "hindi": "बार-बार आने वाला मूल प्रतीक या कथा-ढाँचा।", "net": "A recurring image, symbol, character type or plot pattern; for Frye 'a typical or recurring image' connecting one poem with another (Anatomy of Criticism, 1957)."}, {"term": "Defamiliarization", "pron": "डी-फ़ै-मि-लि-अ-रा-ई-ज़ेशन", "hindi": "जानी-पहचानी चीज़ को नया और अजनबी बनाकर दिखाना।", "net": "Ostranenie, 'making strange': Shklovsky's term ('Art as Technique', 1917) for art's power to renew perception dulled by habit, 'to make the stone stony'."}, {"term": "Fabula and syuzhet", "pron": "फ़ा-बू-ला / स्यू-ज़ेत", "hindi": "घटनाओं का असली क्रम और कथा में उनकी प्रस्तुति।", "net": "Russian Formalist distinction between the chronological sequence of events (fabula, story) and their artistic arrangement in the narrative (syuzhet, plot)."}, {"term": "Literariness", "pron": "लि-ट-रे-री-नेस", "hindi": "वह गुण जो किसी रचना को साहित्य बनाता है।", "net": "Literaturnost, Jakobson's term (1921) for the specific features that make a text literary, the true object of literary study."}, {"term": "Foregrounding", "pron": "फ़ोर-ग्राउंड-इंग", "hindi": "भाषा के किसी हिस्से को उभारकर ध्यान खींचना।", "net": "Aktualisace, Jan Mukarovsky's (Prague School) term for bringing language to the reader's attention by deviation from norms of ordinary speech."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who said that art is 'thrice removed from the truth'?", "options": ["Aristotle", "Plato", "Horace", "Longinus"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Plato in Republic Book X calls the imitative artist a copier of appearances, thrice removed from the Idea."}, {"q": "In which dialogue does Plato compare poetic inspiration to a magnet attracting iron rings?", "options": ["Phaedrus", "Symposium", "Ion", "Republic"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The magnet image of Muse-poet-rhapsode-audience appears in Ion."}, {"q": "Arrange Aristotle's six parts of tragedy in his order of importance: (a) Character (b) Spectacle (c) Plot (d) Diction", "options": ["(c), (a), (d), (b)", "(a), (c), (d), (b)", "(c), (d), (a), (b)", "(a), (c), (b), (d)"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Plot, Character, Thought, Diction, Song, Spectacle."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Aristotle considers poetry more philosophical than history. Reason (R): Poetry expresses the universal, history the particular.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Poetics Ch. 9 gives exactly this reason."}, {"q": "Which unity was NOT insisted upon by Aristotle but added later by Castelvetro?", "options": ["Unity of action", "Unity of place", "Unity of time", "Unity of character"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Aristotle stressed unity of action and mentioned a single revolution of the sun; Castelvetro (1570) added place."}, {"q": "'Even good Homer nods' and 'purple patches' come from:", "options": ["On the Sublime", "Poetics", "Ars Poetica", "De Vulgari Eloquentia"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Both phrases are from Horace's Ars Poetica."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of Longinus's five sources of the sublime?", "options": ["Grandeur of thought", "Vehement and inspired passion", "Noble diction", "Unity of action"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Longinus's sources: thought, passion, figures, diction, elevated composition."}, {"q": "'Sublimity is the echo of a great soul' was said by:", "options": ["Horace", "Longinus", "Burke", "Boileau"], "answer": 1, "exp": "From Longinus's Peri Hupsous."}, {"q": "Match the List I with List II: (a) Dhvanyaloka (b) Vakroktijivita (c) Kavyalankarasutravritti (d) Auchitya-vichara-charcha | (i) Kuntaka (ii) Vamana (iii) Kshemendra (iv) Anandavardhana", "options": ["a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii", "a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii", "a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Anandavardhana-Dhvanyaloka, Kuntaka-Vakroktijivita, Vamana-Riti text, Kshemendra-Auchitya."}, {"q": "According to Bharata's rasa sutra, rasa arises from the union of:", "options": ["Sthayibhava, guna and riti", "Vibhava, anubhava and vyabhicharibhava", "Abhidha, lakshana and vyanjana", "Alankara, riti and dhvani"], "answer": 1, "exp": "'Vibhavanubhava-vyabhichari-samyogad rasa-nishpattih'."}, {"q": "Which rasa was not among Bharata's original eight and was firmly established by Abhinavagupta?", "options": ["Adbhuta", "Bibhatsa", "Shanta", "Karuna"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Shanta is the ninth rasa defended by Abhinavagupta."}, {"q": "Match the sthayibhava with its rasa: (a) Jugupsa (b) Utsaha (c) Vismaya (d) Shoka | (i) Karuna (ii) Adbhuta (iii) Vira (iv) Bibhatsa", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Jugupsa-Bibhatsa, Utsaha-Vira, Vismaya-Adbhuta, Shoka-Karuna."}, {"q": "Abhinavagupta's interpretation of the rasa sutra is known as:", "options": ["Utpattivada", "Anumitivada", "Bhuktivada", "Abhivyaktivada"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Lollata-utpatti, Shankuka-anumiti, Bhatta Nayaka-bhukti, Abhinavagupta-abhivyakti."}, {"q": "According to Anandavardhana, poetry in which the suggested meaning is subordinate to the expressed meaning is called:", "options": ["Dhvani kavya", "Gunibhuta vyangya", "Chitra kavya", "Vakrokti kavya"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Gunibhuta vyangya is the middle grade of poetry."}, {"q": "Who declared 'ritir atma kavyasya' (riti is the soul of poetry)?", "options": ["Dandin", "Bhamaha", "Vamana", "Mammata"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Vamana founded the Riti school."}, {"q": "Identify the correct chronological sequence: (a) Anandavardhana (b) Bharata (c) Kshemendra (d) Bhamaha", "options": ["(b), (d), (a), (c)", "(b), (a), (d), (c)", "(d), (b), (a), (c)", "(b), (d), (c), (a)"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Bharata, Bhamaha (6th-7th c.), Anandavardhana (9th c.), Kshemendra (11th c.)."}, {"q": "Sidney's An Apology for Poetry was a reply to:", "options": ["Peacock's The Four Ages of Poetry", "Gosson's The School of Abuse", "Puttenham's The Art of English Poesy", "Rymer's A Short View of Tragedy"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Stephen Gosson's 1579 attack was dedicated to Sidney."}, {"q": "Who wrote, 'the poet, he nothing affirms, and therefore never lieth'?", "options": ["Dryden", "Johnson", "Sidney", "Shelley"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Sidney, An Apology for Poetry."}, {"q": "In Dryden's An Essay of Dramatic Poesy, which speaker represents Dryden himself?", "options": ["Crites", "Eugenius", "Lisideius", "Neander"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Neander ('new man') is Dryden and defends English drama."}, {"q": "Who called Dryden 'the father of English criticism'?", "options": ["Pope", "Samuel Johnson", "Matthew Arnold", "T.S. Eliot"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Johnson in the Life of Dryden."}, {"q": "'True wit is Nature to advantage dressed, / What oft was thought, but ne'er so well expressed' is from:", "options": ["An Essay on Man", "The Dunciad", "An Essay on Criticism", "Peri Bathous"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Pope, An Essay on Criticism (1711)."}, {"q": "Who described metaphysical poetry as one in which 'the most heterogeneous ideas are yoked by violence together'?", "options": ["Dryden", "T.S. Eliot", "Samuel Johnson", "Coleridge"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Johnson, Life of Cowley in Lives of the Poets."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Johnson defends Shakespeare's mingling of tragic and comic scenes. Reason (R): Such drama exhibits the real state of sublunary nature.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Johnson's Preface to Shakespeare (1765) gives this very reason."}, {"q": "In which chapter of Biographia Literaria does Coleridge distinguish primary imagination, secondary imagination and fancy?", "options": ["Chapter IV", "Chapter XIII", "Chapter XIV", "Chapter XVII"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Chapter XIII; Chapter XIV has 'willing suspension of disbelief'."}, {"q": "'Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world' occurs in a work written in reply to:", "options": ["Gosson", "Peacock", "Rymer", "Jeffrey"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Shelley's A Defence of Poetry answered Peacock's The Four Ages of Poetry."}, {"q": "Which poet does Arnold say lacks 'high seriousness' in 'The Study of Poetry'?", "options": ["Milton", "Chaucer", "Shakespeare", "Dante"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Arnold praises Chaucer's liquid diction but denies him high seriousness."}, {"q": "'All art constantly aspires towards the condition of music' is a statement by:", "options": ["Oscar Wilde", "Walter Pater", "Matthew Arnold", "Swinburne"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Pater, 'The School of Giorgione', in The Renaissance."}, {"q": "Match the List I with List II: (a) Objective correlative (b) Pseudo-statement (c) Intentional fallacy (d) Defamiliarization | (i) Shklovsky (ii) Wimsatt and Beardsley (iii) I.A. Richards (iv) T.S. Eliot", "options": ["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Eliot, Richards, Wimsatt-Beardsley and Shklovsky respectively."}, {"q": "Which four novelists form F.R. Leavis's 'great tradition' in the opening of The Great Tradition (1948)?", "options": ["Fielding, Richardson, Austen, Dickens", "Austen, George Eliot, Henry James, Conrad", "Austen, Dickens, Hardy, Lawrence", "George Eliot, Hardy, James, Joyce"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Leavis's opening sentence names Austen, George Eliot, James and Conrad."}, {"q": "In Frye's Anatomy of Criticism, which mythos is associated with autumn?", "options": ["Comedy", "Romance", "Tragedy", "Irony and satire"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Comedy-spring, romance-summer, tragedy-autumn, irony/satire-winter."}]}, {"id": 9, "title": "Literary Theory post World War II", "overview": "Is unit mein World War II ke baad ki literary theory aati hai: Structuralism, Deconstruction, Foucault, Lacan, Marxism, Reader-response, Feminism, Postcolonialism, New Historicism, Ecocriticism, Postmodernism, Bakhtin aur Queer theory. NET mein mostly theorist-work matching, famous quotes, coined terms aur chronology ke sawaal aate hain.", "topics": [{"title": "Structuralism and Narratology: Saussure, Levi-Strauss, Jakobson, Genette", "points": ["Ferdinand de Saussure, Course in General Linguistics (1916): published posthumously by his students Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye from lecture notes.", "Langue (the abstract system of language) vs parole (an individual utterance); structuralism studies langue, not parole.", "The sign = signifier (sound-image) + signified (concept); the bond between them is arbitrary (manmana, prakritik nahi).", "Meaning arises from difference within the system: 'In language there are only differences without positive terms' (Saussure).", "Synchronic (study of language at one moment) vs diachronic (study over time); Saussure privileged the synchronic.", "Syntagmatic relations (combination, horizontal) vs paradigmatic/associative relations (selection, vertical).", "Claude Levi-Strauss, Structural Anthropology (1958): myths analysed into 'mythemes'; read the Oedipus myth through binary oppositions.", "Roman Jakobson: 'Two Aspects of Language' (1956) opposes metaphor (selection/similarity) and metonymy (combination/contiguity); 'Linguistics and Poetics' (1960) gives six functions of language, incl. the poetic function.", "Vladimir Propp, Morphology of the Folktale (1928, English 1958): 31 functions and 7 spheres of action (villain, donor, helper, princess, dispatcher, hero, false hero).", "A. J. Greimas, Structural Semantics (1966): six actants; Tzvetan Todorov coined 'narratology' (Grammaire du Decameron, 1969).", "Gerard Genette, Narrative Discourse (Figures III, 1972): order, duration, frequency, mood, voice; introduced 'focalization'; homodiegetic/heterodiegetic narrators.", "Jonathan Culler, Structuralist Poetics (1975): 'literary competence', the reader's internalised grammar of literary conventions."]}, {"title": "Roland Barthes: from structuralism to post-structuralism", "points": ["Roland Barthes, Mythologies (1957): myth as second-order semiological system (the Black soldier saluting the French flag on Paris-Match).", "'The Death of the Author' (1967 in English in Aspen; French 1968): 'the birth of the reader must be at the cost of the death of the Author'.", "Barthes: a text is 'a tissue of quotations drawn from the innumerable centres of culture'; the author is replaced by the 'scriptor'.", "S/Z (1970): a lexia-by-lexia reading of Balzac's novella Sarrasine using five codes.", "The five codes of S/Z: hermeneutic, proairetic (action), semic, symbolic and cultural (referential).", "S/Z distinguishes the readerly (lisible) text, consumed passively, from the writerly (scriptible) text, produced by the reader.", "'From Work to Text' (1971): the work is held in the hand, the text is held in language; text is plural and a methodological field.", "The Pleasure of the Text (1973): plaisir (comfortable pleasure) vs jouissance (disruptive bliss).", "Camera Lucida (1980): studium vs punctum in photography; A Lover's Discourse (1977); Elements of Semiology (1964)."]}, {"title": "Post-structuralism and Deconstruction: Jacques Derrida", "points": ["Derrida's paper 'Structure, Sign and Play in the Discourse of the Human Sciences' was read at Johns Hopkins University in October 1966; it is taken as the birth of post-structuralism in America.", "In 1967 Derrida published three books: Of Grammatology, Writing and Difference, and Speech and Phenomena.", "Of Grammatology was translated into English by Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak in 1976, with a long translator's preface.", "'There is nothing outside the text' (il n'y a pas de hors-texte) comes from Of Grammatology, in the reading of Rousseau.", "Logocentrism: Western metaphysics' longing for a 'transcendental signified' or 'metaphysics of presence'; phonocentrism privileges speech over writing.", "Differance (with an 'a'): meaning both differs and is endlessly deferred; the 'a' is audible only in writing.", "Key concepts: trace, supplement (Rousseau), aporia, decentring, free play, sous rature (under erasure, borrowed from Heidegger), pharmakon (in 'Plato's Pharmacy', Dissemination, 1972).", "Deconstruction reverses and displaces violent binary hierarchies (speech/writing, presence/absence, man/woman).", "Later works: Dissemination (1972), Margins of Philosophy (1972), Glas (1974), Spectres of Marx (1993), The Gift of Death (1992).", "Yale School: Paul de Man (Blindness and Insight 1971, Allegories of Reading 1979), J. Hillis Miller, Geoffrey Hartman, Harold Bloom; collective volume Deconstruction and Criticism (1979).", "J. Hillis Miller, 'The Critic as Host' (1977); Barbara Johnson, The Critical Difference (1980); Christopher Norris, Deconstruction: Theory and Practice (1982)."]}, {"title": "Michel Foucault: discourse, power, knowledge", "points": ["Major works: Madness and Civilization (Histoire de la folie, 1961), The Birth of the Clinic (1963), The Order of Things (1966), The Archaeology of Knowledge (1969).", "Discipline and Punish: The Birth of the Prison (1975): uses Jeremy Bentham's Panopticon as a model of disciplinary surveillance.", "The History of Sexuality, Vol. 1: The Will to Knowledge (1976): rejects the 'repressive hypothesis'; sexuality is produced by discourse; introduces biopower.", "Episteme: the underlying grid of knowledge of an age (Renaissance, Classical, Modern), set out in The Order of Things.", "The Order of Things ends with the claim that 'man' is a recent invention who may be 'erased, like a face drawn in sand at the edge of the sea'.", "Power is not merely repressive but productive and everywhere; 'power/knowledge' are inseparable (Power/Knowledge, 1980, ed. Colin Gordon).", "'What Is an Author?' (lecture 1969): the 'author-function' as a discursive classification; ends asking 'What difference does it make who is speaking?'", "Methods: archaeology (early) and genealogy (later, from Nietzsche: 'Nietzsche, Genealogy, History', 1971).", "Foucault strongly influenced New Historicism (Greenblatt), Said's Orientalism and queer theory."]}, {"title": "Psychoanalytic criticism: Freud, Jung, Bloom", "points": ["Sigmund Freud, The Interpretation of Dreams (1899, dated 1900): the dream-work operates through condensation, displacement, considerations of representability and secondary revision.", "Freud's topography: id, ego, superego (The Ego and the Id, 1923); Oedipus complex; 'Creative Writers and Day-Dreaming' (1908).", "Freud, 'The Uncanny' (Das Unheimliche, 1919) reads E. T. A. Hoffmann's 'The Sandman'; Beyond the Pleasure Principle (1920) introduces the death drive and the fort-da game.", "Ernest Jones, Hamlet and Oedipus (1949): Hamlet's delay explained by the Oedipus complex.", "Carl Jung: collective unconscious and archetypes (shadow, anima/animus, persona); basis of myth/archetypal criticism (Northrop Frye, Anatomy of Criticism, 1957).", "Harold Bloom, The Anxiety of Influence (1973): the 'strong poet' misreads (misprision) a precursor; six revisionary ratios: clinamen, tessera, kenosis, daemonization, askesis, apophrades.", "Bloom, A Map of Misreading (1975); The Western Canon (1994).", "Norman Holland, The Dynamics of Literary Response (1968) and 5 Readers Reading (1975): psychoanalytic reader-response, 'identity theme'.", "Peter Brooks, Reading for the Plot (1984); Shoshana Felman on The Turn of the Screw; Slavoj Zizek popularises Lacan through popular culture."]}, {"title": "Jacques Lacan", "points": ["Lacan's call was a 'return to Freud' read through Saussurean linguistics; his collected papers are Ecrits (1966).", "'The unconscious is structured like a language' (Lacan); metaphor corresponds to condensation and metonymy to displacement.", "The mirror stage (paper 1936, revised 1949): an infant of about 6-18 months misrecognises (meconnaissance) itself as a unified whole in the mirror; origin of the ego.", "Three orders: the Imaginary, the Symbolic and the Real.", "Entry into the Symbolic order is governed by the Name-of-the-Father (nom du pere); the phallus is the privileged signifier.", "Lacan inverts Saussure's sign: the signifier (S) over the signified (s), with a 'bar' resisting signification; signifiers 'slide' over signifieds.", "'Man's desire is the desire of the Other' (Lacan); objet petit a is the unattainable object-cause of desire.", "'Seminar on The Purloined Letter' (1956, opens Ecrits) reads Poe's tale as the movement of the signifier; Derrida replied in 'The Purveyor of Truth' (1975).", "Lacan's work underlies the French feminists (Kristeva, Irigaray, Cixous) and film theory (Laura Mulvey, 'Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema', 1975, 'the male gaze')."]}, {"title": "Marxist criticism: Althusser, Macherey, Williams and the Frankfurt School", "points": ["Base and superstructure: the economic base determines the cultural superstructure (Marx, Preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, 1859).", "Louis Althusser, 'Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses' (1970): ideology 'interpellates' (hails) individuals as subjects; Repressive State Apparatus vs Ideological State Apparatuses (school, church, family, media).", "Althusser: ideology represents 'the imaginary relationship of individuals to their real conditions of existence'.", "Pierre Macherey, A Theory of Literary Production (1966): a text is produced, not created; read its 'silences' and gaps, the 'not-said'.", "Georg Lukacs: The Theory of the Novel (1916, book 1920), The Historical Novel (1937), favoured critical realism and 'typicality'; opposed modernist experimentalism.", "Antonio Gramsci, Prison Notebooks (written 1929-35): 'hegemony', rule by consent; also the term 'subaltern'.", "Raymond Williams: Culture and Society (1958), The Long Revolution (1961), The Country and the City (1973), Keywords (1976), Marxism and Literature (1977): 'structure of feeling'; dominant, residual and emergent culture; 'culture is ordinary' (1958).", "Frankfurt School: Adorno and Horkheimer, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947), 'the culture industry'; Walter Benjamin, 'The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction' (1935/36), loss of 'aura'.", "Birmingham Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies founded 1964 by Richard Hoggart (The Uses of Literacy, 1957); Stuart Hall, 'Encoding/Decoding' (1973)."]}, {"title": "Marxist criticism: Terry Eagleton and Fredric Jameson", "points": ["Terry Eagleton: Criticism and Ideology (1976), Marxism and Literary Criticism (1976), Literary Theory: An Introduction (1983), The Function of Criticism (1984), The Ideology of the Aesthetic (1990), After Theory (2003).", "Eagleton's Literary Theory: An Introduction (1983) argues literature has no essence and is defined by value-judgements; ends with 'Political Criticism'.", "Eagleton, Criticism and Ideology: categories such as General Mode of Production, Literary Mode of Production, General Ideology, Authorial Ideology, Aesthetic Ideology.", "Fredric Jameson: Marxism and Form (1971), The Prison-House of Language (1972, on structuralism and Russian Formalism).", "Jameson, The Political Unconscious: Narrative as a Socially Symbolic Act (1981): opens with the slogan 'Always historicize!'", "The Political Unconscious proposes three concentric horizons of interpretation: political history, society (class discourse/ideologeme), and history as modes of production.", "Jameson, 'Postmodernism, or, The Cultural Logic of Late Capitalism' (New Left Review, 1984; book 1991): pastiche as 'blank parody', waning of affect, depthlessness, 'cognitive mapping'.", "Jameson, 'Third-World Literature in the Era of Multinational Capital' (1986): third-world texts as 'national allegories'; answered by Aijaz Ahmad, In Theory (1992)."]}, {"title": "Reader-response and Reception theory", "points": ["Reader-response shifts attention from text and author to the reader; it rejects the New Critical 'Affective Fallacy' (Wimsatt and Beardsley, 1949).", "Louise Rosenblatt: Literature as Exploration (1938) and The Reader, the Text, the Poem (1978): 'transactional' theory, efferent vs aesthetic reading.", "Wolfgang Iser (Konstanz School): The Implied Reader (1972, Eng. 1974) and The Act of Reading (1976, Eng. 1978): gaps or blanks, indeterminacy, 'wandering viewpoint', the 'implied reader'.", "Hans Robert Jauss: 'Literary History as a Challenge to Literary Theory' (1967, Konstanz inaugural lecture): 'horizon of expectations' (Erwartungshorizont); 'aesthetic distance'.", "Jauss builds on Hans-Georg Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960): 'fusion of horizons'.", "Stanley Fish: Surprised by Sin: The Reader in Paradise Lost (1967); 'Literature in the Reader: Affective Stylistics' (1970); Self-Consuming Artifacts (1972).", "Fish, Is There a Text in This Class? The Authority of Interpretive Communities (1980): meaning is produced by 'interpretive communities'.", "Umberto Eco, The Role of the Reader (1979): open vs closed texts, 'model reader'; The Open Work (1962).", "David Bleich, Subjective Criticism (1978); Norman Holland's 'identity theme'; Gerald Prince coined 'narratee' (1973); Judith Fetterley's 'resisting reader' (1978).", "Wayne Booth, The Rhetoric of Fiction (1961) coined 'implied author'; Iser's implied reader is its counterpart."]}, {"title": "Feminist criticism: Beauvoir, Showalter, Gilbert and Gubar", "points": ["Simone de Beauvoir, The Second Sex (1949): 'One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman'; woman as the 'Other' to man as Subject.", "Precursors: Mary Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792); Virginia Woolf, A Room of One's Own (1929, 'a woman must have money and a room of her own') and Three Guineas (1938).", "Betty Friedan, The Feminine Mystique (1963); Kate Millett, Sexual Politics (1970) attacks Lawrence, Henry Miller, Norman Mailer and Jean Genet.", "Elaine Showalter, A Literature of Their Own (1977): three phases of women's writing: Feminine (1840-1880), Feminist (1880-1920), Female (1920 onwards).", "Showalter, 'Towards a Feminist Poetics' (1979) coined 'gynocritics/gynocriticism' (woman as writer) as distinct from 'feminist critique' (woman as reader).", "Showalter, 'Feminist Criticism in the Wilderness' (1981): four models of difference: biological, linguistic, psychoanalytic and cultural.", "Sandra Gilbert and Susan Gubar, The Madwoman in the Attic: The Woman Writer and the Nineteenth-Century Literary Imagination (1979); title from Bertha Mason in Jane Eyre.", "Gilbert and Gubar: images of 'angel' and 'monster'; the woman writer's 'anxiety of authorship' (revising Bloom's 'anxiety of influence'); they opened with 'Is a pen a metaphorical penis?'", "Gilbert and Gubar edited The Norton Anthology of Literature by Women (1985).", "Others: Ellen Moers, Literary Women (1976); Adrienne Rich, 'When We Dead Awaken: Writing as Re-Vision' (1971) and 'Compulsory Heterosexuality and Lesbian Existence' (1980); Toril Moi, Sexual/Textual Politics (1985).", "Alice Walker, In Search of Our Mothers' Gardens (1983) defines 'womanism'; bell hooks, Ain't I a Woman (1981); Chandra Talpade Mohanty, 'Under Western Eyes' (1984)."]}, {"title": "French feminism and Judith Butler", "points": ["Helene Cixous, 'The Laugh of the Medusa' (1975): 'Woman must write her self'; coined 'ecriture feminine' (feminine writing), writing 'in white ink'.", "Cixous and Catherine Clement, The Newly Born Woman (1975), containing 'Sorties', which lists patriarchal binaries (activity/passivity, sun/moon, culture/nature, man/woman).", "Julia Kristeva: coined 'intertextuality' in 'Word, Dialogue and Novel' (1966/67), drawing on Bakhtin; Revolution in Poetic Language (1974): the semiotic vs the symbolic; the 'chora' (from Plato's Timaeus).", "Kristeva, Powers of Horror: An Essay on Abjection (1980); Desire in Language (1980); 'Women's Time' (1979).", "Luce Irigaray: Speculum of the Other Woman (1974), which got her expelled from Lacan's Ecole freudienne; This Sex Which Is Not One (1977).", "Irigaray proposes 'parler-femme' (speaking as woman) and strategic 'mimicry' of patriarchal discourse; female sexuality as plural.", "Judith Butler, Gender Trouble: Feminism and the Subversion of Identity (1990): gender is 'performative', 'the repeated stylization of the body'; drag exposes gender as imitation.", "Butler: Bodies That Matter (1993), Excitable Speech (1997), Undoing Gender (2004); she draws on Foucault, Derrida, Austin's speech-act theory and Beauvoir.", "Monique Wittig, 'One Is Not Born a Woman' (1981) and The Straight Mind (1992): the lesbian is not a 'woman'."]}, {"title": "Postcolonialism: Said, Spivak, Bhabha", "points": ["Edward Said, Orientalism (1978): the Orient as a European construction; Orientalism as a Western style 'for dominating, restructuring, and having authority over the Orient'; draws on Foucault and Gramsci.", "Said, Culture and Imperialism (1993): 'contrapuntal reading'; reads Jane Austen's Mansfield Park (Antigua plantation), Kipling's Kim, Camus, Conrad.", "Said's other works: The Question of Palestine (1979), Covering Islam (1981), The World, the Text, and the Critic (1983), Representations of the Intellectual (1994), Out of Place (memoir, 1999).", "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, 'Can the Subaltern Speak?' (1985 in Wedge; expanded 1988 in Marxism and the Interpretation of Culture, eds. Nelson and Grossberg): concludes 'the subaltern cannot speak'.", "In that essay Spivak analyses sati and the formula 'White men are saving brown women from brown men'; she critiques Foucault and Deleuze; 'epistemic violence'.", "Spivak: 'Three Women's Texts and a Critique of Imperialism' (1985, on Jane Eyre, Wide Sargasso Sea and Frankenstein); In Other Worlds (1987); A Critique of Postcolonial Reason (1999); 'strategic essentialism'.", "Homi K. Bhabha, The Location of Culture (1994); edited Nation and Narration (1990).", "Bhabha, 'Of Mimicry and Man' (1984): colonial mimicry produces a subject 'almost the same, but not quite' (and 'almost the same but not white'); cites Macaulay's Minute (1835).", "Bhabha's key concepts: hybridity, ambivalence, mimicry, 'Third Space' of enunciation, 'unhomely'; 'Signs Taken for Wonders' (1985).", "Said, Spivak and Bhabha are called the 'Holy Trinity' of postcolonial theory (Robert Young)."]}, {"title": "Postcolonialism: Fanon, Ngugi and others", "points": ["Frantz Fanon (Martinique, worked in Algeria): Black Skin, White Masks (1952) on the psychology of colonial racism; 'Look, a Negro!'", "Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961), with a preface by Jean-Paul Sartre; defends anti-colonial violence; includes 'On National Culture' and the pitfalls of national consciousness.", "Fanon's three phases of the native intellectual: assimilation, remembering/return to the past, and the fighting (revolutionary) phase.", "Ngugi wa Thiong'o, Decolonising the Mind: The Politics of Language in African Literature (1986): bids farewell to English and commits to Gikuyu; 'language as culture'.", "Ngugi, Owuor-Anyumba and Taban Lo Liyong, 'On the Abolition of the English Department' (1968, Nairobi).", "Ngugi's novels: Weep Not, Child (1964), The River Between (1965), A Grain of Wheat (1967), Petals of Blood (1977), Devil on the Cross (in Gikuyu, 1980); Moving the Centre (1993).", "Chinua Achebe, 'An Image of Africa: Racism in Conrad's Heart of Darkness' (lecture 1975): called Conrad 'a thoroughgoing racist' (earlier 'bloody racist').", "Aime Cesaire, Discourse on Colonialism (1950); Negritude movement of Cesaire, Leopold Senghor and Leon Damas (1930s).", "Ashcroft, Griffiths and Tiffin, The Empire Writes Back (1989): 'abrogation' and 'appropriation' of English; 'english' vs 'English'.", "Others: Ranajit Guha and Subaltern Studies (from 1982); Benedict Anderson, Imagined Communities (1983); Aijaz Ahmad, In Theory (1992); Robert Young, White Mythologies (1990) and Colonial Desire (1995)."]}, {"title": "New Historicism and Cultural Materialism", "points": ["Stephen Greenblatt coined 'New Historicism' in 1982 (introduction to a special issue of Genre on Renaissance forms of power); he later preferred 'cultural poetics' ('Towards a Poetics of Culture', 1987).", "Greenblatt, Renaissance Self-Fashioning: From More to Shakespeare (1980): identity is fashioned within power structures.", "Greenblatt, 'Invisible Bullets' (1981, on Thomas Harriot and Henry IV): the 'subversion-containment' model, authority produces subversion in order to contain it.", "Greenblatt, Shakespearean Negotiations (1988): 'social energy', 'circulation', 'negotiation', 'exchange'; Will in the World (2004); The Swerve (2011).", "Louis Montrose's formula: 'the historicity of texts and the textuality of history'.", "New Historicism reads literary and non-literary texts side by side ('parallel reading'), often beginning with an anecdote; it draws on Foucault and Clifford Geertz's 'thick description' (The Interpretation of Cultures, 1973).", "Hayden White, Metahistory (1973): history as narrative emplotment (romance, tragedy, comedy, satire).", "Cultural Materialism (British): the term comes from Raymond Williams; key text Political Shakespeare (1985), eds. Jonathan Dollimore and Alan Sinfield.", "Political Shakespeare's foreword names four features: historical context, theoretical method, political commitment and textual analysis.", "Jonathan Dollimore, Radical Tragedy: Religion, Ideology and Power in the Drama of Shakespeare and His Contemporaries (1984); Sexual Dissidence (1991); Alan Sinfield, Faultlines (1992).", "Difference: New Historicism (American) stresses containment; Cultural Materialism (British, Marxist) stresses dissidence and present-day political use of texts."]}, {"title": "Ecocriticism", "points": ["The term 'ecocriticism' was coined by William Rueckert in 'Literature and Ecology: An Experiment in Ecocriticism' (1978).", "Cheryll Glotfelty and Harold Fromm (eds), The Ecocriticism Reader (1996): 'ecocriticism is the study of the relationship between literature and the physical environment'.", "ASLE (Association for the Study of Literature and Environment) founded 1992; its journal ISLE began 1993.", "Joseph Meeker, The Comedy of Survival (1972) coined 'literary ecology'; Rachel Carson, Silent Spring (1962) is a founding environmental text.", "Lawrence Buell, The Environmental Imagination (1995) on Thoreau; The Future of Environmental Criticism (2005) describes first-wave and second-wave ecocriticism.", "Jonathan Bate, Romantic Ecology: Wordsworth and the Environmental Tradition (1991) and The Song of the Earth (2000): British 'green studies'.", "Greg Garrard, Ecocriticism (2004, New Critical Idiom): pollution, wilderness, apocalypse, dwelling, animals, the earth.", "Arne Naess coined 'deep ecology' (1973); Francoise d'Eaubonne coined 'ecofeminism' (1974).", "Timothy Morton, Ecology without Nature (2007), 'dark ecology', Hyperobjects (2013); Rob Nixon, Slow Violence and the Environmentalism of the Poor (2011).", "Amitav Ghosh, The Great Derangement: Climate Change and the Unthinkable (2016); Leo Marx, The Machine in the Garden (1964); Raymond Williams, The Country and the City (1973)."]}, {"title": "Postmodernism: Lyotard, Baudrillard, Hutcheon", "points": ["Jean-Francois Lyotard, The Postmodern Condition: A Report on Knowledge (1979; English 1984, foreword by Jameson), written for the Quebec government.", "Lyotard: 'I define postmodern as incredulity toward metanarratives'; prefers 'petits recits' (little narratives), language games (Wittgenstein) and 'paralogy'.", "Jean Baudrillard, Simulacra and Simulation (1981): simulacrum, 'hyperreality', 'the precession of simulacra'; the map precedes the territory (Borges fable).", "Baudrillard's four phases of the image: reflects a basic reality, masks and perverts it, masks its absence, and finally bears no relation to any reality (pure simulacrum).", "Baudrillard: Disneyland is presented as imaginary to make us believe the rest of America is real; The Gulf War Did Not Take Place (1991).", "Linda Hutcheon, A Poetics of Postmodernism (1988) coined 'historiographic metafiction'; The Politics of Postmodernism (1989); Narcissistic Narrative (1980); A Theory of Parody (1985).", "Historiographic metafiction examples: Fowles's The French Lieutenant's Woman, Rushdie's Midnight's Children, Doctorow's Ragtime.", "Ihab Hassan, The Dismemberment of Orpheus (1971) and his table of modernism vs postmodernism; Brian McHale, Postmodernist Fiction (1987): the 'ontological dominant'.", "Jurgen Habermas, 'Modernity: An Incomplete Project' (1980) defends modernity against postmodernism.", "John Barth, 'The Literature of Exhaustion' (1967) and 'The Literature of Replenishment' (1980); Patricia Waugh, Metafiction (1984); Deleuze and Guattari, A Thousand Plateaus (1980): the 'rhizome'."]}, {"title": "Mikhail Bakhtin and the Bakhtin Circle", "points": ["Problems of Dostoevsky's Art (1929), revised as Problems of Dostoevsky's Poetics (1963): the 'polyphonic' novel of independent, unmerged voices.", "Rabelais and His World (written as a 1940 dissertation, published 1965): 'carnivalesque', 'grotesque realism', the grotesque body, folk laughter.", "The Dialogic Imagination (four essays, Russian 1975; English 1981, ed. Michael Holquist, trans. Caryl Emerson and Holquist) includes 'Epic and Novel' and 'Discourse in the Novel'.", "'Discourse in the Novel' (1934-35): 'heteroglossia' (raznorechie), the novel as an orchestration of social languages; centripetal vs centrifugal forces of language.", "'Forms of Time and of the Chronotope in the Novel' (1937-38): 'chronotope', the intrinsic connectedness of time and space in literature.", "Dialogism: every utterance responds to earlier ones and anticipates replies; opposed to the monologic (epic, Tolstoy in Bakhtin's view).", "'Epic and Novel': the epic belongs to an 'absolute past', the novel is the genre of becoming and contact with the open-ended present.", "Bakhtin Circle: V. N. Voloshinov, Marxism and the Philosophy of Language (1929); P. N. Medvedev, The Formal Method in Literary Scholarship (1928); authorship of these is disputed.", "Speech Genres and Other Late Essays (English 1986); Kristeva introduced Bakhtin to the West and derived 'intertextuality' from his dialogism."]}, {"title": "Queer theory", "points": ["The term 'queer theory' was coined by Teresa de Lauretis at a 1990 conference, published in a special issue of differences (1991).", "Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick, Between Men: English Literature and Male Homosocial Desire (1985): 'homosocial desire', erotic triangles (after Rene Girard).", "Sedgwick, Epistemology of the Closet (1990): the homo/heterosexual definition structures modern Western culture; Axiom 1: 'People are different from each other'.", "Judith Butler, Gender Trouble (1990) and Bodies That Matter (1993): gender performativity is foundational to queer theory.", "Foucault, History of Sexuality Vol. 1 (1976): 'The sodomite had been a temporary aberration; the homosexual was now a species'.", "Michael Warner popularised 'heteronormativity' (Fear of a Queer Planet, 1991/1993); Adrienne Rich, 'compulsory heterosexuality' (1980).", "Gayle Rubin, 'The Traffic in Women' (1975, 'sex/gender system') and 'Thinking Sex' (1984); Diana Fuss (ed.), Inside/Out (1991).", "Later queer theory: Lee Edelman, No Future: Queer Theory and the Death Drive (2004, 'reproductive futurism'); Jack Halberstam, Female Masculinity (1998); Jose Esteban Munoz, Cruising Utopia (2009); Jasbir Puar, Terrorist Assemblages (2007, 'homonationalism').", "Queer reading of canon: Sedgwick on Henry James's 'The Beast in the Jungle'; Alan Sinfield, The Wilde Century (1994)."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Langue", "pron": "लॉन्ग (LONG)", "hindi": "भाषा की पूरी व्यवस्था या नियम जो सब बोलने वालों में साझा हैं।", "net": "Saussure's term (Course in General Linguistics, 1916) for the abstract, collective system of rules and conventions of a language, as opposed to parole."}, {"term": "Parole", "pron": "पा-रोल (paa-ROLE)", "hindi": "किसी व्यक्ति का असली बोला या लिखा हुआ वाक्य।", "net": "Saussure's term for the individual, concrete act of speech or utterance drawn from the system of langue."}, {"term": "Signifier", "pron": "सिग्-नि-फ़ा-यर (SIG-ni-fy-er)", "hindi": "शब्द की आवाज़ या लिखा रूप, जैसे 'पेड़' शब्द।", "net": "In Saussure, the material component of the linguistic sign, the sound-image or written mark, joined arbitrarily to a signified."}, {"term": "Signified", "pron": "सिग्-नि-फ़ाइड (SIG-ni-fyed)", "hindi": "शब्द सुनकर मन में आने वाला विचार या अर्थ।", "net": "In Saussure, the conceptual component of the sign, the mental concept evoked by the signifier."}, {"term": "Binary opposition", "pron": "बाइ-न-री ऑ-पो-ज़ि-शन (BY-nuh-ree op-uh-ZI-shun)", "hindi": "दो उल्टे जोड़े, जैसे दिन-रात, पुरुष-स्त्री।", "net": "A pair of opposed terms (nature/culture, speech/writing) through which, for Levi-Strauss and structuralists, meaning is organised; deconstruction exposes their hierarchy."}, {"term": "Mytheme", "pron": "मि-थीम (MITH-eem)", "hindi": "मिथक (कथा) की सबसे छोटी अर्थपूर्ण इकाई।", "net": "Levi-Strauss's term (Structural Anthropology, 1958) for the minimal constituent unit of a myth, analogous to the phoneme in language."}, {"term": "Narratology", "pron": "नै-र-टॉ-लो-जी (nar-uh-TOL-uh-jee)", "hindi": "कहानी कहने की बनावट का शास्त्र।", "net": "The structuralist science of narrative structure; term coined by Tzvetan Todorov (Grammaire du Decameron, 1969); developed by Genette, Greimas, Bal."}, {"term": "Focalization", "pron": "फ़ो-क-लाइ-ज़े-शन (foh-kuh-ly-ZAY-shun)", "hindi": "कहानी किसकी नज़र से देखी जा रही है।", "net": "Genette's term (Narrative Discourse, 1972) for the perspective through which narrated events are perceived (zero, internal, external), distinct from the narrating voice."}, {"term": "Writerly text", "pron": "राइ-टर-ली टेक्स्ट (RY-ter-lee text)", "hindi": "ऐसा पाठ जिसमें पाठक खुद अर्थ बनाता है।", "net": "Barthes's 'scriptible' text (S/Z, 1970), which makes the reader an active producer of meaning, opposed to the 'readerly' (lisible) text consumed passively."}, {"term": "Jouissance", "pron": "ज़ुई-सॉन्स (zhwee-SONSS)", "hindi": "गहरा, बेचैन कर देने वाला आनंद।", "net": "Barthes (The Pleasure of the Text, 1973): blissful, disruptive pleasure that unsettles the reader's cultural assumptions, opposed to plaisir; also used by Lacan and Cixous."}, {"term": "Logocentrism", "pron": "लो-गो-सेन्-ट्रि-ज़्म (loh-goh-SEN-triz-um)", "hindi": "यह मान्यता कि कोई अंतिम, पक्का केंद्र या सत्य है।", "net": "Derrida's term for Western metaphysics' reliance on a 'transcendental signified' or presence (God, reason, origin) that would ground meaning outside the play of signs."}, {"term": "Differance", "pron": "डि-फ़े-रॉन्स (dee-fay-RONSS)", "hindi": "अर्थ हमेशा फ़र्क से बनता है और आगे टलता रहता है।", "net": "Derrida's coinage combining French 'to differ' and 'to defer': meaning arises from difference and is perpetually postponed; the 'a' is visible only in writing."}, {"term": "Aporia", "pron": "अ-पो-रि-आ (uh-POR-ee-uh)", "hindi": "ऐसी उलझन जिसका कोई हल न निकले।", "net": "Greek 'impasse'; in deconstruction (Derrida, de Man) an undecidable point where a text's logic contradicts itself and meaning cannot be resolved."}, {"term": "Sous rature", "pron": "सू रा-त्यूर (soo ra-TOOR)", "hindi": "शब्द को काटकर भी लिखा रहने देना।", "net": "'Under erasure': Derrida's practice, from Heidegger, of writing a word and crossing it out, since it is inadequate yet necessary."}, {"term": "Episteme", "pron": "ए-पि-स्टेम (EP-i-steem)", "hindi": "किसी युग की सोच का छिपा ढाँचा।", "net": "Foucault (The Order of Things, 1966): the underlying configuration of knowledge that determines what can be thought and said in a given epoch."}, {"term": "Discourse", "pron": "डिस्-कोर्स (DIS-korss)", "hindi": "किसी विषय पर बोलने-सोचने का तय तरीका जो सत्ता से जुड़ा है।", "net": "In Foucault, a historically specific system of statements and practices that produces knowledge and its objects and is inseparable from power."}, {"term": "Panopticon", "pron": "पै-नॉप्-टि-कॉन (pan-OP-ti-kon)", "hindi": "ऐसी जेल जहाँ हर कैदी को लगे कि उसे हमेशा देखा जा रहा है।", "net": "Jeremy Bentham's prison design, used by Foucault in Discipline and Punish (1975) as the model of disciplinary power through constant visibility and self-surveillance."}, {"term": "Mirror stage", "pron": "मि-रर स्टेज (MIR-er stayj)", "hindi": "बच्चा शीशे में खुद को पहली बार पूरा देखकर 'मैं' बनाता है।", "net": "Lacan's concept (1936; 1949 paper in Ecrits): the infant's misrecognition of its unified reflection, founding the ego in the Imaginary order."}, {"term": "Uncanny", "pron": "अन्-कै-नी (un-KAN-ee)", "hindi": "जानी-पहचानी चीज़ जो अचानक डरावनी लगे।", "net": "Freud's 'Das Unheimliche' (1919): the frightening that leads back to the familiar, the return of the repressed; illustrated through Hoffmann's 'The Sandman'."}, {"term": "Interpellation", "pron": "इन्-टर-पे-ले-शन (in-ter-puh-LAY-shun)", "hindi": "समाज की विचारधारा का हमें 'पुकार' कर एक पहचान दे देना।", "net": "Althusser ('Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses', 1970): the process by which ideology 'hails' individuals and constitutes them as subjects."}, {"term": "Ideological State Apparatus", "pron": "आइ-डि-यो-लॉ-जि-कल स्टेट ऐ-प-रे-टस", "hindi": "स्कूल, धर्म, परिवार जैसे संस्थान जो सोच गढ़ते हैं।", "net": "Althusser's term for institutions (school, church, family, media, culture) that secure domination by ideology, as distinct from the Repressive State Apparatus (police, army)."}, {"term": "Hegemony", "pron": "हे-जे-मो-नी (hi-JEM-uh-nee)", "hindi": "ज़बरदस्ती नहीं, सहमति से चलने वाला प्रभुत्व।", "net": "Gramsci (Prison Notebooks): domination of a ruling class achieved through cultural consent rather than force alone."}, {"term": "Structure of feeling", "pron": "स्ट्रक्-चर ऑफ़ फ़ी-लिंग", "hindi": "किसी दौर का साझा अनुभव और भावनाओं का माहौल।", "net": "Raymond Williams (The Long Revolution 1961; Marxism and Literature 1977): the lived, emergent experience of a period, felt before it is fully articulated."}, {"term": "Horizon of expectations", "pron": "हो-राइ-ज़न ऑफ़ एक्स्-पेक्-टे-शन्स", "hindi": "किसी समय के पाठकों की पहले से बनी उम्मीदें।", "net": "Jauss (1967): the set of cultural and literary norms and assumptions against which readers of a given period receive and judge a work."}, {"term": "Implied reader", "pron": "इम्-प्लाइड री-डर (im-PLYD REE-der)", "hindi": "वह पाठक जिसकी कल्पना पाठ खुद करता है।", "net": "Iser (The Implied Reader, 1972): the reader role inscribed in the text's structure, who fills its gaps; parallels Booth's 'implied author'."}, {"term": "Interpretive community", "pron": "इन्-टर-प्रि-टिव क-म्यू-नि-टी", "hindi": "पाठकों का समूह जो एक जैसे नियमों से अर्थ निकालता है।", "net": "Stanley Fish (Is There a Text in This Class?, 1980): shared interpretive strategies of a community that produce, rather than discover, the meaning of texts."}, {"term": "Gynocriticism", "pron": "गाइ-नो-क्रि-टि-सि-ज़्म (guy-noh-KRIT-i-siz-um)", "hindi": "स्त्री लेखिकाओं और उनकी परंपरा का अध्ययन।", "net": "Elaine Showalter's term ('Towards a Feminist Poetics', 1979) for criticism concerned with woman as writer: female literary history, themes, genres and creativity."}, {"term": "Ecriture feminine", "pron": "ए-क्री-त्यूर फ़े-मि-नीन (ay-kree-TOOR fay-mee-NEEN)", "hindi": "स्त्री के शरीर और अनुभव से निकली लेखन शैली।", "net": "'Feminine writing': Helene Cixous ('The Laugh of the Medusa', 1975); writing that inscribes the female body and difference, disrupting phallogocentric language."}, {"term": "Abjection", "pron": "ऐब्-जेक्-शन (ab-JEK-shun)", "hindi": "जिसे घिन या डर से अपने से दूर फेंक दिया जाए।", "net": "Kristeva (Powers of Horror, 1980): the casting off of what threatens the boundary of the self (bodily waste, the corpse, the maternal)."}, {"term": "Intertextuality", "pron": "इन्-टर-टेक्स्-चु-ऐ-लि-टी", "hindi": "हर पाठ दूसरे पाठों से जुड़ा और बुना होता है।", "net": "Term coined by Julia Kristeva ('Word, Dialogue and Novel', 1966/67) from Bakhtin's dialogism: every text is 'a mosaic of quotations', the absorption of another text."}, {"term": "Performativity", "pron": "पर्-फ़ॉर्-म-टि-वि-टी", "hindi": "लिंग पहचान बार-बार किए गए व्यवहार से बनती है।", "net": "Judith Butler (Gender Trouble, 1990): gender is not an essence but produced by repeated, stylized acts; drawn from Austin's speech-act theory."}, {"term": "Orientalism", "pron": "ओ-रि-एन्-ट-लि-ज़्म (or-ee-EN-tuh-liz-um)", "hindi": "पश्चिम द्वारा पूरब की बनाई गई झूठी छवि।", "net": "Edward Said (Orientalism, 1978): a Western discourse and 'style for dominating, restructuring, and having authority over the Orient'."}, {"term": "Subaltern", "pron": "सब्-ऑल्-टर्न (sub-AWL-tern)", "hindi": "समाज का दबा हुआ वर्ग जिसकी आवाज़ नहीं सुनी जाती।", "net": "Gramsci's term for subordinated groups, used by Ranajit Guha's Subaltern Studies and Spivak ('Can the Subaltern Speak?', 1988)."}, {"term": "Strategic essentialism", "pron": "स्ट्र-टी-जिक इ-सेन्-श-लि-ज़्म", "hindi": "लड़ाई के लिए कुछ समय एक पहचान के नाम पर एकजुट होना।", "net": "Spivak's term: the provisional, tactical adoption of an essential group identity for political ends, while knowing it is constructed."}, {"term": "Hybridity", "pron": "हाइ-ब्रि-डि-टी (hy-BRID-i-tee)", "hindi": "दो संस्कृतियों के मेल से बनी नई पहचान।", "net": "Homi Bhabha (The Location of Culture, 1994): the mixing of coloniser and colonised cultures, which unsettles colonial authority's claim to purity."}, {"term": "Mimicry", "pron": "मि-मि-क्री (MIM-ik-ree)", "hindi": "शासक की नकल जो पूरी कभी नहीं होती।", "net": "Bhabha ('Of Mimicry and Man', 1984): the colonised subject as 'almost the same, but not quite', an ambivalence that both secures and menaces colonial power."}, {"term": "Abrogation", "pron": "ऐ-ब्रो-गे-शन (ab-ruh-GAY-shun)", "hindi": "शुद्ध अंग्रेज़ी के अधिकार को नकारना।", "net": "Ashcroft, Griffiths and Tiffin (The Empire Writes Back, 1989): the rejection of a 'standard' English, followed by 'appropriation', its remoulding to new usages."}, {"term": "Thick description", "pron": "थिक डिस्-क्रिप्-शन", "hindi": "किसी घटना का उसके पूरे सांस्कृतिक संदर्भ में विस्तार से वर्णन।", "net": "Clifford Geertz (The Interpretation of Cultures, 1973), after Gilbert Ryle: description of behaviour along with its cultural meaning; adopted by New Historicists."}, {"term": "Ecocriticism", "pron": "ई-को-क्रि-टि-सि-ज़्म (ee-koh-KRIT-i-siz-um)", "hindi": "साहित्य और प्रकृति-पर्यावरण के संबंध का अध्ययन।", "net": "Term coined by William Rueckert (1978); defined by Cheryll Glotfelty (1996) as 'the study of the relationship between literature and the physical environment'."}, {"term": "Metanarrative", "pron": "मे-टा-नै-र-टिव (MET-uh-nar-uh-tiv)", "hindi": "सब कुछ समझाने का दावा करने वाली बड़ी कहानी, जैसे प्रगति।", "net": "Lyotard (The Postmodern Condition, 1979): grand narratives that legitimate knowledge (emancipation, progress, Hegelian spirit); postmodernism is 'incredulity toward' them."}, {"term": "Simulacrum", "pron": "सि-म्यू-ले-क्रम (sim-yuh-LAY-krum)", "hindi": "ऐसी नकल जिसका कोई असली मूल हो ही नहीं।", "net": "Baudrillard (Simulacra and Simulation, 1981): a copy without an original; the final phase of the image, bearing no relation to any reality."}, {"term": "Hyperreality", "pron": "हाइ-पर-रि-ऐ-लि-टी", "hindi": "नकली चीज़ें असली से भी ज़्यादा असली लगने लगें।", "net": "Baudrillard's term for a condition in which simulations replace and precede the real, e.g. Disneyland, media images."}, {"term": "Historiographic metafiction", "pron": "हिस्-टो-रि-यो-ग्रा-फ़िक मे-टा-फ़िक्-शन", "hindi": "इतिहास पर लिखा उपन्यास जो खुद पर भी सवाल उठाए।", "net": "Linda Hutcheon (A Poetics of Postmodernism, 1988): self-reflexive novels that use historical events and figures while questioning how history is known and written."}, {"term": "Pastiche", "pron": "पै-स्टीश (pa-STEESH)", "hindi": "पुरानी शैलियों की बिना व्यंग्य की नकल।", "net": "Imitation of styles; for Jameson (1984) the postmodern 'blank parody', a neutral mimicry lacking parody's satiric impulse."}, {"term": "Heteroglossia", "pron": "हे-ट-रो-ग्लॉ-सि-आ (het-uh-roh-GLOSS-ee-uh)", "hindi": "एक भाषा के भीतर कई सामाजिक बोलियाँ और आवाज़ें।", "net": "Bakhtin ('Discourse in the Novel', 1934-35): the coexistence of diverse social languages and speech types within a language, orchestrated in the novel."}, {"term": "Polyphony", "pron": "प-लि-फ़ो-नी (puh-LIF-uh-nee)", "hindi": "उपन्यास में कई स्वतंत्र आवाज़ें, लेखक की आवाज़ हावी नहीं।", "net": "Bakhtin (Problems of Dostoevsky's Poetics, 1929/1963): a plurality of independent, unmerged voices and consciousnesses, as in Dostoevsky."}, {"term": "Carnivalesque", "pron": "कार्-नि-व-लेस्क (kar-ni-vuh-LESK)", "hindi": "मेले जैसी उलट-पलट जहाँ ऊँच-नीच मिट जाए।", "net": "Bakhtin (Rabelais and His World, 1965): literary mode drawn from folk carnival that subverts hierarchy through laughter, inversion and the grotesque body."}, {"term": "Chronotope", "pron": "क्रॉ-नो-टोप (KRON-uh-tope)", "hindi": "कहानी में समय और स्थान का आपसी जुड़ाव।", "net": "Bakhtin ('Forms of Time and of the Chronotope in the Novel', 1937-38): 'time-space', the intrinsic connectedness of temporal and spatial relationships in literature."}, {"term": "Homosocial desire", "pron": "हो-मो-सो-शल डि-ज़ा-यर", "hindi": "पुरुषों के आपसी रिश्ते जो अक्सर स्त्री के ज़रिए बनते हैं।", "net": "Sedgwick (Between Men, 1985): the continuum of male bonds, often routed through a woman in an 'erotic triangle'."}, {"term": "Heteronormativity", "pron": "हे-ट-रो-नॉर्-म-टि-वि-टी", "hindi": "यह मानना कि सिर्फ़ स्त्री-पुरुष संबंध ही सामान्य है।", "net": "Term popularised by Michael Warner (Fear of a Queer Planet, 1991/1993): institutions and assumptions that make heterosexuality the norm."}], "mcqs": [{"q": "Who wrote 'Structure, Sign and Play in the Discourse of the Human Sciences'?", "options": ["Roland Barthes", "Jacques Derrida", "Michel Foucault", "Claude Levi-Strauss"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Derrida read it at Johns Hopkins in 1966."}, {"q": "'There is nothing outside the text' appears in:", "options": ["Writing and Difference", "Dissemination", "Of Grammatology", "Margins of Philosophy"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Derrida's 'il n'y a pas de hors-texte' is from Of Grammatology (1967)."}, {"q": "Match the critics with their works: (a) Iser (b) Fish (c) Jauss (d) Rosenblatt -- (i) Is There a Text in This Class? (ii) The Act of Reading (iii) The Reader, the Text, the Poem (iv) Toward an Aesthetic of Reception", "options": ["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii", "a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv", "a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Iser: Act of Reading; Fish: interpretive communities; Jauss: reception aesthetics; Rosenblatt: transactional theory."}, {"q": "Arrange in chronological order: (i) Orientalism (ii) The Second Sex (iii) Gender Trouble (iv) Of Grammatology", "options": ["ii, iv, i, iii", "iv, ii, i, iii", "ii, i, iv, iii", "i, ii, iv, iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "1949, 1967, 1978, 1990."}, {"q": "'Incredulity toward metanarratives' is Lyotard's definition of:", "options": ["Modernism", "Deconstruction", "Postmodern", "Structuralism"], "answer": 2, "exp": "The Postmodern Condition (1979)."}, {"q": "Who coined the term 'historiographic metafiction'?", "options": ["Brian McHale", "Linda Hutcheon", "Patricia Waugh", "Ihab Hassan"], "answer": 1, "exp": "A Poetics of Postmodernism (1988)."}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the five codes in Barthes's S/Z?", "options": ["Hermeneutic", "Proairetic", "Semic", "Mimetic"], "answer": 3, "exp": "The codes are hermeneutic, proairetic, semic, symbolic and cultural."}, {"q": "The phrase 'Always historicize!' opens:", "options": ["Marxism and Form", "The Political Unconscious", "Criticism and Ideology", "Marxism and Literature"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Jameson, The Political Unconscious (1981)."}, {"q": "Pierre Macherey's A Theory of Literary Production emphasises:", "options": ["The organic unity of the text", "The 'not-said' and silences of the text", "The author's intention", "The horizon of expectations"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Macherey reads gaps and absences produced by ideology."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): For Lacan the unconscious is structured like a language. Reason (R): Lacan reinterpreted Freudian condensation and displacement as metaphor and metonymy.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Lacan's linguistic reading of dream-work explains his claim."}, {"q": "The 'mirror stage' is associated with:", "options": ["Freud", "Jung", "Lacan", "Kristeva"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Lacan's paper of 1936/1949, reprinted in Ecrits."}, {"q": "Elaine Showalter's three phases of women's writing in A Literature of Their Own are:", "options": ["Feminine, Feminist, Female", "Female, Feminine, Feminist", "Feminist, Female, Feminine", "Gynocritic, Feminist, Female"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Feminine (1840-80), Feminist (1880-1920), Female (1920-)."}, {"q": "The Madwoman in the Attic was written by:", "options": ["Kate Millett", "Ellen Moers", "Sandra Gilbert and Susan Gubar", "Toril Moi"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Published 1979; title from Bertha Mason in Jane Eyre."}, {"q": "'One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman' was written by:", "options": ["Virginia Woolf", "Simone de Beauvoir", "Luce Irigaray", "Judith Butler"], "answer": 1, "exp": "The Second Sex (1949)."}, {"q": "'The Laugh of the Medusa' and ecriture feminine are associated with:", "options": ["Julia Kristeva", "Helene Cixous", "Monique Wittig", "Luce Irigaray"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Cixous, 1975."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Kristeva (b) Irigaray (c) Butler (d) Cixous -- (i) This Sex Which Is Not One (ii) Powers of Horror (iii) The Newly Born Woman (iv) Bodies That Matter", "options": ["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii", "a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii", "a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Kristeva: abjection; Irigaray: 1977; Butler: 1993; Cixous with Clement: 1975."}, {"q": "Who coined the term 'intertextuality'?", "options": ["Mikhail Bakhtin", "Roland Barthes", "Julia Kristeva", "Gerard Genette"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Kristeva in 'Word, Dialogue and Novel', drawing on Bakhtin."}, {"q": "'Can the Subaltern Speak?' was written by:", "options": ["Homi K. Bhabha", "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak", "Ranajit Guha", "Partha Chatterjee"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Spivak, 1985/1988."}, {"q": "Colonial mimicry as 'almost the same, but not quite' is a concept of:", "options": ["Edward Said", "Frantz Fanon", "Homi K. Bhabha", "Aijaz Ahmad"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Bhabha, 'Of Mimicry and Man' (1984), in The Location of Culture."}, {"q": "'Contrapuntal reading' is proposed by Edward Said in:", "options": ["Orientalism", "Culture and Imperialism", "The World, the Text, and the Critic", "Covering Islam"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Culture and Imperialism (1993), e.g. reading Mansfield Park."}, {"q": "Match: (a) Fanon (b) Ngugi (c) Said (d) Ashcroft et al. -- (i) Decolonising the Mind (ii) The Empire Writes Back (iii) The Wretched of the Earth (iv) Orientalism", "options": ["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii", "a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii", "a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii", "a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Fanon 1961; Ngugi 1986; Said 1978; Ashcroft, Griffiths, Tiffin 1989."}, {"q": "The term 'New Historicism' was coined by:", "options": ["Louis Montrose", "Stephen Greenblatt", "Jonathan Dollimore", "Hayden White"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Greenblatt in 1982; he later preferred 'cultural poetics'."}, {"q": "Political Shakespeare (1985), the key text of Cultural Materialism, was edited by:", "options": ["Greenblatt and Gallagher", "Dollimore and Sinfield", "Belsey and Moore", "Williams and Eagleton"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Jonathan Dollimore and Alan Sinfield."}, {"q": "The term 'ecocriticism' was first used by:", "options": ["Cheryll Glotfelty", "Lawrence Buell", "William Rueckert", "Jonathan Bate"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Rueckert, 'Literature and Ecology: An Experiment in Ecocriticism' (1978)."}, {"q": "Which of the following is associated with Jean Baudrillard?", "options": ["Paralogy", "Hyperreality", "Rhizome", "Cognitive mapping"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Simulacra and Simulation (1981); paralogy is Lyotard, rhizome Deleuze-Guattari, cognitive mapping Jameson."}, {"q": "Which concept is NOT associated with Mikhail Bakhtin?", "options": ["Chronotope", "Heteroglossia", "Carnivalesque", "Differance"], "answer": 3, "exp": "Differance is Derrida's."}, {"q": "Who wrote Epistemology of the Closet (1990)?", "options": ["Judith Butler", "Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick", "Teresa de Lauretis", "Michael Warner"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Sedgwick; she also wrote Between Men (1985)."}, {"q": "'Horizon of expectations' is a key concept of:", "options": ["Wolfgang Iser", "Hans Robert Jauss", "Stanley Fish", "Norman Holland"], "answer": 1, "exp": "Jauss, Konstanz lecture of 1967."}, {"q": "Discipline and Punish uses which model of surveillance?", "options": ["Bentham's Panopticon", "Plato's cave", "Hobbes's Leviathan", "Rousseau's social contract"], "answer": 0, "exp": "Foucault (1975) took the Panopticon from Jeremy Bentham."}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Althusser argues that ideology interpellates individuals as subjects. Reason (R): For Althusser, ideology works only through the Repressive State Apparatus such as police and army.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "answer": 2, "exp": "Ideology works chiefly through Ideological State Apparatuses (school, church, family), not the RSA."}]}];